    "id": "seed_botwbettervr_case",
    "type": "case_study",
    "title": "The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild — BetterVR (Cemu-emulator Vulkan-layer VR mod with swing/stab combat detection)",
    "game": "The Legend of Zelda: Breath of the Wild (Wii U)",
    "engineFamily": "Wii U proprietary engine (custom C++, largely un-decompiled — a partial community decompilation is referenced but not required), run under the Cemu Wii U emulator",
    "renderAPI": "Vulkan (Cemu's own renderer), bridged to D3D12 purely for headset submission via Vulkan<->D3D12 interop",
    "injectionMethod": "A Vulkan layer DLL intercepts Cemu's own Vulkan command submission (the emulator, not the game, is the hook target), combined with hand-written PowerPC assembly patches injected into the emulated Wii U game code to drive camera, hand, and attack behavior every frame",
    "platform": "PC VR, Windows only (explicitly no Linux/Wine/Proton path), any OpenXR runtime with enough controller buttons",
    "status": "proven",
    "features": [
      "handsControls",
      "weaponAiming",
      "meleeCombat"
    ],
    "summary": "The deepest embodied-interaction entry in this registry: full tracked hands and arms with equipment-visible clothing, wielded weapons/torches/even enemy limbs, gesture-based equip and throw, motion-control puzzle interaction, and a purpose-built swing-and-stab detection system that scores real controller motion against expected attack windows instead of just remapping a button to a canned animation.",
    "details": "Two techniques here generalize well beyond this one game. First, the rendering bridge: because Cemu wasn't open source when this project started, VR support ships as an external Vulkan-intercepting DLL rather than a Cemu fork (contrast with the PrimedGun/Dolphin-ReduX entry in this registry, which took the fork route instead) — and because OpenXR frameworks aren't designed to be instantiated from inside a Vulkan-command-intercepting hook, the mod pipes the captured frame from Vulkan into a separate D3D12 context solely to hand it to the OpenXR compositor, a concrete pattern for 'the API you need to talk to the compositor in isn't the API the target actually renders in, so bridge between them' that could apply to other Vulkan-only engines needing OpenXR (most runtimes assume D3D11/D3D12). Second, the combat system: stereo alone required PowerPC assembly patches directly in the emulated Wii U game code (render two frames before advancing game state once per real frame, move camera/hand-model positions each frame, trigger attacks off GPU-clear instructions the hook can detect) — the author calls reverse-engineering those exact patch points, against a still-largely-undecompiled engine, the single most time-consuming part of the entire project, more than the rendering bridge. The swing/stab detector specifically classifies real hand motion against expected thresholds so a weapon's hitbox can't be cheesed by holding it stationary against an enemy, while still keeping a plain attack-button fallback for players who don't want the motion requirement — a directly reusable design answer to 'how do I make melee feel physical without letting players skip the physicality for free damage.'",
    "blockers": "Windows-only, no Linux/Wine/Proton support at all; CPU-single-thread-bound performance profile since Cemu itself (not the VR layer) is the bottleneck, so GPU choice matters less than single-core CPU strength; tightly version-coupled to Cemu (only 2.6 tested) and to this one game's reverse-engineered assembly patch points, so the technique does not automatically transfer to other titles run under Cemu without redoing that reverse-engineering from scratch; occasional black-screen-after-menu bug requiring a restart.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/Crementif/BotW-BetterVR, https://github.com/zeldaret/botw",
    "submittedBy": "seed",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-05"
