    "game": "Hogwarts Legacy",
    "engineFamily": "Unreal Engine 4",
    "renderAPI": "DX11/DX12 via UEVR",
    "injectionMethod": "A community fork that layers a full inverse-kinematics player body onto an existing base UEVR Lua profile (HL_UEVR by jbusfield, Pande4360, and DJ), reusing jbusfield's shared uevrlib IK helper rather than writing a bespoke solve for this game's skeleton",
    "platform": "PC VR",
    "status": "alpha",
    "features": [
      "bodyIK",
      "handsControls",
      "weaponAiming"
    ],
    "summary": "A clean example of the incremental-fork pattern that recurs across several entries in this registry (BotW-BetterVR/Dolphin-ReduX, TPVR/Dusklight): rather than building a new profile from scratch, one contributor takes an already-working camera-and-wand base profile and adds one specific, hard feature — a visible, IK-driven player body — as a separate, independently-distributed fork layered on top.",
    "details": "The base HL_UEVR profile already solves the two prerequisites this fork depends on: a motion-controller-linked wand with true 6DOF (target highlighting is computed from a raycast along the controller's live physical pointing direction rather than gaze direction or a fixed reticle), and a dedicated \"wand gesture mode\" — toggled with its own hotkey — that reinterprets the same physical swing as spell-casting gesture input instead of plain pointing, with a companion debug view that overlays the recognized glyph shape on the actual swing path so gesture thresholds can be tuned against real player movement instead of guessed at blind. The IK-body fork adds a visible player body driven by the same head-plus-two-hand VR poses the base profile already tracks, solved through the two/three-point IK machinery in jbusfield's shared uevrlib — the same helper library underlying the hand/IK features in the Dead Island 2 and Subnautica 2 entries elsewhere in this registry — meaning this isn't a bespoke Hogwarts-Legacy-specific IK solve but an application of one general-purpose UEVR Lua IK helper to a different game's own skeleton, exactly the reusability the shared-library pattern entry in this registry describes. Because it's distributed as an independent community add-on (announced and shared via Discord, not merged into the base profile's own repository or release cadence) rather than a single maintained project with its own versioning, keeping the two in sync is a manual player-side step: the base profile's own install instructions explicitly warn to delete the entire existing profile folder before installing the IK fork's zip on top, since the two aren't designed to layer via a partial file overwrite.",
    "blockers": "Not distributed through the same easy-installer tooling (Oduis's Easy Injector) the base profile supports — has to be installed manually into the UEVR per-game profile folder. As an independent fork with no formal integration back into the base profile, a future HL_UEVR update could change or remove something the IK fork depends on without coordination between the two projects.",
