    "id": "seed_palworld_uevr_case",
    "type": "case_study",
    "title": "Palworld — UEVR first-person / aiming / weapon-hold profile",
    "game": "Palworld",
    "engineFamily": "Unreal Engine 5",
    "renderAPI": "UEVR-managed DX11/DX12 native stereo (title/runtime dependent)",
    "injectionMethod": "UEVR profile + Lua scripting + UObjectHook-based object manipulation",
    "platform": "PC VR",
    "status": "alpha",
    "features": [
      "handsControls",
      "weaponAiming"
    ],
    "summary": "A compact UEVR profile focused on converting Palworld from its normal third-person presentation into a more usable first-person VR experience, with aim and weapon-hold corrections and player-body hiding.",
    "details": "The public repository is primarily a UEVR profile rather than a standalone VR engine. Its tree contains scripts/, uobjecthook/, camera/config data, controller action bindings for Oculus Touch, Vive and Knuckles, and the normal UEVR profile artifacts. The author documents four concrete goals: enable first-person mode, improve aiming, change how the weapon is held in VR, and hide the character body for immersion. The reusable implementation pattern is therefore UEVR object-level manipulation rather than renderer replacement: acquire the Palworld pawn/camera/weapon objects through UEVR and UObjectHook, alter the first-person camera/object presentation, and let UEVR continue to own stereo, HMD pose and controller pose. The separate binding JSON files are important because the profile is meant to survive across controller interaction profiles rather than hard-code one physical controller. Because the repository exposes both scripts and a UObjectHook area, it is a useful example of escalating from ordinary Lua/profile configuration to engine-object hooks when a property or component cannot be controlled through the standard profile surface. The README does not establish a custom native renderer or a bespoke OpenXR submission path; do not infer one from the presence of UObjectHook. For an LLM, the safest model is: generic UEVR provides camera/stereo/tracking → profile resolves the Palworld pawn/weapon objects → scripts force first-person/camera state and correct weapon transforms → optional UObjectHook/native hook handles properties that Lua alone cannot reliably change → hide the third-person body/mesh when it intersects the HMD view.",
    "blockers": "The project is explicitly a profile still under development. Public documentation does not fully enumerate the exact UObjectHook functions or every camera/weapon property changed, so those details should be recovered from the current scripts/uobjecthook files before treating them as canonical API names.",
