    "summary": "A full gameplay-oriented Halo CE VR conversion with synchronized stereo, physical weapon/grenade aiming, two-handed weapons, holsters, motion melee, flashlight gestures and vehicle controls.",
    "details": "HaloCEWXR is useful because it demonstrates the feature stack expected after stereo is solved. It advertises synchronized 6DoF stereo rather than an alternate-eye approximation, tracked controllers, independent weapon and grenade aiming, functional picture-in-picture scopes, two-handed aiming, rebindable controls including a left-handed preset, shoulder weapon holsters, hand-to-hand weapon swapping, detached floating UI, a floating crosshair, joystick-steered vehicles and a desktop mirror. Melee and interaction intentionally remain head-aimed in the current implementation, which is an important distinction: not every action has to be converted to controller-origin traces if the native game interaction semantics are still tied to the camera. The scope path preserves an in-world PiP scope rather than forcing the entire screen into a zoomed view. The project is a useful reference for weapon ownership state: the VR layer must know which hand owns a weapon, when it is holstered, when a second hand is supporting it, and which native action should be synthesized for legacy systems. Vehicle entry/exit is called out as a camera lifecycle edge case, showing that stereo camera state often needs explicit attach/detach transitions around vehicle cameras.",
