    "summary": "An experimental native injector that locates the player coordinate block at runtime, injects a VR camera, renders hands at tracked controller poses and reconstructs held items from the live first-person render.",
    "details": "HytaleVRInjector is a useful modern example of an external/native injector whose runtime workflow starts from observable engine state rather than hard-coded UI assumptions. The dashboard asks the game to display a player coordinate block, scans it, lets the user select the detected block and then performs Center VR/injection. The repository contains the dashboard, VR hook, probes, shared headers, UI scaling hook, tests, OpenVR headers/import library, MinHook and JSON support. The UI scaling component is a separate OpenGL hook, so camera/VR injection and UI correction remain independently deployable. Tracked SteamVR controller poses drive rendered avatar hands. The mod then detects Hytale first-person items/tools/weapons/blocks/decorations/shields/torches from the live render and rebuilds them from assets shipped by the game, maintaining correct hand attachment through short gaps caused by native animations. Advanced options expose hand/item scale, position, orientation and depth tolerance. Stereo projection uses SteamVR per-eye projection and depth-based AFW disparity. The dashboard exposes supersampling, sharpening and VR FXAA, while temporal DLSS/FSR/XeSS/TAA are deliberately not exposed because the injector does not have access to the game's motion vectors and temporal history. This is a strong implementation lesson: do not claim temporal reconstruction fixes unless your hook actually owns the engine's history/motion-vector resources. The mod also provides an IPD/stereo separation control and externalizes async reprojection/motion smoothing to SteamVR.",
    "blockers": "Experimental and version-sensitive. Current releases are Windows x64/OpenVR-oriented and rely on runtime scanning/selected coordinate data; renderer internals can change without notice.",
