    "summary": "A weapon system should distinguish the visual weapon pose, aim origin, projectile direction and game-native firing event instead of assuming they are one transform.",
    "details": "Maintain separate weaponGripPose, weaponMuzzlePose, opticalAimAxis, aimOrigin, projectileDirection and native fire event. Controller tracking can own the physical weapon while the game remains authoritative for ammo, damage, spread and hit processing. CyberpunkVR Port demonstrates a particularly reusable hitscan solution: call/retain the game's own ray generation, move only the accepted local-player ray origin to the live muzzle, compute the rotation from the game's original camera cone centre to the barrel axis, and rotate each already-spread raw ray by that delta. A single bullet exits exactly on the barrel while shotgun pellets keep their native distribution instead of collapsing to one point. Snapshot the cone centre and muzzle axis together so a frame boundary cannot split one fire evaluation across two coordinate states. For sights, derive a real optical frame/axis; for two-hand weapons, treat support-hand influence as a constraint/leverage term rather than replacing the primary grip transform.",
    "blockers": "Some games hardcode weapon fire to the camera or animation state. If the native firing method cannot accept a direction/origin, a visual weapon can be made physical without achieving true physical ballistics.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/dariulone/cyberpunk-vr-port",
    "submittedBy": "source-audit",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-05",
    "votes": 0
