    "summary": "A reusable melee model that derives contact from controller/weapon motion while leaving damage, stamina, reactions and networking in the game-native combat layer.",
    "details": "Treat the held weapon as a spatial hit shape driven by tracked motion, then pass accepted contact back into the game's native combat layer. Continuous sweep/CCD is ideal, but CyberpunkVR Port demonstrates a second viable shape for games whose target system is easier to query than their physics callbacks: gate on real weapon speed, model the blade as a finite segment/radius, query candidate NPCs, measure body samples against that segment, choose the nearest valid contact and keep per-target cooldown state. Then instantiate/call the game's own melee Attack/GameEffect or authoritative damage path so hit reactions, armor, crits, VFX/SFX, stamina and damage presentation remain native. Physical intent detection and combat authority stay separate. Also arbitrate semantic gestures: a hand moving toward a holster can look like a slash, so explicit holster intent should temporarily suppress melee recognition rather than relying on velocity thresholds alone.",
    "blockers": "Collision layers, hitboxes and native combat methods vary widely. Fast motion can tunnel through thin targets unless the sweep is continuous or the engine provides CCD; controller noise may also require velocity smoothing and a maximum swing speed.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/dariulone/cyberpunk-vr-port",
    "submittedBy": "source-audit",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-05",
    "votes": 0
