    "renderAPI": "Any",
    "injectionMethod": "Signature scan, pattern scan, vtable hook or inline hook",
    "platform": "PC VR",
    "status": "proven",
    "features": [],
    "summary": "A fail-closed procedure for version-sensitive native VR hooks that prevents an unknown executable build from receiving stale offsets or incompatible patches.",
    "details": "Treat every native hook as binary identity -> candidate/known address -> structural validation -> hook activation, and centralize the version-sensitive facts. Signatures are useful, but a real project may still need direct RVAs for deeply reversed internal functions. CyberpunkVR Port is a good mixed model: some hooks pattern-scan; its stereo layer keeps direct 2.31 RVAs in one EngineRvas table; callsite patches verify expected bytes before rewriting; staged registries install hooks in deterministic order and log every ok/FAILED/skipped result; a failure means the feature is absent rather than 'best-effort' execution at an uncertain address. For an LLM, the general rule is not 'never use offsets' but 'never scatter or silently trust them': pin them to a known build, validate where possible, make their ownership explicit, and fail closed after patches.",
    "blockers": "Signature patterns can become ambiguous after compiler changes. Validation should check multiple independent invariants when practical, and unknown builds should default to disabled rather than attempting a best-effort patch.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/dariulone/cyberpunk-vr-port",
    "submittedBy": "source-audit",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-05",
    "votes": 0
