    "summary": "A source-available universal Unity VR framework that bypasses Unity-version-locked XR packages, supports Mono and IL2CPP builds, and separates game/camera policy from OpenXR/OpenVR runtime code behind a shared camera-setup interface.",
    "details": "UnityVRMod is best treated as a reusable VR bootstrap/rendering architecture rather than a single-game implementation. `VRModCore.cs` owns plugin lifecycle/configuration and starts visualization after UniverseLib initialization; `VrVisualizationManager.cs` then owns camera discovery, scene transitions and rig lifecycle while delegating runtime-specific work through `IVrCameraSetup`. That separation is important for mod authors: scene/camera policy does not need to know whether the active runtime is OpenXR or OpenVR. Camera discovery is explicitly invalidated on `SceneManager.activeSceneChanged`; automatic Safe Mode is engaged during scene loads; and a change to the game camera causes the VR rig to be rebuilt. The OpenXR backend is concrete enough to teach the rendering path: `VrCameraSetup_CoreOpenXR.cs` creates a `UnityVRMod_XrRig` containing distinct left/right Unity `Camera` objects, copies relevant source-camera state, applies world-scale and eye-height calibration plus optional scene pose overrides, locates XR views, converts their poses into Unity camera transforms, derives asymmetric projection matrices from `XrFovf`, renders each eye into intermediate targets, copies those results into native XR swapchain images, and submits `XrCompositionLayerProjectionView` data containing per-eye pose/FOV/swapchain state. The configuration also exposes an asserted camera hierarchy path, which is a practical escape hatch when `Camera.main` or automatic discovery selects the wrong camera. Public runtime logs show OpenXR interaction-profile binding suggestions, a stage reference space, per-view swapchains and an action set containing trigger/grip/thumbstick plus left/right aim/grip poses, but those logs are supplementary evidence rather than a substitute for source-level input inspection. The repository therefore gives an unusually clear example of a managed Unity mod owning the stereo camera/render loop while isolating XR backend code, with compatibility controls for scene transitions and difficult camera layouts. It is not yet a strong reference for physical weapon transforms, body IK or melee collision.",
    "blockers": "Beta (`v0.1.0-beta`). Windows 64-bit DX11 is the documented renderer target. Multi-camera titles and scene transitions remain compatibility hotspots, and OpenVR Safe Mode recovery may require a full VR reinitialization. The public implementation is much stronger on camera/stereo/XR bootstrap than on embodied gameplay; do not infer a complete weapon, IK or melee layer from the controller/input logs.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/NewUnityModder/UnityVRMod",
    "submittedBy": "source-audit",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-05"
