    "details": "This is deliberately retained as a separate entry from the older VRMod substrate already in the registry because the current gVRMod repository documents a different backend split and a much more explicit OpenXR/Linux path. The project has three important seams: native OpenXR C++ under `src/`, the `vrmod-x64` Lua addon as a submodule containing gameplay/UI/bindings, and the sibling `vrmod-module-master` repository for the classic OpenVR native backend. On Windows, `build.bat` compiles `vrmod_log.cpp`, `xr_input.cpp`, `d3d_hooks.cpp`, `xr_session.cpp`, `xr_render_d3d.cpp` and `lua_interface.cpp` into `gmcl_vrmod_xr_win64.dll`; the build explicitly selects `XR_KHR_D3D11_enable` for the OpenXR D3D path. The OpenXR input boundary is unusually explicit: `xr_input.h` keeps action sets and pose-action spaces in native state, parses the existing action manifest, creates/attaches action sets, synchronizes actions per frame, exposes boolean/float/vector2/pose queries, triggers haptics, suggests interaction-profile bindings, creates and destroys pose spaces, and exposes physical controller-source queries for rebinding/chord logic. It also provides an OpenVR-compatible skeletal-summary function that synthesizes finger curl/splay from controller trigger/grip/buttons rather than requiring OpenXR hand-tracking actions. The native module is loaded as `vrmod_xr`; the same Lua addon can use `vrmod` for the OpenVR module, with backend preference selected by `vrmod_prefer_backend`. This creates a useful adapter pattern: XR-specific lifetime, action state and graphics-resource interop stay native, while pickup/collision/locomotion/climbing/menu/hand-panel behavior stays in the Lua layer. The repository further documents separate Windows D3D9/11 and Linux OpenGL/GLX/togl texture-sharing paths, including render-target flip conventions, so platform-specific graphics plumbing is kept below the gameplay API. For an LLM, the most reusable lesson is not `how to make Garry's Mod VR` but how to define a stable VR capability ABI—poses, semantic actions, physical controller sources, haptics and backend identity—then let gameplay code consume that ABI without branching on the XR runtime.",
    "blockers": "This is an evolving fork/ecosystem, not a frozen compatibility snapshot. HMD smoke testing remains manual, and the project itself distinguishes offline green tests from headset-proven behavior. Module/addon revisions must remain compatible. The public evidence reviewed here does not establish a complete network pose packet/interpolation scheme or a full predicted-display-time-to-submit sequence, so those should remain UNKNOWN. The feature tags are intentionally conservative: pickup/collision/locomotion/climbing are documented, but this entry does not claim source-audited physical weapon aiming or melee implementation.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/Abyss-c0re/gVRMod, https://github.com/Abyss-c0re/vrmod-x64, https://github.com/Abyss-c0re/vrmod-module-master",
