    "details": "Poppy Playtime VR is less valuable for renderer code than for the surrounding engineering that makes a VR conversion maintainable across multiple chapters. The repository contains a dedicated installer and launcher, and the launcher can select stable versus nightly UEVR depending on chapter compatibility. That is a broadly useful implementation pattern: do not hard-code one VR runtime and one profile into the mod itself when the underlying game has multiple executables/versions. Instead, represent compatibility as data: game chapter, executable, supported UEVR build, profile, launch arguments and known fixes. The launcher becomes a compatibility resolver that assembles the correct combination before starting the game. For a vibe-coded VR project, the same idea can be applied even to a single game: keep offsets/signatures, runtime selection, camera mode, controller profile and known workarounds in a versioned configuration layer. Then an update can add a new compatibility record instead of forcing the entire VR implementation to fork. This is especially valuable for games that update frequently or ship several executables with subtly different rendering behavior.",
