    "id": "seed_uu3d_depth_case",
    "type": "case_study",
    "title": "UU3D — Unreal universal stereo + depth pipeline",
    "game": "Multiple Unreal Engine games",
    "engineFamily": "Unreal Engine 4/5 / universal VR backend",
    "renderAPI": "D3D12 stereo + depth capture",
    "injectionMethod": "Universal Unreal rendering backend with per-game compatibility paths",
    "platform": "PC VR",
    "status": "proven",
    "features": [
      "stereoRendering",
      "uiProjection"
    ],
    "summary": "A depth-first rendering reference: it treats scene depth as a first-class per-frame resource with multiple capture strategies and uses it for HUD depth, adaptive crosshairs and alternate-eye reprojection.",
    "details": "UU3D is the kind of source to study when the question is not 'how do I attach a controller?' but 'how do I make the rendered scene itself useful to the VR compositor and VR-specific effects?' Its depth pipeline exposes several capture strategies because no single hook is reliable across Unreal titles. Per-Draw Capture reads depth from the rendering path but can miss depth resources allocated once at load time. Engine Pool reads Unreal's SceneDepthZ from the render-target pool and fixes titles where per-draw capture stays flat, but it requires an engine hook that can be unsafe for some games. DSV Observer instead watches D3D12 depth-stencil views and resource barriers at the API level, allowing live depth capture without an Unreal engine hook. DLSS Depth captures the exact depth supplied to DLSS when DLSS is enabled. This is a profound general lesson for a VR mod: do not make 'depth buffer' a single boolean feature. Model it as a source-selection problem with capability detection and per-game fallbacks. Once a trustworthy depth image exists, it can feed several independent VR systems: auto-convergence, depth-aware HUD placement, adaptive crosshairs, and alternate-eye reprojection/warping. The project also documents synchronized sequential stereo as a paired-state problem: the two eye images must correspond to the same game state, while AFR deliberately trades one-eye freshness for compatibility. Its AFW path goes further by reprojecting the older eye from color plus depth plus motion vectors, approaching one scene render per frame while maintaining stereo. For a vibe-coded implementation, start much smaller: capture SceneDepth once per frame, visualize it, validate that both eyes use the same scene state, then use it to place one HUD element or crosshair. Only after that should you attempt depth-based reprojection. The key architecture is resource provenance: every depth texture should carry information about where it came from, when it was captured, which eye/state it belongs to, and whether it is safe for the intended effect.",
    "blockers": "UU3D is a universal rendering project rather than a single game mod, so it is a high-level backend reference. D3D12 and Unreal-version compatibility are substantial constraints; depth capture methods must be selected per title.",
    "sourceLinks": "https://github.com/oneup03/UU3D",
    "submittedBy": "source-audit",
    "createdAt": "2026-09-05"
