    "details": "PHYSICAL INTERACTION / MULTIPLAYER: HandsInteractionController.StowHeldItemToInventory first simulates QuickFindAppropriatePlace and checks CanExecute while the object is still held. On success it records LastOwner, calls ForceDropHeldItem and starts StowAfterRelease. After the configured 0.2-second gap, the coroutine checks the item still exists, rejects another player's newly claimed hold, rechecks fit/authority and calls DirectItemTransfer, which invokes InventoryController.RunNetworkTransaction. The source explains the concrete failure: a native pickup arriving while observers still consider the item custom-VR-held can leave a ghost world item instead of passing through native loot removal.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION / MULTIPLAYER IMPLEMENTATION USE: Validate destination before giving up physical ownership, release the custom synchronization mode, then revalidate and commit the native transfer. Treat the interval as a race window. The 0.2-second wait is the source's heuristic, not a network acknowledgment guarantee; a protocol-level acknowledgment is a possible improvement when the target permits it. Test two-player contention and full inventory.\n\nSOURCE EVIDENCE: Supplied `SPT-VR-1.3.0.zip`, C# source audit dated 2026-09-07. Files: `Source/Player/Interactions/HandsInteractionController.cs`, `ModSupport/FIKASupport.cs`. The implementation paragraph describes recovered code; adaptation and validation guidance is identified separately.",
