    "summary": "One drag simulation with full-ragdoll observer presentation; verified against SPT-VR 1.3.0.",
    "details": "PHYSICAL INTERACTION / MULTIPLAYER: HandsInteractionController.BodyGrab drives the grabbed bone as a kinematic hand anchor in FixedUpdate, retaining positional/rotational grab offsets. Upward delta is attenuated, translation is speed-capped and rotation uses RotateTowards. ThawRagdoll keeps the other bones dynamic, gravity-enabled and awake, with ContinuousSpeculative collision, preventing settle timers or sleeping joints from stretching the mesh. FikaVrSync sends all ragdoll bone world transforms; observers apply the shared pose instead of running competing simulations. Release markers use ReliableOrdered while updates use Unreliable. DownedDragPacket additionally carries the final root position so the downed player's own client relocates and later revives at the dragged location. Release settling eventually freezes bodies again.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION / MULTIPLAYER IMPLEMENTATION USE: Choose one physics owner for a constrained multi-bone drag; distribute the resulting pose when independent observer physics diverges. Make the end of ownership reliable, including final placement, and restore collision/settling policy. Test sleeping limbs, prolonged dragging, packet loss at release and downed-versus-dead identity. Full-bone transport trades bandwidth for consistent presentation.\n\nSOURCE EVIDENCE: Supplied `SPT-VR-1.3.0.zip`, C# source audit dated 2026-09-07. Files: `Source/Player/Interactions/HandsInteractionController.BodyGrab.cs`, `FikaSync/FikaVrSync.cs`, `FikaSync/Packets.cs`, `FikaSync/BodyDragApply.cs`, `FikaSync/DownedDragApply.cs`. The implementation paragraph describes recovered code; adaptation and validation guidance is identified separately.",
