    "details": "VERIFIED FROM THE SUPPLIED RDRVR SOURCE. CALIBRATION: body.cpp::fixed_grip() obtains the animated wrist matrix from the current skeleton, validates that its axes are rigid/unit/orthogonal, computes the weapon-in-wrist relation, and learns only while the native game is in a steady aim state. It waits past the first 20 aiming frames, blends accepted measurements by 10% per sample, orthonormalizes the basis, and after 60 accepted samples stores the relation once per session through a worker item so configuration-file I/O does not occur inside the game matrix hook. Per-weapon user learning wins over a built-in relation; same-family templates are fallbacks (sidearms and long guns remain separate), followed by the game's original pose. The calibration applies to long guns and optionally sidearms through separate FixedGunGrip / FixedSidearmGrip switches because game-held aiming and lowered poses differ. body.cpp::fixed_grip_placed then uses the selected relation when placing the actual weapon object. The README describes 21 built-in gun relations as maintainer-audited; not all were independently runtime-tested in this audit.\n\nGEOMETRY: aim.cpp::hk_aim_ray takes the weapon object's W + 0x80 matrix, maps its axes/origin through body::item_correction(), and converts the weapon-local tune MuzzleOffset (weapon info + 0x310) into a world-space muzzle point using the game's row-vector/rows-as-axes convention. The barrel is -row 2. An implausible/non-finite offset or invalid direction is rejected. Lasso and thrown weapons branch to a controller-hand intent ray because their mesh axes are not ballistic barrels; Dead Eye marked-shot firing preserves the native mark ray. This is the useful architectural separation between rendered mesh transform, hand target, aim ray, and launch trajectory.\n\nNATIVE FIRE / PROJECTILE: hk_fire preserves the original fire trigger and temporarily chooses the gameplay camera where necessary for first-person fire. hk_spawn is scoped to local player-owned ordinary firearms; when PerfectAccuracy is enabled it handles a build-specific animated-muzzle-versus-VR-muzzle obstruction mismatch, aligns the current shot's shoot-from direction, suppresses the game's random bloom and temporarily removes inherited shooter velocity for that spawn call, then restores the velocity. NPC shots, turrets, no-bloom calls, lasso, thrown weapons, the protected Dead Eye mark phase and the secondary-gun case pass or branch without applying primary-gun assumptions. ShotgunPattern copies a bounded pellet matrix array (maximum 32 pellets) and changes only orientation axes when straightened, preserving the original native spawn call and pellet count. hk_launch can separately shift the tracer/launch origin to the measured muzzle while preserving its fourth component; projectile direction/start diagnostics compare launched velocity against the last barrel ray. Do not claim one ray fix makes all four of visible barrel, targeting, trajectory and muzzle-block semantics correct; audit each seam.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Calibrate a rigid weapon-in-hand relation per item class/weapon and represent coordinate spaces explicitly. Derive muzzle and forward axis from the final placed model; then modify only the game's existing aim/projectile data where the native calculation disagrees with the tracked weapon. Preserve native fire cadence, weapon/projectile creation, ammo, damage and enemy shots. Add state-specific exemptions (Dead Eye marks, throwable/lasso no-barrel path, secondary firearm), numeric validity gates and a native fallback. Build a test matrix comparing visible model axis, aim-ray hit, projectile velocity, launch origin, obstruction behavior, shotgun pellet cone and post-switch weapon identity. This is source-verified RDR-specific methodology, not transferable offsets or universal matrix conventions.",
