    "summary": "Keep a 32-bit game renderer separate from a 64-bit XR host and transfer the complete stereo pair on-GPU with bounded synchronization.",
    "details": "ARCHITECTURE / FRAME TRANSPORT: the public README and CMake graph describe `DeusExHRVRHost.exe` as an x64 companion, with x86 proxy targets `d3d11.dll`, `atidxx32.dll` and `atiadlxy.dll`; the game renders both eyes stacked in one native texture. A D3D11 keyed mutex coordinates the complete pair transfer to the host, which copies into a two-slice OpenXR swapchain. OpenXR frame pacing signals the producer after `xrWaitFrame`; on timeout the whole pair is dropped rather than waiting indefinitely or combining eyes from different frames. CPU readback is for F8 diagnostics, not the live transport. Reuse this only when processes share a compatible GPU/adapter and resource-interop path. Add pair sequence numbers, keyed-mutex ownership assertions, resource-generation/device-loss handling and per-stage timing; do not allow one-eye partial commits.",
