    "featuresAudited": true,
    "summary": "Have domains submit bounded interaction candidates to a central resolver, then grant identity- and generation-scoped leases before any commit.",
    "details": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `interaction_coordinator.cpp` uses an explicit provider table and checked schedule covering reporting, collection, settlement and lifecycle. `hand_interaction/core.hpp` normalizes press/release edges using action generation, input sequence, sample age, reference generation and continuity. A stale/inactive sample resets the edge cursors; tracking recovery is not treated as a fresh trigger-down. Candidate decisions expose rejection reasons (invalid/no edge/incompatible/occupied/lower priority/capacity/commit failed/wrong route). The coordinator collects reload items, detachable/tube/cylinder/break-action reloads, underbarrel, equipment, grenades, mission equipment, night vision, heartbeat, world use and ladder proposals, resolves the batch once, and settles granted domains in a checked order. The same hand cannot independently be claimed by a magazine, a support grip and world-use subsystem.\n\nPATTERN: use a single “semantic intent -> candidates -> deterministic arbitration -> granted lease -> native commit -> reconciliation/presentation” pipeline. Lease identity should include input sequence, reference generation, context/target revision and native object generation so recenter, weapon swap, ownership changes or stale entities invalidate sessions. Make rejection a first-class result and keep bounded candidate/session capacity. Domains expose adapters and proposals; the coordinator should not know the internals of each reload family.\n\nTRANSFER: applies to any VR game with overlapping spatial actions (grab vs use vs reload vs climb). It prevents multiple features from responding to the same squeeze/trigger. Context changes should neutral-arm the controls; no stuck/duplicate acquisition should occur just because two domains both poll proximity.",
