    "details": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE/DOCS: `gameplay/campaign/sequences/camera_policies.hpp`, `scripted_camera_reference.cpp`, and the `scripted_arms_runtime` adapter select explicit native scene identity, owner entity, model and animation phase. In the Cliffhanger opening, arms remain native for the authored crouch; tracked wrists blend in based on native stand-up animation progress, while shoulders, torso, legs, root, camera and mission ice-pick attachment remain native. The arm solver runs after the native skeleton calculation under the DObj lock, writes only the permitted arm subtree, and processes each skeleton frame once. Free climbing is a different owner/phase with its own ordinary hand interactions. Camera entry alignment is epoch-scoped and tag-dependent; checkpoint/relink/recenter clear stale baselines.\n\nPATTERN: scripts own story choreography; VR adaptation owns only explicitly granted camera/arm/control dimensions. Represent that as policy + owner identity + phase + lifecycle token, not a global map-name check. Use animation progress for pose handoff; keep native attachment objects and interaction objects from drawing duplicates. On controller loss, body replacement, phase transition or checkpoint restore, revert or rebase once, rather than catching up through a huge pose delta.\n\nLIMITS: this is selected mission-specific bilateral arms and camera policy. The code explicitly says scripted ice-pick arm IK does not implement climbing force/contact/locomotion; that is another adapter. Do not extrapolate it into generic full-body motion capture.",
