    "details": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE/DOCS: `native_menu.cpp`, `menu_overlay.cpp`, `menu_surface.hpp`, native HUD capture, `openxr_menu.cpp` and D3D11 helpers retain native LUI/menu stack, text, mask/cursor and navigation/confirmation semantics. OpenVR uses curved frontend surfaces and flatter in-game panels; OpenXR uses quad/cylinder layers. The fitted in-game output may use 1920x1080 presentation canvas, but its geometry tracks native content extent and transparently padded pixels are not interactive. Ray hits are mapped back to the original desktop layout. The input adapter delivers absolute native pointer/key transitions without moving the Windows cursor; real desktop activity deliberately hands back ownership, while idle native mouse polling cannot steal the VR ray. Layer resize, tracker loss, owner/menu stack changes and binding changes invalidate image/pointer state independently and require neutral re-arming.\n\nPATTERN: reuse the game's own UI renderer and event/navigation model. Spatialize the rendered surface and adapt pointer mapping, not every button/widget. Keep image readiness separate from input readiness, and never allow an invalid/stale/missing image to accept a click. Preserve native stacking/backdrops/briefing hold confirmation and desktop handoff semantics. Render blur/curvature in the VR presentation path without rewriting the source menu layout.\n\nLIMITS: captured menu/HUD routes cover known native streams; they don't imply every game draw surface or debug UI is universally captured. WARP tests and counters do not prove optical alignment in a headset.",
