    "featuresAudited": true,
    "summary": "Resolve engine integration points through validated signatures and fail closed when the target binary does not match.",
    "details": "BOOTSTRAP / COMPATIBILITY — Source-audit of supplied `l4d2vr-master` source tree; code paths listed below are primary evidence. Portable recipes are recommendations, not claims that the project implements every suggested safeguard. `dllmain.cpp`, `game.cpp`, `game.h`, `hooks.cpp`, `hooks.h`, `offsets.h`, and `sigscanner.h` form the native integration path. The project builds a 32-bit Windows D3D9 proxy (`d3d9.dll`); `DllMain` dispatches initialization away from the loader-lock path. `Game` discovers relevant modules/interfaces, and offset resolution is paired with byte-signature verification rather than trusting an address alone. The project’s launch path also checks for `-insecure`, reflecting the mod’s stated anti-cheat risk.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION METHOD: Keep loader-time work minimal; initialize hooks on a controlled worker/startup path; discover modules and engine interfaces before patching; centralize per-build addresses; verify expected bytes at every hook target; and refuse to install a hook when the signature or interface is unknown. Log which integration point failed so compatibility errors are diagnosable.\n\nVALIDATION: Test missing modules, delayed module load, signature mismatch, unsupported game build, and repeated initialization. Never silently continue with a guessed offset. Source-specific offsets remain build-dependent; this does not establish compatibility with every L4D2 executable.",
    "blockers": "Native offsets and byte signatures are build-sensitive. This is a Windows x86 / D3D9-specific pattern, not a portable injector recipe.",
