    "summary": "Let VR own spatial intent while preserving the game’s normal movement and action command pipeline.",
    "details": "INPUT / LOCOMOTION — Source-audit of supplied `l4d2vr-master` source tree; code paths listed below are primary evidence. Portable recipes are recommendations, not claims that the project implements every suggested safeguard. `vr/vr_process_input.inl` polls the action state and computes stick/button intent; its comments explicitly note that movement is handled in `Hooks::dCreateMove` through Source `CUserCmd`, rather than console-command movement. `hooks/hooks_createmove.inl` is the native command hook and contains state transitions for spectator-like→live-player recentering plus combat/throwable command handling. The code therefore has a defined boundary between runtime input and game command simulation.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION METHOD: Normalize device input into semantic actions first. Convert movement axes, turn, jump, crouch, use, attack and reload into the engine’s native command structure at its authoritative command-build stage. Keep edge-triggered presses separate from held states; reset/re-arm state after context changes to avoid phantom inputs. Preserve native command processing for collision, prediction, weapon rules and server authority rather than moving the player directly from the render thread.\n\nVALIDATION: Check short taps, long holds, simultaneous axes, focus loss, menu transitions, spectator transitions and controller reconnect. Verify that no action is issued twice by both a runtime path and the native command path.",
