    "summary": "Treat engine pointers and netvar memory as transient when VR rendering callbacks can run outside ordinary gameplay update timing.",
    "details": "ENGINE MEMORY SAFETY — Source-audit of supplied `l4d2vr-master` source tree; code paths listed below are primary evidence. Portable recipes are recommendations, not claims that the project implements every suggested safeguard. `vr/vr_aiming.inl` begins with a comment that entity pointers/netvar memory can be transient when called from `Present()`. It defines typed guarded read helpers for byte, integer, unsigned integer and float values, along with safer entity-list/index access helpers. `hooks/hooks_combat_network.inl` also contains guarded pointer and scalar read/write helpers in the local world-laser effect path.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION METHOD: Prefer engine interfaces and stable snapshots. Where reverse-engineered memory reads are unavoidable, centralize checked reads, validate null/range/entity identity, and return an explicit failure rather than using an uninitialized/default value as if valid. Keep render-thread queries read-only where possible; defer mutations to a safe engine callback. Avoid assuming an access-violation guard makes an invalid object semantically safe.\n\nVALIDATION: Test entity deletion, map transitions, spectator state, weapon swaps, and concurrent render/game updates. Log rejected reads with the feature path and fail closed. This is defensive handling around unstable game memory, not proof that arbitrary invalid pointers are safe.",
    "blockers": "Raw offsets and entity layouts remain version-specific. Exception/guarded reads do not replace object-lifetime or thread-ownership rules.",
