    "featuresAudited": true,
    "summary": "Use VR pose and gesture data to express intent, then route accepted actions through the game’s established command/entity pathways.",
    "details": "GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY — Source-audit of supplied `l4d2vr-master` source tree; code paths listed below are primary evidence. Portable recipes are recommendations, not claims that the project implements every suggested safeguard. The source is split between runtime/action processing (`vr/vr_process_input.inl`), native command handling (`hooks/hooks_createmove.inl`), throwable/object-interaction paths (`hooks/hooks_throwables.inl`, `vr/vr_object_pull.inl`), and pose-only remote presentation (`hooks/hooks_world_pose.inl`). This separation is a reusable architectural pattern: VR tracking informs gameplay intent and visual pose, while native game code remains responsible for the underlying command/entity rules.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION METHOD: Define an explicit seam for each feature: semantic action → validated intent → native command or entity operation → visual/pose feedback. Do not independently simulate ammo, damage, collision or inventory if the game already owns those systems. For multiplayer, distinguish cosmetic remote pose from server-authoritative gameplay state. Keep failure/cancel paths so invalid tracking does not leave a weapon, object or movement state half-committed.\n\nVALIDATION: Compare VR and non-VR server behavior, check that fire/reload/use reach native command paths, and test interrupted interactions and stale poses. This is an architecture pattern drawn across several files; it is not a claim that every individual L4D2 interaction has identical behavior on all server configurations.",
    "blockers": "Some features are gated by server configuration and the README warns that certain interactions require VR-aware server support. Do not claim complete melee physics, arbitrary grabs or universal multiplayer compatibility.",
