    "details": "UI / HUD — VERIFIED SOURCE: `vr/vr_lifecycle_init.inl` defines an RGBA `HudSurface` with pixel pointer, width, height and row stride, plus clipped CPU drawing helpers (`FillRect`, `Clear`, `DrawRect`) and a small 5×7 glyph renderer. Its comments explicitly say the built-in glyph set is intentionally tiny and that teammate/aim-target player names use a GDI fallback renderer for UTF-8 names, including Chinese/Japanese/Korean characters. The file therefore demonstrates a small software-rendered HUD surface and text fallback strategy.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION METHOD: Define a minimal surface contract (pixels, dimensions, stride) and make drawing primitives clip to bounds and validate the backing pointer. Keep compact fixed glyphs for predictable labels, but route arbitrary/localized strings through a renderer that supports the needed character repertoire. Keep layout/data generation separate from the final texture upload or VR presentation step, and account for row stride rather than assuming tightly packed rows.\n\nVALIDATION: Test odd surface sizes, clipped rectangles, transparent colors, missing pixel buffers, long player names, UTF-8 multi-byte text, and different HUD scales. This code proves the software surface and text-rendering strategy; exact overlay placement/presentation should be attributed only to the separate code that consumes the resulting surface.",
