    "details": "VERIFIED SOURCE — Audited from the user-supplied `cyberpunk-vr-port-main` snapshot. `OpenXRFrameLoop.cpp` owns the XR cycle: `xrWaitFrame`, `xrBeginFrame`, one target locate time, view/controller location, image transfer, and `xrEndFrame`. The code can move XR pacing to a dedicated submit thread because SteamVR-style pacing can block the game’s Present path. If the game has no new completed frame, it can re-submit the most recent completed snapshot using that snapshot’s own render pose rather than pretending fresh pixels were rendered at a new pose.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION METHOD — Choose one owner for `xrWaitFrame`/`xrBeginFrame`/`xrEndFrame`. Publish the predicted target time to camera/tracking consumers. If compositor cadence exceeds game cadence, treat a repeated frame as an immutable image+pose transaction; never pair an old image with the newest head pose. Use bounded synchronization/resource leasing so the game cannot overwrite a texture still consumed by the submitter.\n\nVALIDATION / TRANSFER — Test runtimes with different pacing behavior, game FPS below headset refresh, missing/new frames, pause/loading transitions and thread teardown. Verify repeated images retain their original render-pose identity and that Present no longer blocks on runtime pacing when submit-thread mode is active.\n\nSOURCE FILES — `src/Runtimes/OpenXRFrameLoop.cpp`, `src/Runtimes/OpenXRPresent.cpp`, `src/Runtimes/OpenXRCapture.cpp`.",
    "blockers": "Threaded submission adds lifetime and synchronization complexity; it is useful only when the runtime/game pacing interaction requires it.",
