const CODE_AUDITS = {
  "seed_sn2_uevr_case": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED SOURCE: `scripts/main.lua` v1.0.3 and the bundled uevrlib modules were inspected directly.\n\nARCHITECTURE / GAME-SPECIFIC LAYER:\n- `main.lua` requires `uevr_utils`, controllers, config UI, reticule, hands, attachments, input, pawn, animation, montage, interaction, UI, remap, IK, gestures and collision, then initializes the shared systems and disables automatic hand creation.\n- Subnautica-specific code is therefore primarily policy/adaptation: native object discovery, state detection, transform ownership, collision-to-action routing, and vehicle/UI integration.\n\nCALLBACK ORDER:\n- `on_pre_engine_tick`: detects pawn-vs-Tadpole context, swaps profiles and IK pawn, handles authored movement-locked states, body/hand visibility and camera-lerp policy.\n- `on_post_engine_tick`: neutralizes native `LocalCameraRotation`, updates vehicle steering geometry, updates hover-widget visibility, samples held-object velocity and evaluates swipe/collision resource interactions.\n- `registerOnPreInputGetStateCallback`: consumes or synthesizes XInput for physical driving, physical grip/trigger interactions, inventory gestures and remapped swim controls.\n- `on_level_change`: rebuilds hand state and re-applies game-specific UEVR/input/collision fixes.\n\nSOURCE-VERIFIED LIMITS:\n- No UEVR Native Stereo/OpenXR renderer implementation is included; the profile consumes UEVR.\n- No custom network packet format or remote VR pose reconstruction exists.\n- The profile's release documentation, not `main.lua`, provides the Native-Stereo-only compatibility requirement and specific nightly/backend dependency.",
  "pattern_sn2_vehicle_profile_bundle_switch": "VERIFIED FROM `scripts/main.lua`: `on_pre_engine_tick` changes `status.currentPawn` and `status.currentVehicle`, then switches pawn/input/IK profiles together and calls `ik.setPawn`. `getCustomIKComponent` resolves different descriptor paths for Default versus TadPole.\n\nREUSABLE PATTERN: treat a vehicle, turret, mount or possession state as a full VR-context change, not merely an input remap; update body source, IK rig, camera/input profile and hand lifecycle atomically.",
  "pattern_sn2_two_hand_vehicle_xinput_adapter": "VERIFIED FROM `updateVehicleOrientation`, `scalePitchDeltaToThumbValue`, `scaleYawDeltaToThumbValue` and the pre-input callback.\n\nDATA FLOW:\ntracked left/right rotations -> averaged pitch/roll -> accumulated virtual vehicle yaw / adjusted pitch -> delta against native `ControlRotation` -> clamped XInput right-stick values -> native Tadpole controller.\n\nThe profile does not write vehicle physics directly. Pitch has a deadzone and squared response; yaw is linear. Both-grip press toggles steering ownership, and ordinary grips/sticks are suppressed while that mode owns input.",
  "pattern_sn2_authored_attachment_state_policy": "VERIFIED FROM `on_pre_engine_tick` plus the priority-state implementation in `libs/ui.lua`.\n\nThe profile uses `AttachedToOwner` and selected attachment components as semantic state, then changes rotation ownership, mesh-relative positioning, camera lerp, hands visibility and body-arm policy. `ui.setCustomState` carries explicit priorities, so temporary authored states can override lower-priority defaults and later clear back to cached/native behavior.\n\nLIMITATION: some state classification uses asset/component name matching.",
  "pattern_sn2_live_ui_layer_tracking": "VERIFIED FROM `getTopLevelWidgetsOnScreen`, the one-second widget polling interval, and `ui.addViewportWidget` / `ui.removeViewportWidget` in `libs/ui.lua`.\n\nALGORITHM:\n1. Ask `SN2Statics:GetWindowManager(world)` for `MainScreen`.\n2. Read selected layers (`HUD`, `Modal`, `PauseScreen`).\n3. Take each layer's live `DisplayedWidget`.\n4. Key it by UObject identity (`tostring(widget)`).\n5. Diff current versus previous set.\n6. Add newly displayed instances to the VR UI list and remove disappeared instances.\n\nThis reuses native UMG instances and the game's own window-stack authority rather than reconstructing menu state.",
  "pattern_sn2_native_hover_reticle_filter": "VERIFIED FROM `main.lua` UI polling and `on_post_engine_tick`.\n\nThe profile caches `WBP_HoverTargetInfo`, resolves `CurrentInteractionBrush.ResourceObject` each tick, and branches visibility based on whether that resource name contains `Reticle`. Target selection stays native; only presentation is filtered. This is useful when a game combines a valuable reticle with flat-screen descriptive overlays inside one hover widget.",
  "pattern_sn2_body_offset_fanout": "VERIFIED FROM `on_input_mesh_relative_position_change` and `setBodyOffsets`.\n\nTwo separate transform branches are corrected:\n- player body branch: the same UEVR mesh-relative X/Y offset is written to Mesh, Body, Head, Hair, Neck and Feet;\n- equipment branch: inverse head offset is periodically applied to skeletal mesh components inside equipped items' BlueprintCreatedComponents.\n\nThe source demonstrates why a single root offset is sometimes insufficient in UE games whose visible first-person body and equipment are composed from several independently attached meshes.",
  "pattern_sn2_near_camera_lod_material_swap": "VERIFIED FROM `useLodMats`, `hands.onCreatedCallback`, and `ik.registerOnMeshCreatedCallback`.\n\nThe source comment says non-LOD materials fade near the camera. The fix copies LOD material slots onto the primary visible slots every time VR hands/IK meshes are created. This is a rendering/presentation workaround at the material layer, not a stereo or clipping-plane change.",
  "pattern_sn2_disable_native_mesh_driver_for_tracked_tool": "VERIFIED FROM `disableCopyPoseDriver` and `attachments.registerAttachmentInitializedCallback`.\n\nOn attachment initialization, the profile resolves the tool mesh's `AnimScriptInstance`, calls `SetMeshDriver(nil)` if exposed, and clears `ValidSourceMesh`. The intent is explicit in source: stop native animation from moving the weapon away from the skeletal root after VR takes transform ownership.\n\nGENERALIZATION: tracked-object snapping often means another engine system is still writing the transform. Disable or redirect that writer instead of endlessly overwriting it later.",
  "pattern_sn2_equipped_or_grabbed_attachment_adapter": "VERIFIED FROM `getWeaponMesh`, `getHandComponents`, and `attachments.registerOnGripUpdateCallback`.\n\nRight-hand source priority:\n1. native equipped tool mesh/root;\n2. if no equipped tool and the right hand physically owns an item, the grabbed component.\nLeft hand can independently expose its grabbed component.\n\nBoth routes are normalized into the same attachment callback contract and socket names. This is a useful adapter architecture when a VR profile must support both native equipment and freeform physical grabs.",
  "pattern_sn2_capability_gated_tool_collision": "VERIFIED FROM `attachments.registerAttachmentChangeCallback`, `on_level_change`, and the resource collision checks.\n\n`Right Hand Tool` collision is disabled by default and enabled only when the active attachment's name identifies `SurvivalMultiTool`. Physical resource/predator checks query that dedicated label. The hand therefore gains a collision capability only while the native tool that semantically owns that capability is active.",
  "pattern_sn2_swipe_contact_to_native_action": "VERIFIED FROM the swipe callbacks, `checkForBreakingResourceNode*`, post-engine tick evaluation, and `checkGrabState`.\n\nPIPELINE:\ncontroller swipe -> 600 ms hand-specific intent window -> overlap/contact on MultiTool or bare-hand collider -> target family classification -> one-shot `needsAPress` / `needsRTPress` -> XInput mutation -> native Subnautica action.\n\nNo custom damage or harvesting result is applied by the VR layer.",
  "pattern_sn2_worldpop_pickup_proxy": "VERIFIED FROM `WORLD_POP_PICKUP_CLASSES`, `WORLD_POP_PICKUP_SUBSTRINGS`, `getPickupClassPathForWorldPop`, `hideWorldPopActor`, and `spawnPickupProxyForWorldPop`.\n\nThe source maps optimized world-population actors to real pickup blueprint classes, spawns the gameplay actor at the original component transform, configures its mesh for manipulation, then hides/collision-disables the source proxy. It retries after asset loading and can fall back to gripping the original actor when spawning fails.\n\nThis is a high-value pattern for UE games where optimized world-population objects are not themselves valid physics/inventory actors.",
  "pattern_sn2_proxy_grab_logical_identity": "VERIFIED FROM `gripResourceItem`, `isItemGrippedByOtherHand`, and `checkForCollision`.\n\nEvery grab record can carry both the physically manipulated `parent` and the original `sourceActor`. Other-hand ownership checks compare against both, preventing proxy replacement from creating duplicate logical ownership.\n\nGENERALIZATION: whenever VR introduces a proxy actor/component, keep identity metadata linking it back to the authoritative native object.",
  "pattern_sn2_grab_physics_release_handoff": "VERIFIED FROM `gripResourceItem`, `resetGrabVelocityTracking`, `updateGrabVelocityTracking`, and `releaseGrippedComponents`.\n\nGRAB:\n- record ownership before transform changes;\n- ignore pawn collision;\n- call native `NetMulticast_SetPhysicsEnabled(false, true)` when present;\n- attach component to the tracked hand.\n\nHELD:\n- sample component world position each post-engine tick;\n- compute velocity by finite difference over `delta`.\n\nRELEASE:\n- re-enable native physics;\n- detach tracked attachment;\n- convert sampled velocity directly to `AddImpulse`;\n- ignore hand-vs-PhysicsBody for 1.5 s so the object clears the controller;\n- restore pawn collision.\n\nThe VR profile does not implement its own network replication.",
  "pattern_sn2_overlap_name_to_native_input_adapter": "VERIFIED FROM `triggerNames`, `triggerDPadNames`, `gripNames`, `checkForTriggerCollision`, `checkForGripCollision`, and `checkGrabState`.\n\nThe profile uses controller-overlap components to choose a physical target, then class/name tables to decide which existing native input to synthesize. This covers a heterogeneous set of buttons, levers, lockers, stations, terminals and vehicle interactions without replacing their original gameplay functions.\n\nLIMITATION: string matching is a compatibility tactic, not a robust semantic interface.",
  "pattern_sn2_ear_inventory_context_action": "VERIFIED FROM `checkGrabState`, `releaseGrippedComponents`, inventory deferrals, and `gestures.getHeadGestures` / `detectFace` in `libs/gestures.lua`.\n\nThe gesture library computes ear zones using HMD-relative geometry and grip state. SN2 then adds context:\n- empty hand + ear grip/release -> native inventory/menu request;\n- held item + ear grip/release -> native interact request to put the item into inventory.\n\nThis demonstrates a body-zone gesture whose meaning is selected by hand ownership rather than by a second gesture.",
  "pattern_sn2_repurpose_grip_preserve_swim_semantics": "VERIFIED FROM `checkGrabState`.\n\nPhysical interaction consumes shoulder buttons as left/right grip transitions. To preserve Subnautica's original shoulder-button swim-up/down semantics, the profile maps right-stick Y beyond a 22000 deadzone back into right/left shoulder button presses before the game reads state.\n\nGENERALIZATION: when motion controls steal a legacy input, preserve the displaced native action through a late semantic remap.",
  "pattern_sn2_post_animation_camera_neutralization": "VERIFIED FROM the first block of `on_post_engine_tick`.\n\nAfter native animation updates, the profile resolves `pawn.Mesh.AnimScriptInstance.AnimationComponent` and writes `LocalCameraRotation.Pitch/Yaw/Roll = 0`. The source comment states this prevents the pawn from pitching with arm movements.\n\nThe key timing idea is post-animation channel suppression: allow the body animation to run, then remove only the camera-rotation side effect that is incompatible with HMD authority.",
  "pattern_sn2_level_transition_rebootstrap": "VERIFIED FROM `on_level_change`.\n\nThe transition handler clears runtime status, regenerates the selected hand/IK representation, hides the scuba mask, briefly flips UEVR AimMethod, rehides the low-health overlay, disables the tool collider, and reapplies the aim offset.\n\nThe temporary AimMethod flip is game/profile-specific. The broadly reusable pattern is to treat world/level changes as a complete reacquire/reapply boundary rather than assuming pointers and runtime parameters survived.",
  "pattern_sn2_runtime_render_compat_cvars": "VERIFIED FROM `configDefinition`, `enableFog`, and the config update handlers.\n\nActive advanced controls:\n- `disable_reflections` writes `r.Lumen.Reflections.Allow`; profile UI text says this can mitigate small left/right-eye mismatches, at possible FPS cost.\n- `disable_post_process_materials` writes `r.postprocessing.disablematerials`; UI text says this can improve FPS.\n\nThe source also retains a commented/older volumetric cloud/fog/water-lighting helper. These are per-game compatibility switches above UEVR; they do not expose UEVR's stereo implementation.",
  "pattern_oc_openvr_abi_codegen_version_facade": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE + SUPPLIED DOCS: historical OpenVR headers are split/namespaced, `GEN_INTERFACE` drives generated C++ and FnTable façades, and all versions forward into a single Base implementation.",
  "pattern_oc_apptype_interface_gating": "VERIFIED: generated APPTYPE metadata is checked inside `VR_GetGenericInterface` before constructing an interface façade.",
  "pattern_oc_backend_capability_abi": "VERIFIED: Base implementations call `BackendManager`; OpenXR behavior sits behind `IBackend`/device interfaces.",
  "pattern_oc_temporary_graphics_session_rebind": "VERIFIED: initial session uses temporary graphics; first submitted texture selects the application graphics binding and forces session recreation with deferred frame-start recovery.",
  "pattern_oc_waitgetposes_openxr_frame_contract": "VERIFIED: WaitGetPoses -> xrWaitFrame -> predictedDisplayTime -> xrBeginFrame -> cached xrLocateViews before returning tracked poses.",
  "pattern_oc_accumulate_two_openvr_submits": "VERIFIED: two OpenVR Submit calls populate projection views; xrEndFrame emits a single two-view projection layer.",
  "pattern_oc_postpresenthandoff_deferred_endframe": "VERIFIED: PostPresentHandoff switches submission to a deferred end-frame path so overlay layers can be included after eye Submit calls.",
  "pattern_oc_texture_type_graphics_binding": "VERIFIED: each OpenVR texture type is converted into the corresponding OpenXR graphics binding using the application’s actual native device/context.",
  "pattern_oc_per_api_swapchain_copy_adapters": "VERIFIED: a common Invoke/subimage contract delegates copying and synchronization to API-specific compositor classes.",
  "pattern_oc_texture_bounds_to_subimage": "VERIFIED: OpenVR normalized bounds are converted into XrSwapchainSubImage.imageRect with explicit vertical inversion handling.",
  "pattern_oc_colorspace_swapchain_format": "VERIFIED: swapchain format selection explicitly considers OpenVR color space and format metadata rather than mirroring the source resource format unconditionally.",
  "pattern_oc_runtime_eye_size_supersampling": "VERIFIED: recommended OpenXR view dimensions are multiplied by the configured supersample ratio and returned through OpenVR.",
  "pattern_oc_asymmetric_projection_translation": "VERIFIED: OpenXR XrFovf is converted to OpenVR raw tangents and an asymmetric DirectX projection matrix; OpenGL matrix correctness is explicitly marked TODO/STUBBED.",
  "pattern_oc_visibility_mask_to_hidden_area_mesh": "VERIFIED: XR_KHR_visibility_mask data is converted into OpenVR HiddenAreaMesh_t, including indexed-to-expanded vertex conversion.",
  "pattern_oc_tracking_universe_reference_spaces": "VERIFIED: Standing -> XR_STAGE and Seated -> XR_LOCAL mappings are centralized; raw/uncalibrated aborts as unsupported.",
  "pattern_oc_cached_views_single_time": "VERIFIED: per-space xrLocateViews results are cached at GetBestTime and invalidated on the next WaitForTrackingData frame.",
  "pattern_oc_pose_component_transform_adapter": "VERIFIED: pose actions compose tracked device pose with profile-specific SteamVR/controller component transforms based on the binding point.",
  "pattern_oc_openvr_manifest_to_openxr_actions": "VERIFIED: OpenVR manifest/actions/bindings are parsed and compiled into XrActionSet/XrAction objects and suggested interaction-profile bindings.",
  "pattern_oc_action_name_path_translation": "VERIFIED: source action names are escaped to OpenXR-safe identifiers and unsupported OpenVR binding paths are translated through per-profile path maps.",
  "pattern_oc_legacy_input_parallel_action_set": "VERIFIED: OpenComposite creates and always attaches a legacy controller action set and includes it in action synchronization.",
  "pattern_oc_action_manifest_session_restart": "VERIFIED: manifest/input changes can deliberately restart the OpenXR session and recreate invalidated action spaces before attachment.",
  "pattern_oc_interaction_profile_compat_objects": "VERIFIED: controller-specific paths, properties, transform corrections and skeletal/reference-pose data are centralized in InteractionProfile subclasses.",
  "pattern_oc_real_openxr_handjoints_to_openvr_skeleton": "VERIFIED: real OpenXR hand joints are converted into OpenVR wrist/metacarpal/flexion/auxiliary bones with explicit coordinate and parent-space handling.",
  "pattern_oc_skeletal_input_estimation_fallback": "VERIFIED: inactive/unsupported real hand tracking falls back to controller-input-driven interpolation over authored OpenVR skeletal reference poses.",
  "pattern_oc_controller_pose_fallback_to_handtracking": "VERIFIED: failed controller grip-space location falls back to palm-based XR hand tracking with a compatibility transform and tracked velocities.",
  "pattern_oc_openvr_haptic_to_openxr_feedback": "VERIFIED: OpenVR vibration action parameters are converted to XrHapticVibration and sent via xrApplyHapticFeedback; delayed start is TODO.",
  "pattern_oc_generic_xdev_trackers_to_openvr_devices": "VERIFIED: MNDX xdev enumeration/space creation is translated into OpenVR GenericTracker devices with serial properties and activation events.",
  "pattern_oc_tracked_device_property_emulation": "VERIFIED: OpenVR tracked-device properties are answered from profile maps plus explicit compatibility fallback values; several are documented in-source as guesses/static.",
  "pattern_oc_chaperone_from_stage_bounds": "VERIFIED: OpenXR Stage bounds rect is converted to OpenVR play-area size/rect and one collision quad; many Chaperone control APIs remain stubbed.",
  "pattern_oc_openvr_overlay_to_openxr_quad": "VERIFIED: OpenVR overlay textures are copied into their own OpenXR swapchains and submitted as XrCompositionLayerQuad layers with visibility/size/pose state.",
  "pattern_oc_overlay_fail_open_compat_boundaries": "VERIFIED: visual overlay subset is implemented; intersection/dashboard/cursor APIs are stubbed and keyboard may emit a placeholder done event.",
  "pattern_oc_synthetic_compositor_frame_timing": "VERIFIED: frame timing combines real frame index/submit timestamp/HMD pose with explicit hardcoded conservative values for metrics OpenXR cannot supply.",
  "pattern_oc_skybox_loading_frame_adapter": "VERIFIED: SetSkyboxOverride owns a separate OpenXR Wait/Begin/End frame and submits the loading texture as a quad layer; looping refresh is TODO.",
  "pattern_oc_hmd_presence_probe_without_session": "VERIFIED: HMD presence before initialization uses a temporary xrInstance + xrGetSystem probe and destroys it immediately.",
  "pattern_oc_proton_symbol_and_background_compat": "VERIFIED: Linux path explicitly admits Background apps and exports an HmdSystemFactory symbol solely for Proton recognition.",
  "pattern_oc_input_profile_discovery_info_set": "VERIFIED: an internal info action set is suggested/attached/synced to obtain interaction profiles before real application actions, followed by session restart when needed.",
  "pattern_oc_pose_velocity_space_translation": "VERIFIED: one PoseFromSpace helper fills OpenVR transform, validity/tracking result and XR linear/angular velocity for multiple device classes.",
  "pattern_oc_runtime_extension_capability_ladder": "VERIFIED: optional extensions are enumerated/loaded conditionally and each downstream subsystem has an explicit unavailable/fallback path.",
  "pattern_oc_session_state_to_openvr_events": "VERIFIED: OpenXR READY/STOPPING/LOSS_PENDING/interaction-profile events drive session begin/end and legacy event updates.",
  "pattern_oc_unknown_property_observability": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE/DOCS: unknown tracked-property behavior and request logging are explicit configuration options intended for compatibility troubleshooting.",
  "seed_opencomposite_openxr_case": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied `OpenOVR-openxr.zip`, commit embedded in archive `cff07db75c4823afe93ed7027b03d5f7bc86f164`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless marked as a limitation.\n\nARCHITECTURE: OpenComposite replaces the OpenVR client/runtime ABI while forwarding behavior to OpenXR. Historical OpenVR interfaces are split/namespaced and generated into C++ + FnTable façades; one shared Base implementation handles each subsystem. `VR_GetGenericInterface` performs interface-version lookup and application-type gating. `BackendManager` isolates the façade from the OpenXR backend.\n\nOPENXR/SESSION: `DrvOpenXR::CreateOpenXRBackend` creates an OpenXR 1.0 instance with available graphics/visibility-mask/hand-tracking/generic-tracker extensions. Because OpenVR does not reveal its graphics device until texture submission, OpenComposite begins with a temporary graphics session, then `CheckOrInitCompositors` recreates the session on the actual D3D11/D3D12/Vulkan/OpenGL/GLES binding from the first submitted texture. Session-owned input/action spaces are recreated after restarts.\n\nFRAME CONTRACT: `WaitGetPoses` -> `xrWaitFrame` -> predictedDisplayTime -> `xrBeginFrame` -> cached `xrLocateViews`; eye Submit calls copy into per-eye OpenXR swapchains; both eyes become one `XrCompositionLayerProjection`; `xrEndFrame` uses the same predicted time. PostPresentHandoff can defer the commit so OpenVR overlay layers submitted after the eyes are included.\n\nRENDER: graphics-specific compositor adapters create/acquire/wait/copy/release OpenXR swapchain images. `VRTextureBounds_t` becomes a subimage rectangle with flip handling; color-space intent influences linear/sRGB swapchain formats. Recommended eye size comes from OpenXR view configuration times supersampling. `XrFovf` maps to OpenVR asymmetric projections/raw tangents; visibility masks map to hidden-area meshes.\n\nTRACKING: Stage=standing, Local=seated, View=eye space. Per-space `xrLocateViews` results are cached at one BestTime. `PoseFromSpace` maps located pose + velocity + validity into `TrackedDevicePose_t`; raw/uncalibrated universe is unsupported.\n\nINPUT/HANDS: OpenVR manifests and binding JSON are parsed into OpenXR actions/action sets and suggested bindings, with path/name translation and a persistent legacy compatibility action set. Late input configuration can restart the session because OpenXR attachment is immutable. InteractionProfile objects own bindings, properties, render-model identity, grip/component transforms and skeleton reference poses. Real `XR_EXT_hand_tracking` joints are converted to OpenVR’s 31-bone skeleton; trigger/grip/touch-driven reference-pose interpolation is the fallback. Controller pose can fall back to palm hand tracking.\n\nHAPTICS: OpenVR vibration action -> `xrApplyHapticFeedback`; amplitude/frequency/duration and hand subaction are preserved, but delayed start time is not implemented.\n\nOVERLAYS/COMPAT: visible OpenVR overlays become OpenXR quad composition layers. Rich overlay interaction/dashboard APIs are incomplete, keyboard can emit a placeholder done event, Chaperone derives a rectangular Stage play area while editing/visibility is mostly stubbed. Frame timing exposes real frame index/submit time/HMD pose but several unsupported OpenVR metrics are conservative synthetic values. Generic trackers can be surfaced through `XR_MNDX_xdev_space`.\n\nNO GAMEPLAY CLAIMS: OpenComposite is not evidence for body IK, weapon handling, melee, physical object interaction, locomotion or multiplayer avatar replication.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_native_lua_capability_abi": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nARCHITECTURE: `src/vrmod.cpp` registers a deliberately small `vrmod` Lua table: lifecycle (`Init`/`Shutdown`), action-manifest/action-set configuration, `GetDisplayInfo`, one per-frame `UpdatePosesAndActions`, `GetPoses`, `GetActions`, shared-texture capture/submission, `TriggerHaptic` and tracked-device names. `core/cl_vrmod.lua` loads this with `require(\"vrmod\")` and aliases it to `VRMOD_*`, while all Source-specific camera, viewmodel, locomotion, interaction, UI and networking code remains Lua. The reusable seam is capability-shaped rather than runtime-object-shaped: gameplay never receives `IVRSystem`, action handles or compositor objects.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Native C++ owns XR lifetime and graphics interop; Lua owns game semantics. This makes the runtime module replaceable without rewriting every interaction subsystem.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp`, module `README`; Lua `core/cl_vrmod.lua`, `api/cl_api.lua`, `api/sv_api.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This supplied native module is OpenVR-specific. The separate gVRMod OpenXR backend already has its own registry case; do not merge their exact native APIs.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_one_shot_next_texture_capture": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nRENDERING: `VRMOD_ShareTextureBegin()` patches the platform texture-creation function so the next texture allocation jumps into `CreateTextureHook`. Lua immediately calls `GetRenderTargetEx` from `SetupRenderTargets`; the hook captures that texture/share handle and restores the original bytes on its first invocation. On Windows it intercepts the D3D9 device `CreateTexture` path; on Linux it resolves ToGL/OpenGL entry points and intercepts texture generation. `ShareTextureFinish()` then completes the backend-specific sharing setup.\n\nARCHITECTURE: This is a narrow interception-window methodology for engines where the scripting API can request a render target but cannot expose the native resource needed by an XR compositor. Arming only around the intended allocation is less invasive than permanently hooking every texture creation.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp::{ShareTextureBegin,CreateTextureHook,ShareTextureFinish}`; Lua `core/cl_vrmod.lua::SetupRenderTargets`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Byte/vtable/entry-point interception is platform/build-sensitive. The method assumes no unrelated texture allocation wins the race between arming the hook and creating the intended render target.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_platform_shared_texture_bridge": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nRENDERING: On Windows, `CreateTextureHook` forces the target into the default D3D9 pool with a shared `HANDLE`; `ShareTextureFinish` creates a D3D11 device and calls `OpenSharedResource`, producing an `ID3D11Texture2D` intended for `TextureType_DirectX` compositor submission. On Linux, the module dynamically opens Source's ToGL library, resolves OpenGL entry points, captures the generated GL texture ID and stores `TextureType_OpenGL`. Lua does not branch on D3D objects versus GL objects; it simply requests capture, renders the same way, and asks the module to submit.\n\nARCHITECTURE: Platform graphics-resource plumbing is below the stable scripting ABI. That lets gameplay/render orchestration stay identical while the native module owns incompatible resource-sharing mechanisms.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp` Windows D3D9/D3D11 and Linux ToGL/GL branches; Lua `core/cl_vrmod.lua::SetupRenderTargets`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: In this supplied snapshot `SubmitSharedTexture()` unconditionally changes the texture back to `TextureType_OpenGL` and replaces its handle with `g_sharedTexture`, even on Windows, conflicting with the DirectX resource prepared by `ShareTextureFinish`. Treat the intended Windows bridge as source-established architecture but the final Windows submit as internally inconsistent/unvalidated.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_side_by_side_projection_uv_bounds": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nRENDERING: Native `GetDisplayInfo` returns raw OpenVR left/right projection matrices plus recommended per-eye dimensions. `utils/cl_rendering.lua::CalculateProjectionParams` extracts X/Y scales and projection-center offsets, derives tangent extents, horizontal FOV and aspect. Lua allocates one target at `2 * RecommendedWidth` by `RecommendedHeight`; `PerformRenderViews` renders left/right into the two halves. `ComputeSubmitBounds` uses the retained projection-center terms to shift/scale each eye's UV rectangle, with Linux V inversion, and `SetSubmitTextureBounds` stores those rectangles for the native compositor submit.\n\nCAMERA / TRACKING: Eye positions still use OpenVR eye-to-head transforms/IPD; the UV correction is specifically a projection compatibility layer.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp::GetDisplayInfo,SetSubmitTextureBounds,SubmitSharedTexture`; Lua `utils/cl_rendering.lua::{CalculateProjectionParams,AdjustFOV,ComputeSubmitBounds}`, `core/cl_vrmod.lua::{ComputeDisplayParams,PerformRenderViews,SetupRenderTargets}`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is not a mathematically identical replacement for supplying Source a full asymmetric off-axis projection matrix. It is a practical adaptation to `render.RenderView`'s FOV/aspect interface and should be described that way.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_renderscene_frame_contract": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nFRAME TIMING: `core/cl_vrmod.lua::BindRenderSceneHook` runs: `VRMOD_UpdatePosesAndActions()` → `UpdateTracking()` → `UpdateCollisionsAndWepPos()` → `HandleInput()` → `VRUtilNetUpdateLocalPly()` → `UpdateViewFromEntity()` → `PerformRenderViews()` → `VRMOD_SubmitSharedTexture()` → `VRMod_PostRender`. Native `UpdatePosesAndActions` calls `VRCompositor()->WaitGetPoses` then `VRInput()->UpdateActionState`; the module README states that this call also paces FPS to headset refresh. `PerformRenderViews` issues two Source `render.RenderView` calls into one target.\n\nARCHITECTURE: One hook owns the XR-facing frame ordering, which prevents unrelated Think/UI/network code from independently sampling tracking at inconsistent times.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: Lua `core/cl_vrmod.lua::{BindRenderSceneHook,PerformRenderViews,UpdateTracking}`; native `src/vrmod.cpp::UpdatePosesAndActions`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: There is no source-established freeze of Source simulation, random render state or once-per-frame side effects between the left and right `RenderView` calls. Do not describe this as deterministic stereo replay.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_openvr_to_source_pose_space": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nCAMERA / TRACKING: Native `GetPoses` maps OpenVR position to Source/GMod as `(-Z,-X,+Y)` and applies the same axis remap to linear velocity; angular velocity is remapped and converted rad/s→deg/s. Pose actions are requested with `GetPoseActionDataRelativeToNow(..., TrackingUniverseStanding, 0, ...)`. Lua `UpdateTracking` multiplies position/velocity by `g_VR.scale`, rotates them by `g_VR.originAngle`, translates through `g_VR.origin`, applies per-controller calibration offsets, optional smoothing and stores canonical world poses in `g_VR.tracking`.\n\nARCHITECTURE: World origin/rotation/scale become first-class state. Roomscale, weapons, UI, vehicles and networking can all reference the same converted pose contract.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp::GetPoses`; Lua `core/cl_vrmod.lua::UpdateTracking`, `api/cl_api.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: `TrackingUniverseStanding` is hard-coded in the native pose-action query. The addon then handles seated/artificial offsets at the Lua/world layer.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_actionsets_to_source_commands": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nINPUT: `cl_steamvr_bindings.lua` defines the pose/skeleton/haptic actions and action sets. Native `SetActionManifest` parses the action manifest and resolves handles; `SetActiveActionSets` caches selected action-set handles; `GetActions` returns analog values plus only-changed booleans in a second table. `cl_input.lua` consumes those edge changes to execute Source-style press/release commands such as `+attack/-attack`, `+attack2/-attack2`, `+reload/-reload`, jump/duck, flashlight and menu actions. Vehicle enter/exit changes the active action set to `/actions/driving` or main gameplay.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Weapons, movement and addons that already react to Source commands continue to work; XR bindings do not replace their gameplay state machines.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp::{SetActionManifest,SetActiveActionSets,GetActions}`; Lua `input/cl_steamvr_bindings.lua`, `input/cl_input.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is action-manifest/controller-input abstraction, not hand-tracking gesture recognition by itself.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_skeletal_curls_valvebiped_fingers": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nHANDS: Native `GetActions` detects skeleton actions and calls `IVRInput::GetSkeletalSummaryData`, returning five `fingerCurls` per hand. `player/cl_character.lua` keeps arrays of 15 finger bones per hand and interpolates each bone's offset angle between `g_VR.openHandAngles` and `g_VR.closedHandAngles` according to the corresponding curl. `api/cl_api.lua` can also extract finger-angle deltas from arbitrary weapon/hand models so a weapon's authored grip can become the open/closed pose reference.\n\nBODY / IK: Finger presentation is layered onto the same ValveBiped skeleton used by arm IK but does not require full skeletal-bone data from OpenVR.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp::GetActions`; Lua `player/cl_character.lua`, `api/cl_api.lua::{GetLeftHandFingerAnglesFromModel,GetRightHandFingerAnglesFromModel}`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Only curl summary is consumed; individual OpenVR skeletal bone transforms/splay are not used in this source path.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_measured_two_bone_arm_ik": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nBODY / IK: `player/cl_character.lua::CharacterInit` resolves ValveBiped clavicle/upper-arm/forearm/hand bones and measures segment lengths from the actual model. `UpdateIK::ProcessArm` takes the network/local tracked hand target, points the clavicle part-way toward the target, builds the shoulder→hand vector, clamps the reach to the measured arm chain, and uses law-of-cosines angles (`acos`) to solve upper-arm and elbow bend. Additional body-yaw/hand-height policy biases roll. Wrist orientation is decomposed from the target hand angle; where a ValveBiped ulna/wrist bone exists, `LerpAngle(0.5, forearm, wrist)` shares twist instead of dumping it all into one joint.\n\nARCHITECTURE: The solver works for local and remote VR players because it consumes the normalized per-player frame rather than OpenVR devices directly.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `player/cl_character.lua::{CharacterInit,UpdateIK,ProcessArm}`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is heuristic two-bone avatar IK, not an anatomically constrained full-body solver; tracked waist/feet are not fed into it.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_hmd_height_crouch_reconstruction": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nBODY / IK: `UpdateIK` compares HMD-derived body height with the measured character proportions. When the head is below the standing target it shortens/bends the spine and computes thigh/calf angles using measured upper/lower leg lengths and law-of-cosines relationships, producing a synthetic crouched body pose. The head orientation remains driven from HMD and arms still solve to tracked hands.\n\nLOCOMOTION: Separately, `sh_locomotion.lua` can translate low HMD height into native `IN_DUCK`; the visual skeleton reconstruction and gameplay crouch command are distinct layers.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `player/cl_character.lua::UpdateIK`; `input/sh_locomotion.lua` physical crouch path.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is HMD-height inference, not tracked foot/waist IK. Do not label it as six-point full-body tracking.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_hand_baseline_torso_yaw": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nBODY / IK: The character update derives a candidate body yaw from the vector spanning the tracked left and right hands. That yaw is constrained to roughly ±45° around the HMD-facing direction and smoothed with a frame-time-weighted interpolation. When ordinary locomotion/turning indicates the player is intentionally changing heading, character yaw can snap/reconcile back toward HMD yaw.\n\nCAMERA / TRACKING: This separates head orientation from torso/avatar orientation without a waist tracker and gives the arm solver a stable body reference.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `player/cl_character.lua` character-yaw update and `UpdateIK`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Hand-baseline yaw is a heuristic; it is not a measured chest/waist orientation and can only approximate torso heading.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_sixpoint_transport_without_leg_solver": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: `core/cl_vrmod.lua` sets `g_VR.sixPoints` when waist, left-foot and right-foot pose actions are all present. `buildClientFrame` appends those transforms, and `netWriteFrame/netReadFrame` serializes them behind one presence bit. The server/client relative/world conversion preserves the poses.\n\nBODY / IK: The built-in `player/cl_character.lua` solver does not read the transported waist/foot fields to position pelvis/thigh/calf/feet. Its leg pose comes from HMD-height crouch reconstruction. This makes an important architecture distinction: six-point tracking transport exists, but six-point avatar consumption does not.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `core/cl_vrmod.lua::UpdateTracking`; `network/sh_network.lua::{buildClientFrame,netWriteFrame,netReadFrame}`; `player/cl_character.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Do not classify this source as tracker-driven full-body leg IK solely because `g_VR.sixPoints` and foot/waist network fields exist.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_quantized_relative_pose_network": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: `network/sh_network.lua::netWriteFrame` sends a `SysTime()` double, character yaw crushed to 7 bits, ten finger curls as 7-bit integers scaled by 100, HMD/left/right hand vectors+angles, then one boolean followed by waist/left-foot/right-foot vectors+angles when available. `buildClientFrame(true)` converts to player/vehicle-relative coordinates before transmit; wheel-gripped hands can use their latched vehicle-relative pose so the networked hand stays on the animated control. A replicated `vrmod_net_tickrate` timer sends frames, and unchanged frames can be skipped.\n\nARCHITECTURE: Optional tracker extension is backward within the same frame shape: consumers can ignore it while still receiving core HMD/hands/fingers.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `network/sh_network.lua::{buildClientFrame,netWriteFrame,netReadFrame,SendFrame}` and frame conversion utilities.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The supplied protocol is project-specific and does not show protocol-version negotiation; it assumes matching addon expectations.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_server_pose_prediction": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: `api/sv_api.lua::UpdateWorldPoses` converts the latest relative frame to world, keeps a `HandVelocityCache` per player, smooths observed `dt` into `avgDt` (initialized near 90 Hz), and only updates velocity when a minimum interval `max(0.01, avgDt*2)` has accumulated to reduce noisy derivatives. `PredictPose` advances HMD, left hand and right hand by `predictionHorizon = cache.avgDt` using both linear and angular velocity. Server API getters for pose/velocity then expose this predicted gameplay state to melee, pickup, buttons and physics hands.\n\nFRAME TIMING: This is network/gameplay prediction, not compositor late-latching or OpenVR display-time prediction.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `api/sv_api.lua::{UpdateWorldPoses,PredictPose,GetHMD*,GetLeftHand*,GetRightHand*}`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The predictor is simple constant-velocity/angular-velocity extrapolation about one average frame ahead; it is not client reconciliation or a full network interpolation buffer.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_latest_sample_remote_avatar": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: The client receive path stores the latest frame for each VR player. The function named `LerpOtherVRPlayers` currently shallow-copies that latest frame and transforms it from relative to world coordinates; there is no temporal lerp/nlerp across two pose snapshots in the reviewed implementation. `PrePlayerDraw`/`UpdateIK` then use that frame's HMD, hands, character yaw and finger curls to pose the remote ValveBiped avatar.\n\nBODY / IK: Local and remote arm solving share the same model-measured IK code because the solver consumes frame data rather than runtime device objects.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `network/sh_network.lua` receive/`LerpOtherVRPlayers`; `player/cl_character.lua` remote drawing/IK.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Name notwithstanding, the reviewed function is latest-sample reconstruction. Do not describe it as pose interpolation.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_viewmodel_handbone_autocalibration": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nWEAPONS: On weapon switch `network/sh_network.lua` creates a clientside model when needed, locates `ValveBiped.Bip01_R_Hand`, rotates the authored hand frame into VR convention, and uses `WorldToLocal` to derive the initial weapon offset from that bone. It can also call `vrmod.GetRightHandFingerAnglesFromModel(model)` so the weapon's own authored grip pose drives the tracked hand fingers. `g_VR.viewModelInfo[class]` stores per-class offset plus `wrongMuzzleAng`/`noLaser` policy; `player/cl_viewmodeledit.lua` provides an in-headset editor and persists the results to JSON. `api/cl_api.lua` exposes setters for other addons.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: The Source viewmodel/weapon stays the actual weapon system; VR changes presentation/aim alignment rather than duplicating SWEP state.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `network/sh_network.lua` weapon-switch logic; `player/cl_viewmodeledit.lua`; `api/cl_api.lua`; `utils/sh_weps.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Bone-derived alignment depends on conventional ValveBiped/viewmodel authoring; unusual SWEP rigs still need manual offsets.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_model_derived_weapon_collision_geometry": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nWEAPONS: `utils/sh_collisions.lua::ComputePhysicsParams` spawns/initializes the requested model with VPhysics when possible, reads its physics/AABB dimensions, converts that geometry through the weapon/viewmodel orientation, and caches horizontal/vertical box bounds, radius and reach. `DetectMeleeFromModel` combines filename hints with a long/thin aspect-ratio test (about 4.5:1) and axis alignment to decide whether the shape behaves like a melee tool. `GetWeaponMeleeParams/GetCachedWeaponParams` expose the same geometry to both wall collision and melee code; cached parameters can be synchronized client/server.\n\nARCHITECTURE: One asset-derived geometry primitive feeds multiple systems, avoiding independent approximations for wall collision and combat reach.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `utils/sh_collisions.lua::{ComputePhysicsParams,DetectMeleeFromModel,GetModelParams,GetWeaponMeleeParams,GetCachedWeaponParams}`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: AABB/aspect heuristics cannot infer every weapon's semantic grip/blade orientation; per-weapon offsets still influence the resulting hand-space shape.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_client_hand_weapon_wall_pushout": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: `utils/sh_collisions.lua::CollisionsPreCheck` avoids expensive world checks unless hand spheres are near collision. `UpdateHandCollisions` compares the requested hand pose with cached state, tests sphere/hull collision, and uses the last non-clipped point or a short hull trace along the collision normal to calculate a stable pushout before writing corrected hand positions into the presentation tracking state. `CheckWeaponPushout` uses the asset-derived oriented weapon box; when penetrated it shifts the viewmodel along the normal and projects the weapon forward vector onto the collision plane to correct angle as well as position.\n\nHANDS: This solves visual hand/SWEP wall penetration separately from the hidden server physics-hand proxies.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `utils/sh_collisions.lua::{CollisionsPreCheck,CheckWorldCollisions,UpdateHandCollisions,CheckWeaponPushout}`, `utils/sh_weps.lua::UpdateViewModelPos`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is local presentation collision correction, not authoritative player-body collision or a physics constraint on the real tracked controller.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_server_physics_hand_proxies": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: `physics/sv_physhands.lua` creates hidden `base_anim` entities for each VR hand, marks them with `NWBool(\"isVRHand\", true)`, initializes sphere physics, and can replace the right-hand shape with weapon-derived box geometry after weapon changes. Every `PlayerTick`, each proxy is driven toward `vrmod.GetLeft/RightHandPos/Ang(ply)` plus the configured hand-forward offset using `PhysicsObject:ComputeShadowControl`. Proxies are disabled while the player is in a vehicle and are created/removed across VR start, spawn, death, exit, cleanup and disconnect.\n\nARCHITECTURE: Visual IK hands, client wall-pushout hands and server physics hands are intentionally different objects with different authority responsibilities.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `physics/sv_physhands.lua`; server pose API in `api/sv_api.lua`; geometry in `utils/sh_collisions.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Shadow-controlled proxies approximate tracked motion; they are not the same rigid body as the user's controller and can lag/network-predict independently of local visuals.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_server_validated_motioncontroller_pickup": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: Client `pickup/sh_pickup.lua` searches each hand with a near-hand sphere and fallback trace, using server-published per-player validity flags for feedback. On grip/drop it sends only hand identity and target. Server `ValidatePickup` verifies player/VR state, entity, free hand, pickup policy and hooks, then gets the server VR hand transform. `CreatePickupInfo` stores the object's transform relative to that hand. A shared server `vrmod_pickup` motion controller receives `PhysicsSimulate` and converts the relative transform back to world each tick, using `ComputeShadowControl` on the held rigid body. Release removes the controller association.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Source VPhysics remains responsible for object mass/collision; VRMod only supplies the moving target transform.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `pickup/sh_pickup.lua`, `utils/sh_pickup.lua`, server pose API `api/sv_api.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The reviewed ordinary-prop drop path does not explicitly write controller release velocity into the prop. Do not claim a dedicated physical-throw velocity handoff for normal props.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_ragdoll_multibody_hand_grab": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: Pickup utilities detect ragdolls and enumerate their physics objects. On grab they store each physics bone's transform relative to the authoritative hand. During the pickup motion-controller update, every valid rigid body receives its own `ComputeShadowControl` target derived from that stored offset, so the ragdoll remains an articulated group while following the hand.\n\nARCHITECTURE: This is a general solution for grabbing compound physics entities: snapshot each constituent rigid body's local hand transform, not just entity origin/angles.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `utils/sh_pickup.lua` ragdoll pickup-info/update helpers; `pickup/sh_pickup.lua` server ownership lifecycle.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Driving every ragdoll body is more expensive and can still fight joints/world collision; the source does not implement a physically modeled grip constraint with force limits.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_held_prop_owner_collision_isolation": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: The pickup layer tracks grab ownership. `ShouldCollide` filters held entities against the owning player and the owner's `isVRHand` physics proxies; damage hooks similarly prevent the player's own held prop from damaging them. The object still participates in normal world/other-entity VPhysics.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Collision filtering is narrow and ownership-based rather than globally making held objects non-solid.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `pickup/sh_pickup.lua` / pickup utilities collision and damage hooks; `physics/sv_physhands.lua` proxy marker.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This policy trades some physical realism near the player's body for stability and safety; it is not a general collision-layer solution for every addon entity.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_manual_weapon_pickup_native_inventory": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: `pickup/sh_manualpickup.lua` disables/default-filters touch pickup for VR users and integrates weapon entities into explicit hand-target selection. On validated grip the server uses Garry's Mod/Source weapon ownership (`Give`/selection/equip behavior) rather than treating the weapon as a permanently hand-held physics prop. This preserves SWEP inventory/ammo/state compatibility while making acquisition spatially intentional.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: VR changes the condition that grants the weapon, not the SWEP's inventory/lifecycle implementation.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `pickup/sh_manualpickup.lua`, `pickup/sh_pickup.lua`, pickup utility eligibility lists.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is physical acquisition of a conventional SWEP, not manual magazine/ammunition handling.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_server_retrace_velocity_melee": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nMELEE: On `VRMod_Tracking`, `combat/sh_combat.lua` precomputes hand/weapon geometry. Hand velocity is explicitly measured relative to HMD (`GetLeft/RightHandVelocityRelative`) so player/world locomotion is less likely to count as a swing. After the configured speed threshold is exceeded, the client traces `TraceBoxOrSphere` using model-derived radius/reach/box/angles and sends the proposed geometry/impact class. The server re-derives HMD-relative swing speed from `api/sv_api.lua`, repeats `TraceBoxOrSphere` with server filtering, applies cooldown, then creates `DamageInfo`, damage type/position/force, sound/decal and `VRMod_MeleeHit` extension callbacks. Damage is `base * min(5, 1+swingSpeed*speedScale) * impactMultiplier`.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Entity damage occurs only after server trace validation.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `combat/sh_combat.lua`; `utils/sh_collisions.lua::{TraceBoxOrSphere,GetWeaponMeleeParams}`; `api/sv_api.lua` relative velocity getters.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Contact is a current-pose box/sphere trace rather than a continuous historical sweep. The code reads target velocity but currently sets `relativeSpeed = swingSpeed`; target motion is not actually subtracted from the damage-speed term.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_server_hand_proximity_native_use": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: `input/sh_buttons.lua` receives which hand gripped, asks the server VR API for that hand's world position, searches a very small radius (5 units) for an allowlisted button/charger/ammo-crate/rotating-door/wire-button class, and calls `ent:Use(ply)` on the selected entity. The client does not send an arbitrary world position for this test.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Existing `func_button`, `func_rot_button`, charger, ammo and addon entities remain responsible for their normal state/output logic.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_buttons.lua`; `api/sv_api.lua` hand pose getters.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is proximity Use, not physically depressing/rotating the entity through a continuous hand constraint.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_authored_door_grab_affordances": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: `input/sh_doors.lua` registers grab points through `vrmod.AddUseGrabPoint`, keyed by map/entity class/model/bodygroup/bone plus local position/orientation. Grip searches candidate points, requires squared hand distance under the configured threshold (source uses `<64`) and hand orientation dot above about `0.5`. The chosen visual/network hand pose is lerped/snapped to the moving handle/bone; after attachment the addon requests native `ent:Use(ply)`, then releases and lerps the hand back to tracking.\n\nARCHITECTURE: This is an affordance-adapter pattern for old games where interaction semantics exist but geometry was never authored for physical VR grabbing.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_doors.lua`; hand-pose override API in `api/cl_api.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The reviewed server receive is rate-limited but does not independently repeat the client hand-distance/orientation test before calling Use. Treat the spatial gate as client-established presentation/intent, not fully authoritative server validation.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_physgun_motion_to_legacy_mouse": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: `input/sh_gravity_and_physgun.lua` detects the active `weapon_physgun` while use is held. It projects left-hand translation onto eye-forward to synthesize `IN_FORWARD/IN_BACK`, and converts left-hand pitch/yaw deltas into `cmd:SetMouseX/SetMouseY`. The original physgun continues to interpret those inputs for distance/rotation/manipulation.\n\nINPUT: This is a tracked-motion → legacy input adapter, useful when native gameplay has hidden state or broad addon compatibility tied to existing mouse controls.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_gravity_and_physgun.lua` `CreateMove` physgun path.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The hand is not directly constraining the grabbed rigid body; fidelity is bounded by the physgun's original mouse-control semantics.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_gravitygun_tracked_prop_rotation": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: Server hooks in `input/sh_gravity_and_physgun.lua` detect gravity-gun pickup. While the entity is held, a Think hook retrieves the tracked right-hand angle, blends the physics/entity orientation toward it (`LerpAngle`), clears angular velocity and applies the new orientation. Drop removes the per-entity update hook.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: The gravity gun still owns which entity is held and its ordinary translation/weapon state; VRMod layers tracked rotation on top.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_gravity_and_physgun.lua` gravity-gun pickup/drop/update hooks; server hand API.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is orientation steering, not a two-hand rigid-body grip or force/torque constraint.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_roomscale_origin_createmove_bridge": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nLOCOMOTION: `input/sh_locomotion.lua` derives smooth movement from the stick oriented by HMD or left-controller yaw and writes native forward/side move values. Low physical HMD height can set `IN_DUCK`. Snap/smooth turn rotates `g_VR.origin`/`originAngle` around the player, so the HMD itself is never forcibly yawed. A `PreRender` roomscale-follow step computes horizontal HMD/body displacement; ordinary separations are reconciled using player velocity and moving-ground velocity, while very large separation rebases the origin to avoid runaway drift. `SetViewAngles` chooses muzzle/right-hand/HMD view policy according to context.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Source remains responsible for native movement/collision/grounding; the addon adjusts the mapping between physical tracking space and that player body.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_locomotion.lua`; `core/cl_vrmod.lua` origin/tracking conversion.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This source does not establish an HLVR-style transactional player-move collision solver for physical roomscale displacement.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_privileged_parabolic_teleport": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nLOCOMOTION: The client traces a roughly 16-segment parabolic arc from the configured controller and only marks a destination when the final surface normal has sufficiently large upward Z (about `>=0.7`). The server accepts the teleport only when the feature is enabled and the player passes noclip privilege checks (`PlayerNoClip`/ULX-style permission), then calls `ply:SetPos`.\n\nARCHITECTURE: This is useful in the registry specifically as a minimal/counterexample implementation: it makes the visual arc and policy gate obvious while showing what robust gameplay teleport still needs.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_locomotion.lua` teleport arc/client render and server teleport receive.\n\nLIMITATIONS: No player-hull fit search, path reachability, crossed-trigger replay or native navigation validation is established. It is best understood as privileged/admin convenience teleport.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_authored_rung_climbing": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nLOCOMOTION: `input/sh_climbing.lua` contains a substantial database describing supported HL2/GMod ladders, rung transforms/count/spacing and dismount locations. Grip near a valid rung snaps/latches the visual hand. While latched, hand motion drives body/VR-origin motion in the opposite direction. Releasing can transfer support to the other hand or select an authored dismount using proximity/facing/line-of-sight tests. Server hooks disable/replace native `func_useableladder` behavior and perform the dismount move.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: The physical controller becomes a temporary constraint against a semantic authored rung, not an arbitrary world-mesh grab.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_climbing.lua` ladder database, grip, movement and dismount paths.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The file exits immediately outside single-player (`if not game.SinglePlayer() then return end`). This is map-authored ladder adaptation, not generic geometry climbing.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_vehicle_bone_grip_steering": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nVEHICLES: `utils/sh_vehicles.lua::GetSteeringInfo` searches the vehicle skeleton for exact/approximate steering-wheel/handlebar bones and classifies common vehicle types. On grip, `SteeringGripTransform` stores each hand's pose relative to the selected bone; pre-render reconstructs the hand from the bone's current animated transform so it visually remains attached. `SteeringGripInput` separately captures neutral HMD-relative hand position and maps displacement to steering (car uses one axis, motorcycle another), scales by wheel rotation range/sensitivity, blends both hands when present and smooths the result. Normal Source vehicles receive this through their existing `CreateMove` controls. The network frame can use the latched pose for wheel-gripped hands so remote presentation stays attached too.\n\nINPUT: Vehicle entry switches to the dedicated OpenVR driving action set.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `utils/sh_vehicles.lua`, `network/sh_network.lua::buildClientFrame`, `input/cl_input.lua` action-set switching.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Bone-name heuristics and vehicle-class policies cannot identify every custom vehicle automatically; unknown rigs need explicit compatibility handling.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_glide_aircraft_controller_attitude": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nVEHICLES: `input/sh_glide.lua` integrates with the Glide vehicle addon. Gripping the configured control captures the controller's neutral orientation; later controller angular delta becomes aircraft pitch/yaw/roll values. Throttle, brake, steering and button states are transmitted through Glide's existing `SetInputFloat`/`SetInputBool` API; a timeout resets analog inputs if updates stop. `PatchGlideCamera` replaces the local view with HMD facing while retaining Glide's third-person collision trace where applicable, and VR attack actions can still map to native/turret firing while driving.\n\nNATIVE GAMEPLAY AUTHORITY: Glide remains responsible for vehicle physics; VRMod is an alternate control producer.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/sh_glide.lua`; vehicle state helpers in `utils/sh_vehicles.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This is a virtual attitude controller, not a tracked physical cockpit/yoke constraint.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_vgui_rt_world_pointer": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nUI: `ui/cl_ui.lua` manually paints VGUI panels into render targets and draws them with `cam.Start3D2D` at world/tracking/left-hand-relative poses. The right controller supplies the pointer ray. Ray-plane intersection and local basis conversion yield panel pixel coordinates; the nearest active menu receives focus and the addon drives `gui.EnableScreenClicker`, `input.SetCursorPos`, `gui.InternalMousePressed` and `gui.InternalMouseReleased`. The panel's normal VGUI callbacks/state remain intact.\n\nARCHITECTURE: UI visual placement and input adaptation are separate from widget implementation, making existing addon menus reusable.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `ui/cl_ui.lua`; `api/cl_api.lua` menu API.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Native cursor injection means other systems fighting over the desktop cursor can still conflict; focus arbitration is required when multiple VR panels overlap.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_keypad_scoped_pointer_patch": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nUI: `input/cl_keypad.lua` supports keypad entity implementations by temporarily replacing `CalculateCursorPos`. The override only becomes relevant when the right hand is within about 35 Source units and a tracked-hand trace hits the keypad. It intersects the hand ray with the keypad plane, transforms that point into the keypad's render coordinates and feeds the result to the existing `GetHoveredElement(...).click` path. When VR/proximity/target conditions stop, the original method is restored.\n\nARCHITECTURE: This is a compatibility-patch methodology: scope monkey-patching by spatial context and lifetime rather than permanently replacing third-party UI code.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `input/cl_keypad.lua` keypad patch/unpatch and ray-plane conversion.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The adapter is implementation-specific to supported keypad APIs; it is not a universal world-surface UI detector.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_curved_hmd_hud": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nUI: `ui/cl_hud.lua` suppresses/captures configured conventional HUD drawing into a render target, generates a curved mesh from configurable scale/curve/distance parameters, and renders the texture near the HMD. The original HUD elements remain the content source; the VR layer changes presentation geometry rather than reimplementing health/ammo/etc.\n\nRENDERING: This differs from the VGUI world-panel path: it is a head-relative curved HUD surface, not an interactable flat menu plane.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `ui/cl_hud.lua` HUD capture/mesh/render code.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Curving a captured 2D HUD does not add depth awareness or per-widget spatial interaction.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_muzzle_viewoffset_server_bridge": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nWEAPONS: Each client VR net tick writes `g_VR.viewModelMuzzle.Pos/Ang` before the body frame. The server stores the values and, when the muzzle position is non-zero and `util.IsInWorld`, adjusts `ply.viewOffset` relative to `ply:EyePos()`, calls `SetCurrentViewOffset`, and preserves only the vertical component for ordinary `SetViewOffset`. Invalid/zero muzzle restores the original view offset. This is explicitly named a `viewHack` in source.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: Muzzle pose has a separate network seam from avatar HMD/hands/fingers because it serves gameplay/viewmodel compatibility rather than remote embodiment.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `network/sh_network.lua::SendFrame` and server `vrutil_net_tick` receive; `utils/sh_weps.lua::UpdateViewModel` attachment-1 muzzle update.\n\nLIMITATIONS: This does not prove every SWEP fires from the supplied muzzle; individual weapons may derive origins differently. Treat it as a compatibility adapter for view/eye-derived server logic.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_native_haptic_endpoint_unwired": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nHAPTICS: Native `TriggerHaptic(actionName, delay, duration, frequency, amplitude)` resolves the named action created from the action manifest and calls `IVRInput::TriggerHapticVibrationAction`. The supplied bindings include left/right haptic output actions. This establishes a reusable runtime endpoint and handed action routing.\n\nARCHITECTURE: A haptic output API is useful even before semantic event mapping exists because gameplay addons can target it without touching OpenVR handles.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: native `src/vrmod.cpp::TriggerHaptic`; action definitions in `input/cl_steamvr_bindings.lua`/manifest setup.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Searching the supplied Metastruct Lua source finds no call site to `VRMOD_TriggerHaptic`/native `TriggerHaptic`. Do not claim active weapon, melee, UI or damage haptics from this snapshot.",
  "pattern_gmodvr_public_semantic_lua_api": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nARCHITECTURE: `api/cl_api.lua` exposes local/remote HMD and hand poses, raw and HMD-relative velocity/angular velocity, smoothed visual hand-pose overrides, open/closed finger-pose getters/setters, extracting hand/finger poses from models, input queries, VR menu render/cursor helpers, viewmodel offset/muzzle/laser configuration, locomotion option registration, origin get/set, eye positions and tracked-device names. `api/sv_api.lua` exposes server world HMD/hand pose and derived velocity/angular velocity, relative velocity, held entities and interaction helpers based on the predicted server pose cache.\n\nARCHITECTURE: The API is semantic and game-facing. Addons can implement new SWEPs/interactions without depending on `IVRSystem`, Lua-module internals or the exact tracking network format.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `api/cl_api.lua`, `api/sv_api.lua`, `api/sh_api.lua` plus public VRMod hook aliases.\n\nLIMITATIONS: Some API calls expose current implementation policy (for example predicted server poses); addon code should prefer semantic getters over reaching into the backing cache/network tables.",
  "seed_frik_case": `Source-audit (2026-09-05), verified against the public rollingrock/Fallout-4-VR-Body repository (main branch) plus its declared external/F4VR-CommonFramework submodule (ArthurHub/F4VR-CommonFramework).

ARCHITECTURE: FRIK.dll is an F4SE(VR) plugin, not a stereo renderer or injector — Fallout 4 VR already renders natively in VR, so FRIK's whole scope is: rewrite the player's own NiNode skeleton every frame so a full body follows the HMD and two motion controllers, reposition the equipped weapon in the tracked hand, and drive a physically-interactive Pip-Boy. Source is organized by subsystem: src/skeleton (IK, hand pose, cull geometry, weapon-hand recoil, selfie mode), src/weapon-position (weapon/offhand/throwable/scope offset logic + its own in-game config UI), src/pipboy (device state, operation handling, physical hit-testing), src/config-mode (in-game body/Pip-Boy adjustment UIs), src/api (versioned public API for other mods), src/reload (gun-reload animation capture), src/smooth-movement (locomotion smoothing), plus FRIK.cpp/GameHooks.cpp/Config.cpp as the top-level glue.

INJECTION/BOOTSTRAP (verified — FRIK.cpp, GameHooks.cpp): F4SEPlugin_Query rejects load unless REL::Module::IsVR() and the exact expected VR runtime version match. F4SEPlugin_Load hands off to a singleton FRIK object. onModLoaded() calls hook::patchAll() (four small Xbyak-generated inline patches for an inventory-count overflow bug, a lock-mask bug, a scope-effect null-pointer crash, and a body-pose byte patch, each installed via F4SE::GetTrampoline().write_branch<N> to a code cave that jumps back into the original instruction stream) and hook::hookMain() (redirects a small named set of engine call sites — smooth-movement update, main player update, two Power-Armor re-equip sites — to FRIK wrapper functions via trampoline.write_call<5>, each wrapper doing FRIK work and then calling the original function). blockResetBodyPose() separately NOPs ~0x1FF bytes of one animation virtual-function implementation so the engine can no longer snap body position back under the camera every frame, which is the precondition for FRIK owning body placement afterward.

RENDERER/XR INTEGRATION: UNKNOWN/not applicable by design — FRIK does not hook Present, does not touch swapchains, does not submit poses to OpenVR/OpenXR, and does not modify stereo cameras or projection matrices. All of that is supplied by the underlying Fallout 4 VR engine build. No frame-timing/predicted-display-time code exists in this mod; it runs its own per-frame update from a hooked *gameplay* call site (main player update), not a render-thread hook.

FRAME TIMING/LIFECYCLE (verified — FRIK.cpp): FRIK::onFrameUpdate() is the master per-frame entry, invoked from the hooked main-player-update call. It defers skeleton (re)initialization until required game nodes resolve, and rebuilds the whole Skeleton object when the root node becomes invalid or a Power-Armor enter/exit is confirmed stable for kPowerArmorChangeConfirmFrames consecutive frames (debouncing a transient engine state). Skeleton::create() only returns a live object once every required node (root, common node, flattened bone tree, first-person RArm_Hand/LArm_Hand, Head/SPINE2/Chest, weapon node, camera+cameraRoot) is confirmed present. releaseSkeleton() tears down dependents (Pip-Boy, weapon-position config) before the Skeleton they read from, and broadcasts versioned kSkeletonDestroying/kSkeletonReady lifecycle events over the API so external mods can drop and re-acquire stale node pointers.

CAMERA: FRIK does not touch the VR camera/eye transforms; it repositions the player's own body root under the already-tracked HMD (setBodyUnderHMD), applies a configurable vertical offset (Power Armor, comfort-sneak, and an API-settable dynamic height), and moves the head mesh node back/up (setupHead) purely to reduce the visible avatar's own head clipping into the camera — the HMD-to-eye/projection pipeline itself is entirely native-engine.

COORDINATE TRANSFORMS: Works directly in the engine's own NiNode local/world transform hierarchy (RE::NiTransform local + world per node). The recurring, explicitly-commented pattern is: mutate one or more nodes' *local* transforms, then call an explicit updateDownFromRoot()/updateTransformsDown() world-propagation pass before the next IK stage reads world-space positions — repeated after body-under-HMD, posture, knees/legs, and arms. Bone lengths (arm segments, leg segments, torso length) are measured live from the actual skeleton's world-space node distances rather than hardcoded, so the IK adapts to Power-Armor proportions and body-scale settings.

INPUT: Reads tracked HMD and per-controller poses and analog axes through the vendored f4vr::/vrcf:: framework layer (VRControllersManager/VRControllers.check, exposed by the F4VR-CommonFramework submodule) rather than implementing its own OpenVR/OpenXR action bindings from scratch; button/axis bindings for grip modes, offhand gripping, and Pip-Boy interaction are configurable through FRIK.ini.

HANDS: HandPose (src/skeleton/HandPose.cpp, HandPoseMath.cpp, HandPoseData.cpp) drives five-finger curl per hand. The header comments are explicit that the geometry and the runtime are deliberately separated: HandPoseMath is pure functions over authored bone data (HandPoseData) with no live-skeleton access, so an external mod can resolve or mirror a pose through the API before FRIK has built a skeleton to apply it to, while HandPose alone owns the tagged override registry and per-frame blend into the live bone tree, and depends on HandPoseMath, never the reverse. Non-thumb finger *dynamic* curl is bound to a specific controller input by convention (index -> trigger axis, other three -> grip axis when an analog axis is exposed), with a precomputed "open hand" base pose per Power-Armor state (HandPoseMath::buildOpenPoseTransforms). A tagged, prioritized override system (setHandPoseOverride/clearHandPoseOverride/getHandPoseSetTagState, keyed by string tag + integer priority) lets multiple internal systems (weapon grip, Pip-Boy interaction, throwing, Attaboy) and external API clients request a pose without fighting each other; two named constants make the intended arbitration explicit — PRIORITY_EXTERNAL_DEFAULT = 50 for an external override that names no priority, PRIORITY_FRIK_INTERNAL = 90 for FRIK's own interaction poses, so a third party must deliberately exceed 90 to outrank FRIK rather than doing so by accident. clearHandPoseOverridesForSkeletonRelease() drops every override, FRIK's own and external, at skeleton teardown so nothing survives into a rebuilt skeleton unpublished. Because the two hands do not share a bind pose, HandPoseMath::mirrorBoneRotation cannot mirror a rotation geometrically: it measures the source bone's animated rotation back into flex/splay against its own authored open/closed pair, then re-applies those flex/splay values against the target bone's own pair, special-casing the thumb base whose axis does not decompose cleanly into flex and splay; mirrorFingerLocalTransforms performs this across all 15 bones and rejects the result outright (zeroing outputs) unless every bone mirrors successfully, so a caller can never publish a partial mirrored pose.

IK (verified — Skeleton.cpp, the highest-value file in this repository): onFrameUpdate() runs, in order: restoreNodesToDefault() (reset ~25 tracked bones to cached default local transforms, discarding that frame's native animation output); setupHead(); setBodyUnderHMD() + world propagation; setBodyPosture() (derives a hip target from a torso-length triangle between tracked head/neck and hip, so leaning/crouching bends the spine) + propagation; setKneePos()/walk()/setSingleLeg() (procedural legs — walk() is an explicit idle/starting/walking/stopping state machine driven by horizontal HMD velocity; setSingleLeg() is a two-bone analytic thigh-calf-foot solve against a planted ground contact) + propagation; setArms()/solveArmToHandWorldTarget() for each hand + propagation; then geometry hide/show (hide3rdPersonWeapon, hideFistHelpers, showHidePAHud, CullGeometryHandler) and finally HandPose::onFrameUpdate(). solveArmToHandWorldTarget() is a genuine two-bone (not iterative CCD/FABRIK) analytic IK solver: it rejects non-finite or absurdly-distant targets (silent fallback to native animation, handling lost controller tracking), computes a small shoulder-roll offset toward the target, measures live upper-arm/forearm bone lengths, and solves elbow bend with the law-of-cosines formula for the shoulder/hand/elbow triangle, proportionally stretching bone lengths when the target exceeds max reach so the solve degrades gracefully instead of producing NaNs. A large heuristic layer on top computes a blended wrist "twist" angle from two projections of the hand's own rotation (smoothed frame-to-frame against jitter), then a set of clamped 0..1 terms — how far behind the body the hand is, how far it has crossed the body centerline, how high relative to the chest — that dynamically shift the allowed minimum/maximum elbow-twist range, which is what keeps the elbow visually plausible when the hand crosses the torso or reaches overhead. The elbow's final world position is placed from the solved angles and two basis vectors, then upper-arm/forearm/hand bones are rotated/positioned in sequence to reach it.

WEAPONS (verified — WeaponPositionAdjuster.cpp): handlePrimaryWeapon() runs after arm IK has placed the hand; it snapshots the weapon's original local/world transform, then overwrites the weapon node's *local* transform with a per-weapon, per-Power-Armor-state offset loaded from disk by weapon name (loadStoredOffsets/g_config.getWeaponOffsets), so each weapon can be independently calibrated without moving the tracked hand itself. A companion WeaponPositionConfigMode lets a player live-tune that offset with thumbsticks, applied immediately without restart. Throwables get a structurally separate path (handleThrowableWeapon) applied only while the transient throwable object exists mid-throw. checkIfOffhandIsGripping() implements offhand/two-handed support as an explicit state machine with four documented interaction modes (auto-snap-on-proximity vs. hold-to-grip, independent release conditions) gated on proximity between the off-hand and the weapon's grip/barrel node plus a configured grip button; once gripping, handleWeaponGrippingRotationAdjustment() blends in an orientation derived from the two tracked hand positions so a two-handed weapon points along the line between the hands rather than only the primary hand's own rotation. Firing/ammo/damage are never touched — only node transforms are rewritten, so the game's native weapon-fire path remains authoritative. Default per-weapon offsets for essentially the entire vanilla weapon roster (per hand, per Power-Armor state) are compiled into the DLL as Win32 resources (resources.h; 200+ IDR_WO_* identifiers, generated by a documented "list this directory" script) so a fresh install has correct defaults without any loose data files, and the runtime falls back to these embedded resources whenever no player-saved override exists. ExternalAuthority (g_externalAuthority, verified — ExternalAuthority.cpp/h) is a more general cooperative-ownership mechanism than a single flag: it exposes (1) two independent tag-based blocks, backed by a reusable TagBlockSet type, for "primary weapon node ownership blocked" and "primary weapon pose blocked" — each is a mutex-guarded set of caller tags whose *size* (not identity) determines the blocked state, with a lock-free atomic read (isBlocked()) for the once-per-frame game-thread check, so any number of independent mods can each hold an unblock-until-released claim without clobbering each other; and (2) a priority-plus-sequence hand-world-transform claim registry (setHandWorldTransform/clearHandWorldTransform/getHandWorldTransform) letting an external mod publish an explicit world transform that replaces FRIK's own tracked-controller target for a given hand's arm IK solve, with the highest-priority live claim winning and ties broken by most-recent-publish, and claims persisting until explicitly cleared. Every registration is scoped to one skeleton instance and dropped wholesale by clearForSkeletonRelease() on rebuild; the source explicitly notes the locking makes this thread-safe to *touch* off the game thread but not temporally *correct* to do so — clients are still expected to publish from the game update thread, since the whole registry is read exactly once per frame by the single IK solve.\n\nA second, narrower recoil-specific authority (verified — WeaponHandRecoil.cpp/h) governs only the weapon's kickback animation. Each frame it samples the game's native recoil node (PlayerNodes::primaryWeaponKickbackRecoilNode) and offers it to a registered external "recoil controller" via api::resolveWeaponHandRecoil(); an accepted response's transform (optionally slerp/lerp-damped using the same tuning as ordinary hand dampening) replaces the native kick, and a scope-guarded ScopedNativeKickNeutralizer RAII object zeroes the native kick node for that frame's first-person arm update so native and controlled kicks are never summed. Reprojecting the controlled kick onto an IK hand target requires converting it out of the kick node's local parent frame into a world-space rigid delta — done by composing the local kick transform onto its parent's world transform and back off again (a frame/world/inverse-frame sandwich, composeWorldFromFrameLocal + invertFrameTransform) — which is a reusable technique any time an effect authored in one node's local space must be reapplied to an independently-computed target elsewhere in world space. Because "the hand physically holding the weapon" and "the game's own primary-hand notion" diverge whenever an external mod has taken the weapon node, the same code mirrors the recoil transform across the body's sagittal plane (conjugating the rotation by an X-axis flip, negating only the lateral translation) when the physically-primary hand and the game's primary hand disagree.

MELEE: No dedicated swept-collision melee subsystem was found in the reviewed source; melee weapons get the same per-weapon offset/grip treatment as other weapons (WeaponPositionConfigMode::getMeleeWeaponDefaultAdjustment exists as a melee-specific default-offset heuristic), and combat resolution itself remains the native engine's. INFERRED: any melee "feel" comes from native Fallout 4 combat plus tracked-hand weapon repositioning, not a bespoke hit-detection layer in this repository.

UI: Pip-Boy is the primary VR UI surface. PipboyPhysicalHandler (verified — PipboyPhysicalHandler.cpp) treats the wrist Pip-Boy as a real mesh with named child hit-test nodes (e.g. "PowerDetect", "LightDetect", "RadioDetect") and measures world-space distance from the player's own tracked index-fingertip bone (read off the first-person hand skeleton, f4vr::Skelly::getBoneWorldTransform) to each node every frame, triggering the mapped action plus controller haptics when a threshold is crossed; the same techniques (device-relative hit nodes + fingertip-distance testing) are reused for opening/closing the Pip-Boy and paging through it. Access is gated by state (e.g. suppressed with a weapon drawn, or only while looking at the wrist) to avoid spurious triggers.

LOCOMOTION: SmoothMovementVR (adapted, per its own source comment, "from original code by Shizof mod with permission") buffers recent HMD-relative positions and applies a smoothed positional delta to the player's world position, with an explicit large-squared-distance check to detect and reset on teleports versus ordinary continuous movement. It does not implement roomscale/teleport/snap-turn systems itself — those remain the base VR game's.

MULTIPLAYER: UNKNOWN — Fallout 4 VR is single-player; no networking/replication code exists in this mod, and none would be expected.

MOD INTEGRATION / API (verified — src/api/ApiCore.h, FRIKApi.h, FRIKApiV2.h, PapyrusApi.h, PlayerControlsHandler.h): FRIK exposes a deliberately versioned public API — ApiCore.h holds one shared internal implementation, and FRIKApi.h/FRIKApiV2.h are documented as "thin projections" onto it so a v1 and a v2 external client can both run without silently overriding each other's state. Exposed surface includes a Hand enum (Primary/Offhand/Right/Left, handling left-handed mode), a Feature enum letting other mods declare and disable individual FRIK subsystems they intend to replace (Flashlight, WeaponPositioning, Pipboy, SmoothMovement), config read/override without touching disk, hand-pose override control, and the kSkeletonReady/kSkeletonDestroying lifecycle events. Each Feature's disable path runs a matched reset routine rather than a bare flag flip (disabling weapon positioning also releases any active offhand grip and exits reposition mode; disabling the Pip-Boy also closes it and clears finger-operation state), so handing a subsystem off to another mod mid-session cannot leave FRIK's own transient state stuck. A separate, narrower Papyrus native-function layer (PapyrusApi.h, registered once via RE::GameVM::BindNativeMethod in initPapyrusApis()) exposes a curated subset of this same functionality to ESP-level scripts — opening the main/Pip-Boy config UI, toggling weapon-reposition mode, opening FRIK.ini in Notepad, left-handed-mode queries, body-offset nudges, and a scripted finger-pose override (setFingerPositionScalar2) that is implemented by calling the exact same HandPose::setHandPoseOverride path (under a fixed "frik.papyrus" tag) that native C++ callers use, rather than a second parallel pose mechanism. PlayerControlsHandler is the single place player-input suppression is decided: it centralizes every reason to disable controls (Pip-Boy open, Pip-Boy/body config UI active, weapon-reposition mode, finger-operating the Pip-Boy) behind one idempotent enable/disable pair that snapshots and restores the engine's own force-enabled-input flags (rather than assuming a fixed default), so it composes with other mods that also restrict input; a deliberate carve-out keeps the offhand thumbstick alone active during throwable-weapon reposition mode so a throwable can still be "cooked," and hardReset() additionally clears the engine's persisted actor-restrained flag on session load in case a previous session ended mid-suppression. Every user-facing controller input is declared as data (a small InputBinding value: hand + activation style [Tap/Press/HoldDown] + SteamVR button) rather than hardcoded, so the whole input surface is reconfigurable from FRIK.ini, and Fallout London VR's Attaboy grab gesture reuses a generic configurable proximity "activation sphere" (WandActivationConfig) rather than a bespoke check. Config.cpp implements FRIK.ini with live file-watch hot-reload (the project's own changelog documents replacing an older interval-poll reload with file-watch). Documented third-party integrations include BetterScopesVR (inter-process messaging interface, scope reticle/dampening adjustment), Fallout London VR (auto-detected total-conversion compatibility, swaps Pip-Boy for an "Attaboy" object), and Immersive Flashlight VR (feature-disable coordination).

DEFENSIVE VALIDATION: a small, pervasively-used guard pair (utils.h: isFiniteTransform/isFiniteRotation) checks every component of a transform for NaN/Inf and rejects a near-zero scale before it is ever written onto a live scene-graph node. Arm IK, external-authority hand claims, and recoil world-delta construction all gate on this check and fail safe — silently falling back to the engine's own animation for that hand/frame — rather than propagating a corrupted transform from a lost controller, a bad external claim, or a degenerate intermediate calculation into the renderer.

IMPORTANT SOURCE FILES: src/FRIK.cpp (top-level lifecycle/frame loop), src/GameHooks.cpp (bootstrap patches + trampoline hooks, contains REL::Offset literals — game-version-specific and non-transferable), src/skeleton/Skeleton.cpp (IK core — the single highest-value file for reuse), src/skeleton/HandPose.cpp + HandPoseMath.cpp + HandPoseData.cpp (finger pose split into stateful override arbitration vs. pure pose geometry), src/skeleton/WeaponHandRecoil.cpp/h (cooperative recoil authority + world-delta reprojection), src/weapon-position/WeaponPositionAdjuster.cpp (weapon/offhand/throwable/scope offset logic), src/ExternalAuthority.cpp/h + src/TagBlockSet.h (cooperative hand/weapon-node ownership arbitration between mods), src/pipboy/PipboyPhysicalHandler.cpp (physical UI hit-testing), src/api/ApiCore.h + FRIKApiV2.h (versioned external API surface), src/PapyrusApi.h (ESP-script-facing native functions), src/PlayerControlsHandler.h (centralized, idempotent input suppression), src/Config.cpp/h (hot-reloadable INI config, data-driven InputBinding declarations), src/resources.h (embedded per-weapon default-offset resource IDs), src/utils.h (isFiniteTransform/isFiniteRotation validation guards used throughout), src/smooth-movement/SmoothMovementVR.cpp (locomotion smoothing). External dependency: external/F4VR-CommonFramework (git submodule, ArthurHub/F4VR-CommonFramework) supplies the f4vr::/vrcf:: node-lookup, transform-propagation, and VR controller/haptics wrappers used throughout every file above; a from-source study of FRIK requires pulling that submodule as well.

REUSABLE PATTERNS: (1) analytic two-bone IK with a law-of-cosines elbow solve plus a separate heuristic layer of clamped directional "amount" terms to keep elbow orientation anatomically plausible — generalizes to any VR full-body-IK project far beyond Fallout 4; (2) "restore-to-default, then layer IK stage by stage with an explicit world-transform propagation between each stage" as a robust per-frame update order for engines with an existing animation system that must be overridden; (3) tagged/prioritized pose-override resolution (string tag + integer priority, with named priority constants marking the intended arbitration boundary) for arbitrating multiple systems that all want to drive the same finger/hand bones, including third-party mods, with an explicit release hook so overrides never leak across a skeleton rebuild; (4) a general tag-based blocking set (TagBlockSet: mutex-guarded set + lock-free atomic size read) for any frequently-read/infrequently-written shared "is this resource claimed by anyone" flag touched by independent mods, paired with a priority+sequence claim registry (ExternalAuthority) for the richer case where the winning claimant's actual data (not just a boolean) must be resolved once per frame; (5) fingertip-to-hit-node world-space distance testing as physical VR UI input on an ordinary in-game mesh, rather than a raycast/laser menu; (6) a versioned public API (thin per-major projections over one shared internal core) so old and new API clients coexist safely, each Feature-disable paired with a matching state-reset routine so handing a subsystem to another mod never leaves transient state stuck; (7) reprojecting a locally-authored effect (recoil kick) into a world-space rigid delta via a frame/world/inverse-frame sandwich so it can be composed onto an independently-computed target, including a sagittal-mirror step when the physical and logical "primary hand" diverge; (8) a pure-geometry module with zero live-state access (HandPoseMath) kept strictly separate from and depended-on-by the stateful runtime module (HandPose), so external callers can resolve/mirror poses through the API without needing a live skeleton; (9) measuring an animated pose back into a normalized parameter space (flex/splay) against its own authored reference, then re-synthesizing in the other space, as a way to mirror a pose across two skeletons that don't share a bind pose; (10) declaring the entire controller-input surface as data (hand + activation style + button) rather than hardcoding it, so end users reconfigure bindings from an INI without a rebuild; (11) shipping default per-item calibration data as compiled-in binary resources (indexed by a generated, sequentially-numbered identifier list) so first-run behavior doesn't depend on loose data files surviving intact; (12) a single pervasive isFiniteTransform/isFiniteRotation guard applied before every write to the live scene graph, so IK, external claims and recoil deltas all fail safe to "let the engine's own animation stand" instead of propagating NaN/Inf from a lost controller or bad external input into the renderer; (13) live file-watch config reload for fast iteration on per-weapon/body offsets without an engine restart; (14) small Xbyak-generated inline patches with computed return-address jumps as a lightweight alternative to full function detours for narrow byte-level fixes; (15) centralizing every reason to suppress player input behind one idempotent enable/disable pair that snapshots and restores the engine's own prior flags, so the mod composes with other input-restricting systems instead of assuming a fixed baseline.

LIMITATIONS/CONFIDENCE: All GameHooks.cpp offsets and every bone name are specific to Fallout 4 VR's exact executable/skeleton and do not transfer; only the architectural patterns above generalize. No stereo rendering, frame-timing, or XR-runtime code exists in this mod by design — that responsibility belongs entirely to the underlying Fallout 4 VR engine. No dedicated melee swept-collision system was found in the reviewed source. The f4vr::/vrcf:: implementation details referenced throughout live in a separate submodule repository, not in FRIK's own tree, so claims about exact controller-polling or low-level node-utility implementation should be treated as UNKNOWN until that submodule is separately audited, though its call-site usage inside FRIK is directly observed. ExternalAuthority's own documentation is explicit that its locking guarantees thread-safety of the data structures, not temporal correctness of a client publishing off the game-update thread — treat any claim about *when* a publish takes effect relative to a given frame's IK solve as bounded by that caveat.`,

  "pattern_uevr_native_stereo": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): UEVR README and VR.hpp define Native Stereo as a rendering mode and document it as the preferred path when compatible.",
  "pattern_uevr_sequential": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): VR.hpp defines SYNCHRONIZED rendering, synchronization stages and SKIP_TICK/SKIP_DRAW; the README documents it as a fallback.",
  "pattern_uevr_afr": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): VR.hpp defines ALTERNATING rendering and AFR state helpers; the README documents the eye-synchronization tradeoff.",
  "pattern_uevr_stereo_callbacks": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): ScriptContext.cpp registers stereo-view callbacks and Mod.hpp exposes matching native methods; Jedi Fallen Order CameraManager.lua uses the callback seam.",
  "pattern_uevr_tracking_spaces": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): VR.hpp exposes HMD/controller poses, transforms, velocities, eye offsets and world-to-meters/world-scale controls.",
  "pattern_uevr_semantic_input": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): ScriptContext.cpp exposes XInput callbacks and VR data; Jedi Fallen Order Controls.lua demonstrates physical gesture classification followed by XInput bridging.",
  "pattern_uevr_escalation": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): Mods.cpp composes VR/UObjectHook/PluginLoader/LuaLoader, API.hpp exposes UObject access, and releases document Lua/C++ extension surfaces.",
  "pattern_uevr_render_compat": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): VR.hpp exposes depth, ghosting, native-stereo, SceneView, AHUD, projection and D3D11/D3D12 compatibility controls.",
  "pattern_uevr_lifecycle": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): ScriptContext.cpp and Mods.cpp expose engine, stereo, frame, UI, reset and device lifecycle hooks.",


  "seed_twoforksvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): Two Forks VR exposes separate runtime, patcher and Unity-helper projects. The reusable lesson is to keep XR bootstrap, camera/pose ownership, input translation and installation/patching as distinct layers. VR should own the camera/controller seam while ordinary Firewatch gameplay remains the authority for game actions.",
  "seed_penumbravr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): penumbra_vr contains HPL1 engine/game source and a dedicated VR executable. The project demonstrates source-level integration of head/hand tracking into an engine rather than a detached injector. The transferable architecture is an XR subsystem feeding camera and controller state into the engine's existing world-space systems.",
  "seed_thedarkmodvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The Dark Mod VR is a source-level engine fork spanning renderer/game/framework/UI/system code and currently retains seated keyboard/mouse/gamepad operation. The useful pattern is an alternate VR presentation/input mode with a safe flat fallback, allowing stereo/head tracking to mature independently from physical hand interaction.",
  "seed_redorahnvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): Redorahn Project is a complete Godot source conversion. Its value is the scene-level XR architecture: explicit XR rig, controller interaction state and locomotion systems rather than executable patching. Grab ownership and release/invalidation should be represented as explicit state so scene changes and physics cannot leave stale controller references.",
  "seed_raftvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): RaftVR separates loader/bootstrap code from VR gameplay patches and uses tracked head/arm state that can be presented to multiplayer participants. The broad pattern is to separate high-frequency local XR pose from lower-frequency replicated avatar pose, while keeping runtime selection out of gameplay-specific patches. The canonical repository is outdated in 2026.",
  "seed_preyvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): PreyVR is a standalone source port built from Doom3Quest renderer/VR work plus an Android engine port. The codebase explicitly documents VR adaptations including 3D aiming, 6DoF movement/weapon tracking, HUD/overlay changes, height adjustment, scene-dependent VR/flat switching and selective disabling of uncomfortable or unsupported effects. Reusable pattern: classify each legacy feature as KEEP, ADAPT, REPLACE or DISABLE rather than blindly forcing flat-screen behavior into VR.",
  "seed_megabonkvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): MegabonkVR documents a BepInEx IL2CPP bootstrap, direct UnityOpenXR loading, deferred engine initialization, a separate DontDestroyOnLoad VR rig, preservation of the original Camera.main for game logic/desktop rendering, and screen-space UI captured through an orthographic RenderTexture displayed on a world-space panel. It also records real IL2CPP interop failure modes. This is a strong recipe for modern Unity VR retrofits where replacing the game's camera or calling engine APIs too early would destabilize the host.",
  "seed_voidbreakervr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): VOIDBREAKER-VR combines a UEVR profile with Lua scripts that translate controller gestures into higher-level gameplay intents such as shooting, throwing, gravity-tethering, weapon readying and melee. It also separates dynamic aim behavior from the input gesture and applies VR-specific UI/comfort fixes. The reusable pattern is an intent layer above the flat game's existing verbs rather than rewriting the game's action system.",
  "seed_poppyplaytimevr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): PoppyPlaytimeVR exposes a dedicated installer and launcher that coordinates chapter-specific profiles and stable/nightly UEVR versions. The transferable engineering pattern is compatibility-as-data: select executable, profile, injector version, runtime and launch arguments from a versioned record instead of scattering those assumptions through the VR code.",
  "seed_fnaf_sbvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): SBVR's public repository focuses on the installer/launcher lifecycle around UEVR, creating a dedicated VR launch path rather than requiring manual injection. The reusable pattern is a pre-launch compatibility/bootstrap layer that validates the installation, chooses the VR configuration, and starts the game in a known VR state. The public source does not establish the underlying renderer implementation.",


  "seed_mw5_uevr_plugins_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): MW5-UEVR-Plugins separates head aim, HUD, camera compatibility and external haptics into native plugins. The HUD plugin is especially valuable for depth/render-order work: it injects a manual post-process pass, moves 3D HUD rendering after DLSS/TAA and before bloom, and the Camera plugin fixes Unreal HZB/depth-texture issues that can produce dark scenes or second-eye mismatches. Reusable pattern: treat render order, scene depth and UI composition as independent seams; validate the depth resource consumed by each eye before disabling post effects globally.",
  "seed_stalker2_uevr_case": `Source-audit (2026-09-05, updated from supplied STALKER 2 Lua sources): The provided files expose a concrete spatial-interaction stack rather than merely a README-level feature list. motioncontrollergestures.lua is the sensor boundary: it samples left/right motion-controller position and rotation and exposes Grip/Trigger actions as reusable gesture primitives. Body/weapon zone logic then consumes those primitives without embedding controller hardware assumptions into every action. weaponzones.lua defines mirrored weapon-relative reload, fire-mode and barrel zones by selecting a relative-location gesture for the opposite hand. The important abstraction is that the interaction space is the current weapon/hand relationship, not the global world, and handedness is represented by swapping the relative-hand query.

The physical reload path is unusually implementation-rich. mag_reload.lua creates a real Unreal BoxComponent on the left hand and another around the weapon's magazine socket, enables overlap events, applies configurable dimensions/offsets/rotation, and updates the magazine box on weapon equip. A left-grip rising edge plus hand/magazine overlap becomes the reload gesture; a gestureFired flag lets the higher-level input bridge distinguish an injected physical-X event from a literal physical X press. The box state is treated as a resource with liveness checks because UEVR Lua references can outlive garbage-collected Unreal objects. Reusable pattern: allocate interaction geometry once, reconfigure it per weapon, and explicitly validate UObject lifetime before using it.

two_hand.lua shows the next layer. A ForegripBox is attached to a configurable weapon socket; the left-hand box from mag_reload.lua is reused as the query volume. The update path first compares squared distance and exits early when the hand is more than roughly 60 cm away, then asks Unreal for overlapping components and compares object addresses. Entering the foregrip volume toggles TwoHandedStateActive and gameState.isTwoHanding; leaving it clears them. No left-grip button is required. Reusable pattern: physical hand placement can become a semantic weapon state directly, with cheap spatial rejection before expensive overlap enumeration.

physical_pickup.lua demonstrates a separate world-object ownership model. On grip-down it scans UIDActor_ItemContainer instances, filters inactive interaction components, chooses the closest object within a grab radius, and caches the selected actor/mesh/hook state. While held, the mesh is hooked to the controller but the underlying actor transform is also synchronized so removing the hook leaves the actor at the visual hand position. The source intentionally does not disable collision or enable physics for ordinary inventory-container objects because those operations can trigger STALKER 2's own item-management/despawn behavior. On intentional release, an HMD-yaw-relative shoulder zone can convert the world item into an inventory prototype; outside that zone the actor is dropped at the controller location. Inventory conversion is driven by a verified mesh-name -> PrototypeID mapping, so the VR interaction feeds the game's existing inventory authority rather than inventing a parallel item system. The source also isolates the wider inventory shoulder zone from shared body zones, preventing tuning one gesture from silently changing unrelated gestures.

The hand/presentation side is similarly modular. attachments.lua provides MESH, CONTROLLER, RAW_CONTROLLER and VIRTUAL attachment modes; tracks current grip objects, sockets, animations and offsets; exposes metadata queries for melee/scoped/two-handed attachments; and supports reversible reattachment. The hand layer snapshots an attachment's parent/socket/relative transform before temporarily reparenting it and can restore that snapshot later. hand_poses.lua contains large weapon-specific joint transform tables, demonstrating that the physical weapon layer and finger-pose presentation layer are separate data products. pawn.lua separately controls body, arms and animation-arm meshes and can hide selected arm/forearm bones and apply FOV fixes. Reusable pattern: treat VR-owned components as resources with explicit parent/transform ownership, and keep visual hand posing data-driven per item.

The test material is also useful evidence. body_zones_test.lua constructs left/right hand and HMD states, updates the motion-controller actors, evaluates a head-zone gesture and asserts activation/deactivation as the hand moves in and out of the spatial region. That is exactly the kind of deterministic unit test an LLM-generated VR interaction layer should have: feed synthetic poses into the same zone evaluator used at runtime and assert semantic state changes without requiring a headset.

The current upstream letmein-vr/STALKER2-UEVR repository corroborates the broader behavior: body-zone actions include weapon draw, sidearm, knife, flashlight, night vision, inventory, detector, bolts, grenades and PDA; haptics fire on successful zone interaction; reload is a hand-to-magazine interaction; two-handed aiming is a hand-to-barrel/handle interaction; dynamic weapon grips and animation synchronization are supported; and scope handling is implemented as a separate subsystem. The current repository requires Steam version 1.9 and the latest UEVR nightly, with 2.0 explicitly listed as unsupported. Its scope.lua further shows why scope rendering should remain isolated: it creates a SceneCaptureComponent2D/render target, defers GPU resource creation after injection or level changes to avoid racing UEVR's stereo hook, and computes parallax-corrected reflex-dot placement from eye position and the weapon bore axis.

The strongest reusable architecture is therefore: XR pose snapshot -> coordinate-space adapter -> body/weapon/world spatial query -> semantic state transition -> native game/inventory action -> haptic/animation/visual feedback. Keep pose acquisition separate from gesture recognition; keep gesture recognition separate from the action adapter; keep weapon grip/muzzle/optical aim separate from gesture selection; and make all temporary collision volumes, hooks and attachments explicitly resettable on level change, menu entry and script reset. This source set is especially valuable for vibe-coding because it demonstrates the actual seams where a flat Unreal game can accept physical VR behavior without requiring a wholesale rewrite of the game's gameplay systems. Exact object paths, sockets, prototype IDs, offsets and native action calls remain game/version-specific.`,
  "seed_returnal_uevr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The supplied Returnal UEVR source establishes a concrete ownership-and-presentation architecture. main.lua attaches the current weapon RootComponent through the shared attachment system with permanent/re-attachable UObjectHook ownership, exposes mutually exclusive HMD/controller UI-follow modes, hides parasite actors, and detects BP_Cinematic view targets to disable object hooks and switch VR_AimMethod during cinematic camera ownership before restoring them. returnalhands.lua manages controller creation and configured skeletal-hand lifecycle around UObjectHook state. Shared gesture code provides velocity/orientation punch and swipe detection plus spatial face/head/reload/holster/component-grab primitives; scope, reticule, laser, interaction and UI modules provide optical/UI infrastructure. Reusable pipeline: XR pose/input -> attachment ownership -> semantic gesture/aim state -> native game action/presentation -> cinematic camera arbitration -> UI/optical feedback. Implementation note: the supplied modules are best treated as a representative reference set for extracting reusable VR patterns; prioritize the demonstrated machinery—weapon transform ownership, hands lifecycle, semantic gestures, optical/UI presentation and cinematic camera arbitration—when adapting the architecture.",
  "seed_uu3d_depth_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): UU3D models depth as a selectable resource-provenance problem rather than one global toggle. It documents Per-Draw, Unreal Engine Pool, DSV Observer and DLSS Depth capture paths, each with different compatibility/safety tradeoffs, and uses captured depth for HUD depth, auto-convergence and depth-assisted alternate-eye warping. Reusable pattern: capture, validate, timestamp and label depth before feeding it to VR effects; select the least invasive depth source that the target title supports.",
  "seed_moonlightxr_depth_case": `Source-audit (2026-09-05): Moonlight XR generates stereo entirely on the headset from a normal mono game stream plus a learned depth map, then synthesizes eye views with depth-image-based rendering. Reusable pattern: keep a compatibility fallback below true engine depth—estimate depth from color when no game depth is accessible, update the expensive depth model at a lower cadence, and treat the result as approximate parallax rather than authoritative geometry.`,
  "seed_fatekeeper_fk_uevr_case": `Source-audit (2026-09-05): FK_UEVR is a compact public UEVR Lua mod centered on a large main.lua orchestration layer plus reusable controllers/hands/attachments/interaction/UI/remapping/gestures/IK/pawn/montage/mouse/keyboard helpers. The reviewed source supports a much deeper implementation map than a simple feature list. main.lua chooses between tracked forearms and generated full two-bone IK arms, explicitly destroys/regenerates those representations, cleans up attachments on destruction, gates swipe detection on whether a hand is actually holding a melee weapon, changes the active aim source during physical attacks, and repairs poseable-mesh root rotation every pre-engine tick because the game's shared skeleton can overwrite VR-owned transforms.

Deep implementation details: hands.lua creates PoseableMeshComponents from the game's skeletal mesh, attaches them to controllers, applies configurable component and bone transforms, and handles animation/visibility state. ik.lua owns two-bone arm solvers and can target either the raw controller or an attached accessory socket; it composes socket/controller rotations with configurable offsets, maintains solver state/order, and includes explicit CopyPose arbitration. Because CopyPoseFromSkeletalComponent can overwrite bones after the solver, the IK system can restore captured initial transforms or reset selected bones after animation frames, with callbacks allowing a game-specific bone list. attachments.lua is a real ownership/calibration layer: it stores position/rotation/scale, grip hand, socket and original-parent information, can preserve world transforms on release, and can restore an object to its original socket. Its melee-weight feature inserts a zero-length SpringArm so positional grip remains fixed while only rotation lags; documented mapping is 1 kg -> lag speed 20 and 20 kg -> lag speed 10. Weight changes can rebuild the grip hierarchy. gestures.lua classifies physical swipes using speed, directional discrimination and cooldown rather than a button alone; main.lua enables detectors only for active melee attachments and routes swipe callbacks through weapon-specific melee rotation offsets and controller aim selection. interaction.lua separates mesh and widget interaction, supports hand/head attachment, configurable trace source/distance/channel, hit testing, laser visualization, and depth/Z offsets for widget projection. montage.lua turns authored Unreal montages into a prioritized state machine controlling hand/arm/body visibility, arm animation ownership, motion-sickness compensation, input enablement and active accessories. ui.lua/main.lua treat interactive menus and normal overlays as different states, including controller-driven mouse input, head-locked flat/cylindrical UI, overlay scaling/placement and camera interpolation for comfort-sensitive scenes.

The strongest reusable design is therefore not a particular FateKeeper hook but an ownership pipeline: raw HMD/controller pose -> hand/IK presentation -> attachment/weapon ownership -> gesture/interaction classification -> existing game action -> montage/UI state arbitration -> corrective tick and cleanup. Use callbacks and explicit priorities at the seams. Preserve the game's authoritative combat/animation/gameplay systems where possible; inject VR intent and presentation around them instead of creating a second gameplay simulation. Treat animation vs IK as an explicit arbitration problem, and treat dynamically attached components as resources with reversible parent/transform ownership. The reviewed source does not establish a custom stereo renderer, swapchain, per-eye projection or predicted-display-time scheduler inside FateKeeper; those are UEVR responsibilities. Exact FateKeeper UObject paths, skeleton names, sockets and offsets are game-specific and must be rediscovered for another Unreal title.`,

  "seed_ffxivvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The public WesleyLuk90/ffxiv-vr repository contains the `FfxivVr`, `PluginRepo` and `Tests` trees, is AGPL-3.0 licensed, and has an active release stream through v0.0.74. The project identifies itself as an FFXIV VR plugin compatible with Dawntrail Patch 7.x. Setup uses XIVLauncher/Dalamud custom plugin repositories and recommends an OpenXR runtime. Release history explicitly documents an alternate frame-prediction option, controller-profile gating, head-height offsets and Linux compatibility work. Reusable pattern: integrate XR as a normal live-service client plugin with explicit host lifecycle/configuration boundaries rather than treating the whole game executable as the mod surface. The public overview does not establish a complete native per-eye render-hook map or full predicted-display-time submission schedule; those remain UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_secondlife_p373r_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The public P373R repository is an additive source patch for Firestorm rather than a runtime injector. `llviewerVR.cpp/.h` encapsulate OpenVR initialization/shutdown, framebuffers, controller input, head tracking and compositor submission; a patch to `llviewerdisplay.cpp` calls into that class at the end of the normal viewer render loop. The documented pipeline renders the normal Firestorm scene/UI framebuffer, copies it for VR submission, and applies IPD-based camera processing in `ProcessVRCamera()`. The VR UI remains a hybrid 2D presentation controlled through desktop mouse panning/zooming. Reusable pattern: add a narrow VR seam to a mature renderer while preserving the application's existing UI/event machinery. Exact predicted-display-time ordering and fully independent per-eye rendering are UNKNOWN from the public source overview.",
  "seed_sonsvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): SonsVR's public README exposes two eye cameras, OpenVR runtime-derived per-eye projection/offsets, runtime-sized render targets and end-of-frame compositor submission. It reuses the game's Final IK by pointing existing solvers at tracked controllers, derives real-body turn from the controller/shoulder relationship, moves menus/HUD/loading screens onto world-anchored panels, and dispatches controller rays to the existing UI. VR controls are translated into the game's keyboard/mouse vocabulary so the game logic remains unchanged. Diagnostics include a frame profiler, camera census and UI-layout dumps. Known limits are explicit: held items are not yet aligned to hands, two full camera passes are required, some overlays remain missing, and cutscene camera behavior is handled separately. The public README does not prove a complete wait-frame/simulation/final-submit ordering beyond the documented end-of-frame compositor seam.",
  "seed_techtonicavr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): TechtonicaVR's README explicitly separates dependencies: PiVRLoader performs most VR camera/controller work, TTIK supplies inverse kinematics for the player's body, and PiUtils provides shared utilities. The Techtonica-specific plugin owns configuration such as smooth/snap turn, teleport and locomotion comfort vignettes, menu placement/scaling and body visibility. The source/release target is Techtonica 0.4.0-c, and the README warns that early-access updates may break compatibility. Reusable pattern: keep generic Unity XR/camera infrastructure in a reusable loader, keep game-specific behavior in the BepInEx plugin, and isolate body IK so multiplayer presentation can consume it independently. Exact low-level renderer/frame-timing implementation inside the title-specific layer is UNKNOWN from the reviewed public material.",
  "seed_witcher3vr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The native DX12/OpenXR source tree has separate `src`, launcher, config and script/state-bridge components. The optimized release is one `dxgi.dll`, and the launcher enables only the hook set required by the selected mode because keeping all hooks active degrades performance. The supported renderer explicitly distinguishes same-tick geometry stereo from No-AA/FXAA, TAAU, DLSS and DLAA modes; direct OpenXR eye poses support canted displays, and HUD convergence is derived from eye geometry. First-person exploration, combat handoff, horse placement and cinema/cutscene behavior are treated as separate camera policies. Reusable pattern: make stereo and camera policy explicit state machines, and preserve the game's temporal/upscaler pipeline rather than simply duplicating a final image. Motion controllers are not implemented. Exact predicted-display-time scheduling is not exposed in the public README and remains UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_bioshocktrilogyvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The project uses a thin `xinput1_3.dll` forwarding proxy, a game-agnostic native core, and an executable-selected adapter registry for BioShock Remastered, BioShock 2 Remastered and Infinite. The repository pins MinHook, ImGui, OpenXR SDK and OpenVR headers for the SteamVR shim. Its docs identify D3D11 Present/ResizeBuffers interception, camera scanning and a real scene-build stereo seam as core milestones. Reusable pattern: keep renderer/runtime mechanics in a shared core and isolate per-executable addresses, camera rules and gameplay differences behind an adapter interface. The 32-bit OpenXR limitation is handled as an explicit runtime compatibility layer. Frame-prediction/late-latching details: UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_deliverusthemoon_case": "Source-code audit (2026-09-05): The public repository is small and the detailed implementation is carried primarily by `MoonMan.lua` plus the compiled UEVR plugin. The documented Lua switch `FLOATING_HANDS` changes the PDA presentation from a left-arm-attached tracked presentation to a fixed-camera presentation. The feature list shows the plugin is also acting as a state/scene compatibility layer: it handles puzzles, menus, cutscenes, Zero-G and ASE robot control with camera fixes, while using the game\"s first-person mode as the base camera rather than replacing the entire camera stack. The release is imported through UEVR as a config/plugin bundle and optionally installs a PAK solely for cursor hiding. The public source does not expose lower-level native hook addresses, so the registry does not infer them.",
  "seed_ff7rebirth_uevr_case": "Source-code audit (2026-09-05): The public FF7RB-UEVR repository is intentionally narrow: its declared purpose is a C++ UEVR plugin that fixes broken UI. The important implementation boundary is therefore the UEVR plugin API, not a standalone injector. Releases are packaged as `ff7rebirth_.zip` for UEVR import and are versioned independently of the main UEVR binary; the release history shows repeated builds as the game/plugin compatibility layer evolves. The repo itself does not document a full motion-control implementation, so UI compatibility should be treated as the verified source-level contribution. UEVR\"s public source exposes the underlying plugin architecture\u2014D3D11/D3D12 hooks, UObjectHook, VR components, plugin loading and Lua bindings\u2014which is the host framework this mod is extending, but those generic UEVR internals are not claimed as FF7RB-specific code.",
  "seed_stillwakesthedeep_case": "Source-code audit (2026-09-05): The public mod is a UEVR compatibility plugin rather than a source port. Its useful source-level contribution is therefore the collection of game-state/camera corrections layered on top of UEVR: suppressing head bob, changing camera placement for interactions and cutscenes, adjusting camera height for crouch/crawl, switching body visibility by gameplay state, and temporarily disabling roomscale pushback while inspecting nearby geometry. The implementation should be modeled as state-gated camera overrides with explicit entry/exit conditions, not as a permanent camera transform. The available public documentation does not expose stable native addresses or a complete hook map, so none are asserted here.",
  "seed_rtypefinal2_uevr_case": "Source-code audit (2026-09-05): The public plugin is specifically interesting because its architecture was refactored around a reusable universal free-camera layer. R-Type code adds an orbit-camera branch, three named camera presets, runtime distance adjustment and scene classification for gameplay/cutscenes/hangar/decal mode. The plugin therefore separates generic camera primitives (orbit/free camera/time manipulation/controller state) from game-specific scene policy. The decal-mode fix is evidence that the adapter has to override UEVR\"s normal camera-control assumptions for special in-game tools. The public repository documents these behaviors, but does not expose enough source detail to claim exact hook addresses or object layouts.",
  "seed_painkiller_vr_case": "Source-code audit (2026-09-05): The public repository exposes the deployment/activation boundary but not a detailed renderer implementation. The mod ships `*.exe` and `*.dll` files into the game\"s `Bin` directory and gates VR through `Cfg.VideoVR = true`; the project identifies itself as an OpenXR native VR mod and targets PainKiller 1.64. The repository\"s public README does not document controller-action plumbing, swapchain hooks, camera function names, or stereo render interception. The registry therefore records only what the source/repo actually establishes and intentionally leaves those lower-level mechanisms unknown.",
  "seed_stanleyvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The public development setup shows a two-project architecture: `StanleyVrUnity` is built with Unity 2019.4.33f into a Build directory, while the C# StanleyVR solution is built x64 and published into the RaiManager/BepInEx mod directory. This creates a reproducible iteration loop where VR-authored Unity content and runtime managed patches are built together but remain separate artifacts. The README verifies full motion-control requirements and OpenVR compatibility. LOW-LEVEL RENDERING, POSE SAMPLING AND COMPOSITOR SUBMISSION: UNKNOWN FROM THE PUBLIC SETUP/README, so the registry should not invent those details.",
  "seed_fearvr_case": "Source-audit: the repository documents a two-process x86/x64 split specifically because the game is 32-bit while the usable OpenXR runtime is hosted in x64. FEAR.exe renders through an x86 d3d9.dll bridge and rebuilt GameClient layer; fearvr-host.exe owns OpenXR/D3D11. A versioned pointer-free shared-memory protocol carries HMD/controller poses, FOV and texture handles. The game camera is rendered twice with per-eye matrices. The mod uses retail game-state enums for menu/cutscene classification instead of pixel heuristics, and a separate stereo HUD compositor. Controller actions are normalized in OpenXR and translated into legacy game actions; the physical weapon uses the muzzle as the actual fire origin. The safety pattern is unusually reusable: timestamp + image-size + byte-signature validation disables a hook on mismatch rather than patching an unknown build.",
  "pattern_bfvr_x86_x64_openxr_bridge": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nBFVR.exe launches/injects the x86 game side, BFVRClient.dll owns game hooks and rendering, and BFVRPresenter.exe owns OpenXR/D3D11. SharedPresentationProtocol.h is standard-layout, explicitly versioned and contains no native pointers or cross-architecture object ownership. Use this when an old game's address space/API constraints make an in-process XR runtime impractical.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Keep a legacy 32-bit game in its native architecture while a separate 64-bit process owns modern OpenXR; exchange only versioned POD state and shareable GPU resources.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: docs/AI_DEVELOPER_HANDOFF.md; src/loader/BFVRLoader.cpp; src/presenter/SharedPresentationProtocol.h; src/presenter/BFVRPresenter.cpp; src/client/BFVRClient.cpp.",
  "pattern_bfvr_predicted_time_frame_transaction": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nOpenXRPresentation::BeginFrame performs xrWaitFrame/xrBeginFrame and samples controllers/head/views at pendingFrameState.predictedDisplayTime. The presenter publishes that time with a renderRequestSequence; D3D8SharedPresentationBridge rejects controller data unless both sequence and predictedDisplayTime match. A matching BF1942 frame is consumed and xrEndFrame uses the same display time. This avoids mixing a new hand pose with an old eye request or vice versa.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Treat one OpenXR predicted display time as the identity of the whole frame: eye poses, controller poses, game render request and consumed GPU frame must all match it.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/openxr/OpenXRPresentation.cpp; src/presenter/BFVRPresenter.cpp; src/presenter/SharedPresentationProtocol.h; src/client/D3D8SharedPresentationBridge.cpp.",
  "pattern_bfvr_d3d8_semantic_stereo_replay": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nD3D8StereoPairProbe, D3D8FrameCompositionPolicy and D3D8SemanticDrawPolicy use render state, topology, FVF/shader identity and profiled call sites to decide whether a draw is stereo world or Ref2 UI and to identify special categories such as skinning, skybox, foliage, terrain shadows and water. D3D8StereoShaderTransform rewrites verified skinning constants per eye and restores the originals. The lesson is that old renderers often require semantic repair at individual draw families rather than one global view-matrix patch.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Retrofit stereo into an old renderer by classifying draw semantics and replaying only true perspective world work per eye while routing screen-space Ref2 work to a separate UI target.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/D3D8StereoPairProbe.cpp; src/stereo/D3D8FrameCompositionPolicy.cpp; src/stereo/D3D8SemanticDrawPolicy.cpp; src/client/D3D8StereoShaderTransform.cpp; src/client/D3D8StereoFrameTransfer.cpp.",
  "pattern_bfvr_shared_gpu_texture_ownership": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nD3D8SharedPresentationBridge publishes Reset-owned LeftWorld, RightWorld and Ref2Ui shared targets only after producer GPU completion. BFVRPresenter/SharedTextureConsumer consume them through D3D11 and consumedFrameSequence is advanced only after the source reads are safe. Legacy shared handles are treated as resource-lifetime tokens. This converts cross-process texture sharing from a race-prone handle exchange into an ownership protocol.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Do not let the game overwrite a shared eye target until the XR consumer has actually queued/completed the reads that depend on it.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/D3D8SharedPresentationBridge.cpp; src/presenter/BFVRPresenter.cpp; src/presenter/SharedTextureConsumer.cpp; third_party/d3d8to9-1.15.1/BFVR_PATCHES.md.",
  "pattern_bfvr_tracking_anchor_turn_rebase": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nD3D8TrackingAnchor separates Infantry/Seat contexts, requires stable context samples before recapture and maintains artificial presentation yaw independently of Battlefield's body yaw. When native body orientation catches up, the anchor offsets that body delta; explicit snap/smooth input changes presentation yaw. Controller samples can also be rebased when body yaw advances between XR sampling and skeleton consumption. Use this whenever game body orientation and VR presentation orientation evolve on different timelines.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Keep the OpenXR origin immutable, capture a local neutral pose per gameplay context, and subtract native body-yaw catch-up from the presentation anchor so artificial turning is not applied twice.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/D3D8TrackingAnchor.cpp/.h; src/stereo/InfantryPresentationTurn.cpp; src/stereo/InfantryCameraMath.cpp; ControllerInputOverlay.cpp.",
  "pattern_bfvr_grip_aim_weapon_pose_split": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nWeaponPoseMath computes the visible held-weapon attachment from GRIP. The current/reference grip are first expressed relative to the HMD/calibration frame so common room movement cancels; inverse(referenceGrip) * currentGrip supplies a full rigid delta including pivot translation. The delta is injected before residual per-eye view so both eyes share one weapon pivot. AIM remains available for controller-pointer items and UI. This prevents a common mistake where the runtime's pointing ray is treated as the physical hand grip.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Keep OpenXR GRIP and AIM roles separate: use GRIP to place the physical weapon and AIM only for pointer/ray semantics that actually require it.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: docs/weapon-pose-design.md; src/stereo/WeaponPoseMath.cpp/.h; src/client/BFSoldierNativeArmIk.cpp; src/client/WeaponAimOverlay.cpp.",
  "pattern_bfvr_native_skeleton_arm_ik": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nBFSoldierNativeArmIk validates the local first-person Skeleton::transform and active-item attachment boundaries. After native pose evaluation it can provide tracked hand targets, temporarily move shoulder foundations into an HMD/body frame and supply elbow intent, then run the existing Battlefield two-bone projector. Authored local translations and IK handles are restored before returning. Native fingers, reload, recoil, animation and item relation therefore survive while VR supplies endpoints.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Let the flat game's own animation and two-bone IK remain the final arm solver: temporarily inject VR wrist/shoulder/elbow intent at the exact first-person skeleton pass, then restore authored state.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/BFSoldierNativeArmIk.cpp/.h; docs/first-person-arms-research.md; related native-arm math/tests.",
  "pattern_bfvr_two_hand_fixed_pivot": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nBFSoldierNativeArmIk captures Battlefield's authored left-to-right-hand relation for primary weapons. Left-hand proximity/squeeze acquires support; current source allows full directional fixed-pivot steering. The right grip remains the translation pivot, the weapon basis rotates toward the left grip without scaling, and the exact steered basis feeds right-arm IK, visible weapon presentation and WeaponFire_Core. Sidearm support is deliberately visual-only.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: For long guns, keep the dominant grip translation fixed and rotate the weapon basis toward the tracked support hand; do not scale the gun or move the primary pivot.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/BFSoldierNativeArmIk.cpp/.h and its off-hand support/steering helpers/tests.",
  "pattern_bfvr_weaponfire_native_authority": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nWeaponAimOverlay validates WeaponFire_Core, expected infantry caller branches, local life/ownership and a fresh visual/controller pose. WeaponFireAimMath can replace basis direction and optionally origin, then forwards into unchanged WeaponFire_Core. Normal firearms use the controller gun basis; controller-pointer items use AIM; scope mode preserves native projectile origin while changing direction. This is a reusable way to add motion aiming without inventing a parallel combat system.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Replace only the native fire pose at the last safe gameplay seam, then let the original weapon function continue owning spread, cadence, projectile creation, damage and networking.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/WeaponAimOverlay.cpp/.h; src/stereo/WeaponFireAimMath.cpp/.h; src/stereo/InfantryItemAimPolicy.h.",
  "pattern_bfvr_native_body_aim_convergence": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nInfantryAuthoritativeAim compares controller gun/pointer/scope target against the exact fresh pre-VR source-camera forward. ControllerInputOverlay converts the yaw/pitch error into bounded native mouseLookX/Y corrections in SP and MP. This updates Battlefield's ordinary soldier/body state and native network encoding instead of patching replicated orientation directly.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Allow the presentation/fire direction to follow the controller immediately while the game's own soldier orientation converges through its existing look-input path.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/InfantryAuthoritativeAimRuntime.cpp; src/stereo/InfantryAuthoritativeAim.cpp; src/client/ControllerInputOverlay.cpp.",
  "pattern_bfvr_recoil_to_gun_haptics": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nHandWeaponRecoilRuntime captures Battlefield pitch/yaw recoil steps after accepted WeaponFire activity, accumulates a bounded physical-gun recoil delta and exponentially recovers after release. It never adds the recoil to the HMD. Shot haptic events are generated from the same accepted-fire boundary rather than trigger-down, so dry fire does not falsely vibrate and automatic weapons naturally pulse per accepted shot.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Preserve the game's authored recoil signal but apply it to the held weapon/fire pose instead of rotating the HMD, and trigger haptics only when the native weapon accepted a shot.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/HandWeaponRecoilRuntime.cpp; src/stereo/HandWeaponRecoilMath.cpp; src/openxr/OpenXRHapticOutput.cpp; src/openxr/internal/OpenXRPresentationHaptics.inl.",
  "pattern_bfvr_native_playeraction_input": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nControllerInputOverlay owns infantry mappings for movement/turn/jump/crouch/fire/ADS/use/reload/prone/menu/etc. Movement can be game-, HMD- or off-hand-AIM-relative, then is quantized back to Battlefield's digital locomotion semantics. Multiplayer values are inserted only for the exact local BFPlayer before native PlayerAction encoding, leaving packet quantization and remote simulation untouched. Keyboard/mouse stay available as fallback.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Normalize controller actions in XR, then synthesize the legacy game's own semantic commands before its normal encoding/network path instead of replacing that path.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/ControllerInputOverlay.cpp/.h; OpenXR controller action/binding policy sources; SharedPresentationProtocol.h.",
  "pattern_bfvr_ref2_ui_pointer_layers": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nNon-perspective Ref2 draws are separated from stereo world rendering. OpenXRPresentation uses VIEW space for gameplay HUD and a latched LOCAL panel for menus. UiPointerMath raycasts AIM into the aspect-fitted carrier, removes transparent padding and returns an absolute native menu coordinate; UiPointerSmoothing filters that one coordinate. MenuPointerOverlay forwards the result through Battlefield's own menu/game-input boundary, preserving native hover/activation/sound semantics.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Capture legacy HUD/menu draws once into a neutral UI texture, present gameplay HUD head-locked and menus world-locked, then map an OpenXR AIM ray back into the game's native logical cursor coordinates.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/stereo/UiPointerMath.cpp; src/stereo/UiPointerSmoothing.cpp; src/client/MenuPointerOverlay.cpp; src/openxr/OpenXRPresentation.cpp; SharedPresentationProtocol.h.",
  "pattern_bfvr_scope_eye_quads": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nOpenXRScopeOverlayLayer promotes verified scope Ref2 output into two eye-exclusive quads. Each is centered on the corresponding physical eye and sized from current OpenXR FOV to cover the eye frustum at a fixed distance. Weapon roll is applied around eye-forward in view space, decoupling it from head tilt. The world remains an ordinary tracked stereo view while the legacy scope presentation becomes VR-correct.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Reuse the game's own scope artwork but submit it as one VIEW-space quad per eye, sized from that eye's asymmetric FOV instead of zooming/tilting the HMD camera.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/openxr/OpenXRScopeOverlayLayer.cpp/.h; src/openxr/OpenXRPresentation.cpp; scope math/tests.",
  "pattern_bfvr_world_crosshair_per_eye": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nWorldCrosshairMath selects a hand-weapon, controller-pointer or mounted-weapon source, builds an endpoint along its forward ray and projects that same point through each eye's view/projection. Pixel size is derived from a constant angular diameter. The reticle therefore converges at the intended spatial target and receives the same world grading as the scene.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Do not duplicate a 2D reticle at the same pixels in both eyes; derive one world endpoint from the active aim source and project that endpoint separately for each eye.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/stereo/WorldCrosshairMath.cpp/.h; src/client/D3D8WorldCrosshairRenderer.cpp; InfantryItemAimPolicy.h.",
  "pattern_bfvr_world_motion_comfort": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nThe Win32 producer publishes current infantry/occupied-control-object world origin plus an opaque movement-context token. The x64 presenter derives movement strength from translation; changing token rebaselines the history. OpenXRComfortVignette darkens only the stereo world and is ordered below Ref2 HUD/menu/scope/Quick Menu. Death comfort shares the compositor and takes priority. This cleanly separates authored locomotion from user head motion.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Trigger locomotion comfort from the native player's/control-object's world translation and reset on context changes, so head movement and artificial yaw do not create false positives.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/openxr/OpenXRComfortVignette.cpp/.h; SharedPresentationProtocol.h; presenter movement-context handling.",
  "pattern_bfvr_vehicle_control_adapters": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nControllerInputOverlay has separate branches for infantry, mounted/surface controls and aircraft. Mounted tracked-grip aiming is enabled only when the occupied control object proves a valid weapon vector and rebaselines when the seat/object changes. Aircraft get dedicated throttle/roll/pitch/yaw stick mapping and configuration. Mounted-camera decoupling is also explicit state shared across processes. This is the safer pattern for flat games with heterogeneous control objects.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Treat infantry, surface vehicles/turrets and aircraft as distinct VR interaction state machines instead of forcing one controller map across every gameplay camera.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/ControllerInputOverlay.cpp; mounted-camera/control-object helpers; SharedPresentationProtocol.h.",
  "pattern_bfvr_depth_world_postprocess": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nBFVR can publish packed per-eye device depth alongside color. SharedTextureConsumer passes each eye's projection to D3D11AmbientOcclusion and D3D11WaterReflection, which reconstruct view-space positions from that eye's depth/projection. The water path also consumes a packed water mask. FXAA/sharpening, bloom and color grading are applied to world textures while Ref2 UI receives neutral settings. This avoids applying geometry-oriented post effects to HUD/menu text.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: When modernizing an old renderer externally, export the depth/projection needed to reconstruct view space in the XR presenter and apply effects only to world eyes—not to separately composed UI.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/presenter/SharedTextureConsumer.cpp; src/presenter/D3D11AmbientOcclusion.cpp; src/presenter/D3D11WaterReflection.cpp; D3D8 packed-depth production.",
  "pattern_bfvr_fail_closed_hooks": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED BFVR 1.0.2 SOURCE.\n\nAcross camera, Skeleton, WeaponFire and input hooks BFVR combines profiled addresses with expected byte prefixes/call-site identity and runtime object/state validation. WeaponAimOverlay rejects unprofiled WeaponFire callers; BFSoldierNativeArmIk rejects remote/wrong skeletons; renderer transforms validate source constants; incompatible signatures disable a feature instead of writing blindly. This pattern sharply reduces the cost of game-version drift in binary VR mods.\n\nIMPLEMENTATION USE: Treat a recovered address as only the first gate: verify the instruction prefix, legal callers, local object ownership and sane data before applying a VR override; otherwise forward the original path unchanged.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE SURFACES: src/client/WeaponAimOverlay.cpp; src/client/BFSoldierNativeArmIk.cpp; src/client/D3D8RenderViewPoseHook.cpp; renderer semantic transform validation; MinHook integration.",
  "seed_bfvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-06), verified against the complete supplied `BFVR-Battlefield-1942-VR-Mod-1.0.2` source tree. This replaces the earlier shallow BFVR audit and treats current source/tests as authoritative where older research/design notes describe an earlier state.\n\nARCHITECTURE / PROCESS SPLIT (verified — docs/AI_DEVELOPER_HANDOFF.md, src/loader/BFVRLoader.cpp, src/presenter/SharedPresentationProtocol.h, src/presenter/BFVRPresenter.cpp, src/client/BFVRClient.cpp):\nBattlefield 1942 remains a 32-bit process. BFVR.exe starts BF1942 suspended, selects the BF42++ loading path, injects BFVRClient.dll, resumes the game and follows process replacement. The Win32 client owns recovered game hooks, D3D8 interception, stereo replay, first-person arms/weapons, native input and render production. BFVRPresenter.exe is x64 and owns OpenXR 1.0, D3D11 shared-texture consumption, OpenXR controller actions/haptics, desktop mirror and composition layers. D3D8 is redirected through the pinned/modified d3d8to9 layer so Reset-owned D3D9Ex resources can be shared with D3D11. SharedPresentationProtocol.h uses protocol version 23 in the supplied source, is standard-layout/pointer-free and explicitly avoids native pointers/C++ ownership/architecture-sized fields. This is the central reusable architecture for a legacy x86 game that cannot conveniently host a modern x64 XR stack in-process.\n\nOPENXR INITIALIZATION / ACTIONS (verified — src/openxr/OpenXRPresentation.cpp, OpenXRControllerBindingPolicy.cpp and haptic sources):\nThe x64 presenter creates the OpenXR instance/session with D3D11; XR_KHR_D3D11_enable is required. It creates PRIMARY_STEREO world swapchains plus a Ref2 UI swapchain and LOCAL/VIEW spaces. Controller actions are hardware-neutral: pose actions exist for AIM and GRIP, plus trigger, squeeze, thumbsticks/buttons/menu and haptic output. Suggested bindings cover major controller families such as Oculus Touch, Valve Index, Vive Wand, Microsoft motion controllers and simple controllers. The game side consumes normalized semantic state rather than talking to device-specific APIs.\n\nFRAME TIMING / TRANSACTION OWNERSHIP (verified — OpenXRPresentation.cpp, BFVRPresenter.cpp, SharedPresentationProtocol.h, D3D8SharedPresentationBridge.cpp):\nOpenXRPresentation::BeginFrame follows xrWaitFrame -> xrBeginFrame. At pendingFrameState.predictedDisplayTime it samples controllers, locates head/standing-height data and calls xrLocateViews for PRIMARY_STEREO. The presenter publishes the predicted display time, per-eye poses/FOV, head/controller data and a renderRequestSequence to Win32. D3D8SharedPresentationBridge only accepts controller input whose controllerSampleSequence equals the active request sequence and whose predictedDisplayTime exactly equals the render request. A produced BF1942 frame is likewise associated with a sequence. The presenter consumes the matching source, submits with xrEndFrame using the same predicted time, and only after queued source reads are safe does it advance consumedFrameSequence. If the game is late during bounded loading/death transitions, the presenter can end the XR frame with the last accepted image while preserving the producer-ownership contract rather than keeping xrBeginFrame open. There is no custom speculative future-pose scheme in the current path: OpenXR prediction owns the cadence.\n\nSHARED GPU RESOURCE LIFETIME (verified — D3D8SharedPresentationBridge.cpp, BFVRPresenter.cpp, SharedTextureConsumer.cpp, modified d3d8to9):\nThe producer publishes three color slots (LeftWorld, RightWorld, Ref2Ui) and optional left/right packed-depth resources. D3D9Ex shared resources are produced on the game adapter and opened on the D3D11 side. Publication happens after producer GPU completion, and the game will not reuse a shared target until the x64 consumer acknowledges it. The x64 consume/finalize split ensures the source is not acknowledged merely because a handle was seen: source reads must actually be safe. Legacy shared handles are resource-lifetime tokens, not ordinary handles to CloseHandle. A CPU readback/upload fallback remains available if GPU sharing cannot be established.\n\nSTEREO RENDERER RETROFIT (verified — src/client/D3D8StereoPairProbe.cpp, src/stereo/D3D8FrameCompositionPolicy.cpp, D3D8SemanticDrawPolicy.cpp, src/client/D3D8StereoShaderTransform.cpp, D3D8StereoFrameTransfer.cpp):\nBFVR does true per-eye rendering by intercepting Battlefield's D3D8 draw stream and replaying eligible perspective world work into BFVR left/right render/depth targets. Screen-space/full-size non-perspective work is routed once to Ref2Ui. The decision is semantic rather than purely geometric: render state, primitive topology, FVF/shader hashes/identity and profiled return/call sites classify world, skybox, animated meshes, foliage/billboards, terrain shadows, water, sprites, fonts and menus. Shader-based skinning requires per-eye constant repair; D3D8StereoShaderTransform verifies the source constants and rewrites the relevant matrices for each eye, then restores the original D3D state/constants after replay. Specialized water/reflection paths also receive stereo repair. This demonstrates why old D3D8/D3D9 conversions often need draw-family classification and shader-specific repairs beyond replacing one camera matrix.\n\nEYE PROJECTION / COORDINATE CONVENTIONS (verified — OpenXRPresentation.cpp, src/stereo stereo math, docs/stereo-math.md):\nOpenXR supplies each eye pose and asymmetric angleLeft/Right/Up/Down. Those values are preserved through the render request and used both for BF1942 eye projection and the final XrCompositionLayerProjectionView. The project's math explicitly converts OpenXR's metre-based +X right/+Y up/-Z forward convention to its D3D row-vector, left-handed +Z-forward convention, and builds off-center perspective matrices. Per-eye FOV is never assumed symmetric. This math is separately unit-tested from game hooks.\n\nTRACKING BASIS / RECENTER / STANDING HEIGHT (verified — src/openxr/OpenXRTrackingBasis.cpp and D3D8TrackingAnchor.cpp):\nThe OpenXR base/application spaces remain immutable LOCAL spaces. The presenter attempts LOCAL_FLOOR for standing height and can fall back to STAGE; it does not mutate the OpenXR runtime origin when the user recenters. Recenter is an application sequence consumed by the Win32 camera/tracking owner. D3D8TrackingAnchor then captures a gameplay-context neutral pose for Infantry or Seat and requires context stability before recapture. This prevents camera/control-object transitions from randomly redefining forward.\n\nCAMERA / ARTIFICIAL TURN / NATIVE BODY CATCH-UP (verified — src/stereo/InfantryCameraMath.cpp, InfantryPresentationTurn.cpp, src/client/D3D8TrackingAnchor.cpp):\nFor infantry, BFVR preserves the source camera translation but rebuilds presentation rotation from a stable yaw basis rather than inheriting Battlefield pitch/roll/recoil/shake into the HMD. HMD orientation is then applied separately. Snap/smooth turn modifies presentation yaw. Smooth turn uses the XR predicted-display-time cadence with bounded frame time and configurable deadzone/exponent/speed; snap has activation/rearm thresholds. Battlefield's native soldier/body yaw is allowed to catch up, but D3D8TrackingAnchor subtracts the observed body delta from the presentation anchor so the user does not turn twice. A controller sample can be rebased to the newest body yaw if PlayerAction advances between XR sampling and the skeleton/weapon consumer.\n\nCONTROLLER INPUT / SEMANTIC ACTIONS (verified — src/client/ControllerInputOverlay.cpp and OpenXR action code):\nThe x64 side samples hardware-neutral OpenXR actions; Win32 maps them into Battlefield's recovered PlayerInput/PlayerAction semantics. Infantry covers move, turn, fire, alt-fire/ADS, use, jump/parachute, crouch toggle, reload, prone, scoreboard, map, Quick Menu and off-hand support. Keyboard/mouse remain native. The overlay is game-state aware and does not blindly translate every controller button into a global keypress.\n\nLOCOMOTION DIRECTION (verified — ControllerInputOverlay.cpp):\nMovement can use Game, Head or OffHandController orientation. Head-relative movement derives yaw from the matching tracked head; off-hand-relative movement uses the left AIM orientation. The rotated analog vector is quantized into Battlefield's digital/full-strength movement vocabulary, and low stick magnitude can produce the native Walk state. This is a useful adapter pattern when the flat game's movement protocol is not truly analog.\n\nMULTIPLAYER INPUT AUTHORITY (verified — ControllerInputOverlay.cpp, WeaponAimOverlay.cpp):\nFor multiplayer, BFVR inserts VR controller values only for the exact local BFPlayer before Battlefield's ordinary PlayerAction encoding. It does not define a replacement network packet or send raw XR poses. Remote players remain ordinary Battlefield actors. This preserves native packet quantization, prediction/simulation and server compatibility.\n\nGRIP/AIM POSE SEPARATION (verified — docs/weapon-pose-design.md, src/stereo/WeaponPoseMath.cpp, controller/weapon consumers):\nGRIP is the physical held-object source; AIM is reserved for pointing semantics. WeaponPoseMath expresses reference/current grip in the HMD/calibration frame, then computes a full rigid inverse(referenceGrip)*currentGrip attachment. Including translation induced by rotation around the grip pivot is important; a rotation-only delta would make the weapon orbit around the head/controller incorrectly. The common weapon delta is composed before residual per-eye view so both eyes see a single physical weapon transform. Stale tracking, focus loss, invalid quaternions or discontinuity reset/disable the calibration path.\n\nNATIVE FIRST-PERSON ARM IK (verified — src/client/BFSoldierNativeArmIk.cpp/.h plus native-arm tests/current research):\nThe current 1.0.2 source has progressed beyond the early research-only arm design. BFSoldierNativeArmIk validates the local first-person Skeleton::transform path and active-item attachment update path with expected prefixes/callers/object identity. Battlefield first produces its native animation pose. At the accepted local 1P pass BFVR can temporarily supply tracked wrist/hand targets; at the exact consumed pass it can temporarily move upper-arm origins to HMD/body-relative shoulder anchors and supply elbow intent, then asks the unchanged native two-bone solver to evaluate. Authored local translations and IK handle indices are restored before returning. Native hand/finger poses, reload/recoil/animation and item relationships are therefore retained. The solver and animation remain Battlefield-owned; BFVR provides temporary spatial constraints.\n\nARM VISIBILITY (verified — current handoff/draw policy):\n`show_arms` supports arms_and_hands, hands_only and no_hands_or_arms. Hiding is a render policy, not a simulation policy: tracking, IK, controller weapon transforms and hand placement continue. Hands-only recognizes explicit left/right hand mesh template names and fails closed for combined/unrecognized meshes. Scope/world-soldier ambiguity is handled conservatively rather than hiding any narrow-projection mesh.\n\nTWO-HAND SUPPORT / LONG-GUN STEERING (verified — BFSoldierNativeArmIk.cpp):\nPrimary long guns capture Battlefield's authored native left-to-right-hand relation. Left squeeze/proximity can acquire support and holds it until explicit/lifecycle release. The current steering path gives the weapon full directional range: the right GRIP translation stays the fixed pivot, no scale is introduced, and the weapon basis rotates toward the tracked left grip. The exact steered gun basis is reused by right-hand IK, visible weapon presentation and WeaponFire_Core. Close sidearms remain visual-only support/cup behavior rather than pretending to be a mechanically constrained two-hand gun.\n\nWEAPON FIRE / MUZZLE AUTHORITY (verified — src/client/WeaponAimOverlay.cpp, src/stereo/WeaponFireAimMath.*, InfantryItemAimPolicy.h):\nWeaponAimOverlay detours the accepted WeaponFire_Core boundary only after checking the expected function prefix, legal profiled caller branches, exact local player/life/item state and a fresh valid visual/controller pose. Ordinary firearms use controllerGunWorld. Controller-pointer items (including knife/throwables/gadgets in the configured item policy) can use the raw AIM pointer. WeaponFireAimMath replaces direction basis and optionally origin, then passes control back to the native WeaponFire_Core. Battlefield still applies weapon/barrel offsets, spread, cadence, projectile construction and all downstream damage/network rules. In verified scope mode controller direction is used while native projectile origin is deliberately preserved.\n\nAUTHORITATIVE BODY/NETWORK AIM CONVERGENCE (verified — src/client/InfantryAuthoritativeAimRuntime.cpp, src/stereo/InfantryAuthoritativeAim.cpp, ControllerInputOverlay.cpp):\nA controller-directed fire matrix alone could diverge from native soldier orientation. BFVR separately compares the current controller gun/pointer/scope direction to the exact fresh pre-VR native source-camera forward and derives bounded mouseLookX/Y correction. It injects that through Battlefield's normal look path in both SP and MP, causing native body/soldier aim to converge without writing networked orientation fields. Scoped aim increases convergence precision. The pattern cleanly separates immediate VR presentation/fire intent from slower game-authoritative body state.\n\nRECOIL (verified — src/client/HandWeaponRecoilRuntime.cpp, src/stereo/HandWeaponRecoilMath.cpp):\nAccepted Battlefield recoil pitch/yaw steps are captured and turned into a bounded held-gun/fire-pose recoil transform instead of HMD rotation. The accumulated weapon kick can use different one-/two-hand behavior and exponentially recovers when firing stops. Scope recoil modifies fire pose rather than the viewer. Native authored recoil remains useful as a signal while the comfort-hostile camera component is redirected into the physical object.\n\nHAPTICS (verified — ControllerHaptics/openxr haptic sources and protocol):\nHaptic events cross the process boundary as monotonic counters. Shot haptics are based on an accepted weapon shot, not trigger press, and can pulse one or both hands depending on fresh off-hand support. Death and menu-hover haptics are distinct semantic events. The OpenXR output path can address all hand subactions together for simultaneous two-hand feedback rather than making two same-action calls that may replace each other on some runtimes.\n\nWORLD CROSSHAIR (verified — src/stereo/WorldCrosshairMath.cpp, D3D8WorldCrosshairRenderer.cpp):\nThe crosshair is not a duplicated flat HUD pixel. The active mode selects a hand weapon, controller pointer or mounted weapon. BFVR builds one world-space aim endpoint and projects that point separately through each eye view/projection. Marker size is derived from an angular diameter, and the draw stays in the world eye path so it receives world grading. The hit marker shares the relevant presentation policy.\n\nSCOPES (verified — src/openxr/OpenXRScopeOverlayLayer.cpp, OpenXRPresentation.cpp):\nVerified native scope Ref2 imagery is promoted into two eye-exclusive VIEW-space quads. Each eye quad is sized from that eye's current asymmetric FOV and centered on the physical eye. Weapon roll is applied around the eye-forward axis so weapon tilt does not become a head-roll transform. The 3D world remains head tracked instead of adopting the legacy full-screen zoom camera. The scope layer is ordered above the world/comfort pass.\n\nREF2 HUD / WORLD-LOCKED MENUS / POINTER (verified — OpenXRPresentation.cpp, src/stereo/UiPointerMath.cpp, UiPointerSmoothing.cpp, src/client/MenuPointerOverlay.cpp):\nNon-perspective UI is captured separately as Ref2Ui. Gameplay HUD is presented in VIEW space; menus can be a latched LOCAL world-space panel. Current source maps the right-hand AIM ray into that panel, performs ray-plane intersection, removes aspect-fit padding and maps into Battlefield's native logical menu canvas. Adaptive smoothing operates on one normalized absolute point and is reset on discontinuity/panel changes. MenuPointerOverlay hooks the native menu/game-input boundary and invokes the game's own feedback/sound route on the Win32 side. This avoids mixing an absolute VR pointer with a separately accumulated synthetic mouse cursor.\n\nQUICK MENU / SETTINGS (verified — src/openxr/OpenXRQuickMenu.*, settings modules):\nA presenter-owned Quick Menu and VR Settings UI are OpenXR layers separate from Battlefield's native menus. Quick-menu selections translate back into ordinary game key/action events or shared BFVR toggles/settings. UserConfig is versioned, atomically written and preserved by installer upgrades. Runtime settings include controller/turn/comfort/presentation/graphics/arm-visibility options.\n\nCOMFORT VIGNETTE (verified — src/openxr/OpenXRComfortVignette.cpp and protocol movement state):\nThe compositor submits a world-only vignette above the projection world but below all HUD/menu/scope/Quick Menu layers. Movement strength is based on producer-published world translation of the local infantry or occupied control object plus a movement-context token. Head look, artificial yaw and vehicle yaw-in-place therefore do not falsely trigger locomotion comfort. A new context token resets the baseline. Death comfort reuses the same compositor and has priority.\n\nVEHICLE / MOUNTED / AIRCRAFT INPUT (verified — ControllerInputOverlay.cpp and mounted-camera state):\nBFVR explicitly branches by control context. Surface vehicles/mounted weapons map drive/turret controls separately from infantry. Tracked right-grip movement can augment mounted aim only when the occupied control object proves a valid non-empty weapon vector and is rebaselined on seat/object changes. Aircraft have a separate configurable throttle/roll/pitch/yaw mapping. Mounted-camera decoupling is its own verified shared state. This source strongly argues for per-control-object VR adapters rather than one global input mapping.\n\nDEPTH / AO / WATER REFLECTIONS / WORLD-ONLY EFFECTS (verified — D3D8 packed depth production, src/presenter/SharedTextureConsumer.cpp, D3D11AmbientOcclusion.cpp, D3D11WaterReflection.cpp):\nBFVR can export packed per-eye device depth from the D3D8/D3D9Ex side together with the eye projection. The D3D11 presenter reconstructs per-eye view space for modern screen-space effects. D3D11AmbientOcclusion implements a native-resolution view-space sample/denoise path; D3D11WaterReflection uses packed depth plus a water mask and a per-eye screen-space reflection traversal. World FXAA/sharpening, bloom and color grading are also applied in the x64 world pipeline. Ref2 UI is explicitly kept neutral, preventing text/HUD from receiving world post-processing.\n\nBINARY HOOK SAFETY (verified across camera, render, input, Skeleton and WeaponFire sources):\nBFVR repeatedly uses a fail-closed sequence: profiled address/RVA -> expected instruction prefix -> expected caller/context -> exact local object ownership -> finite/sane transform/state -> apply override. If any gate fails, the original path is forwarded. WeaponAimOverlay rejects unexpected WeaponFire callers; native arm IK rejects remote/wrong skeletons and mismatched items; renderer repair validates source shader constants/state; camera/input hooks validate expected code. This is a reusable reverse-engineered VR-mod discipline: offsets are not implementation authority by themselves.\n\nMULTIPLAYER / REMOTE PLAYERS (verified):\nNo custom VR pose replication or remote hand/body reconstruction is introduced. VR is local presentation/input. PlayerAction, WeaponFire, projectile/damage and remote actor state remain Battlefield-native. This is why BFVR can coexist with ordinary server/client gameplay without defining a second VR networking protocol.\n\nNEGATIVE / NON-FEATURE FINDINGS (verified by source/docs and important for accurate reuse):\n- No manual reload or physical magazine-handling system; reload is a native semantic action.\n- No controller-velocity grenade/throwable launch system; throwable/gadget direction may be controller-pointed but the native item/action still owns the throw.\n- No swept/velocity-driven motion-melee subsystem; a knife can use controller-directed item aim, but this is not physical swing collision.\n- No OpenXR skeletal hand/finger tracking; visible fingers/hand animation are native Battlefield animation.\n- No remote XR pose replication/full-body reconstruction for other players.\n- No generic roomscale collision/teleport/climbing locomotion system is demonstrated; physical HMD pose affects presentation, while native game locomotion remains PlayerAction-driven.\n\nMOST REUSABLE PATTERNS:\n1. Split legacy x86 game integration from x64 OpenXR ownership.\n2. Make predictedDisplayTime + sequence number the identity of a cross-process frame.\n3. Use semantic D3D8 draw classification/replay instead of assuming one camera matrix controls every pass.\n4. Treat shared eye textures as explicitly owned producer/consumer resources.\n5. Keep runtime tracking origin immutable and maintain a context-specific game tracking anchor.\n6. Separate GRIP held-object pose from AIM pointing pose.\n7. Feed temporary VR wrist/shoulder targets into an existing native arm solver rather than replacing animation wholesale.\n8. Solve two-hand weapons around a fixed dominant grip and reuse the resulting basis everywhere.\n9. Replace the native fire matrix at WeaponFire_Core while retaining native weapon authority.\n10. Let native/network body aim converge through ordinary look input.\n11. Redirect authored recoil into the gun and haptics rather than the HMD.\n12. Convert XR semantics into the game's existing PlayerAction vocabulary.\n13. Keep world, HUD, menus and scope as deliberately separate presentation layers.\n14. Build reticles from world endpoints and project per eye.\n15. Trigger comfort from native object translation, not head/camera movement.\n16. Use explicit control adapters for infantry, vehicles and aircraft.\n17. Transport depth/projection to a modern presenter for per-eye effects while keeping UI neutral.\n18. Fail closed at every reverse-engineered binary boundary.\n\nCONFIDENCE: High for the architecture and behaviors above because they are present in the supplied 1.0.2 source and current developer handoff/tests. Older design/research notes that describe a feature as not yet wired were not used to contradict current implementation where the present source contains the completed path.",
  "seed_halocewxr_case": "Source-audit: the public feature set shows a complete gameplay conversion layer rather than only stereo. The VR camera is synchronized 6DoF; weapon and grenade aiming are controller-driven; two-hand aiming, shoulder holsters, hand-to-hand weapon swapping and a floating UI/crosshair are explicit. Motion melee and flashlight/crouch gestures are present, while melee/interaction still use head aiming in the current build. Vehicle transitions are treated as a separate camera lifecycle problem. The implementation should therefore model weapon ownership/holster state and camera attach/detach state explicitly rather than assuming one global first-person camera.",
  "seed_hytalevr_case": "Source-audit: native Windows x64 injector/dashboard. The repository has a VR camera hook, OpenGL UI scaling hook, OpenVR action manifests, tests, MinHook and shared headers. Runtime workflow scans an in-game player coordinate block and injects/aligns VR from that discovered data. Controller poses drive hand meshes; first-person items are detected from the live render and rebuilt from game assets so tools/weapons/blocks/shields/torches stay attached to the correct hand through native animation gaps. The injector exposes supersampling, sharpening, VR FXAA, stereo projection, IPD/separation and hand/item transform/depth tolerances. Temporal DLSS/FSR/XeSS/TAA are intentionally not exposed because the hook does not own motion vectors/history. This is a good example of respecting the data actually available to a hook.",
  "seed_scrapmechanicvr_case": "Source-audit: guarded native OpenXR patcher with a hard supported-build check. The feature layer covers tracked mechanic hands, held tools, gun barrel targeting, hammer swings, laser pointers, construction interactions and floating menus. The installer verifies the exact Steam build, preserves backups/conflicts and refuses unknown versions. The key reusable pattern is fail-closed native patching plus a gameplay interaction layer that treats the controller ray/hand transform as the source of tool/menu interaction instead of simply remapping buttons.",
  "seed_sodvr_case": "Source-audit: the release package separates the BepInEx IL2CPP plugin/preloader from the native OpenXR loader under RuntimeDeps/Native. The mod is therefore a managed gameplay/camera layer backed by a native XR runtime dependency. Right-hand trigger pointer interaction is used for menus/context actions. When porting another Unity IL2CPP title, keep BepInEx interop/reflection patches separate from OpenXR lifecycle and native loader deployment; do not assume the managed plugin alone can create an XR session.",
  "seed_ravenfieldvr_case": "Source-audit: VRControllers.cs is unusually explicit. It calls OpenVR.Compositor.GetLastPoses for frame-local controller transforms, resolves left/right tracked-device roles, converts OpenVR's 3x4 matrices with an explicit handedness flip, derives tracking-to-world rotation from the active camera and HMD pose, and updates hand objects every frame. World-space UI is implemented by ray/plane intersection plus Unity EventSystem pointer dispatch, including fallback Graphic bounds. Weapon positioning is deferred to LateUpdate after PlayerFpParent.LateUpdate is patched out, then the weapon parent and shoulder parent are driven from one- or two-hand physical aim. This 'disable competing native transform, own it in LateUpdate' pattern is directly reusable for Unity first-person weapons.",
  "pattern_lcvr_openxr_runtime_fallback": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Source/OpenXR.cs` enumerates Khronos registry runtimes plus SteamVR/Virtual Desktop/Oculus JSON paths, sets `XR_RUNTIME_JSON` while iterating candidates, and keeps the Unity XR loader construction independent of the selected runtime. Transfer: make runtime choice a bootstrap retry policy, not a gameplay branch.",
  "pattern_lcvr_version_hash_gate": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Plugin.cs` hashes `Assembly-CSharp.dll`, compares against supported/known-compatible hashes, and refuses normal VR startup on an unrecognized build. Transfer: put one explicit compatibility gate before invasive patch application.",
  "pattern_lcvr_universal_vs_vr_patch_tiers": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `HarmonyPatcher.cs` has distinct `VRPatcher` and `UniversalPatcher`; `LCVRPatchAttribute` declares `Universal` vs `VROnly`, and `Plugin` applies them at different lifecycle points. Transfer: spectator/replication compatibility should not require local XR activation.",
  "pattern_lcvr_unity_openxr_spi_bootstrap": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `OpenXR.cs` creates XR Management/OpenXR loader objects, enables SPI, disables depth submission and registers controller profiles; `XRPatches.cs` restores features if vanilla initialization overwrites them.",
  "pattern_lcvr_spi_shader_compat_contract": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED DOCUMENTATION: `Docs/COMPATIBILITY.md` explicitly ties LCVR SPI rendering to instancing + stereo shader variants and recommends exporting AssetBundles with the Unity OpenXR package installed.",
  "pattern_lcvr_promote_native_hdrp_camera": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `VRSession` promotes the actual Lethal Company camera to XR (`StereoTargetEyeMask.Both`, HDRP `xrRendering=true`, no targetTexture) and selectively repairs postprocessing rather than building a second world camera stack.",
  "pattern_lcvr_desktop_spectator_camera": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: the custom camera is a cloned non-XR, non-stereo camera with removed pose driver, independent FOV and smoothing; three forced-update frames are used after discontinuities.",
  "pattern_lcvr_xr_origin_pose_driver_rig": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: HMD/controllers are `TrackedPoseDriver` children of an XR Origin; body/turn/floor offsets are applied to the origin/player hierarchy, not folded into the raw device pose.",
  "pattern_lcvr_two_bone_arm_ik": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: both local/full visible rigs disable `ChainIKConstraint`, create explicit `TwoBoneIKConstraint` chains with target/hint transforms, and remote VR players rebuild the same solver. No tracked leg solver is present.",
  "pattern_lcvr_input_derived_finger_curls": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: five controller-derived curls animate finger bones, produce fist/pointer state and serialize as five bytes per hand. Do not classify it as skeletal hand tracking.",
  "pattern_lcvr_vr_action_map_native_callbacks": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: LCVR swaps in a VR InputActionAsset and tracking actions while retaining the game-facing semantic action names/methods. This is an input-adapter pattern, not a new gameplay input engine.",
  "pattern_lcvr_roomscale_charactercontroller_reconciliation": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: roomscale XZ is accumulated, fed through `CharacterController.Move`, and counter-applied at the XR origin so tracked displacement is not rendered twice; native collision/grounding remains in the CharacterController.",
  "pattern_lcvr_calibrated_physical_crouch": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: calibrated standing height feeds a 0.5 ratio threshold, which calls native crouch and transmits an explicit crouch-mode enum. This is true physical crouch integration.",
  "pattern_lcvr_pose_sensitive_interactor_boxes": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `VRInteractor` changes overlap-box geometry from semantic finger state and performs explicit overlap queries on the interaction layer.",
  "pattern_lcvr_interactable_hand_ownership_state_machine": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE + SUPPLIED API DOCS: physical interactions implement a shared enter/exit/press/release interface; acknowledged presses retain hand ownership, and the API is explicitly documented for third-party mods.",
  "pattern_lcvr_ray_fallback_native_interact_grab": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `VRController` retains tracked ray-based native `InteractTrigger` and grab behavior, while `VRSession` disables only named flat triggers that have physical replacements.",
  "pattern_lcvr_tracked_shotgun_native_fire": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: shotgun fire position/forward are replaced from the tracked weapon ray and passed to both native local fire and server RPC. All ordinary shotgun gameplay remains native.",
  "pattern_lcvr_two_hand_shovel_local_speed": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: two-hand shovel geometry is controller-derived, and attack speed uses a five-sample player-local tip history before calling native reel/swing/hit methods. It should not be mislabeled as swept physical melee.",
  "pattern_lcvr_physical_knife_speed_and_tracked_cast": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: player-relative speed + physical head-zone collision activates the knife, while native hit resolution receives tracked knife-oriented sphere casts. No temporal swept blade is implemented.",
  "pattern_lcvr_tracked_spray_tool_origin": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: tracked item/controller pose is substituted into spray/weedkiller emission while the native local effect and server RPC remain in control.",
  "pattern_lcvr_external_item_alignment_registry": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Items.LoadConfig` loads versioned `.lcvr-cfg.json` item offsets and custom shovel classifications from all plugin subdirectories, enabling third-party alignment without code changes.",
  "pattern_lcvr_hand_authored_build_ray": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: build-mode camera rays are surgically replaced by the primary-controller `InteractOrigin`, and pivot input alters the native ghost rotation. No parallel build-validation system is introduced.",
  "pattern_lcvr_face_zone_native_item_use": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: a head-attached volume plus item whitelist triggers native `UseItemOnClient`, with 0.25 s exit hysteresis and button suppression to prevent duplicate action ownership.",
  "pattern_lcvr_mouth_cover_voice_semantics": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: mouth-zone state changes native voice-amplitude detection locally and sends a compact boolean that remote clients translate into a 1 kHz voice low-pass. It is a semantic network state, not replicated hand geometry.",
  "pattern_lcvr_physical_lever_native_state": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: tracked controller geometry temporarily owns lever presentation; release/threshold conditions invoke native Interact/PullLever, while a separate small channel mirrors VR lever interaction state to peers.",
  "pattern_lcvr_extensible_physical_door_adapter": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE + API DOCS: custom `DoorLock` network objects can register an authored VR handle transform; native key/lockpicker/open state remains authoritative. This is a concrete extension-surface pattern.",
  "pattern_lcvr_physical_charge_dwell_native_rpc": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: physical charger presence starts native charge effect, uses a 0.75 s dwell before full battery sync, and explicitly mirrors cancellation presentation to LCVR peers.",
  "pattern_lcvr_shake_gesture_native_item_semantics": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: belt bag and Maneater baby independently consume shake detectors and translate physical motion into their normal item/UI/RPC behavior. This is gesture-to-native-state, not general object physics throwing.",
  "pattern_lcvr_dissonance_p2p_sidechannel": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: LCVR uses Dissonance `SendReliableP2P`, magic 51083, protocol 9 handshakes, VR-capability response, typed/instance channels and a 4 KB payload cap. This directly establishes host-independent mixed-client VR transport.",
  "pattern_lcvr_reflection_serializer_bounded_channels": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: LCVR serializer requires `[Serialize]` for composite types, supports a narrow primitive/vector/quaternion surface, caches fields, caps arrays at 4096 and channels cap data at 4096 bytes.",
  "pattern_lcvr_remote_rig_arm_ik_direct_samples": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Rig` contains controller transforms/fingers + head/body/crouch offsets; remote clients reconstruct the same two-bone arm IK. `OnRigDataReceived` directly assigns newest state, so do not claim interpolation.",
  "pattern_lcvr_networked_spectator_ghost": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: dead VR players send a distinct parent-aware `SpectatorRig`; remote `SpectatorGhost` reparents and applies head/hand transforms. This is separate from living arm-IK replication.",
  "pattern_lcvr_physical_steering_wheel_native_vehicle": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: wheel-local signed hand geometry drives a smoothed ±480° state mapped into native steering; network events mirror wheel/hand presentation and validate the driver.",
  "pattern_lcvr_physical_gear_ignition_native_car": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: physical shifter/ignition geometry produces native gear/ignition calls; LCVR does not replace vehicle simulation or drivetrain state.",
  "pattern_lcvr_spatial_hud_reparenting": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: existing Lethal Company HUD GameObjects are moved into multiple world-space canvases tied to camera/hands/world interactions; the underlying widget/state objects are reused.",
  "pattern_lcvr_vr_safe_camera_shake_vignette": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: LCVR shake explicitly zeros local rotation and uses translation only; damage/death/muffle feedback is shifted into vignette/color plus active XR haptics.",
  "pattern_lcvr_unity_xr_haptic_endpoint": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: active Unity XR `SendHapticImpulse` calls are centralized in `VRSession.VibrateController`; multiple gameplay and physical-interaction callsites use it.",
  "pattern_lcvr_public_physical_interaction_api": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED API DOCS + SOURCE: LCVR intentionally exposes VR-state checks, `VRInteractable` lifecycle, trigger-disable helpers and custom-door registration for third-party mods.",
  "seed_lcvr_case": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied `LCVR-1.5.0.zip`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nARCHITECTURE: BepInEx `Plugin` verifies the game assembly hash, loads runtime dependencies, registers XR Toolkit runtime-init pieces manually, applies Universal Harmony patches for all LCVR clients, initializes Unity OpenXR, then applies VR-only patches. `HarmonyPatcher` explicitly splits `LCVRPatchTarget.Universal` and `VROnly`. `Entrypoint` creates `VRSession` after the game camera exists on both VR and flatscreen LCVR clients.\n\nOPENXR/RENDER: `OpenXR.cs` constructs XR Management/OpenXR settings and loader at runtime, selects Single Pass Instanced, disables depth submission, installs common interaction-profile features and can retry installed runtimes by setting `XR_RUNTIME_JSON`. `VRSession` promotes the native HDRP camera (`stereoTargetEye=Both`, `xrRendering=true`, no flat target texture), adjusts incompatible postprocess settings and optional mirror camera. Unity OpenXR owns swapchain/prediction/compositor timing; no custom explicit `xrWaitFrame` timeline is established in this C# source. `Docs/COMPATIBILITY.md` makes SPI shader instancing/stereo-variant requirements explicit for other mods.\n\nTRACKING/IK: `VRPlayer` creates an XR Origin/HMD/controllers with `TrackedPoseDriver`. The existing `ChainIKConstraint` arms are disabled; local and full models receive explicit `TwoBoneIKConstraint` upper/lower/hand chains with elbow hints, driven by tracked hand targets through early `RigTracker`. `VRNetPlayer` reconstructs the same arm IK remotely. There is no waist/leg/full-body tracker solver. `FingerCurler` derives five curls from input, animates finger bones, creates fist/pointer semantics and serializes five bytes per hand.\n\nLOCOMOTION: roomscale HMD XZ displacement is accumulated, rotated by turn offset and reconciled through native `CharacterController.Move`, then subtracted at the XR origin. HMD overlap/stick movement prompts reconciliation. Calibrated HMD height drives native `PlayerController.Crouch` and a networked crouch-mode enum. Native movement/collision/grounding remains authoritative.\n\nINTERACTION: `VRInteractor` changes overlap-box geometry according to open/pointer/fist pose; `InteractionManager` centralizes per-hand enter/exit/press/release ownership. `VRInteractable` is public/documented. `VRController` retains a tracked ray fallback that calls native `InteractTrigger`/grab server RPC; physical adapters disable only selected original triggers. Physical buttons/levers/doors/charger/body zones eventually call native Interact/component/RPC methods.\n\nITEMS/COMBAT: shotgun fire passes tracked origin/forward to native `ShootGun` + server RPC. Spray/weedkiller patches replace emission rays. Shovel is visually two-handed and uses player-local tool-tip history to drive native reel/swing/hit states. Knife uses player-local speed/body-zone contact to start native activation and replaces native hit query with tracked knife-oriented sphere casts. External `.lcvr-cfg.json` can add item offsets/custom shovel behavior. Ordinary item dropping can use the real hand as floor-position origin at large reach, but no controller-release-velocity throwing is demonstrated.\n\nUI/COMFORT: `VRHUD` reparents existing HUD objects into world-space face, pitch-locked, interaction and hand canvases. Pause/terminal environments are spatialized. Camera shake is translation-only with rotation reset; HDRP vignette/color handles damage/death/muffle. XR haptics use active `SendHapticImpulse` callsites.\n\nVEHICLES: physical wheel points snap tracked hands; local signed hand geometry produces a smoothed ±480° wheel state mapped to native steering. Gear/ignition adapters snap hands and call native vehicle methods. Vehicle-scoped channels mirror hand/wheel/gear presentation.\n\nNETWORK: `NetworkSystem` uses Dissonance reliable P2P with magic 51083, protocol 9 handshakes, VR capability negotiation, typed/instance channels and 4096-byte packet cap. `Rig` sends controller transforms, ten finger bytes, head/body/crouch/turn/floor state; remote LCVR clients reconstruct hand targets and arm IK. `OnRigDataReceived` directly applies the latest state: interpolation/extrapolation is not established. Dead VR users send a separate parent-aware `SpectatorRig` for head/hands.\n\nSOURCE BOUNDARIES: Body IK means arms only in this source; no tracked legs/waist. Shovel is gesture/state-driven, not a temporal swept melee volume. Knife uses current-pose sphere casts, not historical sweep. Normal item drop does not explicitly assign throw velocity. OpenXR low-level frame timing remains Unity-owned.",
  "seed_ror2vr_case": "Source-audit: RoR2VRMod is a BepInEx + HookGenPatcher project with a separate VRAPI intended for other mods/custom characters. SteamVR bindings support multiple controller families and the mod can run in Oculus mode. Custom characters can consume the VRAPI for full VR support or fall back to a dominant-hand pointer. Haptic-suit support is abstracted as a configurable backend. The important LLM pattern is to expose VRAPI-level concepts\u2014dominant hand, controller pose/aim, haptics, handedness, VR state\u2014so gameplay extensions do not own the XR runtime themselves.",
  "seed_submersedvr_case": "Source-audit: SubmersedVR started from SN1MC source and uses SteamVR/OpenVR input through a BepInEx Unity layer. The right-hand laser drives context-sensitive UI/tool aiming; quickslots are replaced with a thumbstick-flick radial; vehicle HUD is fixed instead of head-following. Release history adds articulated SteamVR hands and optional game/UI haptics, plus hand/tool aiming and UI scrolling fixes. The project also documents a virtual keyboard and hand-reticle/laser endpoint experiments. This is a strong reference for translating flat inventory/tool UI into controller-directed VR while keeping the game's inventory/vehicle state authoritative.",
  "seed_valheimvr_case": "Source-audit: VHVR combines a Unity asset-generation project with a BepInEx/Harmony C# plugin. The Unity project builds a SteamVR Player prefab, OpenVR/XR bootstrap assets and an occlusion postprocessor into AssetBundles. The runtime instantiates that prefab, swaps the game's main camera, positions the VR rig and translates GUI. BepInEx Publicizer exposes game assemblies and regenerates publicized DLLs after updates. Motion controls were explicitly WIP, so this entry is best treated as a camera/XR-bootstrap architecture reference rather than a finished weapon-physics implementation.",
  "seed_virtualrockgalactic_case": "Source-audit: public project is a UE4.27 mod project with Config/Content/Plugins/Source and the README states the mod is ~99% Blueprint. VR hands/weapons are project assets and the intended path is to compile/package the project rather than inject a closed executable. For LLM implementation, this means using UE's native VR pawn/controller/interaction abstractions when source access exists, reserving C++ for low-level systems and using Blueprint for hand/weapon/gameplay state. Do not apply an injector architecture to this source-level project.",
  "seed_nomai_case": "Source-audit: NomaiVR is an OWML/Unity managed mod with a separate SteamVR project and explicit SteamVR bindings. Its controller design intentionally uses motion/aim context to compensate for the smaller number of physical controller buttons. Ship controls can be aimed at and interacted with from the controller. The development setup references the game's Managed assemblies and builds directly into OWML/Mods. The transferable pattern is context-sensitive pointer interaction as a replacement for one-to-one legacy gamepad mapping.",
  "seed_anvilengine2vr_case": "Source-audit: AnvilEngine2VR is a C++23/CMake multi-game framework loaded as dxgi.dll. Odyssey, Valhalla and Mirage share the OpenXR/DXGI/core infrastructure while game-specific code is separated under games/. Supported behavior includes 6DOF head tracking, head aim and HUD scale. The build produces per-game artifacts and the README explicitly derives the project from REFramework. The architectural lesson is a shared engine adapter plus per-title hook/data layer, with renderer/UI quirks isolated to each game rather than copied into a monolithic injector.",
  "seed_starfield2vr_case": "Deep source audit (supplied starfield2vr-master snapshot, 2026-05-05): Main.cpp loads the mod through DllMain -> delayed InitThread -> Framework construction; CMake defaults to a DXGI proxy and also supports dinput8, PhysX and inject output modes, with HOOK_DX12_FIRST and HOOK_XINPUT enabled. CreationEngineEntry installs CameraManager, RendererModule and InputManager. RendererModule's strongest timing seam is the internal NVIDIA Reflex marker hook: markers 6/0/1 form the engine/wait boundary and trigger SetWindowSize, on_wait_rendering, engine-frame assignment, on_begin_rendering, HMD pose update, ImGui and UpdateWorldCamera; marker 2 becomes render_frame and marker 4 presenter_frame. After >100 frames, an unexpected mid-frame marker can set m_skip_next_present to re-phase AFR stereo. Render-graph hooks also snapshot/swap D3D12 temporal resources by render-frame parity, explicitly protecting TAA/history between alternating eyes. CameraManager hooks NiAVObject UpdateWorld, FirstPersonState camera rotation, NiCamera SetFrustum/CalcFrustum and Scaleform MovieImpl SetViewport. For an active HMD it replaces the valid camera frustum with the runtime's current-eye frustum, preserves FOV-derived adjustments, composes the HMD transform with the engine camera, converts between the framework transform basis and Havok space, and applies the result to the world camera; first-person rotation can additionally use pawn-control yaw and decoupled pitch. Scaleform viewports are resized/offset per eye using backbuffer size, HUD scale, dominant-eye and menu-specific perspective settings. InputManager hooks BSPCGamepadDevice polling, updates VR action states, reads the framework's XInput semantic state and feeds an emulated Xbox 360 controller via ViGEm, keeping Starfield's native gamepad path authoritative. WeaponModule hooks an object update path and equipped-weapon lookup; the supplied code includes an optical-sight flag adjustment and debug weapon-node transform path. HavokModule hooks NiPointerSet plus two weapon raycast paths. ScanHelper supports RTTI vtable lookup, byte-pattern function scanning and instruction-relative relocation in debug/SIGNATURE_SCAN builds, but release builds can use static offsets; the weapon module still contains raw module-relative addresses, so version resilience is mixed rather than uniformly signature-based. The ZIP leaves extern/vrframework empty because it is a git submodule, so exact OpenXR/OpenVR submission/runtime internals are not directly verified from this snapshot. Public vrframework documentation independently identifies starfield2vr as its Creation Engine 2 reference and describes the shared Layer-1 core / Layer-2 engine adapter / Layer-3 per-game data architecture. For the registry, the reusable pattern is therefore: proxy/bootstrap -> shared VR/D3D core -> engine-specific timing/render/camera/input hooks -> per-build offsets/data; do not collapse these layers or invent missing framework internals." ,
  "seed_neonwhitevr_case": "Source-audit: HeavenVR adds a 6DOF stereo camera, motion controls, smooth/snap turning, handedness, VR-specific weapon models, an optional animated body and VR-specific scoring. The notable implementation pattern is separating physical input from physical presentation: if the original game lacks a suitable world-space weapon model, spawn a VR-only model and bind it to the tracked hand while preserving the original weapon/game-state system underneath.",
  "seed_uevr_pattern": "Source-audit: public UEVR repository plus the registry's documented behavior. The important reusable boundary is that UEVR supplies the engine-level stereo/head-pose infrastructure, while per-game profiles/plugins operate above it. The repository ecosystem exposes controller poses, attachments, reticules, UI, gestures, gunstock support, pawn access and IK helpers; this makes the practical architecture 'generic injector + game-specific gameplay layer', not one monolithic VR implementation. Treat native-stereo/synced-sequential/AFR as renderer strategies selected per title rather than assumptions every UE4/5 game shares. Deep pass: The current UEVR source makes the layer boundary concrete: D3D11Hook.cpp/D3D12Hook.cpp establish the graphics interception, while VR.cpp owns OpenVR/OpenXR runtime setup, swapchains, controller detection and rendering-mode selection. The VR class explicitly supports NATIVE_STEREO, SYNCHRONIZED and ALTERNATING modes, plus roomscale, snap-turn, world scale, projection overrides and decoupled pitch; UEVR also has separate fake-stereo and render-target-pool hooks. This means a new Unreal profile should first determine which rendering mode the title can tolerate, then add game-specific camera/input/object logic above the generic VR layer. The build also separates LuaLoader, PluginLoader and UObjectHook, which is the source-level reason profiles can stay lightweight while deeper engine access remains available.",
  "seed_realvr_pattern": "Source-audit: no public implementation source for the R.E.A.L. binary. The registry therefore retains only externally documented behavior: ScriptHookV loading, camera/FOV/view-matrix manipulation, alternate-eye stereo, cutscene-specific camera handling, HUD modes and vehicle stabilization. Do not present inferred matrix-hook internals as source-verified; this entry is evidence of the observable technique family, not a code-level reference implementation. Deep pass: Treat this as an observable retrofit family, not a source-verified implementation. The strongest reusable engineering fact is the separation between camera/projection replacement and the game’s existing script/mod-loader lifecycle: the VR layer must intercept a view state that is authoritative for the rendered frame, then keep headset pose and game camera state synchronized across gameplay, scripted camera cuts and vehicles. Any new RAGE target should therefore be reverse-engineered around actual camera/projection call sites instead of copying GTA V offsets or assuming identical matrix layouts between RAGE generations.",
  "seed_gtav_case": "Code-audit status: closed binary. The public repository is documentation/release history rather than the mod's implementation. The README exposes useful control seams: alternate-eye stereo, position tracking, decoupled third-person camera, view-matrix fix, dominant-eye ADS, cut-relative cutscene pitch, HUD tracking, vehicle gyro stabilization and zoom/FOV overrides. These are reliable behavioral requirements, but the exact hook addresses/functions are not publicly inspectable. Deep pass: The existing audit is intentionally closed-binary. A useful implementation map can still be extracted from the documented controls: view-matrix correction, decoupled third-person camera, cut-relative camera modes, HUD reprojection, dominant-eye ADS and vehicle gyro stabilization imply separate camera-state machines rather than one global HMD transform. For an LLM, those are requirements to reproduce and test, not permission to invent function names or addresses; every memory seam must be rediscovered against the target executable.",
  "seed_rdr2_case": "Code-audit status: closed binary. No public RDR2 implementation was available in the cited release source. The reusable lesson is performance architecture: the VR conversion inherits the RAGE-style camera/projection retrofit, while vrperfkit is an independent render-resolution/upscaling layer. Do not infer RDR2-specific function names or offsets from GTA V. Deep pass: Keep the RDR2-specific layer distinct from GTA V even though both are RAGE-family examples. The registry should treat vrperfkit/upscaling as an independent performance stage, not part of the VR hook itself. The transferable porting sequence is therefore: locate camera/projection ownership, establish mono or one-eye compositor submission first, validate frame timing, then add stereo and only afterward optimize render resolution/upscaling; do not copy GTA V offsets, camera assumptions or cutscene handling blindly.",
  "seed_gtasa_android_case": "Source-audit: public source kit is explicitly an ARM64 OpenXR layer plus Android loader/tooling; it does not contain Rockstar's libGame.so. The build pipeline validates the exact package/version/signer and preserves the original game binary byte-for-byte while adding the native VR library and loader. The repository is therefore a useful model for 'patch the package boundary without replacing the game binary', but the VR camera/hand implementation must be read from native/src in the specific snapshot rather than inferred from the packaging scripts. Deep pass: The source kit is unusually strict about the package boundary: the native VR layer, Android loader and validation tooling are public, while the user-supplied libGame.so remains untouched input. The repository pins OpenXR headers/loader versions and records a source snapshot, so reproducibility depends on exact source/tool versions as well as the game build. For an Android IL2CPP/native port, the important seam is therefore native library loading plus symbol/function discovery inside the target ARM64 library, with XR session creation kept separate from gameplay patches and with build/signature checks performed before installation.",
  "seed_vicecity_quest_case": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED SOURCE (Vice City Quest v0.5.5). ARCHITECTURE: tools/assemble.sh reconstructs a pinned reVC checkout through an ordered patch stack, overlay, and bundled librw; XR/session code, renderer semantics, pose-space authority, weapons, driving and reVC gameplay patches are distinct layers. FRAME TIMING/RENDERING: xr_vulkan_session.cpp owns xrWaitFrame/xrBeginFrame/xrLocateViews/xrEndFrame and uses one predictedDisplayTime for eye/controller pose sampling; both eyes are array layers of one Vulkan multiview OpenXR swapchain, with per-eye asymmetric XrFovf projections and scope FOV scaling. GameRenderCommand gives legacy draws semantic roles before the RenderWare/Vulkan backend.\n\nCAMERA/SPACES: librw centralizes live HMD pose, latched tracking origin, character/vehicle anchor, eye poses and bidirectional play↔world transforms. Controllers are transformed relative to the latched head so physical leaning is not applied twice. Full vehicle-basis anchoring preserves pitch/roll. Large tracking-origin jumps relatch. VrUpdateFirstPersonAnchor runs before input and post-physics before render; visual hand rebasing is separated from gameplay interaction. CULLING: stereo sphere/box tests use the exact render play space and union of asymmetric eye clip planes, with bounded safety margin and fail-open invalid-state behavior. INPUT/LOCOMOTION: OpenXR grip/aim/actions map into native CControllerState; head-relative movement rotates by local latched HMD yaw only; snap/smooth/head-turn modes feed native camera/player heading.\n\nHANDS: VRHandModel is a four-baked-pose per-vertex morph (open/grip/trigger/both), not skeletal finger tracking or body IK.\n\nWEAPONS: body-yaw holster frame, calibrated per-hand weapon/support poses, physical grab/return/drop/catch/hand-transfer state, rigid fixed-pivot two-hand solve, calibrated muzzle aim, physical scope gating and centre-ray convergence. MANUAL RELOAD: Colt45/TEC9/Uzi/Silenced Ingram use a magazine gesture state machine (leave body socket, delay, enter magwell) that gates native CWeapon::Reload and requires post-insert trigger release. THROWABLES: tracked source/direction + release semantics, obstruction-safe source trace, constant launch speed, native CWeapon::Fire/projectile/fuse/ammo, trajectory preview.\n\nMELEE: weapon root/tip motion is measured in camera-relative tracking space so locomotion/snap turns cannot synthesize strike velocity; fast inception plus a short continuation window publishes swept segments; real contact routes into native CWeapon::FireMelee.\n\nUI: native HUD widgets are rendered to private wrist textures then hung on tracked wrist/vehicle anchors; separate Im2D transform preserves flat screen coordinates on a headset plane. DRIVING: car wheel grips solve two-hand chord or one-hand unwrapped spoke with ownership rebasing, discontinuity rejection and slip-at-lock; visible DFF wheel rotates about calibrated real pivot; horn requires an approach gesture. Bike steering has a tracking-space frozen reference, one/two-hand rebasing, throttle twist and both-hand lean gestures. CINEMATICS: upright latched theater quad or stereo director/actor-head anchor. BOUNDARIES/NEGATIVES: no full-body IK; no OpenXR skeletal fingers; throwable speed is not hand velocity; manual reload is limited to an explicit subset; TAA/SGSR/runtime-only foveation are disabled in the current Quest configuration. REUSABLE PATTERNS: reproducible patch-stack source port; predicted-time multiview; semantic draw boundary; one bidirectional pose authority; union-of-eyes culling; cheap analog morph hands; native input bridge; explicit physical ownership/holsters; fixed-pivot two-hand solve; physical optic convergence; physical-reload intent around native ammo; obstruction-safe native throw; tracking-space swept melee; authority/render transform split; stateful physical wheel/bike controls; render-to-texture wrist UI; local-yaw locomotion; pre-input/post-physics anchor ownership; cutscene presentation state machine.",
  "pattern_vcq_patch_stack": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `tools/assemble.sh` applies a fixed sequence of public-base compatibility, Quest, v0.5.0 integration and runtime-v2…v8 patches to the pinned reVC base, then overlays new files and the bundled librw. This creates an auditable boundary between upstream engine code, VR modifications and renderer replacement. For another source-accessible flat game, pin the upstream commit, keep invasive edits as ordered patches, isolate genuinely new XR/renderer modules as overlays, and make the assembly script the canonical build recipe. Later runtime patches can deliberately supersede earlier experiments without pretending every historical hunk is still current. GENERALIZATION: Build a VR source port as a reproducible transformation of one pinned upstream revision instead of maintaining an opaque divergent fork. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: The recipe is tied to one upstream revision; patch drift must be resolved when rebasing.",
  "pattern_vcq_predicted_multiview": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `xr_vulkan_session.cpp::renderFrame` performs xrWaitFrame → xrBeginFrame → syncInput → xrLocateViews(predictedDisplayTime) → locateControllerPoses(predictedDisplayTime) → renderLayer → xrEndFrame(displayTime=predictedDisplayTime). One OpenXR swapchain has two array layers and the Vulkan render pass uses `VkRenderPassMultiviewCreateInfo` with viewMask 0x3. Each eye projection comes from its own `XrView.fov`. The pattern keeps pose/input/view timing coherent and gives the legacy game only one stereo render transaction to satisfy. GENERALIZATION: Sample actions, eye views and controller spaces against one xrWaitFrame predicted display time, then render both eyes into array layers of one multiview swapchain. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: If STAGE space is not valid the source can retry in LOCAL; callers still need a deliberate recenter/anchor policy.",
  "pattern_vcq_semantic_render_commands": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `GameRenderCommand.h/.cpp` introduces `IBackend` plus explicit entity/object/immediate-3D/immediate-2D roles such as `OBJECT_DRAW_VR_WEAPON`, `IMMEDIATE3D_DRAW_VR_HAND`, `IMMEDIATE3D_DRAW_VR_LASER`, `IMMEDIATE3D_DRAW_WATER`, `IMMEDIATE2D_DRAW_RADAR` and `IMMEDIATE2D_DRAW_POST_FX`. The compatibility backend still executes the original RenderWare calls immediately, preserving order/state, while the Vulkan path can consume role metadata for water/depth/UI policy. This is a strong migration strategy when a flat engine is too large to rewrite at once: first make draw intent explicit, then change the backend behind that seam. GENERALIZATION: Classify legacy draws by gameplay meaning before they hit the renderer so a modern VR backend can treat world, water, hands, lasers, radar and post-FX differently without GPU-state heuristics. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Semantic roles must be added at meaningful call sites; unlabeled legacy paths still behave as generic draws.",
  "pattern_vcq_pose_space_authority": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `librw/src/vulkan/vkdevice.cpp` exposes `setFirstPersonAnchor`, `setFirstPersonAnchorBasis`, `getFirstPersonViewFrame`, `playPoseToFirstPersonWorld`, `firstPersonWorldVectorToPlay` and `firstPersonWorldPositionToPlay`. Controller positions are measured from `fpLatchedHeadPos`, not the live head; otherwise each physical lean would move both the camera and the hand/world conversion and double-apply translation. The inverse APIs are used by vehicle solvers and wrist UI. A >3 m head-position discontinuity relatches the play-space origin as recenter/tracking loss. Vehicle mode can supply a full basis so pitch/roll survive while latched physical yaw is removed. GENERALIZATION: Use one renderer-owned transform service for head, eye, controller, weapon, vehicle and UI spaces so every subsystem agrees on the same latched tracking origin and world anchor. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Every consumer must use this authority; mixing raw world-space deltas with these converted poses reintroduces double transforms.",
  "pattern_vcq_stereo_union_culling": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `isFirstPersonWorldSphereVisibleInStereo` and `isFirstPersonWorldBoxVisibleInStereo` transform bounds with the active world→play matrix, test the exact asymmetric clip-side planes for each eye, and keep an object when either eye sees it. A bounded angular margin (clamped around tan 10°) plus positional cushion absorbs authored-bound errors. Non-finite/missing state returns visible (fail open), because overdraw is safer than geometry disappearing from one eye. This is directly reusable in old engines whose CPU culling predates stereo. GENERALIZATION: Cull legacy world bounds against the union of the two physical eye frusta, in the same play space used for rendering, rather than a mono/centre approximation. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: More conservative culling costs draw calls; aggressive content-specific modes need separate validation.",
  "pattern_vcq_morph_hands": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRHandModel.cpp` stores four positions and normals per vertex. `InterpolatePose` uses weights `(1-grip)(1-trigger)`, `grip(1-trigger)`, `(1-grip)trigger`, and `grip*trigger`, blends both positions and normals, normalizes them and submits the result as `IMMEDIATE3D_DRAW_VR_HAND`. The imported UltimateXR mesh is reoriented with a true 180° roll so handedness is preserved. This avoids runtime finger bones/IK while still conveying trigger and grip state. GENERALIZATION: Animate lightweight tracked hands on mobile by bilinearly blending baked open/grip/trigger/both vertex poses from the controller analog values. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: It cannot reproduce arbitrary finger articulation or hand tracking; only the two analog dimensions represented by the baked poses.",
  "pattern_vcq_native_input_bridge": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `xr_vulkan_session.cpp` owns grip/aim action spaces, sticks, buttons, triggers, grips and haptics; Android `CapturePad` clears/fills reVC `CControllerState` with the normalized OpenXR values. It deliberately scales analogue axes to the game’s expected ±128 range, applies deadzones, and debounces menu navigation so one Touch stick does not feed both analogue and D-pad edges. Snap/smooth/head-turn modes are implemented by transforming the same native camera/player inputs rather than inventing a parallel movement protocol. GENERALIZATION: Keep XR tracking/actions as the input source while preserving the flat game’s existing command vocabulary and state machines. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Per-context ownership matters: menus, cutscenes, scopes and vehicles suppress or remap actions so one physical control does not trigger multiple native meanings.",
  "pattern_vcq_physical_holsters": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `QuestWeaponVR::GetBodyFrame` derives a yaw-only body frame from the real HMD position so waist/chest/back sockets follow the player without pitching when they look up. Support grip engages near the calibrated support socket (~0.18 m); releasing the primary with support still closed transfers ownership; direct gun-body transfer uses ~0.20 m; dropped guns can be caught at ~0.22 m; release within ~0.24 m of the assigned holster returns the item, otherwise a short dropped-object transform is simulated; empty hands can grab a holster within ~0.27 m. The lesson is to make ownership transitions first-class state rather than infer them from the rendered mesh each frame. GENERALIZATION: Represent weapon ownership as explicit per-hand/holster/dropped/support state and resolve physical proximity transitions before invoking native weapon selection. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Distances and socket offsets are content/body-scale calibration values, not universal constants.",
  "pattern_vcq_two_hand_weapon": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `BuildTwoHandRotation` obtains the expected support vector from the weapon calibration, compares it with the actual support-hand vector, forms a shortest-arc axis/angle and clamps extreme rotation. The same rotation is applied to weapon presentation and `BuildAim` source/direction, so the muzzle, laser and visible gun agree. There is no scale/stretch term. For another port, calibrate one primary pivot and one expected support socket per weapon, then solve a rigid delta rather than placing the two ends independently. GENERALIZATION: Rotate the whole calibrated weapon/aim frame around the primary grip pivot until the authored support vector points toward the tracked support hand. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Support sockets are weapon-specific; poor calibration produces correct math around the wrong physical grip point.",
  "pattern_vcq_scope_convergence": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `UpdateScope` gates activation by head-relative distance/lateral/height/alignment and requires support on two-handed scoped weapons. `BuildScopeCentreTarget` traces from the first-person view centre into the world. `GetTrackedWeaponAim` converges the physical muzzle direction to that point; if the centre target cannot be formed the source deliberately refuses an inconsistent shot. In `xr_vulkan_session.cpp` `VrScopeZoomFactor` scales each eye’s own FOV tangents rather than replacing them with a single symmetric projection. GENERALIZATION: When a physical scope is brought to the eye, treat the HMD-centre world ray as the optic truth and converge the tracked muzzle onto that target while zooming each runtime eye FOV. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: This is immersive zoom/convergence, not evidence of a separate picture-in-picture scope render in the current Quest path.",
  "pattern_vcq_manual_reload": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `UpdateManualReloadInput` requires a free hand to grip the magazine near its body socket, move it >=0.08 m away, wait >=150 ms, then enter a <=0.115 m magwell sphere. `ConsumeManualReloadRequest` is consumed by the reVC patch, which calls the existing `CWeapon::Reload()`, sets native READY/timer state and deliberately does not subtract reserve ammo twice. A `vrWaitForTriggerRelease` latch prevents an automatic weapon firing in the same frame as insertion. This is a clean pattern for physicalizing reload without forking ammo accounting. GENERALIZATION: Use tracked magazine motion to decide *when* a reload is allowed, while leaving ammo transfer and weapon state to the game’s native reload function. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Current source enables this only for Colt .45, TEC9, Uzi and Silenced Ingram.",
  "pattern_vcq_native_throwables": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `GetTrackedThrowableLaunch` derives tracked source/direction, line-traces from the first-person view anchor to the hand, and retracts the launch point to ~8 cm before an obstruction so roomscale reach cannot spawn a projectile through a wall. Trigger press arms and trigger release calls native `CWeapon::Fire`; remaining inventory is prepared through native reload/state. The visible trajectory preview uses the same launch vector, game gravity and collision queries. VR supplies spatial intent; native projectile authority remains intact. GENERALIZATION: Let tracked pose choose where a grenade leaves the hand and when it is released, but call the flat game’s projectile/fuse/ammo implementation. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Launch speed is intentionally constant in this source, not controller-velocity-derived; do not generalize this entry as velocity throwing.",
  "pattern_vcq_tracking_space_melee": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `QuestWeaponVR::ToMeleeTrackingSpace` converts world root/tip points through the first-person camera frame before velocity tests, explicitly preventing locomotion, snap turns and anchor refreshes from appearing as high-speed strikes. Per-weapon root/tip calibration prefers the actual recently rendered weapon matrix. Fast inception opens an ~180 ms continuation window, tracking jumps are rejected, cooldown/rearm state suppresses repeat hits, and misses can keep the swing alive until true contact. The reVC patch resolves the physical sweep and calls `CWeapon::FireMelee`; `ResolvePhysicalMeleeStrike` closes the strike only on real contact. Katana handling adds a short body→head grace window. GENERALIZATION: Measure weapon swing velocity in tracking/camera-relative space, publish swept root/tip segments through the follow-through, then hand confirmed contact to the native melee damage path. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Thresholds/root-tip geometry are weapon-scale tuning. The reusable idea is the space choice and native damage handoff, not the exact numbers.",
  "pattern_vcq_visual_authority_split": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `QuestDrivingVR` and `QuestWeaponVR` expose authoritative tracked matrices separately from `GetTrackedVisualHandMatrix`; `vrhands_quest.cpp` uses the visual result for wrist-panel/render anchors. Bike grip orientation and two-hand visual locking can therefore rotate/pull the displayed wrist after the interaction anchor has already been solved. This is broadly useful anywhere a controller’s ergonomically pleasing model pose differs from its mathematical grip/steering point. GENERALIZATION: Keep the transform used for grabbing/steering/gameplay distinct from cosmetic hand corrections so presentation tweaks cannot feed back into control geometry. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Systems must be disciplined about which transform they consume; using the visual matrix for authority recreates feedback/drift.",
  "pattern_vcq_car_wheel": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `UpdateImmersiveCarInput` acquires grips near wheel anchors with 0.65/0.30 hysteresis. Two hands use the chord between tracked hands and latch a reference on ownership change; one hand tracks an unwrapped spoke angle, reseats after an impossible step or near-centre ambiguity, and at steering lock deliberately moves the reference so the hand can slip instead of accumulating hidden over-rotation. `UpdateImmersiveCarModelSteeringWheelInternal` transforms the calibrated wheel centre/normal into the DFF frame parent and rotates both basis and origin around that local pivot, handling wheel meshes whose authored frame origin is unusable. Horn input requires an inward approach before centre contact, avoiding static overlap triggers. GENERALIZATION: Solve one- or two-hand steering as a stateful physical control and make the visible vehicle wheel follow the same physical angle around a calibrated pivot. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Wheel centre/radius/axis are per-model/category calibration data.",
  "pattern_vcq_bike_controls": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CaptureBikeOneHandSteering` converts the bike right/forward basis and neutral handle chord with `firstPersonWorldVectorToPlay` and stores the controller position in raw tracking space. One-hand movement reconstructs a virtual chord from hand displacement; two-hand ownership changes latch the current applied steering angle. A >45° discontinuity or clamp event rebases rather than jumping. The same system supports right-hand throttle twist and requires both real hands for height-based wheelie/stoppie lean gestures. This is a strong pattern for physical controls attached to animated bodies/vehicles. GENERALIZATION: For moving/leaning vehicle controls, capture the gesture’s neutral reference in tracking space so later vehicle animation cannot move the reference under the player’s hand. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Handlebar/lean/throttle calibration varies by bike model; one universal cockpit geometry is insufficient.",
  "pattern_vcq_wrist_ui": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. The librw wrist-panel API renders a requested panel target before the multiview scene. Runtime-v8 HUD patches call `BeginVrWristPanel`, reuse native `CRadar`, `CFont`, weapon/ammo/status/clock drawing, then `EndVrWristPanel`; world quads use tracked wrist or vehicle anchors and can depth-test against the scene. The 2D screen plane separately uses `setIm2DTransform`, derived from the game view window, so legacy pixel/UI coordinates map to a stable headset plane. This gives an incremental route from flat HUD to embodied UI. GENERALIZATION: Reuse the flat game’s existing HUD drawing code by rendering selected widgets into private textures, then mount those textures as world-space wrist/dashboard quads instead of rewriting the UI system. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Each widget still needs a sensible physical placement/scale and state policy; not all flat UI should automatically become wrist UI.",
  "pattern_vcq_head_relative_locomotion": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CapturePad` asks librw for `getFirstPersonLocalHeadYaw`, rotates the left stick by that yaw for head-relative locomotion, never uses HMD pitch/roll for walking, and keeps world/vehicle heading out because it is already represented by the anchor. Snap turn has engage/release thresholds and applies one delta to camera Beta/true/target Beta and player rotation; smooth turn scales native right-stick input. Optional head-turn assist turns local yaw outside a dead zone into bounded native right-stick intent while the player is moving. GENERALIZATION: Rotate locomotion only by the headset yaw relative to the latched facing, while artificial turn remains an ordinary native camera/player-heading operation. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: The implementation intentionally changes native character/camera heading as well as presentation; a camera-only snap would desynchronize movement/body state.",
  "pattern_vcq_prepost_anchor": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VrUpdateFirstPersonAnchor(false)` runs from `CapturePad` so movement/hand conversion sees the current heading. The same function runs post-physics before `RwCameraBeginUpdate`, because using the pre-physics player/vehicle position made the rendered view trail by speed×frame-time. On foot it anchors near the player head (with optional stable no-bob eye height); in vehicles it can latch a seat point and call `setFirstPersonAnchorBasis` with the vehicle right/up/forward frame so cockpit pitch/roll and translation are correct. Visual hand matrices are rebased after the post-physics anchor without re-running interaction logic. GENERALIZATION: Update the VR world anchor at the two engine moments that need it: before input-space calculations and again after physics immediately before camera rendering. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: The two updates serve different ownership stages; running gameplay interaction twice would duplicate inputs, while rendering from only the earlier anchor introduces visible lag.",
  "pattern_vcq_cutscene_modes": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. The OpenXR layer can latch an upright theater quad in the active reference space, using yaw-only orientation so the screen does not roll/pitch with the original movie camera. The game layer also exposes stereo cutscene mode: `VrUpdateFirstPersonAnchor` can anchor to the director camera or a selected cutscene actor head; the debug/settings layer cycles and persists a preferred camera per cutscene name. This illustrates a reusable state machine: flat/cinema content gets a stable 2D carrier, while selected 3D scenes can opt into a headset-compatible camera anchor. GENERALIZATION: Treat authored cutscenes as a camera-state problem with explicit presentation modes instead of blindly applying the gameplay HMD transform. SOURCE-DEMONSTRATED LIMIT: Authored camera cuts and actor availability remain title-specific; stereo mode needs per-scene judgement.",
    "pattern_shar_predicted_visual_frame": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `Initialize`, `PollEvents`, `BeginFrame`, `EndFrame`). `BeginFrame` performs OpenXR event handling, applies pending swapchain recreation only after the prior frame has relinquished its images, calls `SyncInputActions`, then `xrWaitFrame` and `xrBeginFrame`. When rendering is requested, `xrLocateViews` uses `frameState.predictedDisplayTime`; both `hand_pose` action spaces are also `xrLocateSpace`d at that exact display time. `EndFrame` reuses the cached `XrView.pose/fov` values in two `XrCompositionLayerProjectionView`s and submits them at the same predicted time. `XR_TYPE_EVENT_DATA_REFERENCE_SPACE_CHANGE_PENDING` is converted into an app-origin invalidation/rebuild, with three located frames of settling, optional preservation of standing Y and forced invalidation of movie/frontend anchors. REUSABLE PATTERN: make predicted display time a frame transaction for visual poses and compositor submission; cache located results rather than letting arbitrary subsystems sample the runtime at unrelated times. IMPORTANT SOURCE BOUNDARY: action synchronization is before `xrWaitFrame`; only view/hand pose location is proven to use predicted display time.",
  "pattern_shar_two_layer_multiview": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `CreateSwapchains`, `BeginMultiview`, `BeginEye`, `EndMultiview`; `render/RenderManager/RenderManager.cpp`). One OpenXR color swapchain is created with `arraySize=2`; eye 1 aliases eye 0. GLES provides both a multiview framebuffer/depth array and a separate layer framebuffer; Vulkan exposes the same two-layer swapchain to its backend. `BeginMultiview` acquires and waits once, broadcasts to both layers with `glFramebufferTextureMultiviewOVR` or Vulkan view mask. If GLES extension/program capability checks fail, `RenderManager` executes two `BeginEye` passes that attach array layer 0 then 1. Acquire failures are paired with release where needed to prevent starvation. Persistent 24-bit depth addresses both allocation stalls and legacy far-plane precision. GENERALIZATION: a shared array swapchain gives one resource model for single-pass and fallback stereo, simplifying compositor ownership while preserving compatibility.",
  "pattern_shar_midpoint_culling": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `PrepareMultiviewCamera`, `GetLatestCullingCamera`; `render/RenderManager/RenderManager.cpp`; `render/Culling/WorldScene.cpp`). Each physical eye gets `GetEyeCamera` plus an asymmetric `MakeProjection`. For one-pass traversal, the source deliberately uses a tracked head midpoint as the common camera, then computes per-eye view adjustments as centre-world multiplied by inverse eye-world. `RenderManager` temporarily substitutes this camera into Pure3D render layers and restores the authored camera afterward; `WorldScene` likewise temporarily substitutes the active VR culling matrix for visibility marking. The source comment calls out the left-eye-only rejection failure directly. GENERALIZATION: separate the camera used to decide what to submit from the final per-eye view matrices. LIMIT: this is midpoint/common culling, not a formal union-frustum constructor.",
  "pattern_shar_multiview_legacy_gui_split": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`render/RenderManager/RenderManager.cpp`, `vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `BeginMultiviewGuiEye`, `EndMultiview`). Gameplay world layers may use single-pass multiview, but the legacy GUI layer is skipped from that broadcast. `BeginMultiviewGuiEye` preserves the world result while switching to one array layer for conventional eye-specific GUI/FMVs; Vulkan ends only the current render pass inside the same command buffer, GLES binds a dedicated layer framebuffer. Active movies, front-end/loading states and pending pause transitions can force the proven two-eye path from the start. GENERALIZATION: multiview is a capability of individual render phases/shaders, not an all-or-nothing application state. Preserve a compatibility phase for legacy 2D or side-effectful passes.",
  "pattern_shar_tracking_origin_roomscale": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `YawOnly`, `RelativePose`, `PoseToGame`, `RecenterVrPose`, `ConsumeRoomscaleMovement`, `GetPhysicalHeadHeight`; `camera/firstpersoncam.cpp`). The recenter origin removes horizontal translation plus yaw; `PoseToGame` is the explicit RH OpenXR to SHAR conversion and establishes 1 unit = 1 metre. On foot, horizontal head-midpoint delta is computed relative to the origin, origin X/Z is advanced so the same movement disappears from subsequent local HMD pose, the delta is rotated into `vrBaseHeading`, then `FirstPersonCam` adds it to the native character position. Large >~0.5 m jumps are rejected. In-car roomscale is suspended; exit snaps origin X/Z to the current head to discard accumulated cockpit lean. Physical head height comes from captured STAGE origin Y so live crouch displacement is not doubled. GENERALIZATION: tracked-space consumption is an ownership transfer—once physical translation becomes avatar movement, subtract it from the tracking anchor.",
  "pattern_shar_semantic_input_bridge": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `CreateInputActions`, `SyncInputActions`; `worldsim/character/charactermappable.cpp`; pause/display GUI sources). One `gameplay` OpenXR action set provides named locomotion/look, face semantics, analog triggers/grips, stick clicks, hand poses and haptics. The adapter reads actions and writes the old `UserController` virtual-input names rather than teaching every gameplay system OpenXR. It applies a radial deadzone, locks menu gestures to one dominant axis until neutral, suppresses look stick while first-person physical driving is active, lets yoke state replace gas/brake triggers, reserves grip for wheel ownership where required and merges physical interaction into native A/Y action pulses. `CharacterMappable` omits normal attack mapping in VR so tracked punch logic becomes authoritative. GENERALIZATION: put XR/controller-specific policy in one semantic bridge and keep the legacy command vocabulary as the gameplay/network compatibility boundary.",
  "pattern_shar_physical_push_native_action": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `UpdatePhysicalPushInteract`; `ai/actionbuttonhandler.h`). Hand deltas create velocity samples only while VR/on-foot/player-controller state is valid. No gesture can fire unless the current `ButtonHandler` exists and `AllowPhysicalInteract()` returns true. The default semantic policy excludes GET_IN_CAR, GET_IN_USER_CAR, SUMMON_PHONE, PURCHASE_CAR and PURCHASE_SKIN; `GenericEventButtonHandler` rejects `EVENT_TALK_TO_NPC`; other specialized handlers can override to false. Accepted motion requires ~0.80 m/s and loose alignment to controller/head forward, creates a two-frame native action pulse, cooldown and haptic. When target state is invalid, pose history is still refreshed to avoid a false large delta on entry. GENERALIZATION: recognize physical intent only after querying the flat game’s current semantic interaction state, then invoke its ordinary action route.",
  "pattern_shar_physical_collectible_grab": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`mission/objectives/collectibleobjective.cpp`; `ai/actionbuttonhandler.cpp`: `Collectible::OnEnter`, `OnUpdate`, `TryHandGrabCollect`, `FinishCollect`). On-foot VR converts body overlap into pending/armed state. Collection requires grip >=0.20 plus a tracked hand within 0.55 of the item/locator. Pending remains through a small lean out of the trigger but is canceled once the avatar walks away. Mission objectives call existing `OnCollection`/`Collect` and completion checks; normal collectibles call `OnCollectEffects`, hide the icon, deactivate native triggers, set `mbCollected` and trigger `EVENT_COLLECT_OBJECT`. Vehicles/non-VR retain walk-through pickup. GENERALIZATION: preserve the existing trigger as broad eligibility, add a spatial confirmation stage, then return to the native finalizer so rewards/mission bookkeeping remain authoritative.",
  "pattern_shar_tracking_velocity_sweep_melee": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`worldsim/character/character.cpp`: `PreSimUpdate`, `VrPunch`; `worldsim/character/charactermappable.cpp`). Each controller stores two positions: tracking-local for speed and world-space for contact. Velocity uses `GetControllerLocalPose` so walking/recentering cannot create swing speed. Below 0.65 m/s re-arms; >=3.50 m/s triggers one swing, and the world-space segment is extended ±0.10 m through the visual hand. The swing is consumed even on miss until speed falls again. `VrPunch` ray-tests the native dynamic intersection list and reuses original kick events/forces, NPC smackdown, state-prop collision checks, breakables and vehicle/object events. GENERALIZATION: measure intent in a motion-only space, resolve contact in world space, and hand authority back to native damage/physics. LIMIT: dynamic-object ray/sweep only; interiors return false; no persistent fist rigid body.",
  "pattern_shar_vehicle_local_vr_camera": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`camera/firstpersoncam.cpp/.h`; `vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `RecenterVrPose`). Cockpit anchor uses deterministic `Vehicle::GetDriverLocation()` plus `VR_DRIVER_EYE_HEIGHT` because the animated driver head can be stale during camera transition. Entry captures the vehicle-local anchor, establishes base heading and recenters current HMD pose. `BuildVrStabilizedVehicleTransform` reconstructs a vehicle basis with unrestricted yaw, pitch/roll derived from chassis axes, ±45° clamps and optional 0.25 s low-pass comfort filtering. `UpdateForPhysics` bypasses generic camera collision push-out while VR is enabled. GENERALIZATION: for moving interiors, choose a stable authored socket in vehicle space and keep physical head motion local to it; filter vehicle attitude separately from HMD tracking.",
  "pattern_shar_snapshot_steering_wheel": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `UpdateVrSteeringWheel`, `GetVrSteeringWheelValue`; `worldsim/redbrick/vehiclecontroller/humanvehiclecontroller.cpp`). Round-wheel grabs use grip hysteresis plus calibrated radial/depth proximity. Acquisition snapshots hand polar angle and current wheel angle. While held, target = snapshot wheel + unwrapped(current hand - snapshot hand); snapshots never advance. Near-hub angles are ignored; tracking reacquisition re-snapshots against the current wheel; huge deltas are treated as teleports; dual-hand targets average. The authoritative angle is bounded, lightly curved/deadzoned and returned by the normal vehicle controller. Release springs to centre. GENERALIZATION: fixed grab snapshots are more reversible/stable than accumulating hand deltas into a moving reference. Use unwrapping and explicit reacquisition to survive 2π boundaries and tracking loss.",
  "pattern_shar_live_wheel_calibration": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: wheel offset load/store, `UpdateVrSteeringWheel`). Both stick clicks held for `kWheelAdjustHoldSec` enter calibration. With two hands: midpoint -> centre; across-hands direction -> yaw; half hand distance -> radius; averaged controller-forward pitch -> wheel pitch. The values are followed smoothly, yoke anchor derives from centre, and the calibration control remains rendered. Exiting persists centre/yaw/pitch/radius/yoke by vehicle name and haptically confirms. GENERALIZATION: convert awkward per-vehicle coordinate tuning into an in-VR authoring mode. The calibration artifact should be treated as a versioned game/profile asset rather than scattered constants.",
  "pattern_shar_physical_yoke_adapter": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: Honor Roller branch of `UpdateVrSteeringWheel`, `SyncInputActions`, `GetVrSteeringWheelValue`). Each hand acquires a T-yoke spatial volume and snapshots rest height. Differential hand vertical displacement drives steering; aggregate fore/aft displacement drives `g.yokeThrottle`. Positive values become the game’s right-trigger accelerator, negative values become left-trigger brake. Full gas/brake edges are latched for haptics. The visible yoke and hands use the same steering/throttle state, while the final steering output alone is inverted to match this vehicle’s native convention. GENERALIZATION: special vehicles need physical-control adapters that map their real interaction affordance into existing normalized commands rather than pretending every cockpit contains the same wheel.",
  "pattern_shar_authority_vs_visual_control_pose": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: end of `UpdateVrSteeringWheel`, `RenderControllerHands`). `wheelAngle` remains authoritative and responsive; a separate `wheelVisualAngle += (wheelAngle-wheelVisualAngle)*0.55` filters mesh jitter. A grabbed hand is rendered from the visual rim/yoke position and stored grab orientation instead of the latest raw controller transform, and remains attached through brief pose-invalid frames. Raw pose resumes after release. Character-specific static hand meshes are selected by controlled character UID; hand render is suppressed in third-person vehicles and animated/relative/conversation cameras. GENERALIZATION: do not filter the variable the simulation consumes merely to hide rendering jitter. Maintain a presentation state that can smooth/contact-lock independently.",
  "pattern_shar_native_hud_spatial_planes": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: HUD capture state, `DrawMissionHudPlanes`, radar/spatial HUD drawing; `presentation/gui/ingame/*`). Existing Scrooby groups render into offscreen targets with crop rectangles, UVs, measured aspect and frame serials. The VR compositor then assigns semantic anchors: left wrist/hand for objective information, right-hand/other offsets for selected widgets, wheel/dashboard for first-person driving and centre-camera space for transient notifications. Each eye recomputes world-to-eye and exact OpenXR projection. Radar reuses the authored 640x480 map group and its mask/crop instead of rebuilding radar gameplay. A critical implementation detail separates visibility/layout occupancy from whether a cached texture was refreshed that exact frame, preserving stable HUD stacking during animation. GENERALIZATION: capture mature native UI logic, then spatialize the finished visual groups; keep placement semantics separate from the code that computes their content.",
  "pattern_shar_asymmetric_legacy_ui_convergence": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `GetActiveUiHorizontalOffset`). For legacy screen-space GUI, each eye uses its true `XrFovf` tangents and its actual position relative to the live two-eye midpoint. The eye displacement is rotated to eye-local space, projected toward a common carrier plane, and combined with the asymmetric optical-centre term. A tuned residual factor is applied because the flat orthographic projection already contributes part of the offset. GENERALIZATION: when a legacy UI cannot be rebuilt as geometry immediately, derive convergence from runtime eye geometry rather than a guessed IPD or symmetric frustum. LIMIT: this is an empirical compatibility correction, not a replacement for full per-eye plane projection.",
  "pattern_shar_latched_2d_planes": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `SetMovieRendering`, `BeginMoviePlane`, `GetActiveMovieProjection`, frontend-plane functions, `EndFrame`; `presentation/fmvplayer/fmvplayer.cpp`). Movie/frontend carriers capture the current two-eye midpoint and yaw-only headset orientation once, yielding a stable upright world-locked screen that does not follow head pitch/roll every frame. Eye-specific projection is computed at draw time. `GetActiveMovieProjection` explicitly uses full 4x4 multiplication because Pure3D’s affine fast multiply destroys perspective W. Reference-space recenter invalidates both anchors. Vulkan iris fade uses `XrCompositionLayerColorScaleBiasKHR` chained onto the projection layer, avoiding a visible black raster quad under foveation. GENERALIZATION: flat/cinematic states deserve their own presentation-space state machine and must participate in recenter lifecycle.",
  "pattern_shar_stereo_side_effect_gating": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`worldsim/redbrick/geometryvehicle.cpp`, `worldsim/coins/sparkle.cpp`, `render/RenderManager/WorldRenderLayer.cpp`). Legacy display paths create particles/effects. In VR, vehicle damage particle generation is allowed only on the normal left-eye/non-CSM pass; gag sparkle emission similarly rejects right-eye and CSM calls and is rate reduced. Shared smoke is not redundantly rendered from every vehicle. Player-cockpit draw scopes temporarily hide siren nodes, enable VR glass treatment, suppress embedded driver/self rear lights, configure enhanced materials/CSM receiver state, then restore globals after the draw. GENERALIZATION: when adding stereo/multiview/shadow/reflection replays to an old engine, audit every render callback for simulation mutation and make those mutations execute exactly once per game frame.",
  "pattern_shar_openxr_vulkan_enable2": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/vulkan/openxr_vulkan_context.cpp`, `vr/openxrmanager.cpp`). The Vulkan backend uses OpenXR `vulkan_enable2`: runtime graphics requirements bound the Vulkan version; `xrCreateVulkanInstanceKHR` creates the instance; `xrGetVulkanGraphicsDevice2KHR` selects the compatible physical device; `xrCreateVulkanDeviceKHR` creates the logical device. Feature queries enable multiview and fragment-density-map support only when present. OpenXR swapchain creation/enumeration chains Meta foveation structures; a high fixed foveation profile is updated on the swapchain; the Vulkan multiview render pass uses a two-view mask and conditionally chains a fragment-density-map attachment. GENERALIZATION: let the XR runtime define graphics-device compatibility and negotiate optional performance capabilities rather than creating a renderer device first and hoping it is valid for XR.",
  "pattern_shar_safe_swapchain_reconfigure": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`vr/openxrmanager.cpp`: `SetRenderScale`, beginning of `BeginFrame`, `CreateSwapchains`, perf instrumentation). Runtime render-scale change is a deferred state change. The next safe frame boundary idles queued graphics work, destroys immutable swapchains/targets and recreates them; if requested scale fails, it restores the last applied scale and recreates again. Eye sizes are clamped to OpenXR maximums and aligned. Frame telemetry measures wait/render/submit maxima/averages plus per-layer CPU work, materials, uploads and draw counts. GLES GPU timing uses a ring of `EXT_disjoint_timer_query` objects and reads only ready slots. GENERALIZATION: treat XR swapchain configuration as lifecycle state, not an arbitrary mid-frame render target resize, and make diagnostics nonblocking wherever possible.",
  "seed_simpsons_hnr_case": "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-06; supplied `code` tree). ARCHITECTURE: this is a native source-port conversion, not a camera-only wrapper. `vr/openxrmanager.cpp/.h` owns XR session/actions/reference space/eye and hand pose normalization, stereo targets, HUD carriers and physical vehicle-control state; `render/RenderManager/RenderManager.cpp` owns when the game renders one world pass vs two eye passes; native camera/gameplay code consumes the VR service through narrow functions. FRAME TIMING: `BeginFrame` does `SyncInputActions` before `xrWaitFrame`, then waits/begins the XR frame and locates both `XrView`s plus hand action spaces at `predictedDisplayTime`. `EndFrame` submits the same cached poses/FOVs and two array layers at that time. Quest reference-space-change events cause yaw-only origin rebasing after a short settle window, preserving standing height on crouched recenter and invalidating spatial 2D anchors. RENDERING: `CreateSwapchains` creates a single two-layer stereo swapchain. GLES uses `GL_OVR_multiview2` only if extension and multiview Pure3D shaders are ready; otherwise `BeginEye` renders each layer separately. Vulkan uses an OpenXR-created compatible device (`XR_KHR_vulkan_enable2`), true multiview and optional Meta fragment-density/foveation. `PrepareMultiviewCamera` builds separate asymmetric eye projections/view adjustments but uses the tracked HMD midpoint for shared traversal/culling. Render scheduling intentionally broadcasts only compatible world work; Scrooby GUI/FMVs get conventional per-eye passes. Persistent 24-bit depth, a distinct single-layer FBO, careful acquire/wait/release pairing, safe deferred swapchain recreation and asynchronous telemetry are explicit stability/performance work. The source disables the existing mono-depth GTAO in VR rather than presenting it as stereo-correct. COORDINATES/CAMERA: `RelativePose` removes app recenter state; `PoseToGame` performs the one RH OpenXR -> SHAR conversion, with 1 unit treated as 1 metre. `GetEyeCamera` composes local eye then base game camera. `FirstPersonCam` owns artificial yaw separately from HMD yaw, consumes horizontal roomscale movement into native character position while moving the tracking origin to avoid double application, replaces authored eye height with physical STAGE height, and applies child height scaling for Bart/Lisa. Vehicle camera anchors on `Vehicle::GetDriverLocation` + eye height, recenters at entry, keeps HMD motion local, and optionally bounds/filters chassis pitch/roll. Generic third-person collision push-out is bypassed for the tracked first-person eye.\n\nINPUT: one OpenXR `gameplay` set contains semantic axes/buttons, analog triggers/grips, hand pose and haptics. `SyncInputActions` deadzones and maps them into existing `UserController` virtual names. VR-specific arbitration occurs here (driving look suppression, yoke gas/brake, grip ownership, menu single-axis lock, physical interaction pulse).\n\nHANDS: `RenderControllerHands` uses character-specific static hand meshes at tracked grip poses, with context-based visibility and physical wheel/yoke attachment. No full-body/arm IK or skeletal finger retargeting is demonstrated. PHYSICAL PUSH: `UpdatePhysicalPushInteract` recognizes deliberate hand velocity/direction but first requires current `ButtonHandler::AllowPhysicalInteract`; unsafe car/phone/purchase/talk semantics are excluded. Success injects the same native DoAction edge. COLLECTIBLES: `CollectibleObjective` and `ActionButton::Collectible` convert body overlap to pending eligibility; grip + tracked hand proximity confirms collection; then original mission/reward/event code finalizes it.\n\nMELEE: normal flat attack mapping is removed in VR. `Character::PreSimUpdate` measures hand speed from controller-local tracking deltas, not world motion, then world-sweeps an extended hand segment. `VrPunch` uses native dynamic intersections and original kick forces/events/breakables; one swing is consumed even on miss until the hand slows. VEHICLES: `UpdateVrSteeringWheel` is a complete physical solver. Round-wheel grip uses spatial hysteresis and fixed snapshot/angle-unwrapping; tracking loss resnapshots; one/two hand targets drive authoritative wheel angle; trim/centering/output shaping feed `HumanVehicleController::GetSteering`. A separately smoothed visual angle drives wheel mesh and glued hand presentation. An in-VR two-hand calibration mode authoritatively sets centre/radius/yaw/pitch/yoke anchor per vehicle and persists it. Honor Roller has its own T-yoke: differential vertical hand movement steers; shared fore/aft movement becomes native gas/brake triggers; full edges haptically signal. Wheel-hub proximity can invoke native horn.\n\nUI: authored radar/mission/gameplay HUD pieces are captured/cropped and drawn as per-eye physical planes with semantic wrist, dashboard/wheel and centre-camera anchors. Legacy screen GUI can use eye/FOV-derived convergence correction. Movies and front-end/pause/loading screens use latched yaw-only planes and re-anchor after recenter; Vulkan iris fade can be a composition-layer color scale/bias. RENDER-SIDE EFFECTS: legacy particle emission called from draw paths is gated to one canonical normal eye/pass so stereo/CSM replay cannot multiply simulation; cockpit draw temporarily suppresses intrusive sirens, driver geometry, flapping hood and self-light cost while restoring shared state afterward. REUSABLE MODEL: explicit XR frame timeline -> one pose/recenter conversion -> stereo renderer with multiview compatibility phases -> native camera/player/vehicle anchors -> semantic input bridge -> tracked intent detectors -> original gameplay authority -> stereo-safe spatial UI. SOURCE LIMITS: no full-body IK, skeletal finger tracking, physical weapon/reload/throw system or remote XR-pose networking; roomscale SetPosition path does not show dedicated collision reconciliation; physical punch is a dynamic-object segment test and disabled indoors; Oculus Touch bindings are explicitly authored rather than a data-driven multi-profile layer.",
  "seed_kisakcod_case": "Source-audit (public KisakCOD VR headers/impls and project docs): the VR work is source-level on top of KisakCOD (GPL), not binary injection. CMake builds 32-bit KisakCOD-sp, Configurator, and Input-Mapper against OpenXR submodules; the player supplies only original COD4 data beside iw3sp.exe. Architecture seams: (1) Runtime backend — KISAK_VR_BACKEND=auto|openxr|openvr; OpenXR primary, experimental 32-bit OpenVR only when no usable 32-bit OpenXR runtime exists; compatibility evaluator reports 32-bit vs 64-bit ActiveRuntime manifests independently and can Block OpenXR-only when the 32-bit manifest is absent. Meta Link 32-bit OpenXR can hard-crash in xrCreateSession after D3D11 init, so auto cannot recover; documented XR_RUNTIME_JSON override points at Virtual Desktop virtualdesktop-openxr-32.json. (2) Stereo capture — same-frame packed D3D9 backbuffer with optional dedicated scope panel (vr_packed_layout ResolveCaptureLayout requires mainStereoWidth + scope panel to fit; rejects 3072x1536). Verified layouts: Quest Native 6016x2688/1.0, Performance 4768x2016/0.75, Pimax Crystal Light Full FOV 7924x4082/0.80 (two 3450x4082 eyes + 1024 px scope). D3D9 capture (vr_d3d9_capture) offers a D3D9Ex GPU-shared path with four fenced slots, sequential serial order, and producer query completion, plus a reset-safe D3DPOOL_SYSTEMMEM CPU fallback. Pose metadata (renderFrameId, producerSequence, timestamps) crosses the D3D9 producer / D3D11 consumer boundary without OpenXR types in the legacy renderer. (3) Packed post-FX policy — vr_saved_screen_policy captures the full packed frame only after the final view index; ResolvePackedBlendRegion maps each eye to its packed sub-rectangle so shellshock/flash does not resample a shared incomplete image. vr_postfx_policy forces eye-local color when retail fullscreen glow/DoF/blur would otherwise read across packed view boundaries (Ultimatum sky stretch). (4) Controller Input V4 — semantic controller-neutral Source/Action enums and chord Bindings; OpenXR profiles resolve component paths per interaction profile (Touch, Index, Vive, MR, PICO, etc.); OpenVR adapter reads legacy VRControllerState_t, rejects thumbrest sources (no capacitive component), special-cases Index primary=grip / squeeze=second trigger axis, and ships an OpenVR-safe binding layout. GameplayButtonGate requires one observed release after menu/loading before publishing edges (stance-through-load fix). (5) Weapon / two-hand — vr_weapon_calibration builds a physical two-controller frame then RelativeTwoHandWeaponAxis applies only support-hand movement relative to the engagement-anchored one-hand calibrated pose (V108; eliminates attach/release snap). SupportGripProximityQualifies uses attach radius plus 4.0 in hysteresis on release. Per-weapon hip/shouldered and gunstock Pose documents live under %LOCALAPPDATA%\\KisakCOD-VR with 142-value save verification. Physical scope uses a dedicated weapon-free panel and publishes optic pose for ballistic convergence. (6) Physical interaction — dominant-hand swap of weapon/off-hand roles, muzzle, scope, reload, grenades, pointer, haptics; manual magazine state machine (eject / hip draw / well insert) for detachable-clip weapons only; manual grenades with still-grip drop semantics; optional night-vision spatial gesture (crown/visor pull, head-zone gated). Palm surface prefers XR_EXT_palm_pose; Pimax-only grip-pose fallback when palm is absent. Deep pass: Reusable patterns for source ports are (a) explicit per-frame render-pose + capture serial pairing so the compositor never infers which pose produced a queued eye image, (b) semantic input layer with backend-specific adapters and a carried-input gate across non-gameplay ownership, (c) engagement-anchored relative two-hand deltas instead of absolute dual-controller frames, (d) packed-view policies that isolate capture and post-FX to the final view and eye-local regions, and (e) dual 32/64-bit runtime registration checks for 32-bit game processes. OpenVR remains intentionally less capable than OpenXR and must not be presented as equivalent.",
  "seed_mousepi_vr_case": "Binary/asset audit (no public game-plugin source): MousePI_VR.dll is a BepInEx BaseUnityPlugin (MousePIPlugin/GamePlugin, net472, Harmony + Fody) built from a path labeled Simple CustomOpenXR for bepinex. OpenXR is a separate MinimalOpenXR-style OpenXR.dll (InitializeOpenXR/FinalizeOpenXR, D3D11/D3D12/Vulkan/OpenGL session bindings, swapchain acquire, interaction profiles for Touch/Index/simple_controller). Camera seams: VRCamFix.UpdatePosition, VR_Rig_LeftEye/RightEye, StereoTargetEyeMask, ScreenSpaceCameraPositionLeftHand/RightHand, OnBeginCameraRendering, SetupFullscreenEffectsCamera for URP fullscreen passes.\n\nUI: InitCanvasFixLeft/Right, PatchCanvas, InventoryControllerToggleCanvas, DivingSuitUIControllerSetCanvasVisibility; AssetBundles TwoPassAlphaMask and UIWithFakeAlpha shipped for Built-in, URP, and HDRP so transparent HUD survives stereo. Hands: AssetBundles hands / hands_animated (Unity 6000 manifests) with left_hand and right_hand prefabs as VR_LeftHand/VR_RightHand. Gameplay Harmony targets: PlayerWeaponController spread/reload/shoot VFX, KickForwardPatch and kick trigger reposition, PlayerTailRenderer.RefreshTail, death and flashlight handlers. Input bridge: VRInputManagerCustomOpenXR plus Nefarius.ViGEm.Client Xbox 360 virtual pads (ViGemVRControllers). Haptics: ForceTubeVR_API_x64 Shot/Kick/Rumble channels (InitializeProTube) and Bhaptics.Tact TactsuitVR PlaybackHaptics/TriggerHapticFeedback. Deep pass: Treat this as a BepInEx + custom-OpenXR pattern for Unity FPS titles where XR Plugin Management alone is not enough. Preserve the dual input path (OpenXR poses for hands/aim, ViGEm for gamepad-only systems), pipeline-specific UI shaders, and weapon-hook-driven dual haptics. Do not invent IK solvers or full physical weapon collision — those are not evidenced in the shipped strings/assets. Discord-only distribution means update risk is high after game patches.",
  "seed_grwxr_case": "Source-audit: the public implementation uses a dxgi.dll proxy, forwards unrelated DXGI exports, hooks IDXGISwapChain::Present and creates OpenXR against the game's live D3D11 device. Camera/projection targets are relocated with byte signatures. Stereo is alternate-eye: the hook changes camera transform/FOV, renders one eye per frame, copies that eye into the corresponding OpenXR image and submits the stored render pose. Controller poses are overlaid on gamepad input rather than driving native weapon/hand objects, so this is a strong example of 'tracked aim + legacy input' but not full hand presence. Deep pass: The most reusable part is the fail-safe DXGI proxy lifecycle: forward every unrelated export, hook Present, use the game’s live D3D11 device, and refuse to continue when a signature no longer matches. The current audit already captures the alternate-frame stereo and gamepad fallback; the added lesson is to treat signature results as a versioned hook table with per-feature health, so one broken function does not disable unrelated rendering/input features. This is particularly important for proxy DLLs because a bad hook can crash before the game has created a recoverable state.",
  "seed_bytesig_pattern": "Source-audit: signature scanning is implemented as a relocation strategy, not a discovery strategy. You still need to identify the target function once, derive a sufficiently distinctive byte pattern, and validate the match; at runtime the scanner converts that pattern into the current address. The Wildlands case shows the important failure behavior: when recompilation makes a signature unmatchable, disable that feature rather than guessing. A signature scanner should therefore have explicit match-count validation, logging and safe no-op failure. Deep pass: A production signature scanner should validate both match count and the bytes/instructions around the match before installing a hook. Prefer signatures anchored to stable instruction sequences and calculate RIP-relative/relative-call targets from decoded instructions rather than embedding absolute addresses. Keep a per-build diagnostic record of which signatures matched, which failed and which feature was disabled; Wildlands demonstrates why this turns an update from a mysterious crash into an actionable porting task.",
  "seed_cyberpunk_vrik_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-06), VERIFIED FROM THE USER-SUPPLIED current cyberpunk-vr-port source snapshot and cross-checked against the current public project README. The supplied tree contains 109 C++ source files, 52 project headers, 97 CET Lua files, 16 redscript files, 47 CyberpunkVR_TwoHandGrip_*.ini captures/configs, and 13 weapon-specific reload modules plus their index. This audit supersedes the registry's older split Stereo/Hands and AER pair-lock description.\n\nARCHITECTURE / BOOTSTRAP: CMakeLists.txt defines one C++20 RED4ext shared library, output CyberpunkVR_Stereo. It GLOB_RECURSEs all src/*.cpp + include/*.hpp, explicitly because the former CyberpunkVR_Hands code is now part of the same binary. Dependencies: Khronos OpenXR-SDK release-1.0.34, ImGui 1.90.9, MinHook, RED4ext SDK, d3d12, dxgi and d3dcompiler. Current source comments document two migrations: the old dxgi.dll proxy is gone, and the old CyberpunkVR_Hands.dll is gone. Main.cpp Load order is intentional: InitRuntimePaths -> CyberpunkVR_RegisterHandsNatives (before the registration window closes) -> ApplyFirstLaunchGameSettings -> CyberpunkVRPort_InitStereo (before the game's D3D12CreateDevice so device/queue/descriptor changes can be captured) -> CyberpunkVRPort_PluginBootstrap (before game swapchain creation) -> delayed WorkerThread pattern-scan/game-hook pass. Unload drops overlay device/queue references and shuts down OpenXR before D3D12 teardown. This is a single-process native plugin with CET/redscript gameplay extensions, not two competing native plugins and not a DXGI forwarding proxy.\n\nHOOK ORGANIZATION / REVERSE ENGINEERING: Hooks/Registry.cpp implements static hook registration with PreDevice, Boot and PostStereo stages plus explicit order; hooks are collected/sorted deterministically instead of trusting link order. Disabled is logged separately from failure; every attempted hook logs ok/FAILED and failure means its feature is absent. Stereo/DetourRegistry.cpp keeps a second deterministic registry for engine detours. Utils/AobScanner.hpp provides process-module byte/mask scanning over committed readable/executable pages. Do not generalize that into 'the mod is signature based': Stereo/EngineRvas.hpp contains a large centralized table of Cyberpunk2077.exe RVAs explicitly verified against build 2.31, and several callsite patches validate expected instruction bytes before writing. The repository's docs contain the investigation trail for camera write chains, render-to-texture creation, frame-graph builders/node executors, D3D12 replay/in-place replay dead ends, VRCAM discovery, per-view render state, PhysicalRay weapon hits and VRIK frame-phase measurements. Reusable rule: keep known-build RVAs centralized, document how each was proven, validate bytes/invariants at the patch edge, log every install, and fail closed.\n\nOPENXR INITIALIZATION / RUNTIME SELECTION: Runtimes/OpenXRManager.cpp calls ConfigurePreferredOpenXRRuntime before xrCreateInstance. XR_KHR_D3D12_enable is required. The code enumerates extensions and enables XR_KHR_composition_layer_depth when available. It creates a PRIMARY_STEREO system/view configuration, reads recommended view dimensions, obtains D3D12 graphics requirements, creates an OpenXR session from the captured game ID3D12Device + ID3D12CommandQueue, then creates LOCAL and VIEW reference spaces. SteamVR selection is an OpenXR runtime choice: the code can load openvr_api.dll only to call VR_GetRuntimePath/find steamxr_win64.json and sets process XR_RUNTIME_JSON before OpenXR initialization. The runtime identity is read with xrGetInstanceProperties and classified (SteamVR/Virtual Desktop etc.). There is no separate OpenVR compositor submission backend in the current implementation.\n\nOPENXR ACTIONS / CONTROLLER\n\nINPUT: OpenXRManager creates a subactioned grip/palm pose action (m_handPoseAction), a distinct aim pose action (m_handAimPoseAction), thumbstick Vector2, trigger float, grip float, stick-click bool, primary/secondary button bool and menu bool. It suggests bindings for /interaction_profiles/oculus/touch_controller, /valve/index_controller, /htc/vive_controller, /microsoft/motion_controller and /khr/simple_controller. Grip pose is the hand/VRIK source; aim pose is available for pointing and hand-relative locomotion because a controller's aim -Z is more stable for that semantic than grip orientation. The frame loop xrSyncActions/reads action state into VRControllerState. Hooks/XInput.cpp merges that semantic controller state into gamepad 0 during gameplay but deliberately leaves menus/braindances on the game's raw pad bindings. The gameplay bridge handles fixed movement magnitude, sprint detent, dash, crouch, snap/smooth turn, scanner gesture, D-pad/quickhack paging and trigger/grip arbitration. Pose and gameplay actions can be disabled separately (pose-only fallback). SOURCE CHECK: no XR_EXT_hand_tracking symbol/extension and no XR_ACTION_TYPE_VIBRATION_OUTPUT/xrApplyHapticFeedback were found in the supplied source, so this is controller-driven motion control rather than optical skeletal hand tracking, and OpenXR haptics are UNKNOWN/absent from this snapshot.\n\nFRAME TIMING / DISPLAY-TIME OWNERSHIP: Runtimes/OpenXRFrameLoop.cpp owns the XR cycle. Its runtime-paced mode performs xrWaitFrame, xrBeginFrame, chooses one locateTime, uses that time for xrLocateViews and controller/hand locates, captures/copies the completed game images into OpenXR swapchains, then xrEndFrame. SetFrameAimTime publishes the chosen target so camera-side LocateHeadPoseAt uses the same instant. The code contains optional display-time fitting/pipeline-depth instrumentation, but the current default PoseAimScale is intended to add no private lead beyond the runtime target; OpenXRManager explicitly marks the old xr_motion_predict_ms custom extrapolation REMOVED because runtime predicted display time + compositor reprojection are already the prediction stages. This is a critical correction to the older registry entry.\n\nPAIR LOCK REMOVAL / SAME-FRAME NATIVE STEREO: OpenXRManager.cpp explicitly says UpdatePairLock/GetPairLockedHeadPose were removed with AER. The old lock was meaningful when one engine camera alternated eyes across two engine frames. The current architecture has two cameras in one engine frame, and AnimPose caches/replays one skeleton solve for that animation epoch. FlushHandsToShared therefore publishes live hands/head rather than freezing a second independently clocked snapshot. The source also removes the old pair-lock overlay control (legacy config key can be round-tripped). Reusable rule: do not cargo-cult 'freeze one pose for both eyes'; first determine whether the eyes are separate game frames or two views of the same engine frame.\n\nTHREADED SUBMISSION / LOW GAME FPS: OpenXRFrameLoop supports inline or dedicated submit-thread ownership. Source comments/logic specifically explain that SteamVR's xrWaitFrame/compositor pacing can stall the game's Present thread if run inline, while other runtimes can use inline. When the dedicated thread owns XR pacing, the game Present returns instead of blocking on xrWaitFrame. The XR loop can re-submit the most recent completed game snapshot with its OWN render pose when no new game frame exists, maintaining compositor cadence rather than skipping XR frames. Producer/consumer work uses bounded waits and a small allocator/resource pool so submit never blindly shares a mutable texture with the game's next capture.\n\nPOSE-TO-IMAGE COHERENCE: StoreRenderEyePose records the camera pose that was actually used by an engine sequence and queues it by sequence ID. Captured MAIN/VRCAM images have serials; the submit path finds the image slot for the requested serial and rejects mismatched pairs. This fixes a subtle failure where a CPU serial could still match while the GPU had already overwritten the same shared texture with the next frame. OpenXR submission expects raw LOCAL-space poses while camera/game code uses a recentered base-relative pose, so StoreRenderEyePose composes the base pose back in before the projection layer is submitted; otherwise the compositor's timewarp delta would include the recenter transform and warp incorrectly. Reusable rule: bind image, render pose, eye identity and serial as one transaction, and make resource ownership agree with CPU bookkeeping.\n\nNATIVE STEREO / VRCAM: Stereo/SyncStereo.cpp drives the right eye through an authored entRenderToTextureCameraComponent. Selection data lives in CET's CyberpunkVRPort_Stereo/vrcam.json and generated archive assets; VRCAM is identified by the selected virtualCameraName CName hash from the view context. MAIN is not 'anything not VRCAM': structural MAIN-only nodes/view context are used, and unknown views are passed through. This prevents desktop window, reflection probes, braindance or other auxiliary views from being mutated as the left eye. The plugin also patches descriptor-heap capacity because the extra engine view consumes descriptors the flat game never budgeted.\n\nFRAME GRAPH / PER-VIEW STATE: Stereo/FrameGraph.cpp and the Stereo/* modules treat the second eye as a real engine view whose graph must be reconciled, not copied wholesale. The source observes/diffs view feature bits/upscaler groups, mirrors/forces required flags from MAIN and hooks the per-frame flag writer because REDengine regenerates view flags each frame. There are dedicated modules for NodeDispatch, ViewReuse, CullVisibility, Grading, VisionLayer, Hud, Dlss, Capture and profiling. Source comments document why indiscriminate cloning/re-run is wrong: sun-cascade atlases, foliage/SpeedTree wind, reflection march/shader clock and other resources are shared/once-per-frame structures, while environment override, composition/scanner tint, culling and view-dependent depth need to be valid for VRCAM itself. The implementation either lends/reuses shared results or reruns per-view work based on live evidence. Final color is picked up from the engine's node epilogue/acquire barrier, avoiding a mid-chain resource guess.\n\nDEPTH / CULLING / VISIBILITY: VRCAM keeps its own depth range; copying MAIN's near/depth setup was observed to empty clustered-light grids in interiors. Cull/visibility modules track the actual second-view candidates/materialization rather than assuming MAIN visibility is sufficient. The camera/view-context FOV scalars can be synchronized for culling, but source comments distinguish culling state from the actual render camera's projection and warn that scripted cameras can require different handling. This distinction is useful in any deferred renderer where visibility/light clustering reads a different view structure than the shader projection.\n\nDLSS / STREAMLINE / TEMPORAL STATE: Stereo/Dlss.cpp + Hooks/Ngx.cpp/NGX resolution hooks create/route a separate Streamline/DLSS viewport/context for VRCAM and force compatible feature-group selection when MAIN uses DLSS. Reusing one MAIN viewport for both eyes would make temporal/upscaler history cross-contaminate. The port also has dedicated shader constants and per-view dimensions. The broader lesson is that true second-view stereo must account for temporal history ownership, not just duplicate camera matrices.\n\nOPENXR COLOR/DEPTH SWAPCHAINS: Runtimes/OpenXRCapture.cpp creates per-eye D3D12 color swapchains with XR_SWAPCHAIN_USAGE_COLOR_ATTACHMENT_BIT | XR_SWAPCHAIN_USAGE_TRANSFER_DST_BIT, enumerates runtime-supported formats and chooses a compatible typed format. Optional depth submission creates OpenXR depth swapchains when XR_KHR_composition_layer_depth is supported; the capture path can add depth later without tearing down working color swapchains. Source comments move depth copies onto the game depth-writer queue/ordered path where necessary rather than forcing unsafe cross-queue waits. Mono/menu fallback copies the backbuffer to both eyes when the second view has no fresh usable frame, preventing a stale VRCAM image from surviving into loading/menu states.\n\nHUD / UI / OVERLAY: Cyberpunk's engine HUD is composited into the second eye with the same HUD shader logic; default placement is same pixel in both eyes (zero disparity) with optional finite-distance placement. F10 ImGui is rendered into both eye textures. Overlay code avoids draining/stalling the entire game queue: its allocator/upload resources are fenced directly. HUDitor now owns individual widget placement; the port's old shared-root HUD mover was removed, while the native plugin still performs the part an external HUD editor cannot: duplicating the engine HUD composite into the second eye. CET/redscript modules fit 16:9 interfaces to the square-ish VR view (phone, vehicle/radio lists, subtitles, dialogue, tutorial/welcome/loot), head-lock the world map, and handle braindance/scanner/device-camera presentation. A wrist-HUD module and F10 settings overlay are additional examples of changing spatial presentation while leaving underlying game state intact.\n\nCAMERA / GAME STATES: Hooks/LocateCamera.cpp, PatchCamera.cpp, FinalCamera.cpp, CameraPitch/Fov/BodyYaw files and Camera/CameraLink separate the engine/body heading from HMD view. Normal gameplay can suppress mouse Y so pitch belongs to the HMD while yaw/body policy remains independent. Physical body rotation optionally writes through the engine heading channel so mesh/collision/aim/movement turn with the user without feeding that yaw back into the headset view. Braindance uses one base + one head sample for both eyes. Surveillance/device/AV turret camera takeover keeps the native camera's aim/base, layers HMD movement on top and identifies special cameras through entity/buffer paths when they never reach the ordinary classifier. Cutscene sceneTier is obtained through RTTI; VRIK and certain view interventions suspend at configured cinematic tiers. Camera/pose snapshots use seqlocks/epochs to avoid combining camera(N-1) with entity(N) or head/hand samples from different phases.\n\nCOORDINATE SPACES: The implementation distinguishes OpenXR LOCAL/view/action spaces, recentered base-relative tracking, engine world/entity space, avatar/model space, camera/view space, controller grip/aim space, wrist/hand targets, weapon rig space, muzzle world space and OpenXR submission space. CharacterRig maps OpenXR position axes into model coordinates (representative helper mapping {x,-z,y}) and applies body/camera rotation explicitly rather than copying an XrPosef into a REDengine transform. VRIK view targets can be built from a latched view packet/body heading so hand positions remain anchored to the same camera/entity frame. The solve publishes solved palms/body bones/world view for CET/redscript consumers so those modules can reason in the right space instead of re-deriving controller->world transforms independently.\n\nVRIK INSTALL / RIG IDENTIFICATION: Hooks/AnimPose.cpp detours the engine's pose-apply function when script/natives arm it. It is intentionally not a boot registry hook because the script controls when the pose path is installed. The hook runs for many skeletons, so steady-state identification is by named bones/remembered track buffers rather than expensive VirtualQuery/statistical heuristics on every call. VrikArming/PoseRig/native helpers resolve the player rig and known bone indices. The weapon skeleton is recognized separately and supplies moving weapon-part slots to reload/aim modules.\n\nVRIK UPDATE ORDER / ANIMATION EPOCH: The pose apply detour can execute several times within one animation batch. Source instrumentation measures microsecond same-batch gaps vs frame/batch gaps. The current path can clock freshSolve by the engine animation batch instead of Present; fresh VRIK writes are cached for the relevant body/limb bones and replayed on remaining pose passes of the same epoch. This fixes phase errors where a Present-based clock could replay the previous arms while the camera/world had advanced. Two-hand pose capture deliberately executes BEFORE VR writes because it needs one frame of the game's authored hold with VRIK off. Ownership transitions such as HeadAim invalidate the solve cache so a pose from the previous owner cannot be replayed after control changes.\n\nFULL BODY / SPINE / LEGS: VRIK_PlaceBodyUnderHMD captures foot positions, derives physical squat/drop from HMD/head height with deadzone/filter/clamp, recenters the foot pair under the head anchor, moves hips to maintain captured torso height, performs multiple CCD passes through spine/neck to reduce head residual, solves both legs back to planted feet with forward-knee constraints, rotates the head bone to the VR camera, and publishes hips/spine/chest/neck/head + leg bone positions for interaction modules. The hips yaw is not produced by this solver; body yaw is a separate engine heading policy. That separation avoids mixing 'where the body root faces' with 'how the avatar bends to meet the HMD'.\n\nARMS / SHOULDERS / ELBOWS / WRISTS: VRIK_ScaleArmBonesFromRest captures rest translations and scales upper/forearm segments toward measured user arm length with safety clamps; arm length calibration therefore changes skeleton segment lengths rather than only multiplying controller offset. CharacterRig contains shoulder/clavicle anchoring/protraction for far reaches, anatomical bend-plane construction from body axes, cross-body correction, near-extension handling, reach stretching instead of permanently clamping the hand short, elbow/pole smoothing and twist distribution. Hand target builders convert the tracked pose into avatar/model space and apply wrist calibration. VRIK_ApplyHandStop publishes the raw target but can clamp the solved target when collision/interaction says the wrist cannot pass farther; reload can also lock wrist orientation during a specific hand-owned action.\n\nHAND SMOOTHING: Current source explicitly moved smoothing away from the raw XR controller pose. OpenXRManager::FlushHandsToShared applies a UEVR-style delta-time-based follow filter to the pose the ARMS consume, leaving other semantics/aim less contaminated by arm smoothing. Effective hand lerp can vary by weapon class: melee can follow faster to avoid a trailing blade, sniper slower to damp aim noise, while wheel grab effectively disables smoothing because steering needs immediate tracked positions. Reusable rule: filter the attachment/consumer that needs visual stability, not the global XR pose every subsystem depends on.\n\nFINGERS / HAND COLLISION: Finger rotations are not optical hand-skeleton data. They come from authored/captured interaction poses: two-hand support capture stores per-finger rotations; reload poses own finger states while a magazine/slide is held; smoking records its own grips; wheel driving can hand the arm/fingers back to authored animation. CyberpunkVRPort_HandCollision contains body/weapon capsule construction and a one-way hand-stop path, but current init.lua declares COLL_ON=false. Source comments explain prior body capsules using world-dynamic/queryable behavior could launch/push the player/vehicle; the basketball module explicitly forces VR body capsules non-queryable for character-controller sweeps while preserving simulation contacts. Registry must distinguish implemented collision research from an enabled default feature.\n\nWEAPON MUZZLE / PHYSICALRAY PRODUCTION PATH: Hooks/WeaponAim.cpp and docs/cp2077-hitscan-physicalray-muzzle.md establish gameEffectObjectProvider_PhysicalRay::Execute as the live firearm damage-ray provider. The production hook patches two exact evaluator callsites rather than globally detouring every PhysicalRay user. WaPlayerOwnedWeapon reads effect-data fields including weaponItemRecord/playerOwnedWeapon; this filters to the local player's firearm. The origin wrapper calls the original evaluator first, checks the current published muzzle against the native ray origin (3 m sanity bound for stale/foreign data), marks the same thread's invocation and writes the ray origin to the live muzzle. It snapshots both muzzle forward and the original game camera/cone centre at that instant.\n\nSHOTGUN-SPREAD-PRESERVING DIRECTION: The forward wrapper calls the original evaluator and only acts if the paired origin wrapper accepted the same thread/invocation. It computes a quaternion/rotation from the game's original cone centre (camera forward) to the live muzzle/barrel forward and applies that rotation to the raw ray the game already generated. Therefore raw==coneCenter for a single bullet becomes exact barrel forward, while six shotgun pellet rays retain their relative offsets. This was specifically derived after direct 'write barrel vector into every ray' collapsed shotgun spread. PhysicalRay counters are Interlocked because pellet workers can run concurrently. Native attack/damage/hit systems remain untouched; only the local player's ray origin/basis changes.\n\nWEAPON VISUAL AIM / CROSSHAIR: Weapon pose, muzzle pose and projectile ray are kept separate. The plugin publishes the real muzzle/forward from the weapon rig/provider; CET can hide Cyberpunk's flat crosshair when VR weapon aim is enabled. A barrel-dot overlay/debug path can render aim in both eyes. Legacy/diagnostic target/crosshair hooks remain in source, but the PhysicalRay path is the current high-confidence combat redirect and should be taught as such.\n\nTWO-HAND GRIP: Anim/TwoHandGrip.cpp captures one native two-hand animation frame with VRIK disabled, storing support-wrist transform relative to the primary/right wrist plus finger rotations to CyberpunkVR_TwoHandGrip_<weapon>.ini. At startup it scans all configs into memory; the supplied tree contains 47 files, including aliases/reskins. Runtime computes the support point from the primary wrist every pose pass. Proximity can preview fingers; an unspent/fresh grip edge locks the support hand. Grip ownership is explicitly spent/swallowed when reload/carry owns the same hand so one physical squeeze cannot activate two interactions. Aim correction is based on the error between tracked support hand and the captured support point, with spring/time smoothing, deadzone, maximum correction and leverage weighting. Long rifle baselines receive stronger authority; short pistol baselines get much less because centimetres of tracking noise imply huge angular changes. Support-hand finger pose is written before more specific reload finger layers so the most specific current interaction wins.\n\nRECOIL: Anim/Recoil.cpp uses a damped spring. A shot adds velocity impulse rather than teleporting spring position, so rapid shots accumulate naturally. Integration uses fixed 2 ms substeps (bounded) to reduce frame-rate-dependent peak behavior. Per-weapon/class data determines kick; rise/back travel can be scaled separately, and two-hand leverage/support state reduces recoil. RecoilSample applies direct kick only to the weapon-owning hand; the support hand inherits through its constraint/weapon relation instead of receiving a duplicate impulse. This is intentionally hand/weapon recoil, not camera recoil.\n\nADS / SIGHTS: Anim/AdsEyeAlign.cpp keeps a stable authored ADS base/pivot while re-anchoring alignment toward the configured/dominant VR eye so fine head movement does not drag the gun arbitrarily. AdsMuzzleStabilizer handles non-VRIK authored ADS raise motion so the muzzle direction does not drift just because the vanilla animation raises the weapon. Shaders/sight_reflex_ps.hlsl builds a sight tangent/bitangent/optical-axis frame, computes eye-to-pixel angular offset and places reticle UV by angle rather than glass screen coordinates. Glass/world dimensions are carried explicitly from vertex stage instead of relying on ddx/ddy under DLSS, reducing temporal derivative jitter. The reticle therefore behaves like a collimated optic aligned to the bore.\n\nPHYSICAL RELOAD CORE: mods/cet/CyberpunkVRPort_Reload/reload/reload.lua is a generic geometry/state layer. The native pose hook provides weapon-rig bone/part transforms and functions to write local transforms; CET derives live magazine well, slide, grip and weapon axes and converts world/hand data into weapon-local coordinates. States cover magazine grab/extraction/carry/insertion/return/drop and slide/palm grab/release/locked behavior. Magazine travel follows the physical well/path rather than only elapsed animation time; slide displacement is a scalar projection of real hand motion onto the weapon's live slide axis. Reload finger poses, wrist lock/hand-stop and ownership flags keep visual hands attached to the part they are manipulating. redscript/manualReload prevents flat auto-reload behavior from racing the physical path; ammo/action/audio transitions occur at interaction thresholds. The supplied tree contains 13 weapon-specific modules + index, matching the current README's tuned list.\n\nSPECIALIZED REVOLVER: Malorian Overture is not forced through the box-magazine abstraction. Its module represents cylinder/crane/rotation/hammer, six live/spent cases and a speedloader. Cases can remain seated and then slide/eject along their chamber axes under gravity/friction/orientation before transitioning to dropped-body behavior. This is a strong abstraction lesson: generic framework + per-mechanism adapter, not one condition-heavy universal reload algorithm.\n\nMOTION MELEE CONTACT: mods/cet/CyberpunkVRPort_Weapon/init.lua uses MELEE_SWING_SPEED=2.5 m/s of weapon motion relative to the player as the current hit gate; whoosh has separate 3.0/1.0/4.2 m/s thresholds. CET only calls PlayerPuppet::VRMeleeBladeHit while the physical swing gate is satisfied. redscript/CyberpunkVRPort_Melee models the weapon as a blade segment from grip along blade direction, takes the weapon's native attack Range but caps physical segment length to ~1.25 m to avoid the flat game's lunge range hitting neighbours, queries NPC target parts, samples body centreline at shin/hip/chest/neck/head heights, chooses the closest body within hitRadius, and applies a 0.1 s cooldown only for repeat hits on the same EntityID. Different NPCs can still be hit on one fast slash.\n\nNATIVE MELEE AUTHORITY: VRMeleeBladeHit reads the weapon's own EffectiveDamagePerHit/DamagePerHit (strong swing can scale it), creates the weapon's own Attack_GameEffect from its record, configures the effect's position/forward/range/fxPackage/playerOwnedWeapon and calls StartAttack. That native stage restores Cyberpunk's reaction, blood/material VFX, impact sound/impulse and normal combat pipeline behavior. Because native damage calculation can zero script-initiated melee in the wrong combat state, a DamageSystem::CalculateDamageVariants wrap re-injects the queued VR melee damage for player-instigated melee attacks before armor/crit/markers/ApplyDamage. If Attack_GameEffect creation fails, a bare gameHitEvent/QueueHitEvent is a fallback. A holster-intent timestamp suppresses melee for ~1 s while physically reaching to holster, preventing a false swing from putting the weapon in a busy state and dropping the unequip. WeaponUp redscript also blocks idle auto-lower/auto-unequip logic that expects button attacks to reset timers; real VR swings bypass that input.\n\nHOLSTERS: CET Holster uses solved/published wrist-to-right-hip, left-hip and back-anchor distances. Visual-holster mode classifies outfit item names/slots to decide where melee/ranged props are visually carried; simple mode maps fixed slots. Zones have proximity/cooldown and cross-body thresholds. The module suspends while a cigarette occupies the weapon slot, and wheel proximity pre-claims the grip so grabbing the steering wheel cannot equip a weapon. This is a clean semantic-intent arbitration layer above raw grip input.\n\nSMOKING / PROP INTERACTION: CET feeds left trigger + left/right grip states into redscript VRSmokeTick. redscript owns proximity using actual world cig-tip/lighter/head positions rather than controller-centre distance, attaches vanilla cigarette/lighter props through game slots, uses captured finger poses, has a mouth anchor for hands-free hold and invokes the game's stock EffectSpawner FX/audio. This is a reusable 'native prop + tracked spatial state + existing effects' pattern, not a weapon-only subsystem.\n\nDRIVING: Anim/WheelGrab.cpp detects hand proximity/grip at the authored wheel position. When grabbed, that arm is handed back to the game's driving animation and rest translations are restored so VRIK's arm scaling/protraction does not distort the wheel pose. Steering comes from tilt of the line through controller positions, with one-hand/two-hand variants, deadzone and lock angle; wheel hub proximity drives horn. Input arbitration lets the right trigger become weapon fire when a gun is drawn while a throttle latch/trim allows the vehicle to keep moving. This demonstrates per-state ownership transfer between tracked IK and authored animation rather than insisting VR IK always wins.\n\nBODY YAW / LOCOMOTION: Hooks/OnFootMoveXY.cpp and related heading/delta hooks support Game, HMD, LeftHand and RightHand movement sources. Hand-relative movement uses the OpenXR aim pose. Optional physical body rotation feeds headset yaw through the engine's own heading channel; free-look/deadzone/snap logic keeps view rotation separate. This lets mesh/collision/weapon/movement share a body heading while the HMD remains an additive view transform. No teleport/climbing implementation was identified in this snapshot; do not infer them from generic VR locomotion terminology.\n\nBASKETBALL / BODY PHYSICS RESEARCH: CyberpunkVRPort_Basketball is a separate interaction experiment worth preserving as generalized evidence. CET supplies exact dt and grip state; redscript owns a real entPhysicalMeshComponent ball, uses plugin native calls for rigid-body linear/angular velocity, and tops up only normal bounce energy because Cyberpunk/PhysX material restitution cannot reach a basketball-like target without globally changing materials. Tangential physics/spin is left to PhysX. Solved palm/model-space positions become hand anchors. VR body capsules are forced non-queryable so character-controller scene queries ignore them while simulation contacts can still occur — a useful collision-layer distinction for embodied VR.\n\nUI / WORLD MAP / DEVICE CAM / WRIST HUD: CET/redscript modules exist for braindance HUD, crosshair suppression, ForceFPP, world map/head lock, scanner HUD, device camera, loot UI and wrist HUD. The source separates native render duplication from script-side widget/layout policy. Menus intentionally bypass gameplay input remapping. HUDitor now handles individual widget transforms; the port's VR layout assets/config provide a starting layout while avoiding a second competing HUD-transform system.\n\nMULTIPLAYER / REPLICATION: NOT APPLICABLE in this source audit. Cyberpunk 2077's port here has no VR network pose packet, timestamp/interpolation protocol or remote player hand reconstruction. Do not reuse this case as evidence for multiplayer VR replication.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE FILES: CMakeLists.txt (single-DLL build/history); src/Main.cpp (RED4ext lifecycle/order); src/Hooks/Registry.cpp + src/Stereo/DetourRegistry.cpp (hook staging); include/Stereo/EngineRvas.hpp + include/Utils/AobScanner.hpp (version-sensitive RE surfaces); src/Runtimes/OpenXRManager.cpp, OpenXRFrameLoop.cpp, OpenXRCapture.cpp, OpenXRPresent.cpp (runtime/actions/timing/swapchains/pose-image binding); src/Stereo/SyncStereo.cpp, FrameGraph.cpp, NodeDispatch.cpp, ViewReuse.cpp, CullVisibility.cpp, Grading.cpp, Hud.cpp, Dlss.cpp, VisionLayer.cpp, Capture.cpp (native second view); src/Hooks/LocateCamera.cpp/PatchCamera.cpp/FinalCamera.cpp/OnFootMoveXY.cpp/XInput.cpp (camera/input/locomotion); src/Hooks/AnimPose.cpp + src/Anim/CharacterRig.cpp (full-body solve/update order); src/Hooks/WeaponAim.cpp + docs/cp2077-hitscan-physicalray-muzzle.md (PhysicalRay); src/Anim/TwoHandGrip.cpp, Recoil.cpp, AdsEyeAlign.cpp, AdsMuzzleStabilizer.cpp; src/Shaders/sight_reflex_ps.hlsl; mods/cet/CyberpunkVRPort_Reload/reload/*; mods/redscript/CyberpunkVRPort_Melee/vrport_melee.reds; Holster/Smoking/Basketball/WorldMap modules; docs/vrik-map.md and the cp2077-* reverse-engineering notes.\n\nREUSABLE PATTERNS FOR AN LLM: (1) use an engine-owned secondary camera/view when possible, but audit the entire per-view/frame-global render graph; (2) explicitly identify views instead of classifying 'everything else' as an eye; (3) bind render pose + eye image + frame serial and preserve that transaction through OpenXR submission; (4) let OpenXR/compositor prediction own late head motion instead of stacking arbitrary custom prediction; (5) solve full-body IK once per engine animation epoch and replay it to repeated pose passes; (6) filter the visual hand attachment/arm consumer, not the global tracking pose; (7) keep weapon visual pose, muzzle, optical axis, native spread cone and combat event separate; (8) preserve shotgun spread by rotating the native cone rather than overwriting rays; (9) make physical reload a generic geometry/state core with per-weapon mechanism data; (10) detect physical melee intent spatially but execute the game's native attack/damage pipeline; (11) use explicit ownership/arbitration for grips shared by reload, foregrip, wheel, holster and props; (12) centralize direct RVAs and byte validations, log every hook result and fail closed on unknown builds.\n\nLIMITATIONS / CONFIDENCE: High confidence for the source-described current snapshot. Build-specific addresses are verified in-source for Cyberpunk 2.31, not universal. README claims were not promoted when current code contradicted legacy text: AER pair lock/custom motion prediction are recorded as removed. Hand collision is recorded as disabled by default because source says so. OpenXR skeletal hand tracking/haptic output are not claimed because their required API symbols/actions are absent. The public web index can lag the supplied source restructuring, so this audit treats the user-provided snapshot as the primary current code artifact and uses the current public README as an independent feature/architecture cross-check." ,
  "seed_peak_case": "Source-audit: BepInEx is loaded through the winhttp.dll proxy and VR controller state is converted to a virtual Xbox controller through ViGEmBus. This deliberately avoids requiring the closed Unity game to understand XR input. The documented Vulkan/D3D12 × OpenVR/OpenXR matrix is a reminder that a Unity VR mod can fail at the runtime/graphics-backend boundary even when the gameplay plugin itself loads correctly. This entry is primarily a compatibility/input-translation pattern, not a native hand-object implementation. Deep pass: This is a strong example of the 'XR on one side, legacy input on the other' architecture. The VR layer can own stereo/tracking while ViGEmBus presents a conventional Xbox controller to the closed Unity game, allowing locomotion/actions to work without native XR object access. The important engineering consequence is that physical hand presence is not automatically available just because 6DoF tracking exists; a separate object/weapon layer is required if the game does not expose controller-aware gameplay hooks. The documented runtime/render-API matrix should be treated as a compatibility table, not a generic Unity rule.",
  "seed_sptvr_case": "SOURCE AUDIT UPDATED: SPT-VR 1.3.0 C# source inspected. This mod includes substantial rendering patches: OpenVRLoader startup, per-eye DLSS/FSR wrappers, shared jitter and subsystem culling fixes. Use the detailed case sections and focused SPT-VR methodology entries for exact implementation information.",
  "seed_shipwright_case": "Source-audit status: VR fork is source-level, but the cited public upstream Shipwright repository is the main engine/decompilation reference. The key transferable property is the renderer boundary: once game logic and rendering are buildable source, head pose and eye projection can be introduced directly into the renderer instead of intercepting GPU calls. The registry's current VR-fork-specific limitations (DX11/4:3/MSAA constraints and HUD alignment) should be treated as fork-specific rather than properties of Shipwright itself. Deep pass: The source-level advantage is that the VR fork can own the renderer’s camera/projection state directly, but its documented DX11/4:3/MSAA/HUD constraints are properties of the VR fork, not proof that Shipwright itself requires them. The right abstraction for future source ports is an XR render target layer at the renderer boundary plus a separate 2D/UI composition layer. Keeping those paths separate makes it possible to fix HUD convergence without destabilizing world stereo.",
  "seed_outlast_case": "Code-audit status: methodology remains unverified from the public end-user documentation. The release is a drop-in binary and the cited repository exists, but this registry should not invent the actual hook point, stereo path or camera implementation until those source files are inspected. Keep this as a deliberate 'known existence, unknown method' record. Deep pass: Keep this entry explicitly low-confidence. The existence of a source repository is not the same as a verified source audit; until the relevant source files are inspected, the registry should not claim whether the mod uses a UE3 stereo path, Present hook, camera patch, or external compositor. The useful lesson is methodological: mark unknown hook surfaces as unknown instead of filling them with the most likely Unreal technique.",
  "seed_halomcc_case": "Source-audit: the current project is a native C++20 OpenXR mod with a launcher/config layer and title-specific hooks. Its public state documents per-eye stereo, runtime-provided eye poses, arm IK, motion aiming, melee/grenades and a room-fixed 3D theatre for cutscenes. The source/release notes also show campaign transitions detach stale title-specific stereo state and recreate hooks when returning to a campaign; this is a useful lifecycle pattern for multi-game executables. Cutscene handling deliberately preserves authored shot direction rather than forcing gameplay camera behavior into pre-rendered/authored sequences. Deep pass: The multi-title lifecycle is the key engineering detail: MCC is one executable family containing multiple title-specific rendering/gameplay states, so VR hooks cannot assume one permanent camera/renderer configuration. Campaign transitions need explicit teardown/reinitialization of title-specific state, and authored/pre-rendered sequences are safer on a stable theater surface than by forcing gameplay camera logic through them. This pattern generalizes to launchers/compilations that switch engines or content packs without restarting the process.",
  "seed_sw1racer_case": "Source-audit: the decompilation project is intentionally incremental, not matching compilation. `data_symbols.syms` holds recovered globals and `src/generated/hook_generated.c` is generated from the hook template; the HD replacement layer uses a dinput hook and the PCVR fork adds OpenXR to that partially understood codebase. This makes the project a concrete example of selective source replacement: only reimplemented/verified functions need to be swapped, while the untouched binary code remains the reference implementation. VR should therefore be introduced first at recovered renderer/input seams rather than waiting for 100% decompilation. Deep pass: The incremental decompilation architecture is particularly useful for LLM-assisted reverse engineering because the hook template marks exactly which reimplemented functions replace original behavior. VR work should begin at verified renderer/input seams and keep the original binary path available as a reference/fallback. That lets a port progress without pretending the entire engine has been reconstructed; each new VR hook can be gated behind the corresponding recovered function rather than depending on global 100%-decompilation parity.",
  "seed_gtaiv_case": "Source-audit status: the key enabling layer is DXVK: GTA IV's D3D9 calls become Vulkan, then the ASI/MinHook layer operates on the translated path and submits to SteamVR. The current implementation intentionally starts with mono submission, proving compositor integration before stereo. Experimental AER and TrueStereo paths demonstrate a separate problem: stereo depth can work while head rotation still exposes look-around blur/temporal mismatch. This is a useful staged-development pattern for old D3D9 titles. Deep pass: The mono-first development path is worth preserving: first prove that a D3D9→DXVK→Vulkan→SteamVR chain can submit a stable image, then introduce alternate-eye or true-stereo rendering. The look-around blur reported by both experimental stereo paths shows that stereo submission alone does not guarantee correct temporal pose: the eye image, game camera pose and compositor pose must remain associated closely enough that head rotation does not reveal stale geometry. This makes frame timing/pose ownership a first-class problem, not a final polish step.",
  "seed_dxvk_bridge_pattern": "Source-audit: DXVK is the bridge, not the VR runtime. Replacing d3d9/d3d11 with DXVK moves the hook surface from legacy Direct3D to Vulkan, where a mod can intercept swapchain submission and then hand frames to OpenVR/OpenXR. The trade is a second renderer layer with its own shader/state/cache compatibility surface. This pattern is especially useful when the original D3D API is too awkward to integrate with the desired compositor. Deep pass: DXVK should be treated as a translation boundary with its own lifecycle and compatibility matrix. A VR hook should not assume that a Vulkan swapchain means the game itself is Vulkan-native; resource ownership, synchronization and color/depth semantics are inherited from the translation layer. If stereo requires re-rendering, the mod still has to move the engine camera before the relevant D3D draw sequence, not merely duplicate the final Vulkan image.",
  "seed_gtasa_de_case": "Source-audit status: the registry currently cites only the Nexus entry, not a source repository. Treat the UEVR/plugin behavior as profile-level rather than as a verified source implementation until the actual plugin/profile files are added. The useful architectural point is that a generic UEVR stereo/camera layer can be extended with per-game locomotion, driving, jetpack and interaction logic without modifying the Unreal engine itself. Deep pass: Because the current registry source citation is only a Nexus listing, keep this entry at profile-level confidence. The useful UEVR pattern is still valid: generic UE stereo/camera support can be extended with a game-specific controller/vehicle/jetpack layer, but exact implementation details belong in the plugin/profile source before being treated as code guidance. A future audit should replace the Nexus-only source with the actual public plugin/profile files if available.",
  "seed_re3_pattern": "Source-audit: reVC is a real reversed source tree and exposes platform/rendering seams directly. Its renderer abstraction can target original RenderWare D3D8 or the open librw implementation (D3D9/OpenGL/GLES), and platform code owns camera/raster/update and input initialization functions. This is exactly the source-level seam VR needs: add XR camera state and stereo presentation at the renderer/platform boundary instead of patching an opaque executable. The Quest fork demonstrates how the same source can be moved to Vulkan multiview. Deep pass: The important source-level seam is the renderer abstraction: re3/reVC-style projects separate gameplay from a modernized rendering backend, so VR can enter where camera state, raster creation, frame presentation and platform input are already centralized. This is safer than scattering eye transforms across individual draw calls. When porting another reversed title, first identify the equivalent camera update and renderer-present boundaries, then add XR state there and leave game logic unaware of the compositor where possible.",
  "seed_blackmesavr_case": "Source-audit: the public tree is organized around L4D2VR-derived native code, an OpenXR helper, VR configuration and a dedicated source directory. The README confirms true stereo, 6DOF controls, weapon wheel, crowbar melee and motion-controller menus. The most transferable lesson is technique reuse across close engine families: start from a proven Source-engine hook architecture, then re-identify the engine-specific camera/render/input seams instead of assuming offsets or object layouts transfer unchanged. Deep pass: The L4D2VR lineage demonstrates a practical technique-transfer rule: copy architecture, not addresses. Source-engine derivatives can share broad lifecycle ideas—DLL load, engine interface discovery, render/camera interception, OpenXR presentation—but Black Mesa still requires its own camera/render/input seams. The current WIP crowbar and scope limitations also show that stereo/6DOF can be stable while individual gameplay systems remain independently incomplete.",
  "seed_sh3vr_case": "Source-audit status: the public project uses a 32-bit dinput8 proxy plus a 64-bit host for heavier XR work, while depending on separate PC-fix/camera projects. The weapon INI is a practical calibration interface: per-weapon position/rotation/scale and firearm AimPitch/AimYaw can be hot-reloaded. That means the physical controller pose is treated as a stable base transform and weapon-specific offsets correct the model/aim relationship, rather than baking one universal controller offset into the core. Melee remains a separate collision/registration problem and is explicitly less reliable. Deep pass: The two-process design separates the 32-bit game-side compatibility/injection boundary from heavier 64-bit XR work, while the weapon INI provides a calibration surface without recompiling the core. That is a useful pattern when a game’s coordinate conventions differ per weapon: keep one canonical controller pose, then apply data-driven local position/rotation/scale and aim corrections per asset. Melee should remain a separate subsystem with its own hit-registration validation rather than being assumed solved by controller tracking alone.",
  "seed_bioshockvr_case": "Source-audit: the current repo documents a thin xinput1_3.dll export-forwarding shim, a game-agnostic core, and executable-name adapter dispatch. MinHook handles D3D11 Present/ResizeBuffers and engine hooks; OpenXR/OpenVR are separated behind the VR layer. The codebase treats each game as an adapter because shared engine lineage does not guarantee shared offsets or camera semantics. Hand/weapon transforms are driven through engine-side bone/object seams, while the HUD is separately captured/composited. The project also uses a simulated 32-bit XR runtime to automate head/hand/controller pose testing and per-eye output capture, which is a valuable test-harness pattern for VR injection work. Deep pass: The current source is a particularly strong reference for multi-title adapters. The thin xinput1_3 proxy only loads the mod; the core owns MinHook/D3D11/OpenXR/UI/stereo; an executable-name registry selects the per-game adapter. That keeps BioShock 1/2/Infinite offsets and camera semantics isolated even though the games share lineage. The roadmap also shows an incremental test strategy—inject/log/overlay first, then renderer hooks, then stereo, then camera/aim and interaction—which is a good order for any closed 32-bit title.",
  "seed_tpvr_case": "Source-audit: TPVR exposes `vr_main.cpp`, `vr_stereo_render.hpp`, `vr_swing_detector.hpp`, `vr_xr_bootstrap.hpp` and `vr_xr_submit.hpp` directly. The swing detector is engine-agnostic: raw controller position deltas produce speed, distance, hysteresis and cooldown checks before a dominant-axis classification. The game then receives a legacy A-button press/held flag for the detected swing, avoiding a new gameplay input path. Rendering uses OpenXR with D3D12 texture interop through Aurora/Dawn, so the eye image is copied into OpenXR swapchain textures rather than requiring Aurora to become an XR renderer itself. Deep pass: The swing detector is a clean reusable physical-input primitive: raw position deltas become velocity, distance and dominant-axis classification, with hysteresis/cooldown preventing duplicate hits. The detected gesture is then converted into the game’s existing A-button state for one frame, preserving native combat semantics. On the rendering side, Aurora/Dawn remains the renderer while OpenXR owns the swapchain, so the integration uses D3D12 external/shared textures instead of forcing the engine renderer to become XR-native. This separation is valuable for other WebGPU/Dawn-backed ports.",
  "seed_primedgun_case": "Source-audit: PrimedGun is a Dolphin ReduX source fork, so XR is integrated into the emulator's own input/rendering pipeline rather than injected into Metroid Prime. The public build exposes OpenXR controller bindings, tracked visor/arm-cannon behavior, height calibration and an in-headset settings layer. The arm cannon is game-specific integration on top of emulator-level VR; do not generalize it as a generic Dolphin hand/IK system. Deep pass: PrimedGun is best understood as emulator-level VR plus game-specific interaction, not a generic Dolphin VR API. The fork owns OpenXR input/rendering, while Metroid Prime-specific logic maps visor gestures, arm-cannon pose and targeting onto emulated game state. The mod also exposes height/cannon calibration and an in-headset settings menu, showing that calibration state belongs in the VR layer rather than being baked into game coordinates.",
  "seed_valve_unityxr_pattern": "Source-audit: Valve's Unity XR plugin has a managed Unity package plus a native XRSDKOpenVR provider under Providers. The provider exposes tracked camera pose through Unity's TrackedPoseDriver; real controller interaction is supplied by the separate SteamVR Unity Input System package. This is useful for source-access Unity projects, but it does not solve XR integration for a shipped closed binary because XR Plugin Management cannot be retroactively enabled without another injection/modding layer. Deep pass: The provider is a real Unity XRSDK native plugin: CMake builds XRSDKOpenVR from Providers/OpenVRProviderContext, OpenVRSystem, Display and Input plus Unity XR provider interfaces. The package is therefore a useful source reference for how Unity asks a runtime for display, input and pose data, but it is not a retrofitting mechanism for a shipped binary. In a closed Unity game, a BepInEx/native mod still needs to discover the game camera and gameplay objects and either instantiate compatible XR components or implement an equivalent runtime bridge.",
  "seed_botwbettervr_case": "Source-audit: BetterVR combines two independent hook domains. A Vulkan layer intercepts Cemu's renderer, while PowerPC patches operate inside the emulated Wii U game to alter camera/hand/attack behavior. Because the compositor path is D3D12, the captured Vulkan output is bridged into a D3D12 context for XR submission. The melee detector is intentionally not just 'button on swing': controller motion is scored against expected swing/stab conditions, with a button fallback. This separation—graphics hook outside, gameplay patch inside the emulated title—is highly reusable for emulator-based VR. Deep pass: BetterVR demonstrates a two-domain hook architecture that is easy to miss: the external Vulkan layer owns emulator rendering interception and OpenXR submission, while PowerPC patches own the emulated game’s camera, hand models and attack semantics. The melee detector is intentionally independent of the renderer and can fall back to a legacy attack input. For emulator-based ports, this suggests keeping graphics interception and emulated-game patches as separate modules with an explicit synchronization point per frame rather than mixing them in one hook callback.",
  "seed_uevr_lua_vs_native_pattern": "Source-audit: uevrlib's public examples cover controllers, hands, attachments, reticules, UI, interaction/laser pointers, IK, gestures, gunstock and pawn access. The library is loaded from a per-game scripts folder, so common VR interaction primitives can be shared without recompiling the base injector. Native plugins are the escalation path when Lua cannot reach the required engine object/render state. For an LLM generating a new UEVR profile, the important abstraction is: acquire controller pose → transform into game/world space → resolve actor/bone/attachment → apply transform or synthesize legacy input → calibrate through profile UI/config. Deep pass: The current UEVR source confirms that the native backend is already split into LuaLoader, PluginLoader, UObjectHook and VR components, so Lua/native is not just a community convention—it matches the base framework’s own modular architecture. uevrlib’s IK module can copy a selected game mesh into a PoseableMeshComponent and manipulate bones for Two Bone IK, while its examples cover attachments, interaction, reticules and hands. That makes the recommended escalation ladder concrete: use profile Lua/helpers for per-game transforms and interaction, UObjectHook/reflection when object-level access is enough, and a compiled plugin when renderer/native-function access is required.",
  "seed_di2uevr_case": "Source-audit update (2026-10-10, supplied DI2_UEVR v1.0.6 snapshot): VERIFIED FROM THE SUPPLIED DI2_UEVR v1.0.6 SOURCE SNAPSHOT (main.lua version string; supplied archive audited 2026-10-10). DI2_UEVR is a composition layer over bundled uevrlib modules: UI/montage/interaction/attachments/reticule/pawn/remap/input/gestures/gunstock/scope/IK/collision plus game-specific melee, curveball, portable, ranged, weapon-FX, flashlight, body and Alyx-wheel helpers. The source now exposes several important authority-transfer seams beyond the earlier melee/IK audit. Ranged hitscan is redirected from `RaycastLocalCameraFallbackToMuzzle` to `RaycastFromMuzzle`, while launch adjustment and cached auto-aim are suppressed so the native fire path follows tracked weapon geometry. Curveballs use a two-hook pose transaction: `PerformQuickThrow` captures the attachment pose while the weapon exists; `ServerRequestSpawnProjectileActors` later rewrites origin/direction/camera/cosmetic/target fields after the weapon object has disappeared. Thrown melee similarly patches `ServerRequestThrowItem` camera/projectile/visual transforms and target data from the active tracked weapon. Portable throwing samples hand-minus-HMD velocity to reject locomotion, supports a physical flick mode and an aimed/native mode, temporarily suppresses collision during attachment, then detaches and hands velocity back to Unreal physics. Attachment ownership is explicitly reversible across mesh/socket, controller and raw UEVR motion-controller state; `AttachParent` is treated as source of truth, reattach can be suppressed by another subsystem, and a three-failure circuit breaker stops broken objects from retrying forever. Per-attachment data composes scope/laser/weight/two-hand/melee/grip-animation capabilities. Weighted melee remains the verified SpringArm rotation-lag model; physical melee remains gesture → native attack request → native sweep component with montage arbitration. UI and animation are policy systems rather than scattered hacks: `ui.lua` resolves widget/game/custom states by priority; `montage.lua` emits priority state for hands/arms/body/input/accessories; main.lua bridges non-UMG Scaleform menus into normalized `scaleform_ui_change` events and has explicit cutscene/counter/knockdown ownership handoffs. The Alyx-style wheel projects tracked hand displacement onto the HMD right/up plane and injects the result as native right-stick XInput with sector haptics. Physical scopes use SceneCapture2D + 1024x1024 RGBA16f RenderTarget2D ocular lenses and can deactivate by HMD proximity. Shared line tracing deduplicates camera/HMD/controller/custom traces for reticule/laser/interaction; reticule target acquisition is decoupled from headset-space display and supports eye-dominance offsets. Controller collision proxies use swept Unreal components and return hand velocity with HitResult. Body presentation can clone the first-person pose into a render-only PoseableMeshComponent, hide arm bones there and suppress the source render pass. Body yaw inference uses head/hand alignment and synchronized angular motion; input.lua additionally supports decoupled yaw, game-owned-yaw mirroring, roomscale pawn follow with sweep/rebase, multi-source aim, body/eye animation compensation and stereo-callback scheduling that avoids applying frame-global pawn transforms twice per eye. Hand poses are semantic-input and attachment aware. Gunstock calibration is intentionally applied to hands/IK rather than the weapon attachment hierarchy. Flashlight tracking reuses the native ScopedSpotlight via UEVR motion-controller state. Weapon particle FX are reparents of game-owned PSCs, not recreated effects. Configuration is reactive/persistent, and interaction control modes switch complete remap profiles while body-zone gestures synthesize existing XInput actions. SOURCE BOUNDARY: `plugins/uevr_utils.dll` is present only as a binary; its native implementation remains UNKNOWN. AFW/UEVR config files are configuration evidence, not source proof of UEVR renderer internals. Reusable top-level architecture: tracked runtime state → semantic aim/body/input policy → hand/IK/attachment presentation → physical gesture/trace/collision/physics intent → narrow rewrite/native gameplay authority → priority-based animation/UI/cutscene arbitration → visual feedback (scope/reticule/laser/FX).",
  "pattern_uevr_utils_lib": "Source-audit (2026-09-06): Full uevr_utils.lua surface verified. Safe navigation: getValid(object, propertyPath) and validate_object prevent nil crashes; hierarchy DSL parseHierarchyString/getObjectFromDescriptor supports strings such as Pawn.Mesh(Arm).Glove. Timing: setTimeout/setInterval, doOnce(Once.EVER|Once.PER_LEVEL) with fault-tolerant error() retry and :reset(), named createDeferral/updateDeferral/destroyDeferral cooldowns. Math: reusable/cached vector/quat/rotator/color structs. Lifecycle: spawn_actor, destroy_actor, create_component_of_class, cloneComponent, createPoseableMeshFromSkeletalMesh, set_component_relative_transform. Input: isButtonPressed/press/unpress, isButtonDoubleTapped, doDoubleTap (queued after other handlers so remaps cannot clear). Camera: fadeCamera with hard/soft lock, set_2D_mode. Traces: getCleanHitResult + getLineTraceHitResult (channel, complex, ignore, min/max distance). Hooks: hook_function with optional native flag; automatic ClientRestart → on_client_restart callback. Also: cvars, asset load, deepCopy, FGameplayTag helpers, JSON dump, logging levels. This is the de-facto shared foundation referenced by DI2, HL body-IK and other uevrlib profiles.",
  "pattern_gesture_melee_native_sweep": "Source-audit update (2026-10-10): VERIFIED/RE-VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`scripts/helpers/melee.lua`, `scripts/libs/gestures.lua`, 2026-10-10). Gesture/swing callbacks call the DI2 melee helper, which resolves the equipped melee item and native sweep component, can invoke `RequestBeginStandardAttack` or `RequestBeginHeavyAttack`, links the weapon path, activates `SetSweepActive(true)`, and terminates the sweep through explicit swing-end/deferral logic. Montage handling suppresses known attack montage presentation while the tracked controller owns physical swing motion; gameplay consequences stay on the native attack/sweep/damage path. The newer snapshot also cleanly separates thrown-melee authority: `ServerRequestThrowItem` is patched only for tracked spawn/target transforms, so swing combat and thrown combat remain distinct native transactions.",
  "pattern_weighted_springarm_weapon": "Source-audit update (2026-10-10): VERIFIED/RE-VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`scripts/libs/attachments.lua`, 2026-10-10). Weight is implemented as transform response, not game-physics mass: a zero-length Unreal `SpringArmComponent` is inserted between hand socket and melee weapon, camera collision/translation lag are disabled, rotation lag is enabled, and configured kg maps to `CameraRotationLagSpeed` (1 kg→20, 20 kg→10). Live config updates reconfigure or create/destroy the spring arm. Because scope/laser/muzzle/FX remain children of the weapon, they inherit the same weighted motion automatically. `AttachParent` is used as hierarchy truth during reconciliation.",
  "pattern_two_bone_ik_copypoose_arbitration": "Source-audit update (2026-10-10): VERIFIED/RE-VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`scripts/libs/ik.lua`, `scripts/libs/hands.lua`, `scripts/libs/animation.lua`, `scripts/main.lua`, 2026-10-10). The two-bone arm solver supports controller/accessory/socket targets, pole/twist/wrist controls and hand animation. It shares a poseable skeleton with authored animation, so the library explicitly arbitrates `CopyPoseFromSkeletalComponent` vs IK ownership and can restore captured initial transforms after animation to prevent residual montage pose. Accessory cycle-breaking and lifecycle cleanup prevent circular parenting. DI2’s `regenerateHands` detaches grip attachments before destroying/recreating hands/IK, proving the solver is integrated into an explicit ownership lifecycle rather than being a post-render bone patch."
,

  "pattern_di2_muzzle_authority_retarget": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/ranged.lua`: `applyMuzzleAimToProjectileSpawner`, `applyMuzzleAimToFireMode`, `applyRangedWeaponMuzzleAim`. Dead Island 2 defaults hitscan to `RaycastLocalCameraFallbackToMuzzle`, which follows FP camera/body yaw + aim pitch. The mod selects `ETargetPointRaycastType_RaycastFromMuzzle = 1`, preserves the game-owned projectile/fire-mode path, allows a full 180° muzzle alignment tolerance, zeroes `LaunchAdjustmentHandler.MaxAdjustScreenSpacePercentage`, and disables cached player auto-aim results. This is a narrow authority transfer: tracked attachment code owns the muzzle transform; Dead Island still owns trigger state, projectile/raycast execution, ammo and damage. Use this pattern when the engine exposes a native muzzle-origin mode but camera aim-assist would otherwise re-center VR shots.",
  "pattern_di2_deferred_curveball_pose_snapshot": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/curveball.lua`: `captureCurveballAim`, `buildCurveballAimData`, `applyCurveballAimToSpawnParams`, hooks for `PerformQuickThrow` and `ServerRequestSpawnProjectileActors`. The file explicitly notes that `PerformQuickThrow` still has the weapon while spawn does not. The profile therefore stores a minimal `(location, rotation)` snapshot, derives forward at consume time, writes both gameplay and cosmetic transforms, sets a far vector target and marks it valid, then clears the snapshot. This is a reusable temporal-context pattern for VR hooks: capture transient controller/weapon state where it still exists; consume it at the later authoritative function instead of trying to reconstruct a missing object.",
  "pattern_di2_native_melee_throw_transform_rewrite": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/melee.lua`: `getHandForMeleeItem`, `getMeleeThrowAimLocationRotation`, `getMeleeThrowTargetLocation`, `buildMeleeThrowAimData`, `applyMeleeThrowAimToThrowItem`, hook on `DIPlayerCharacter.ServerRequestThrowItem`. Target acquisition can use the game-world line trace with a 10-unit minimum hit distance and 10,000-unit maximum; otherwise it falls back to a far point along weapon forward. The request receives camera, projectile and visual transforms from the same tracked pose plus vector/rotator target validity. This keeps the server/native throw state machine authoritative while moving only spatial authority to VR.",
  "pattern_di2_hmd_relative_throw_sampler": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/physics.lua`: `createThrowSampler`, `resetThrowSampler`, `updateThrowSampler`, `getThrowVelocity`. The source comment is explicit: “Differentiate hand-minus-HMD so locomotion does not look like a swing.” The sampler uses a ring buffer, `minMoveSq`, `dropIdle`, `maxSpeedSq` hitch rejection, `dropSpeed/fullSpeed` effort scaling, `releaseSamples`, assumed release height and gravity. It preserves HMD velocity separately, allowing final launch behavior to reintroduce world motion deliberately rather than accidentally. This pattern generalizes to throws, melee and gestures whenever player locomotion contaminates controller world velocity.",
  "pattern_di2_portable_release_handoff": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/portables.lua`: `beginPortableAttachNoCollision`, `updateHoldSampling`, `applyPortableDrop`, throw/drop state; `scripts/libs/physics.lua`: sampler and velocity application. The carry mesh is set to `NoCollision` while attachment settles, then on release detaches from its parent, may be placed at the last sampled mesh world location, restores `QueryAndPhysics`, ignores pawn collision, and receives the computed velocity. The design prevents immediate self-collision/attachment fighting during ownership transfer. Use this pattern for held physics props where the VR hand should control presentation while Unreal physics should resume authority cleanly on release.",
  "pattern_di2_dual_throw_modes": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/portables.lua` defines `AIM_OFFSET_YAW`, `AIM_OFFSET_PITCH`, `THROW_LOFT_PITCH`, tracks left-trigger state and stores pending throw aim pose. The physical branch uses `physics.getThrowVelocity`; the aimed branch composes controller rotation and uses game/native speed where available. The vanilla path temporarily substitutes PlayerCameraManager POV pitch/yaw at the throw boundary. This is a good example of transient semantic input bridging: do not make the entire camera follow the controller just because one legacy action reads camera aim.",
  "pattern_di2_reversible_attachment_ownership": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/attachments.lua` attachment metadata stores parent, socket, original transform, state and optional weight spring arm. `detach()` removes raw motion-controller state if necessary, detaches according to policy, restores relative transform and reattaches to the cached socket. The reconciliation code checks `attachment.AttachParent` directly and supports `attachment_suppress_mesh_reattach` so accessory/reload systems can temporarily claim ownership. This prevents circular ownership and lost weapons when the native game reparents components asynchronously.",
  "pattern_di2_attachment_retry_circuit_breaker": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/attachments.lua`: `runGripUpdate`, `failedAttachments`, `maxAttachFails`. The updater differentiates right and left, selects `attachToRawController` when no hand mesh exists or `attachToMesh` otherwise, resets the failure count on success, and refuses further attempts after three failures. This is especially valuable in UObject-heavy mods where invalid/recreated components are common: a reconciliation loop needs a circuit breaker to avoid repeated exceptions/perf spikes.",
  "pattern_di2_data_driven_attachment_capabilities": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/attachments.lua` reads and updates attachment properties through its parameter/config layer (`updateAttachmentIsScoped`, `updateAttachmentIsTwoHanded`, `updateAttachmentIsMelee`, `updateAttachmentHasLaser`, `updateAttachmentWeight`). During active grip it creates/destroys capability modules and broadcasts grip-animation state. The code therefore avoids one handler per gun. This pattern scales VR weapon support: generic modules implement scope/laser/weight/pose behavior; per-weapon data selects and calibrates them.",
  "pattern_di2_hmd_plane_radial_wheel_xinput": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/alyx_wheel.lua`: `getHmdScreenBasis`, `setWheelState`, `updateHandMovement`, `triggerHapticClick`, pre-input callback. It supports Kismet basis vectors with a manual Unreal Euler fallback, configurable 2 cm deadzone and 6.5 cm full-scale radius, eight 45° sectors and UEVR `trigger_haptic_vibration`. The normalized vector is injected as XInput right-stick state only while the Scaleform weapon wheel is open; B/Y-style modifier behavior provides an escape hatch to native stick cycling. This is a strong flat-UI retrofit pattern for radial menus.",
  "pattern_di2_existing_flashlight_mc_rebind": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/flashlight.lua`: `FlashlightLocation`, `flashlightHandFromLocation`, `applyFlashlightOffsets`, `attachFlashlightToController`. The head is represented by hand index 2; left/right use `Handed.Left/Right`. Position/rotation offsets come from reactive config, and the helper rebinds periodically/after level changes. The game still owns flashlight existence/state; VR only replaces transform authority.",
  "pattern_di2_pose_clone_body_only_rendering": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/body.lua`: `createRenderMesh`, `ensureRenderMesh`, `updateRenderMesh`, `setSourceRendering`, `stopPoseableBody`. The clone is `bOnlyOwnerSee`, non-colliding and shadowless, with the source mesh retained as the animation authority. This creates a body-only first-person presentation without rewriting the native AnimInstance. The source is explicitly restored on stop/reset, making the method reversible.",
  "pattern_di2_priority_ui_policy_bus": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/ui.lua`: `setCustomState`, `updateStateIfHigherPriority`, `updateUIState`, `executeUEVRCallbacksWithPriorityResult`. Widget/game/custom policies carry both value and priority. Cutscene/pause/character-hidden callbacks force re-evaluation. The result is a small policy bus: modules consume resolved semantic state instead of inspecting every widget class themselves. This is a robust way to prevent “menu opened while montage/cutscene is active” ordering bugs.",
  "pattern_di2_montage_priority_overlay": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/montage.lua`: priority handling around `updateStateIfHigherPriority`/accessory state, montage change tracking and callback emission. Each configured value has a companion priority; accessory GUID/state is handled similarly. Consumers can therefore arbitrate montage requests against UI/game-state requests. This is particularly useful in UE games where full-body finishers/reloads/knockdowns temporarily need authored animation to own bones or input.",
  "pattern_di2_scaleform_normalized_ui_bridge": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/main.lua`: `hookEquippedItemsWheel`, 500 ms `ActiveMenus` monitor, explicit `HUDObject_Credits`, `HUDObject_Fader`, fail-screen handling, `scaleform_ui_change` callback, optional black-overlay fix. The source distinguishes special HUD objects because normal widget discovery does not cover them. This bridge lets UI arbitration, Alyx-wheel input and character visibility share one event vocabulary across fundamentally different UI technologies.",
  "pattern_di2_authored_state_presentation_handoff": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/main.lua`: `setCounterSequenceActive`, CutsceneActivity hooks, CoopSequenceTask_Counter hooks, montage change callback, `UseFPCameraRotation=false`. The implementation is transactional: enter state → refresh/handoff presentation and input; exit state → restore VR behavior. This prevents two systems writing the same bones/camera/input simultaneously.",
  "pattern_di2_proximity_scope_rtt": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/scope.lua`: `Scope:create`, `createOcularLens`, `createObjectiveLens`, `attachTo`, `findClosestScopeToHead`, active-state callbacks. Render target: 1024×1024 `RTF_RGBA16f`. Objective and ocular transforms are separately parameterized. The module tracks active instances and chooses the closest displayed optic to the HMD for adjustment input. This avoids tying optic focus to whichever weapon instance was created last and lets the rest of the HUD respond to scope activation semantically.",
  "pattern_di2_shared_trace_subscription_bus": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/linetracer.lua`: `subscribe`, `updateOptions`, `unsubscribe`, `executeTrace`, `update`. Trace types include CAMERA, HMD, LEFT_CONTROLLER, RIGHT_CONTROLLER and arbitrary custom keys. Highest-priority options define the shared trace, while all subscribers receive the result/location. This creates predictable geometry agreement between reticule, laser and interaction while avoiding redundant Unreal traces and repeated struct allocation.",
  "pattern_di2_reticule_trace_display_decoupling": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/reticule.lua`: line-trace subscription/update, `update`, eye-dominance handling, `scope_active_change`. The eye offset uses configured IPD in mm divided by 20 (half and convert to cm), then applies HMD-right displacement. The displayed component is placed on the origin→target ray and backed off from the hit surface. This avoids forcing a 2D crosshair to define the actual firing ray while still giving a comfortable depth cue.",
  "pattern_di2_controller_collision_proxy": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/collision.lua`: collider creation/configuration, update loop and `checkCollisionEvent`. Swept proxies prevent simple endpoint-only contact tests, while overlap-only variants support sphere/component overlap queries for softer interaction zones. The library reports velocity together with the engine-native hit structure, so gameplay code can decide intent/strength without embedding collision implementation in every feature.",
  "pattern_di2_body_yaw_intent_inference": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/body_yaw.lua`: `positionAlignmentConfidence`, `signedAngularVelocity`, `isRotationSynchronized`, `update`/advanced update. The system gates rotations, scales response to turn velocity, and snaps once within a small threshold. It explicitly distinguishes “look around” from “turn the body,” which is valuable for IK torso orientation and avoiding constant avatar twisting.",
  "pattern_di2_body_zone_gesture_to_xinput": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/gestures.lua`: face-zone parameter tree and `getHeadGestures`; `scripts/main.lua`: interaction modes and XInput synthesis. Zone classification is relative to HMD location/forward, making it robust to world position. The mode system can supplement normal controls (“Mixed”) or deliberately replace them (“Full Immersion”), which is a clean way to offer accessibility/fallback without maintaining two gameplay implementations.",
  "pattern_di2_attachment_aware_hand_animation": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/hands_animation.lua`: `createAnimationHandler`, pre-input handler, `updateAnimationState`, `handleInputForHands`. Base IDs use `left/right_trigger`, `grip`, `thumb`; weapon variants use `*_trigger_weapon_<attachment>` and `*_grip_weapon_<attachment>`. The input handler runs before remapping so visual fingers reflect the physical control state rather than a later synthesized button. This is a robust controller→finger-pose pipeline without requiring optical hand tracking.",
  "pattern_di2_widget_world_interaction_ray": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/interaction.lua`: `createWidgetInteractionComponent`, `create`, trace/laser configuration, `registerOnHitCallback`. The widget component remains Unreal-native while world selection is line-trace based. Keeping the transform and targeting policy shared prevents a common VR bug where the visible laser and the clickable cursor disagree because they use different rays.",
  "pattern_di2_hand_solver_lifecycle_swap": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/main.lua`: `HandsType` and `regenerateHands`. The source comment states attachments are detached first so they do not get “lost” when hands are destroyed. This is a simple but important VR lifecycle pattern: held-object hierarchy must be dismantled in dependency order, then rebuilt under the new representation.",
  "pattern_di2_throw_grip_ownership_lock": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — DI2 main orchestration uses the portable throwing callback as the ownership boundary. The normal attachment resolver is suspended and ranged presentation is hidden for the whole throw interval, then ordinary grip resolution resumes. This avoids a “weapon pops back into the hand mid-throw” race caused by independent periodic systems.",
  "pattern_di2_gunstock_solver_layer_calibration": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/main.lua` contains the explicit layer choice; `scripts/libs/gunstock.lua` persists per-attachment rotation/offhand offset with default fallback and reactive callbacks. Because DI2 weapons are attached to native hand sockets, moving the attachment itself would shift every child capability (muzzle, FX, scope, laser). Applying stock correction to hand/IK targets preserves weapon-local geometry.",
  "pattern_di2_controller_component_cache": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/controllers.lua`: `cachedComponents`, `getCachedController`, `createControllerComponent`, `createHMDControllerComponent`, `restoreExistingComponents`, level reset. The module supports both older UE `Hand` and newer `MotionSource` fields. HMD controller state is marked permanent. This prevents duplicate controller actors/components across repeated initialization and makes script restart/level transition lifecycle explicit.",
  "pattern_di2_multi_source_aim_abstraction": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/input.lua` public API and `updateAim`/body-yaw code. Weapon aim rotations can be published by attachment logic, while controller/HMD paths read tracked components. The layer supports transient overrides without changing persisted config. This is useful for temporary states (scopes, mounted actions, cutscenes) where aim ownership changes but locomotion/body-facing should not.",
  "pattern_di2_roomscale_pawn_follow_sweep": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/input.lua`: `updatePawnPositionRoomscale`. Two integration modes are explicit: native movement input for animation-friendly locomotion, or direct swept root following for literal roomscale. The calculation uses `world_to_meters`, decoupled yaw and no vertical displacement. Finally `vr.set_standing_origin` consumes the applied HMD translation. This is a reusable pattern for engines where physical movement must become collision-aware pawn motion.",
  "pattern_di2_mesh_neck_eye_compensation": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/input.lua`: comments/functions around `getAnimationHeadDelta`, `getEyeOffsetDelta`, `updateMeshRelativePosition`. The module exists specifically because running animations can move the mesh ahead of the pawn and because eyes may not be centered on the origin. Applying those as explicit deltas keeps the body under the camera without rewriting the native animation itself.",
  "pattern_di2_body_yaw_write_suppression": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/input.lua`: `bodyYawWritesSuppressed`, `lastSuppressedBodyYaw`, `updateBodyYaw`. Deltas over ~1° are mirrored into decoupled yaw, so authored rotations do not leave the player looking sideways relative to the avatar. This is more robust than disabling VR body rotation and snapping back afterward because the native motion is incorporated continuously.",
  "pattern_di2_stereo_body_yaw_single_eye_optimization": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/input.lua`: early/post stereo callbacks, `optimizeBodyYawCalculations`, `optimizeBodyRotationCalculations`, `optimizeBodyLocationCalculations`, `lateYaw`. The code explicitly guards frame-global root writes from double execution while still letting each eye receive its camera offsets. This is a subtle but reusable XR integration concern in engines where a “camera callback” occurs once per eye.",
  "pattern_di2_profiled_input_remap": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/remap.lua` defines input mappings for buttons, triggers and analog axes, input/action state machines, thresholds, unpress behavior and profile management; `scripts/main.lua` binds profile selection to the four interaction modes. This lets one mod support conservative and immersive control schemes while all paths still feed the same gamepad-facing game logic.",
  "pattern_di2_reactive_persisted_config": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/configui.lua`: panel creation, widget maps, JSON load/save, callback registries, deferred/throttled save, visibility/disable helpers; `scripts/main.lua`: extensive `onCreateOrUpdate` orchestration. Loaded values invoke lifecycle code, so the config file is not merely passive data. This is especially useful for VR calibration where offsets/policies must be tunable inside the headset.",
  "pattern_di2_emissive_trace_laser": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/laser.lua`: `Laser:createVisual`, `setUseEmissive`, length settings, line-tracer subscription and optional particle target. Emissive intensity is intentionally boosted (25×) for auto-exposure stability; the visual mesh is non-colliding/shadowless and attached to the internal capsule. `linetracer` supplies hit distance; `particles` can render an endpoint marker. This preserves geometry agreement between weapon aim, reticule and visible laser.",
  "pattern_di2_accessory_montage_proximity_binding": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/accessories.lua`: marker resolution, proximity checks, `attachHandToTargetAccessory`, `attachHandToAccessory`, segmented montage monitor and `attachment_suppress_mesh_reattach` integration. Activation modes distinguish left/right/either, montage-only and always-on. This creates a general animation↔tracked-hand arbitration seam suitable for reload handles, doors, steering elements or scripted prop interactions.",
  "pattern_di2_particle_loop_double_buffer": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/libs/particles.lua`: looping constructor, `spawnBuffer`, `start`, `stop`, `setLoopRange`. Niagara and Cascade creation paths are handled separately; emitter overrides propagate into buffers. The generation token makes delayed callbacks harmless after restart/stop. This is useful for VR-attached muzzle/laser/hand effects where a visible loop must survive component reattachment and engine-specific emitter limitations.",
  "pattern_di2_weapon_fx_reparenting": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE — `scripts/helpers/weapons.lua`: `reparentWeaponFx`; DI2 main attachment-change callback. The comment states the core problem directly: “Grip only moves WeaponMesh; PSC FX on the actor root stay on MeshFirstPerson.” The fix does not recreate particle systems; it moves the existing components, preserving game-owned effect state while aligning presentation with tracked weapon geometry.",
  "seed_jfovr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05) against the supplied Jedi Fallen Order UEVR Lua set (Main.luabb, SaberMelee.lua, Controls.lua, CameraManager.lua, GestureScript.lua, Trackers.lua, MeleePower.lua, Handsmain.lua, hands.lua, hand_animations.lua, UEHelper.lua, assetloader.lua, ControlInput.lua). ARCHITECTURE: Main.luabb composes SaberMelee, Controls, CameraManager, UEHelper, Trackers, HolsterScript/GestureScript, MeleePower. TRACKERS: spawn Actors + MotionControllerComponent (Left/Right, MotionSource) + SceneComponent (HMD); UObjectHook hand 0/1/2; Diff_Rotator_LR / Diff_Vector_LR / RightRotator / LeftRotator / HmdRotator each tick; level-change destroy/respawn. MELEEPOWER: samples raw VR poses via vr.get_pose each frame; PosDiffWeaponHand / PosDiffSecondaryHand = world-space speed. SABERMELEE: enables generate-overlap on pawn.weaponCollision; GetOverlappingComponents; filters Hero/Blaster/Volume/Pull/Push; velocity-scaled RsApplyDamage via RsGameplayStatics with RsDamageParams; block path on Blaster/Cannon/Electrostaff/Arrow overlap drives HC_Defense Block Pressed/Released; LightsaberMarks StartTracing/StopTracing when blade extended. CONTROLS: two-handed wield (lShoulder) positions Lightsaber_01 + weaponCollision at L/R controller midpoint with Diff_Rotator_LR orientation after removing MC state; grip switch (rShoulder) lerps CurrGripAngle 0↔π and updates MC location/rotation offsets; blade toggle (A hold timing) calls ExtendRetractBlade on LightsaberChild_01/02; saber throw (rShoulder+RTrigger+velocity) detaches from MC follow, projects along right forward by CurrThrowRange with spin, returns on release; Force gestures capture trigger-start hand position and fire push/pull/slow from displacement thresholds, mapped to XInput triggers/shoulder. CAMERA: right-hand rotation drives SetCameraRotation; decoupled yaw from ThumbRX (snap or smooth); on_early_calculate_stereo_view_offset locks position to pawn and applies DecoupledYawCurrentRot; stick axes rotated by AlphaDiff (HMD vs hand horizontal angle).\n\nHANDS: poseable components from pawn.Mesh attached to controllers; hand_animations.lua grip/open/trigger poses; reset on level/menu. GESTURES: HMD-relative RHandNewX/Y/Z zone tests; grab+zone → quick-slots / ToggleUI; haptic enter/exit. UEHELPER: isMenu / isCinematic / isSaber1Extended / isSaber2Extended / isSaberDetached / isInAir / isNavMode globals; raw XInput. REUSABLE PATTERNS: (1) spawn-and-own MC components, (2) velocity + overlap melee into native damage API, (3) two-handed midpoint attachment, (4) detach-and-project weapon throw, (5) displacement-threshold Force/gesture → legacy input, (6) decoupled yaw + stick-frame rewrite, (7) poseable hand mesh + authored grip poses.",
  "seed_hlbodyik_case": "Source-audit: the project is a UEVR profile fork with a visible first-person body, hand/finger/leg animation, calibration and automatic equipment reconstruction. Public documentation says the hand/body system resolves bones and keeps the avatar synchronized as equipment changes; the repo is explicitly based on the earlier HL_UEVR profile and jbusfield's shared IK ecosystem. It also adds body/camera behavior for crouching and mounts, showing that IK systems need explicit non-foot locomotion states rather than assuming the player is always standing on a flat ground plane. Deep pass: The body-IK approach should be treated as a hierarchy problem, not just hand parenting: head pose establishes the torso reference, controller targets drive upper-limb IK, equipment changes must update the held-object/hand relationship, and crouch/mount states require alternate body/camera rules. A robust implementation should therefore separate calibration (height/reach), target acquisition (HMD/controllers), IK solving, and equipment synchronization. The existing profile is a useful reference for that separation but exact bone names/offsets remain game-specific.",
  "seed_systemshockuevr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The public SystemShock-UEVR project is a profile plus compiled C++ plugin. The plugin handles game-specific features that require deeper engine access: motion-control alignment, interaction mode, physical crouching thresholds, melee gesture thresholds, culling-FOV correction, per-eye transparency fixes, first-person scripted-sequence repairs and UI/cutscene state. The Cyberspace sequence is deliberately mapped to a different control model instead of forcing 6DOF. A maintained successor adds persistent plugin configuration and keeps physical melee/player-arm changes as optional Lua/PAK augmentations. Reusable pattern: keep generic XR configuration in the profile and escalate only game-specific engine fixes to native callbacks. Exact renderer/frame timing is not established by the public source audit and should remain UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_systemreshock_uevr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): SystemReShock uses a UEVR native C++ plugin as the game-specific control layer. plugin.hpp exposes on_initialize(), on_xinput_get_state() and on_pre_engine_tick(), with explicit UWorld/HUD/pawn/hand-controller pointers and MemoProperty/MemoBoolean state for pawn, game mode, MFD, ADS, crouch, interaction and weapon-pull state. The plugin's game-state routing separates cinematic, pause, MFD/MFD_PRE, booting, crashing, interactable and normal Citadel Station states, allowing VR input and presentation to change with native game state. initialize_mcs() creates VRModActor containers and UMotionControllerComponent instances, uses MotionSource/Hand assignment and attaches wrist offsets; the source comment specifically notes that recreating the controller component changes transform-evaluation order and reduces hand lag relative to the AnimBP tick. The VR body groups controller, WidgetInteractionComponent, laser-pointer and attached HUD systems. Weapon handling includes spatial backpack-to-hand weapon retrieval: when the right hand leaves the backpack state, the plugin checks that no weapon/grab component is already held and invokes the game's native ToggleEquip action. try_melee() chooses a native fast-attack or two-hand power-swing montage, triggers haptics, starts the montage through COMP_ActionManager->ForceBeginAction and invokes the weapon's native melee-start path. The HUD layer reuses native UMG widgets through UWidgetComponent: minimap, vital bars and media display are attached to the VR body, while hotbar and hardware selector components reuse the same widget instances used by the MFD so inventory state stays shared. Widget components use a dedicated WIDGET_INTERACTION_TRACE_CHANNEL with selective overlap responses, and the controller WidgetInteractionComponent points at that channel; the visible pointer/laser is a separate spatial presentation layer. Reusable pattern: UEVR XR runtime -> central game-state router -> dedicated motion-controller actors -> VR-body presentation/interaction -> semantic spatial gesture or grab state -> native Unreal action/animation/gameplay call -> shared native UI state. This is a strong reference for implementing embodied VR in a flat Unreal game while keeping existing gameplay and UMG systems authoritative.",
  "seed_palworld_uevr_case": "Source-audit: the public tree contains UEVR profile artifacts, scripts/, uobjecthook/, camera/config data and controller binding JSON for multiple controller families. The README states the concrete behavior: first-person mode, improved aiming, altered weapon hold and player-body hiding. The source tree therefore supports a UEVR + object-hook architecture, but it does not justify inventing a custom renderer or OpenXR submission path. Treat the generic UEVR layer as the stereo/HMD/controller provider and the profile/UObjectHook layer as the Palworld gameplay/camera seam. Exact property names should be read from the current script/UObjectHook files before code generation. Deep pass: The source tree is best modeled as UEVR providing stereo/HMD/controller infrastructure, with Palworld-specific scripts/UObjectHook code resolving camera/player/weapon state. The README-supported goals—first-person mode, improved aiming, altered weapon hold and hiding the player body—fit that boundary. Before generating code, inspect the current UObjectHook/script files for exact reflected properties and functions; do not promote guessed Unreal object names into a stable API contract.",
  "seed_sh2r_uevr_case": "Source-audit: C++ Plugin.cpp contains real native hook machinery. It resolves SHProto.SHItemWeaponRanged virtual functions by locating the native implementation, scanning for an FF 90 indirect virtual call, reading the vtable offset and registering an inline hook. GetStartTraceLoc is replaced with the weapon FirePoint location; GetEndTraceLoc is rebuilt from RootComponent forward direction and corrected 90 degrees for rifles/shotguns. MeleeWeaponEnvTrace is located through a string reference and call-site scan, then inline-hooked. In controller mode the replacement performs multi-hit traces, identifies enemy/glass/environment components, classifies bone names, emits Lua events, applies impulses and uses the game physical-animation component; outside controller mode it calls the original. Lua also hooks LegacyCameraShake functions and zeros ShakeScale. The README confirms two-handed firearms, 3D crosshair projection, roomscale, physical weapon-size melee, contextual leg hits, hand-attached items/map and cutscene/full-body VR-state gating. This is a strong example of using C++ only where Lua cannot reach the required native seam. Deep pass: The native hook path is unusually explicit and should be preserved as a reference recipe: locate the reflected Unreal function, find the real native implementation, identify the indirect vtable call pattern, calculate the slot, and install an inline hook. Ranged traces use FirePoint for start and RootComponent orientation for end; melee uses a separate environment-trace hook and controller-only multi-hit logic. Lua remains responsible for higher-level events/camera-shake behavior, demonstrating a clean C++-for-native-seam/Lua-for-gameplay-policy split.",
  "seed_outerworlds_uevr_case": "Source-audit: the plugin spawns explicit left/right VR controller actors and UMotionControllerComponents with MotionSource Left/Right. The weapon module attaches a particle-system interaction pointer to the right controller and enables collision/query behavior. Ranged-mode tuning removes FineAimFovAdjustment and nearly disables FineAimLookStickRateMultiplier so physical controller aiming is not contaminated by flat-screen fine aim. The HUD uses UIndianaWidgetComponent attached to hand actors; original viewport widgets are removed, the component is configured as a transparent 3D widget, and its transform/scale/opacity are exposed through ImGui and persisted to INI. The repository also has dedicated flicker-fixer, rendering, Indiana game, weapon and HUD modules. The README explicitly lists 6DOF guns and wrist HUDs, but also explicitly marks melee and scoped weapons as missing. Do not add those features to an LLM-generated implementation unless another source supplies them. Deep pass: The controller actors are actual Unreal UMotionControllerComponents, so the plugin demonstrates the difference between emulated input and native controller objects. The HUD path is equally important: removing the original viewport widget and replacing it with a transparent UIndianaWidgetComponent attached to a hand gives the flat UI a true 3D anchor while keeping the widget implementation intact. The missing melee/scoped support should remain explicit—do not infer it from the presence of controller/weapon modules.",
  "seed_oblivionvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-06, supplied full Oblivion UEVR ZIP): The profile is the Pande4360 Oblivion Remastered / Altar 6DOF Attached Items profile. CONFIG.lua identifies UEVR commit baseline 1343 and exposes JSON-backed settings for head-vs-right-controller movement, snap turn, UI follow view, radial quick menu, holster haptics, first-person riding, sword-sideways blocking, visible body, lighting, projectile speed and melee power. ARCHITECTURE: MainHandler routes the UEVR on_xinput_get_state callback through UpdateInput -> HolsterInput -> MeleeInput -> CameraInput -> ControlInputInput; level changes clear stale state and recover HMD/left/right controllers. TRACKERS: Trackers.lua resolves GameEngine/Actor/MotionControllerComponent/SceneComponent, spawns HMD and hand actors with BeginDeferredActorSpawnFromClass/FinishSpawningActor, and uses UEVR_UObjectHook existence/destruction checks across level changes.\n\nMELEE: Main/Melee.lua enables the weapon VHitBox, sets collision object type, attaches it to the weapon root, dynamically changes BoxExtent, and reads GetOverlappingComponents; when a valid non-player owner is found it can temporarily SetAllBodiesBelowSimulatePhysics and AddImpulse for ragdoll feedback. Motion-distance thresholds gate SendMeleeHitOnPairedPawn calls with stamina/fatigue consumption and haptic feedback; if no weapon actor exists, right/left first-person hand hitboxes are used. The module also synthesizes a right-trigger pulse into the existing attack path after a physical swing and contains shield/sword blocking logic. PHYSICS: PhysicsMassEmu.lua and PhysicsMassEmu-OneHandedbb.lua create intermediary actors with StaticMeshComponents and PhysicsConstraintComponents, add pawn-side anchor components, constrain weapon/intermediary meshes to tracked hands, isolate collision channels, and explicitly set tick groups/prerequisites (anchor early, constraint/affected mesh later) to stabilize the hand/weapon physics proxy. ATTACHMENTS/BODY: Attachements.lua applies UEVR motion-controller state to weapon/shield/bow/arrow meshes and HMD-relative offsets; the visible-body path hides selected arm/leg components, keeps hands visible, and applies HMD-derived motion-controller offsets to the first-person body mesh, removing the motion-controller state during ragdoll. CAMERA/INPUT: CameraManager supports head- or right-controller-based locomotion orientation, recentering and decoupled yaw/snap turn; bow state switches aiming toward the arrow/hand direction, while menu/riding/bow contexts restore game/default aim. HOLSTERS: HolsterScript uses HMD-relative coordinates and named body/weapon zones with haptic enter/exit and quick-slot/inventory/UI/reload actions. HUD: VRHud creates a WidgetComponent on the right-hand actor, reuses the game’s WBP_ModernHud_Reticle, removes it from the normal viewport, applies a translucent no-depth-test material, and places it at a hand-relative offset. OTHER CONTEXT: the tree contains separate riding, bow, weapon-wheel, fader/menu, hands, addon, physics and auxiliary scripts, so lifecycle/context arbitration is distributed across modules. REUSABLE DESIGN: tracked pose -> UEVR UObject/actor state -> context-aware camera/input -> attachment or constrained physics proxy -> semantic melee/block/holster -> native Altar gameplay call -> haptic/UI feedback. IMPORTANT LIMITATION: the supplied ZIP is Lua/profile source; no native UEVR/plugin implementation is included, so UEVR renderer internals and any external native binary behavior remain outside this audit. Exact Altar property names and the required UEVR build are version-sensitive and should be re-derived for future targets.",
  "seed_bnm_pattern": "Source-audit: BNM is a general Android Unity IL2CPP object-manipulation layer, not a VR renderer. The current docs require C++20, a target Unity version in GlobalSettings.hpp, a selected loading method and a configured Android hook backend. Its public API includes named IL2CPP Classes/Images/Fields/Methods/Properties, custom class/method declarations, original-method calls, invoke-hook marking, Unity Vector/Matrix/Quaternion/Raycast structures, Mono arrays/lists/dictionaries/strings and delegates/events. The project lists Dobby, ShadowHook and Substrate/And64InlineHook as example Android hook backends and supports Unity 5.6.4f1 through 6000.0.x. For VR ports the useful division is OpenXR for tracking/submission plus BNM for resolving and modifying the game's camera, player, hands, weapons, physics and interaction objects. Exact IL2CPP layouts still need validation against the compiled target because stripped/changed builds can diverge from decompiled metadata. Deep pass: BNM belongs in the 'gameplay/object seam' layer of an Android VR port. OpenXR should own HMD/controller tracking and swapchain submission; BNM can resolve IL2CPP classes, fields, methods and properties, declare wrappers, call originals and install invoke hooks so the VR layer can reach the game camera, player, weapon and interaction objects. The critical porting step is to validate Unity/IL2CPP metadata and native layouts for the exact target build; a generic class name is not enough when stripping or updates alter offsets.",
  "seed_repoxr_case":"Source-audit: RepoXR separates BepInEx/IL2CPP preload, managed VR/gameplay and Unity asset/addressables layers; compatibility is dynamically measured at startup and multiplayer exposes remote VR arm motion without requiring an XR runtime on flatscreen clients.",
  "pattern_halocevr_d3d9ex_d3d11_bridge": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Key files: dllmain.cpp; DirectXWrappers/IDirect3D9ExWrapper.cpp; VR/OpenVR.cpp::OnGameFinishInit/CreateTexAndSurface/PostDrawFrame. Direct3DCreate9 is deliberately upgraded to CreateDeviceEx; each D3D9Ex eye/UI/scope allocation exposes a shared HANDLE that a D3D11 device opens as ID3D11Texture2D for OpenVR submission. Transfer: preserve the old renderer and bridge only the render resource ownership/API required by the XR compositor.",
  "pattern_halocevr_asymmetric_fov_uv_crop": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. OpenVR.cpp::Init reads GetProjectionRaw for both eyes, constructs one symmetric max-tangent fov/aspect, enlarges the game eye targets, and derives per-eye VRTextureBounds_t. UpdateCameraFrustum supplies eye-to-head translation; PostDrawFrame submits each common-frustum texture with different UV bounds. Transfer: a symmetric-frustum engine can approximate runtime asymmetry by overscanning and cropping instead of gaining a true off-axis projection path.",
  "pattern_halocevr_multipass_drawframe_replay": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Hooks.cpp::H_DrawFrame executes DrawFrame.Original for scope, left eye, right eye and optional mirror after one PreDrawFrame; Game::PreDrawEye swaps frustum/render targets. IDirect3DDevice9ExWrapper::BeginScene/EndScene can return S_OK once when bIgnoreNextStart/bIgnoreNextEnd are set so replay passes share one D3D scene. Transfer: multi-view replay needs explicit suppression of per-view renderer lifecycle calls that are only legal once per physical frame.",
  "pattern_halocevr_openvr_dual_pose_timing": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. OpenVR::UpdatePoses calls WaitGetPoses(renderPoses, gamePoses) once before rendering. The IVR transform and velocity getters carry bRenderPose; render-facing systems request renderPoses while action/gesture paths can consume gamePoses. No explicit predicted-display-time field or post-render late-latch exists in this source.",
  "pattern_halocevr_tracking_frame_offsets": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. OpenVR.h::ConvertSteamVRMatrixToMatrix4 centralizes axis/sign conversion; GetHMDTransform/GetRawControllerTransform then apply -positionOffset and -yawOffset consistently. Game::MetresToWorld/WorldToMetres hold scale; Recentre updates positionOffset and snap/smooth turning updates yawOffset. Transfer: keep one named tracking-frame transform shared by every subsystem.",
  "pattern_halocevr_roomscale_origin_consumption": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Game::UpdateRoomScaleMovement converts horizontal render-HMD displacement to Halo units, moves the local UnitDynamicObject, then adds the consumed displacement to the VR locationOffset. InputHandler::UpdateCrouch converts head-height change to the native crouch bit; P_DisableCrouchCamera removes the flat camera lowering. Limitation from source/config: collision is deferred to Halo and can create physical/game desync.",
  "pattern_halocevr_viewmodel_wrist_graft": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Hooks::H_SetViewModelPosition replaces the original call with WeaponHandler::UpdateViewModel, whose opening ReferenceUpdateViewModelImpl documents the decompiled native algorithm. The replacement rebuilds real animation transforms, identifies l wrist/r wrist/gun/display bones, hides proximal geometry in 6DoF, moves wrist transforms to tracked poses and preserves descendant authored animation. This is viewmodel skeletal grafting, not body IK.",
  "pattern_halocevr_runtime_wrist_tip_alignment": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Extras/Bindings/actions.json declares skeleton and LeftTip/RightTip pose actions; controller bindings map tips to /user/hand/*/pose/tip. OpenVR::UpdateSkeleton caches wrist bone 1 from 31 WithController bones; GetControllerTransform composes it with raw device pose. TryGetControllerFacing consumes the tip pose. Transfer: prefer runtime semantic grip/wrist/aim poses over one controller-model origin when available.",
  "pattern_halocevr_two_hand_snapaware_smoothing": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. InputHandler::GetCalculatedHandPositions makes the two-hand ray from dominant to non-dominant raw controller positions; CalculateSmoothedInput applies per-zoom exponential smoothing and rotates cached state on yawOffset snap changes; WeaponHandler::GetDominantHandTransform lookAt()s the smoothed target. The visible support wrist in UpdateViewModel keeps its authored hand-to-hand delta rather than exactly tracking the support controller.",
  "pattern_halocevr_asset_socket_muzzle_calibration": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. WeaponHandler::UpdateCache walks player weapon → Asset_Weapon → ViewModelID → Asset_GBXModel and locates the \"primary trigger\" socket. UpdateViewModel then combines that cooked socket with the live gun bone expressed relative to the controller hand to derive fireOffset/fireRotation. Transfer: derive VR muzzle calibration from native asset semantics and current animation instead of maintaining a manual weapon-offset table.",
  "pattern_halocevr_transactional_native_fire_spoof": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Hooks::H_FireWeapon/H_ThrowGrenade bracket native calls. WeaponHandler::RelocatePlayer caches local player position/aim, writes controller/HMD-derived launch state, original Halo code fires, then PostFireWeapon/PostThrowGrenade restores state. 6DoF uses hand+fireOffset and fireRotation; 3DoF uses HMD origin + controller rotation. Preserve native weapon/projectile authority while adapting its hidden spatial inputs.",
  "pattern_halocevr_spatial_intent_native_controls": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. H_HandleInputs executes native input first; InputHandler maps VR action state into Controls/Controls_Custom. UpdateFlashlight, UpdateHolsterSwitchWeapons, UpdateMelee and UpdateCrouch detect spatial/velocity conditions and output Halo’s ordinary 0/127 control values. Motion melee is only an intent trigger and still uses Halo’s look-based attack direction.",
  "pattern_halocevr_handed_actionset_weapon_transfer": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. InputHandler::UpdateHandsProximity/CheckSwapWeaponHand toggles Game::bLeftHanded only when hand-distance and action transitions imply a transfer, then UpdateRegisteredInputs selects handles from the default/left-handed action set. Viewmodel rendering mirrors culling/winding and repairs display geometry; the same state selects dominant controller and haptic roles.",
  "pattern_halocevr_flat_ui_to_vr_layers": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Game::PreDrawHUD/PreDrawMenu redirect only one pass into uiSurface; P_EnableUIAlphaWrite patches alpha output. OpenVR::PositionOverlay/PostDrawFrame submit the detached HUD/menu as an interactive overlay; overlay events feed Halo’s 640x480 mouse path. PreDrawCrosshair captures the native centered crosshair into a separate surface and SetCrosshairTransform draws it as a weapon/HMD-aim world quad.",
  "pattern_halocevr_weapon_scope_rtt": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Game::PreDrawScope builds a native Halo frustum from WeaponHandler::GetWorldWeaponAim and dedicated scope RT dimensions; H_DrawFrame executes an extra full render. PostDrawScope masks the optic, and SetScopeTransform places the texture at a tracked weapon offset. Main-eye HUD code forces zoom=-1 so the whole VR camera is not magnified.",
  "pattern_halocevr_data_driven_weapon_haptics": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. WeaponHandler::PreFireWeapon triggers only for a local-player-owned weapon. WeaponHapticsConfigManager hot-reloads VR/OpenVR/haptics.json and chooses OneHand vs TwoHand.Dominant/Nondominant profiles; OpenVR outputs vibration actions/pulses. Plasma pistol charge maintains ramp state. Transfer: native accepted event → semantic weapon ID → data-driven hand profile → runtime haptic.",
  "pattern_halocevr_signature_rebase_hooks": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Hooking/SigScanner.h stores known RVA + signature; UpdateOffset validates the fast path then scans the full executable image and rebases. Hook<T>::CreateHook skips MinHook creation on failure. Indirect engine globals share the same scan mechanism; thread suspension surrounds direct byte patches. Caveat: direct patch functions are not all individually guarded after scan failure, so copy the self-healing idea but extend fail-closed validation to every write.",
  "pattern_halocevr_vr_provider_emulator": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. VR/IVR.h is the game-facing runtime contract. Game::Init selects OpenVR or VREmulator at compile time; VREmulator supplies synthetic 64 mm stereo separation, controller transforms, render surfaces and a desktop D3D window. The README recommends Emulated-VR for rapid debugging. Transfer: make headset-free execution a first-class provider rather than a collection of test conditionals.",
  "pattern_halocevr_camera_mode_arbitration": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Game::PreDrawFrame tracks load transitions, vehicle/viewmodel state and recentering; UpdateCamera branches ordinary vs vehicle-without-weapon behavior. OpenVR::UpdateCameraFrustum rebases HMD yaw to authored cutscene yaw; H_DrawViewModel suppresses first-person weapons in cutscenes; direct patches remove bars/FPS cap. The documented vehicle-transition bugs are evidence that camera mode transitions need explicit lifecycle handling.",
  "pattern_halocevr_3dof_compat_aim": "Source-audit 2026-09-06. Use3DOFAiming changes viewmodel, fire origin/direction, crosshair, scope and two-hand eligibility together. WeaponHandler uses HMD translation/cosmetic offset for the model, controller pitch/yaw with roll removed for facing, HMD origin + controller direction for native firing, and snap-turn-aware smoothing. Transfer: compatibility aiming should be a consistent spatial mode across all consumers.",
  "seed_halocevr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-06, supplied HaloCEVR full source ZIP). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE.\n\nARCHITECTURE: `dllmain.cpp` is a d3d9.dll proxy bootstrap. It loads the real D3D9 runtime, initializes `Game`, and exports replacements for `Direct3DCreate9`/`Direct3DCreate9Ex`; the non-Ex entry is deliberately routed through the Ex path so Halo receives an `IDirect3D9ExWrapper`. `Game::PatchGame` composes this renderer bootstrap with reverse-engineered x86 hooks/patches. `IVR` isolates the rest of the mod from the runtime provider (`OpenVR` or the desktop `VREmulator`).\n\nRENDERER / INTEROP: `IDirect3D9ExWrapper::CreateDevice` forces a wrapped `CreateDeviceEx`. `OpenVR::OnGameFinishInit` creates a D3D11 device. `OpenVR::CreateTexAndSurface` creates shareable D3D9Ex textures for left eye, right eye, UI, crosshair and scope, retrieves each D3D9 surface, opens its shared HANDLE on D3D11, and stores an `ID3D11Texture2D`. After rendering, `OpenVR::PostDrawFrame` waits for a D3D9 event query to complete and submits the shared D3D11 eye textures directly to the OpenVR compositor.\n\nSTEREO / PROJECTION: `OpenVR::Init` queries raw per-eye tangent bounds and recommended target size. Since Halo’s frustum uses one symmetric FOV/aspect, the mod takes max tangent extents across both eyes, enlarges the target to hold that common overscan, and computes different `VRTextureBounds_t` for each eye. `Game::PreDrawEye` restores the original native frustum, calls `UpdateCameraFrustum`, then swaps Halo’s primary render target to that eye. `GetHMDMatrixPoseEye` supplies the eye-to-head transform/IPD. The result is true separate-eye scene rendering, with headset asymmetry represented by compositor UV crop rather than a game-side off-axis projection matrix.\n\nFRAME TIMELINE: `Game::PreDrawFrame` calls `OpenVR::UpdatePoses` once. That calls `WaitGetPoses(renderPoses, gamePoses)`, updates skeletal/tip action pose state and processes overlay events. `Hooks::H_DrawFrame` can run an extra scope pass, left eye, right eye and optional game mirror; `IDirect3DDevice9ExWrapper` suppresses duplicate BeginScene/EndScene calls for replayed views. `OpenVR::PostDrawFrame` fences GPU work, submits eyes, positions/submits the UI overlay and calls `PostPresentHandoff`. No explicit predicted-display-time value or second late-latch stage is present.\n\nPOSE ROLES / COORDINATES: `IVR` transform accessors carry `bRenderPose`. `OpenVR::GetHMDTransform`, raw controller transform, wrist/controller transform and velocity choose `renderPoses` or `gamePoses` and then apply the same central coordinate conversion, recenter `positionOffset` and artificial-turn `yawOffset`. `Game::MetresToWorld`/`WorldToMetres` centralize scale. This is a clean pose-space contract: runtime raw pose → Halo-axis matrix → tracking-origin/yaw adjustment → title world-scale consumer.\n\nCAMERA / LOCOMOTION: `InputHandler::UpdateCamera` makes Halo’s native camera converge to HMD yaw/pitch by calculating the HMD-vs-game look delta; snap/smooth turn is accumulated separately in `yawOffset`. `UpdateRoomScaleMovement` moves the native local unit horizontally by the physical HMD displacement, then consumes that movement into `locationOffset` so the player’s new game position becomes the room origin. Collision is not swept by the mod; Halo handles it later, and the config notes possible desync when blocked. Hand-relative locomotion rotates the stick vector from camera forward to selected hand forward. Real crouch maps HMD height into the native Crouch control and a code patch suppresses Halo’s own camera crouch translation when physical crouch is active.\n\nCAMERA STATES: vehicle-without-weapon mode uses a dedicated thumbstick camera delta path; cutscenes rebase HMD yaw to the authored cutscene camera yaw while preserving tracked view, suppress the viewmodel, remove cinematic bars and remove the cutscene FPS cap; loading completion schedules recenter. This is explicit camera-state arbitration rather than one global camera overwrite.\n\nINPUT / ACTIONS: `actions.json` defines default and left-handed sets, boolean/analog gameplay actions, skeleton actions, tip pose actions and haptic outputs. `H_HandleInputs` executes Halo’s original input first; `InputHandler` then writes VR results into the discovered native `Controls`/`Controls_Custom` structs. Impulse inputs are gated by `bChanged`; movement uses native analog fields. Short MenuBack is bridged to Escape through Win32 `SendInput`, while a long hold recenters.\n\nHANDS / VIEWMODEL: the mod does not implement full body IK. It replaces the native first-person skeleton-position function with `WeaponHandler::UpdateViewModel`, whose reference implementation documents Halo’s original bone traversal. The replacement calculates all normal animation transforms, finds wrist/gun/display bones by name, retains a copy of real/unmodified bone transforms, then overrides VR-relevant transforms. In 6DoF, proximal arm/root geometry is hidden, wrist bones are moved to tracked transforms, end caps suppress stretched connections, and child animation remains native. In two-hand mode the visible non-dominant hand keeps its original animated delta from the dominant hand. OpenVR skeletal input is used to obtain a runtime wrist reference; individual finger bones are not retargeted into Halo.\n\nCONTROLLER CALIBRATION / AIM POSES: controller bindings expose `/pose/tip` actions plus OpenVR hand skeletons. The provider caches skeletal wrist bone 1 and composes it with raw device tracking for hand placement. `TryGetControllerFacing` uses the tip pose for one-hand aiming where available. Per-user ControllerOffset/ControllerRotation remains available for final calibration.\n\nTWO-HAND AIM: `InputHandler::GetCalculatedHandPositions` uses the vector from dominant to support raw controller position when two-hand mode is active; `CalculateSmoothedInput` applies zoom-dependent exponential smoothing. Snap-turn changes rotate the cached smoothing target before the next lerp, preventing a turn from leaving the gun to interpolate through the previous world-space direction. `GetDominantHandTransform` points the dominant hand at the smoothed target. Grip mode/distance controls two-hand state; hand proximity is separately reserved for weapon transfer.\n\nWEAPON / MUZZLE CALIBRATION: `WeaponHandler::UpdateCache` resolves the active weapon and its GBX viewmodel, finds l/r wrist, gun and display bones, identifies weapon type from the tag path, and searches GBX sockets for `primary trigger`. The socket’s transform is combined with the live animated gun bone expressed in controller-hand space to derive `fireOffset`/`fireRotation`. This lets crosshair, scope and firing inherit native asset placement instead of using a manual muzzle table.\n\nFIRING / GRENADES: `H_FireWeapon` and `H_ThrowGrenade` bracket the original Halo functions. `RelocatePlayer` checks local ownership, caches the true player position/aim and temporarily substitutes VR launch state. In 6DoF the player origin is moved to the tracked hand/gun-barrel offset and aim comes from the calibrated controller/gun rotation; in 3DoF bullets/grenades originate from HMD position and use controller rotation. Native Halo code performs the real fire/throw, then state is restored. This preserves native projectile creation/spread/damage contracts but the source has no controller-velocity grenade throw.\n\n3DOF MODE: `Use3DOFAiming` is implemented across viewmodel, fire, crosshair, scope and two-hand state. It keeps controller rotation authoritative but avoids physical controller-origin projectile launch; the weapon model follows body/HMD translation with a cosmetic offset, rolls are removed, smoothing handles snap turns, bullets use HMD origin + controller direction, and two-hand mode is disabled.\n\nGESTURES / PHYSICAL SEMANTICS: shoulder holsters are HMD-relative proximity spheres gating the native weapon-switch input; hand-head proximity toggles native flashlight; controller vertical velocity over a configurable threshold toggles native melee; HMD height triggers native crouch; hand proximity + grip transition toggles persistent weapon handedness/action-set state. These systems detect semantic intent and then reuse Halo’s original gameplay controls. Motion melee remains head/look aimed, not physical collision melee.\n\nUI: Halo’s HUD/menu/loading draws are redirected only once per VR frame to a transparent `uiSurface`; executable patches enable texture/text alpha output. `OpenVR::PositionOverlay` places the UI as a yaw-only absolute standing overlay with configurable distance/scale/curvature; `SetOverlayInputMethod` makes it interactive in menus. Overlay mouse events are converted to Halo’s 640×480 mouse coordinates and original button state. The VR settings UI can invoke SteamVR’s keyboard. The native center crosshair is intercepted into its own surface and then rendered as a world quad at the current weapon/HMD aim target.\n\nSCOPE: `PreDrawScope` reuses Halo’s complete scene renderer as an offscreen camera pass pointed from the physical weapon; dedicated scope render targets are masked and drawn on a world-space optic. Normal eye rendering suppresses flat zoom by temporarily forcing zoom=-1. Original-scope mode is retained for compatibility.\n\nHAPTICS: `WeaponHapticsConfigManager` hot-reloads `VR/OpenVR/haptics.json`, maps native detected weapon type to one-hand or two-hand dominant/support profiles, and emits OpenVR vibration actions/pulses from the native fire hook. Plasma pistol charging maintains ramp state so feedback changes during charge.\n\nHOOKING / COMPATIBILITY: `Offsets` stores known RVAs plus byte signatures for functions/globals. `SigScanner::UpdateOffset` validates the fast-path RVA then scans the full executable image if it moved. `Hook<T>::CreateHook` skips MinHook creation when resolution fails; indirect globals use scanned instruction anchors and byte offsets. Direct patches are applied while other threads are suspended. Caveat: not every direct patch is guarded after a failed scan, so the robust reusable version of this pattern should validate every memory write. Chimera conflicts are detected and conflicting HaloCEVR patches are disabled.\n\nDEBUG BACKEND: `IVR` allows the same game integration to compile against `VREmulator`, which supplies synthetic 64 mm eye separation/controller transforms/render surfaces and a desktop D3D mirror window. This is explicitly documented as a fast development/debug path.\n\nMULTIPLAYER / LIMITS: no VR pose packet or remote avatar reconstruction exists. README marks multiplayer untested and notes weapon aiming may only work for the host. No full-body IK, OpenVR-finger-to-Halo-finger retargeting, controller-velocity throwing, swept physical melee, or manual magazine/reload interaction is implemented.\n\nTRANSFER MODEL: proxy/bootstrap and binary discovery → shareable legacy render targets → modern compositor texture bridge → one pose sample with explicit render/game roles → replay native render function per camera → central tracking-space state → animation-preserving viewmodel wrist graft → native-asset muzzle calibration → transactional launch-state substitution around native combat → semantic VR gestures into native controls → extracted VR UI/scope/crosshair → native event-driven haptics.",
  "seed_jkxr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-06, supplied OpenJK.zip). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE.\n\nARCHITECTURE: JKXR is compiled into the OpenJK source tree rather than injected into a retail binary. `JKXR/TBXR_Common.*` owns the cross-title OpenXR/session/eye-buffer layer; `OpenXrInput.cpp` owns actions/controller spaces/haptics; `VrInputDefault.cpp`, `VrInputCommon.cpp`, `VrClientInfo.h` and platform `JKXR_SurfaceView.cpp` translate runtime tracking into a shared `vr_client_info_t`. The ordinary OpenJK client/cgame/game/renderer source is modified to consume that state. `code/` is the Jedi Academy SP lineage and `codeJK2/` carries Jedi Outcast-specific equivalents. This architecture is useful because renderer timing, game combat, UI and controller semantics are source-level seams rather than opaque hooks.\n\nOPENXR FRAME TIMELINE: `TBXR_FrameSetup()` processes XR session state, calls `xrWaitFrame` to obtain `FrameState.predictedDisplayTime`, then `xrBeginFrame`. `TBXR_updateProjections()` calls `xrLocateViews` for that display time in `LocalSpace`; `TBXR_GetHMDOrientation()` locates `ViewSpace` against `StageSpace` at the same predicted time; `VR_HandleControllerInput()` reaches `TBXR_UpdateControllers`, whose aim/grip `xrLocateSpace` calls also use that same predicted display time. `TBXR_ProcessHaptics()` runs before rendering. `SCR_UpdateScreen()` then renders eye 0 and eye 1 into acquired OpenXR framebuffers and `re.SubmitStereoFrame()` reaches `TBXR_submitFrame()`, whose `xrEndFrame` uses the original predicted time. This establishes one frame-level pose time for views, HMD and controllers. No extra renderer late-latch after this sample was found.\n\nSEQUENTIAL STEREO / STATE FREEZE: `SCR_UpdateScreen()` calls `SCR_DrawScreenField(STEREO_LEFT)` and `SCR_DrawScreenField(STEREO_RIGHT)`, ending the engine renderer after each eye but submitting XR only after the pair. `CL_CGameRendering()` updates `cl.serverTime`/Ghoul2 time only on the left eye and passes the frozen time into both cgame renders. `CG_DrawActiveFrame()` changes `cg.time` and calls `CG_ProcessSnapshots()` only for STEREO_LEFT. It deliberately runs `Rand_Init(cg.time)` for both eyes so random effects produce the same result. This is an explicit sequential-stereo side-effect contract rather than simply drawing the same scene twice.\n\nPROJECTION / CULLING: `TBXR_updateProjections()` retains the runtime-provided `XrView.fov` and `pose` per eye. `VR_GetVRProjection()` applies any zoom scaling in tangent space and builds a true asymmetric OpenGL matrix with `XrMatrix4x4f_CreateProjectionFov`; both `rd-gles/tr_main.cpp` and `rd-vanilla/tr_main.cpp` request it before using their flat symmetric projection path. At submission, each `XrCompositionLayerProjectionView` uses the corresponding runtime eye pose and FOV. Separately, `TBXR_submitFrame()` derives `vr.fov_x`/`vr.fov_y` from broad combined eye extents for cgame FOV/culling consumers. This scalar is a conservative compatibility extent, not a claim of a mathematically exact stereo-union frustum.\n\nASYMMETRIC HUD CORRECTION: `TBXR_prepareEyeBuffer()` computes `off_center_fov_x/y` from each eye's asymmetric angular centre. `cg_drawtools.cpp` adds those offsets to the legacy HUD coordinate conversion and zoom overlays use the same values. This keeps the old 640×480 HUD visually centered on headsets whose optical centre is not the arithmetic centre of the eye texture.\n\nSCREEN-LAYER / UI PRESENTATION: `VR_UseScreenLayer()` identifies fullscreen UI, non-immersive cinematic/misc cameras, loading, cinematic state, UI catcher and console. In normal gameplay `TBXR_submitFrame()` emits the two-eye projection layer. In screen mode it also acquires a null/black projection and submits an `XrCompositionLayerQuad` in `StageSpace`, sourced from the game framebuffer, sized in metres and positioned using the latched `hmdorientation_snap` and configurable distance. `VR_GetVRProjection()` returns false in this mode so the game renders the screen with its conventional projection. `interactWithTouchScreen()` converts controller yaw/pitch relative to menu yaw into the legacy absolute mouse path (`PortableMouseAbs` / `CL_MouseEvent`); the active menu hand can switch dynamically.\n\nTRACKING / CAMERA REBASE: `vr_client_info_t` holds current/last/snap HMD pose, offsets/deltas, weapon and offhand pose histories, eye/FOV state, handedness, camera modes, body height, stabilization, velocity-attack and interaction state. `VR_SetHMDPosition()` snapshots HMD position/orientation when `take_snap` is requested or the screen-layer mode changes, then derives the current physical offset. `VR_SetHMDOrientation()` stores angular deltas and first-person reference angles. `BG_ConvertFromVR` and `BG_CalculateVRPositionInWorld` are the shared runtime-to-Raven/world conversion seam. Camera consumers branch deliberately for first person, remote NPC/turret, zoom, vehicles, third person and cinematics.\n\nIMMERSIVE CINEMATICS: `cg_camera.cpp` uses an explicit rebase policy. Entering immersive camera mode, switching FOLLOWING/ROFFING/MOVING/PANNING state, or jumping a non-moving camera by more than roughly half an in-game metre requests `vr->take_snap`. The authored camera yaw is stored; final immersive yaw is authored yaw + HMD yaw delta since the snap + snap turn, while pitch/roll come from the HMD. Non-immersive cinematics instead use the flat quad screen.\n\nLOCOMOTION / TURN / CROUCH: `VrInputDefault.cpp` applies a nonlinear stick filter. Movement can be controller-relative by subtracting HMD yaw from offhand yaw. Physical HMD deltas are scaled for configured world positional factor/refresh and rotated into native forward/side movement. `VR_GetMove()` only exposes that positional component in appropriate first-person states and uses separate policies for remote turret, zoom, possessed NPC, vehicles and third person. Snap/smooth turn accumulates `vr.snapTurn`; vehicle changes re-synchronize first orientation. `bg_pmove.cpp` implements IRL crouch by comparing `curHeight` with calibrated `maxHeight`, adjusting the native stance/collision path and avoiding a duplicate flat camera-height change. The source does not establish a separate swept roomscale body solver beyond native movement processing.\n\nVEHICLES: Jedi Academy vehicle input is a separate adapter. `VR_GetMove()` can use HMD direction or a snap-latched body direction; fighter/flier pitch can come from the average of weapon/offhand controller pitch. `VrInputDefault` also uses controller roll/pitch to influence vehicle yaw/acceleration depending on configuration. This is not physical steering-wheel interaction; it is controller-orientation adaptation into native vehicle controls.\n\nOPENXR\n\nINPUT: `OpenXrInput.cpp` creates one gameplay action set with left/right subaction paths, aim/grip pose spaces, vibration output, boolean actions, float triggers/squeeze and vector thumbstick/trackpad state, and suggests bindings for multiple known interaction profiles. `TBXR_SyncActions()` calls `xrSyncActions` once per update and `TBXR_UpdateControllers()` populates aim pose, grip pose and `XrSpaceVelocity`. `VrInputDefault` translates states into `sendButtonAction`, `sendButtonActionSimple`, `Sys_QueEvent` and movement variables so Raven continues receiving its existing semantic commands.\n\nWEAPON HANDS / PRESENTATION: The source exposes tracked weapon/offhand models and hand points but does not demonstrate a tracked full-body or shoulder/elbow IK avatar. Generic Ghoul2 IK APIs elsewhere in OpenJK must not be confused with a JKXR body-IK feature. Per-weapon `vr_weapon_adjustment_<id>` stores scale, xyz offset and pitch/yaw/roll corrections. `CG_CalculateWeaponPositionAndScale()` composes them after `BG_CalculateVRWeaponPosition`, mirrors relevant axes for left handedness, and the special weapon-alignment input mode can tune them live.\n\nTWO-HAND\n\nWEAPONS: Offhand grip is context-sensitive. Saber stabilization uses inter-controller proximity. Firearms additionally require the line between the two hands to align with dominant-controller forward (dot > 0.6). If accepted, `vr.weapon_stabilised` is set and averaged support-hand position drives the adjusted weapon direction; saber direction is inverted appropriately. Virtual-stock and scope-specific solvers are separate. If the offhand grip is not accepted as a weapon support, it becomes the Force/item selector instead. This separation prevents one grip button from meaning everything at once.\n\nPHYSICAL\n\nSCOPE: Disruptor and Blaster scope entry requires a healthy player, `weapon_stabilised`, and tracked weapon/HMD offset below `vr_scope_engage_distance` (~0.25 m). Moving the weapon away (>~0.26 m), losing stabilization or changing weapon sends the native exit-scope action. Scope rendering narrows the actual OpenXR eye FOV at submission; weapon solution can place the firing origin at the HMD while aligning the supported muzzle toward the headset. This is a physical intent gate around Raven's existing zoom state.\n\nFIREARMS / PROJECTILES: `BG_UseVRPosition()` guards local first-person VR usage. Numerous JA/JO weapon implementations call `BG_CalculateVRWeaponPosition()` to replace `muzzle/start` and derive forward angles immediately before `WP_TraceSetStart`, hitscan traces or `CreateMissile`. Examples include blaster pistol/rifle, bowcaster, disruptor, repeater, DEMP2, flechette, rocket launcher, concussion and vehicle/AT-ST paths. VR therefore changes where/direction the weapon fires but does not replace Raven's ammo, rate-of-fire, spread, projectile, damage/effect or weapon-state systems.\n\nLIGHTSABER — TRACKED BLADE INTO NATIVE COLLISION: In `cg_players.cpp`, the local first-person saber normally begins from the Ghoul2 blade bolt, but `CG_DoSaber` replaces `org_` and axis with `BG_CalculateVRSaberPosition()` when the saber is held and not in a lock/camera path. Those values propagate into the blade's current/old `muzzlePoint`/`muzzleDir` and `renderInfo.muzzlePoint`/`muzzleDir`. `WP_SaberDamageTrace()` then uses current versus old blade state through Raven's original saber sweep/Ghoul2 traces, water/world checks, `CONTENTS_LIGHTSABER`, damage accumulation and saber-intersection/parry logic. Saber entity bounds/block checks and missile deflection therefore consume the same physically tracked saber geometry. This is a true tracked-weapon/native-swept-collision architecture, not 'controller velocity = damage'.\n\nSABER ATTACK INTENT / THROW: `VrInputDefault` reads OpenXR controller linear velocity into `primaryswingvelocity`/`secondaryswingvelocity`. With velocity-triggering enabled, saber/stun-baton motion above a configurable threshold asserts native `+attack`; WP_MELEE maps right/left motion to the native alt/primary punch actions. For saber, attack intent and hit detection are separate: velocity gates attack state, while the tracked blade sweep determines contact. Physical saber throw is still the native `+altattack` mechanic; controller velocity acts as an arming gesture combined with trigger/release, not as a calculated projectile launch speed.\n\nSABER PARRY FEEDBACK: When the local saber receives native SEF_BLOCKED/SEF_PARRIED with a `saberBounceMove`, cgame records `saberBlockDebounce` and the Raven bounce direction and emits a haptic event. During that short debounce `VrInputDefault` adds a triangular time-shaped pitch/yaw correction based on LS_B1_* bounce direction before final tracked saber orientation. The physical hilt therefore reacts to the accepted native clash without becoming gameplay authority.\n\nFORCE PUSH/PULL GESTURES: When configured, a fast offhand movement above `vr_force_velocity_trigger` stores a gesture-start hand position. When the hand slows, the code constructs an approximate chest below the HMD, compares start/end radial distance, derives a palm-away flag from HMD-forward versus controller-right (mirrored by handedness), and if the distance delta exceeds `vr_force_distance_trigger` invokes configured native push or pull via `useGivenForce`. Dual sabers, melee and two-hand weapon stabilization deliberately suppress this classifier.\n\nFORCE RAYS / GRIP DEPTH: First-person Force Grip/Lightning use the tracked offhand. `BG_CalculateVROffHandPosition` supplies direction and `renderInfo.handLPoint` supplies origin for traces/effects. For high-level Grip, the code records initial physical offhand-to-HMD distance when the target is established. Subsequent physical hand depth changes alter the desired native hold distance, clamped to a safe range, while Raven's LOS checks, target eligibility and `WP_ForcePowerStop` remain authoritative. This is direct spatialization of an existing telekinesis mechanic, not a replacement physics-grab system.\n\nPHYSICAL USE: `VrInputDefault` turns weapon/offhand distance beyond a body boundary into `+use`/`+altuse` while appropriate. `G_TouchTriggersLerped` suppresses body activation of USE_BUTTON triggers under this mode. `G_TouchTriggersWithHand()` then queries a small box around the corresponding tracked hand (with an offset to compensate trigger depth), enforces facing for directional buttons, excludes hurt triggers, emits hand haptics, and calls the original `GEntity_TouchFunc`. VR changes the spatial activator while native trigger scripts stay untouched.\n\nSPATIAL SELECTORS: Dominant grip opens weapon/stance/inventory selection; unsupported offhand grip opens Force/quick selection. `CG_DrawItemSelector()` snapshots the opening hand pose, creates a tracked radial selector, converts controller angular displacement into a 2D selection vector, slows `timescale` to 0.22, emits per-icon haptics and commits via the original selection commands when the grip is released.\n\nHAPTICS: OpenXR exposes a vibration output action per hand. `TBXR_Vibrate` stores per-channel duration/intensity and `TBXR_ProcessHaptics` applies/stops `XrHapticVibration`. Game code sends semantic `VR_HapticEvent` names for pickups, weapon switches/fire, saber feedback, Force effects/use buttons, etc.; platform code selects dominant/support/both channels and can forward the same named event to an external haptics service. This is an event-driven feedback layer rather than haptics tied directly to raw buttons.\n\nCOMFORT: `CG_DrawVignette` activates a configurable vignette based on native player velocity, airborne state and smooth-turn yaw deltas; it fades the effect rather than simply turning it on/off. This correctly keys comfort to artificial/world movement rather than raw HMD movement.\n\nMULTIPLAYER / BOUNDARIES: The supplied tree contains OpenJK multiplayer source as well as SP code, but this JKXR integration does not demonstrate a VR pose packet/state or remote tracked-hand/body reconstruction comparable to L4D2VR. Do not infer remote XR embodiment merely because OpenJK has networking. Likewise, the source does not establish full-body IK, OpenXR skeletal finger retargeting, manual magazine reloads or controller-velocity projectile throwing.\n\nREUSABLE MODEL: predicted-time XR frame owner -> freeze legacy simulation across stereo replay -> true asymmetric eye projection + conservative legacy FOV -> explicit camera/screen-mode rebasing -> centralized tracking state -> semantic OpenXR actions -> native movement/input -> tracked weapon/muzzle adapters -> tracked saber into native swept blade history -> native block event back to hilt/haptics -> physical Force/use gestures into original gameplay contracts -> spatial legacy UI/selection.",
  "pattern_jkxr_openxr_frame_owner": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`JKXR/windows|android/TBXR_Common.cpp`, `OpenXrInput.cpp`, `code/client/cl_scrn.cpp`). `TBXR_FrameSetup` calls `xrWaitFrame`/`xrBeginFrame`; views, HMD and controller aim/grip spaces are located at `FrameState.predictedDisplayTime`; both eye buffers are rendered and `xrEndFrame` submits with the same display time. Reusable rule: make one engine-frame subsystem own the XR frame and cached prediction.",
  "pattern_jkxr_stereo_replay_freeze": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`code/client/cl_scrn.cpp`, `cl_cgame.cpp`, `code/cgame/cg_view.cpp`). The native cgame is run for STEREO_LEFT and STEREO_RIGHT. Server/Ghoul2 time and snapshots advance only on the left; `Rand_Init(cg.time)` is repeated for both eyes. This prevents animation/particle/random divergence while retaining sequential native rendering.",
  "pattern_jkxr_asymmetric_projection_culling": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`TBXR_Common.cpp`, platform `JKXR_SurfaceView.cpp`, `rd-gles/tr_main.cpp`, `rd-vanilla/tr_main.cpp`). `XrView.fov` is preserved per eye and used by `XrMatrix4x4f_CreateProjectionFov`; submission uses the matching FOV/pose. `vr.fov_x/y` stores a broad combined extent for legacy camera/culling consumers. Do not confuse that scalar with an exact union frustum.",
  "pattern_jkxr_screen_layer_arbitration": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`JKXR_SurfaceView.cpp`, `TBXR_Common.cpp`). `VR_UseScreenLayer` identifies menus/loading/console/nonimmersive cinematics. Gameplay submits an XR projection layer; screen states submit a Stage-space yaw-latched `XrCompositionLayerQuad` backed by the game framebuffer, with a null/black projection underneath. This cleanly separates immersive and flat presentation contracts.",
  "pattern_jkxr_asymmetric_hud_compensation": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`TBXR_prepareEyeBuffer`, `code/cgame/cg_drawtools.cpp`, `cg_draw.cpp`). Per-eye `off_center_fov_x/y` comes from the angular center of the asymmetric runtime frustum and is added back into the legacy 640×480 HUD/zoom coordinates. This is a compact way to fix off-axis HMD optical centers without rewriting every HUD widget.",
  "pattern_jkxr_tracking_snap_camera_modes": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrClientInfo.h`, platform `JKXR_SurfaceView.cpp`, `code/cgame/cg_camera.cpp`). JKXR stores current/last/snap HMD pose and deltas. Screen-mode changes, cinematic state transitions and large camera keyframe jumps request `take_snap`; immersive yaw becomes authored stored yaw + HMD delta since snap + snap turn. Tracking rebasing is therefore an explicit camera lifecycle primitive.",
  "pattern_jkxr_native_locomotion_irl_crouch": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`, `JKXR_SurfaceView.cpp`, `code/game/bg_pmove.cpp`). HMD translation delta is yaw-rotated into native positional movement; stick locomotion can be controller-relative; IRL crouch compares `curHeight` to calibrated `maxHeight` and modifies native stance/view-height handling to avoid a double crouch camera drop. No separate custom swept roomscale mover is demonstrated.",
  "pattern_jkxr_openxr_actions_native_input": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXrInput.cpp`, `VrInputDefault.cpp`). One gameplay action set exposes left/right subaction paths, aim/grip spaces, boolean/float/vector controls and haptics; suggested bindings cover multiple interaction profiles. State is translated to existing OpenJK commands/events rather than creating a second gameplay command format.",
  "pattern_jkxr_legacy_menu_pointer": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`, platform input files). Controller angles relative to a latched menu yaw feed `interactWithTouchScreen`/`PortableMouseAbs`, which reaches the existing mouse-event path; menu handedness can switch. It is a pragmatic angular-pointer adapter over legacy 640×480 UI.",
  "pattern_jkxr_two_hand_weapon_solver": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`). Offhand grip becomes weapon stabilization only when geometry is plausible: sabers use close hand distance; firearms also require controller-forward/inter-hand dot > 0.6. Stabilized mode computes weapon orientation from the support vector, with virtual-stock/scope variants. Otherwise the same grip remains a Force-selector command.",
  "pattern_jkxr_physical_scope_engagement": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`, `TBXR_Common.cpp`). Supported weapon + stabilization + weapon/HMD distance below `vr_scope_engage_distance` sends native `enterscope`; moving away/loss of stabilization sends `exitscope`. OpenXR submission narrows the real eye FOV during zoom. Physical pose is the gate, Raven zoom remains authority.",
  "pattern_jkxr_per_weapon_calibration": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`code/cgame/cg_weapons.cpp`, `VrInputWeaponAlign.cpp`). `vr_weapon_adjustment_<weapon>` persists scale, xyz and pitch/yaw/roll. `CG_CalculateWeaponPositionAndScale` composes it after the common tracked weapon pose and mirrors left-handed corrections; a dedicated calibration control mode tunes values live.",
  "pattern_jkxr_native_weapon_muzzle": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (JA/JO `code*/game/wp_*.cpp`, `bg_misc.cpp`). Weapon routines conditionally call `BG_CalculateVRWeaponPosition` for local first person immediately before native trace/projectile construction. This preserves Raven ammo/cadence/spread/projectiles/damage while changing the spatial launch ray.",
  "pattern_jkxr_tracked_saber_native_sweep": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`code/cgame/cg_players.cpp`, `code/game/wp_saber.cpp`). Local first-person `CG_DoSaber` replaces the Ghoul2 blade origin/axis with `BG_CalculateVRSaberPosition`; current/old blade muzzle state feeds `WP_SaberDamageTrace` and native `CONTENTS_LIGHTSABER`/parry/deflection logic. This is genuine tracked blade geometry entering Raven's native swept saber system.",
  "pattern_jkxr_saber_parry_feedback": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cg_players.cpp`, `VrInputDefault.cpp`). Accepted native block/parry state stores `saberBounceMove`/debounce and produces haptics; the VR input layer turns the specific Raven bounce move into a brief triangular pitch/yaw hilt offset. Gameplay decides the clash; VR renders the consequence.",
  "pattern_jkxr_velocity_attack_gate": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXrInput.cpp`, `VrInputDefault.cpp`). `XrSpaceVelocity` is captured with controller poses. Configurable speed thresholds map physical punches/saber/baton swings to native `+attack`/`+altattack`. This is an intent gate; saber contact itself is separately handled by the tracked native blade sweep.",
  "pattern_jkxr_force_push_pull_gesture": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`). A high-speed offhand motion starts capture; when speed drops, chest-relative start/end radial delta plus controller-derived palm orientation and handedness distinguish push from pull, then `useGivenForce` invokes the configured native Force power. The classifier is disabled during conflicting dual-saber/two-hand states.",
  "pattern_jkxr_force_grip_depth": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`code/game/wp_saber.cpp`, `bg_misc.cpp`). First-person Force Grip/Lightning use tracked offhand direction/`handLPoint`. Level-3 Grip stores initial offhand-HMD distance and later hand-depth deltas modify native desired hold distance, while LOS/eligibility/Force resource rules remain Raven-owned.",
  "pattern_jkxr_hand_touch_native_use": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`, `code/game/g_active.cpp`, `g_trigger.cpp`). Body-relative hand extension asserts `+use/+altuse`; ordinary body touching skips USE_BUTTON triggers; `G_TouchTriggersWithHand` tests a small hand box, respects directional facing, excludes hurt triggers and calls original `GEntity_TouchFunc` with hand-specific haptics.",
  "pattern_jkxr_spatial_radial_selector": "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrInputDefault.cpp`, `code/cgame/cg_weapons.cpp`). Grip captures weapon/offhand pose and opens a hand-anchored weapon/Force/quick radial selector. Controller angular displacement chooses wedges; `timescale` is set to 0.22; selection haptics fire; release commits through existing game selection commands.",
  "seed_farcryvr_case":"Source-audit (2026-09-05): The repository exposes a real CryEngine Mod SDK Sources tree plus steamvr, Installer, Tools and FCData directories. The .gitmodules file pins DXVK, MinHook and OpenVR as explicit native dependencies. The README verifies roomscale 6DOF, two-handed weapons, binocular/scope handling, manual ladder climbing and haptics; release notes also record a pre-render weapon-position synchronization fix to prevent laser-sight jumping. Reusable pattern: keep hand ownership, support-hand weapon constraints, tool/scope state and pre-render weapon pose synchronization as separate layers. Known limits are equally important: two-hand orientation can reverse at some angles, machete hit detection is imperfect, grenades are button-triggered rather than physically thrown, and multiplayer motion controls are incomplete. FRAME TIMING beyond the documented pre-render weapon sync: UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_gmodvr_case":"FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-07; supplied Metastruct `vrmod-main` + classic `vrmod-module-master-170625`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly marked as a boundary.\n\nARCHITECTURE: The supplied source is deliberately two-layered. `vrmod-module-master-170625/src/vrmod.cpp` starts OpenVR as `VRApplication_Scene`, exposes a compact Lua ABI (`GetDisplayInfo`, `UpdatePosesAndActions`, `GetPoses`, `GetActions`, texture-share/submission calls, `TriggerHaptic`, tracked-device names), and keeps OpenVR/D3D/GL handles out of Lua. `vrmod-main/lua/vrmod/core/cl_vrmod.lua` loads it with `require(\"vrmod\")`, aliases the exported functions as `VRMOD_*`, then coordinates Source rendering, tracked-world state and the independent gameplay subsystems. This is the classic OpenVR generation; keep it distinct from the registry's separate `seed_gvrmod_openxr_case` modern OpenXR backend.\n\nRENDERING: `GetDisplayInfo` supplies OpenVR recommended per-eye resolution, raw left/right projection matrices and eye-to-head transforms. `utils/cl_rendering.lua::CalculateProjectionParams` converts each projection to the horizontal FOV/aspect Source's `render.RenderView` accepts, while preserving the projection-center terms for later UV correction. `SetupRenderTargets` creates one shared render target whose width is twice the recommended eye width. `PerformRenderViews` draws the left eye into one half and the right eye into the other. `ComputeSubmitBounds` converts the raw projection-center asymmetry into distinct compositor UV rectangles; Linux also flips V. The native module then submits the same shared texture twice with those bounds. This is not equivalent to feeding Source a complete asymmetric off-axis projection matrix: Source receives FOV/aspect, while part of the asymmetry is compensated at compositor texture bounds.\n\nFRAME TIMING: The Lua `RenderScene` hook is the explicit frame contract: `VRMOD_UpdatePosesAndActions()` → `UpdateTracking()` → local hand/weapon collision and viewmodel pose update → input processing → local multiplayer-frame construction → camera/view-entity update → sequential left/right `RenderView` calls → `VRMOD_SubmitSharedTexture()` → `VRMod_PostRender`. Native `UpdatePosesAndActions` calls `VRCompositor()->WaitGetPoses` and `VRInput()->UpdateActionState`; the module README explicitly says this call also synchronizes game FPS to headset refresh. The source does not freeze simulation/nondeterministic render state between the two `RenderView` calls, so do not generalize this as a deterministic stereo replay architecture.\n\nCAMERA / TRACKING: Native OpenVR poses are converted to Garry's Mod axes before Lua sees them: position/velocity map OpenVR XYZ to `(-Z,-X,+Y)` and angular velocity is remapped the same way with rad/s→deg/s. `UpdateTracking` then applies world scale, `g_VR.origin`, `g_VR.originAngle`, controller offsets and optional pose smoothing. Room/world conversion is centralized around `LocalToWorld` rather than recomputed independently by weapons, networking and UI. Seated/artificial-crouch code post-processes HMD and both hands together so their relative geometry survives the mode change.\n\nINPUT: `cl_steamvr_bindings.lua` defines hand/foot/waist pose actions, left/right skeleton actions, left/right haptic outputs, a main action set and a separate driving action set. Native `GetActions` returns booleans, analog1/analog2 and OpenVR skeletal-summary curls; the Lua input layer consumes only changed booleans for edge-triggered `+attack/-attack`, `+reload/-reload`, movement, flashlight and menu commands, and swaps the active action set when entering/exiting vehicles. This is a semantic XR-action → legacy Source command bridge rather than a replacement input stack.\n\nHANDS: Tracked hand poses feed two distinct presentation paths: floating hand models in `cl_character_hands.lua`, or full ValveBiped embodiment in `cl_character.lua`. OpenVR skeleton summaries provide five curl values per hand, which are interpolated through authored/open/closed ValveBiped finger-angle arrays. Client-side collision can alter the presented hand pose without changing the raw tracking sample.\n\nBODY / IK: `cl_character.lua::CharacterInit` measures the active player model's clavicle, upper/lower arm, spine and leg lengths. `UpdateIK::ProcessArm` partially aims the clavicle toward the tracked hand, solves shoulder/elbow bend with law-of-cosines geometry using those measured segment lengths, applies body-yaw/hand-height roll policy, and decomposes forearm/wrist orientation so wrist twist can be shared with an explicit ulna bone when present. HMD height also reconstructs crouched spine/leg posing, but waist/foot trackers are not consumed by this avatar solver. The source therefore establishes arm/body IK but not tracker-driven full-body leg IK.\n\nWEAPONS: The ordinary Source viewmodel remains the local weapon representation. On weapon switch, the addon can derive an initial per-class tracked offset from the model's `ValveBiped.Bip01_R_Hand` bone and extract a compatible right-hand finger pose from the weapon model; `cl_viewmodeledit.lua` then allows per-class position/angle, muzzle-direction and laser corrections saved to JSON. `utils/sh_collisions.lua` can build weapon collision geometry from the actual model's VPhysics/AABB shape, while `utils/sh_weps.lua::UpdateViewModelPos` applies tracked pose, wall pushout and the calibrated offset before refreshing attachment 1 as the VR muzzle. The client also sends that muzzle pose to the server, where `sh_network.lua` adjusts the current view offset when the supplied muzzle is valid/in-world so view-derived native weapon logic can follow the VR presentation more closely.\n\nMELEE: `combat/sh_combat.lua` measures hand velocity relative to HMD motion, gates attack attempts by a replicated speed threshold, and traces current hand/tool geometry derived from the model cache. The client sends intended geometry, but the server re-obtains HMD-relative hand speed from its VR pose API and repeats the box/sphere trace before creating `DamageInfo`, damage type, force, sound/decal and `VRMod_MeleeHit` callbacks. Damage scales with the server-side swing speed and impact classification. This is velocity-gated current-pose geometry with server re-tracing, not a historical swept blade volume; although target velocity is read, the current source sets `relativeSpeed = swingSpeed` rather than subtracting target motion.\n\nPHYSICAL INTERACTION: Local hand/SWEP wall collision, server physics-hand proxies and gameplay manipulation are separate layers. `UpdateHandCollisions`/`CheckWeaponPushout` perform client presentation pushout. `sv_physhands.lua` creates hidden server physics proxies and drives them toward server VR hand poses using `ComputeShadowControl`. Pickup selects a nearby candidate client-side but the server runs `ValidatePickup`, retrieves the authoritative server hand transform, stores the object's hand-relative transform and drives the rigid body with a single `vrmod_pickup` motion controller; ragdolls retain per-physics-bone offsets. Physical world buttons query a server-side hand position and call native `Entity:Use`. Door interaction instead uses authored map/model/bone grab anchors and snaps the visual hand before invoking native Use; that particular server message is rate-limited but does not re-check the spatial orientation constraints.\n\nLOCOMOTION: `sh_locomotion.lua` keeps Source movement authoritative. Stick input is oriented by HMD or controller yaw and written through `CreateMove` forward/side movement; HMD height can set `IN_DUCK`; snap/smooth turning rotates `g_VR.origin` around the player rather than rotating the physical HMD; roomscale follow reconciles the VR origin against player velocity and moving-ground velocity. The included parabolic teleport is intentionally weaker: server teleport requires noclip privilege and uses `SetPos` without full player-hull/trigger-path validation. `sh_climbing.lua` is a different, single-player-only system built from map-authored HL2 ladder rung/dismount definitions and inverse hand displacement.\n\nVEHICLES: Vehicle code searches the actual vehicle skeleton for steering-wheel/handlebar bones, captures each gripping hand's pose relative to the selected bone, and reattaches the presented/network pose as the wheel animates. Steering is calculated from hand displacement around a captured neutral point, blended if both hands grip, then written into native vehicle `CreateMove` controls. Glide aircraft use a related policy but map controller angular displacement into Glide's pitch/yaw/roll input API. Vehicle entry also switches to the dedicated driving OpenVR action set and anchors the VR origin to the vehicle driver's-eye attachment.\n\nUI: `cl_ui.lua` paints ordinary Garry's Mod VGUI into render targets, places them as `cam.Start3D2D` quads in hand/world space, intersects a controller ray with the panel plane, converts the hit to panel pixels and reuses native VGUI cursor/mouse press/release functions. `cl_keypad.lua` temporarily patches supported keypad `CalculateCursorPos` only while the tracked hand is close and actually rays onto the panel, then restores the original method. `cl_hud.lua` captures selected flat HUD groups and redraws them on a curved mesh near the HMD.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: Client frames contain timestamp, 7-bit quantized character yaw, ten 7-bit finger curls, HMD/left/right hand transforms, and optionally waist/left-foot/right-foot transforms. Frames are transformed to player/vehicle-relative space, identical samples can be skipped, and the server rate/size-limits receive before relaying. The client remote path called `LerpOtherVRPlayers` currently copies the newest received frame and transforms it to world; it is not interpolation. Separately, `api/sv_api.lua::UpdateWorldPoses` derives world-space linear/angular velocities from timestamped samples using an adaptive interval and predicts HMD/hands about one average frame ahead for server gameplay queries. That server prediction is distinct from OpenVR compositor pose prediction.\n\nHAPTICS: The native module exposes `TriggerHaptic(actionName, delay, duration, frequency, amplitude)` and directly calls `IVRInput::TriggerHapticVibrationAction`; left/right haptic output actions exist in the supplied manifest. However, no gameplay/addon call site invokes `VRMOD_TriggerHaptic` in the supplied Metastruct Lua tree. Treat this as a working native capability endpoint whose semantic gameplay wiring is absent in this snapshot, not as evidence of active combat/UI feedback.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: Native: `src/vrmod.cpp`, module `README`. Lua core/render: `core/cl_vrmod.lua`, `utils/cl_rendering.lua`. Input: `input/cl_input.lua`, `input/cl_steamvr_bindings.lua`. Avatar: `player/cl_character.lua`, `player/cl_character_hands.lua`. Weapons/collision: `network/sh_network.lua`, `utils/sh_weps.lua`, `utils/sh_collisions.lua`, `player/cl_viewmodeledit.lua`. Physics/interaction: `physics/sv_physhands.lua`, `pickup/sh_pickup.lua`, `utils/sh_pickup.lua`, `combat/sh_combat.lua`, `input/sh_buttons.lua`, `input/sh_doors.lua`. Movement/vehicles: `input/sh_locomotion.lua`, `input/sh_climbing.lua`, `utils/sh_vehicles.lua`, `input/sh_glide.lua`, `input/sh_gravity_and_physgun.lua`. UI/API: `ui/cl_ui.lua`, `input/cl_keypad.lua`, `ui/cl_hud.lua`, `api/cl_api.lua`, `api/sv_api.lua`.\n\nLIMITATIONS: The supplied native Windows sharing code is internally inconsistent at final submission: `ShareTextureFinish` opens the D3D9 shared handle as a D3D11 resource and sets `TextureType_DirectX`, but `SubmitSharedTexture` later overwrites the texture handle with `g_sharedTexture` and forces `TextureType_OpenGL` unconditionally. Preserve this as a source-snapshot bug/boundary rather than claiming the Windows D3D11 compositor submit path is validated. The full stereo path does not establish a second-eye simulation freeze. Tracker poses for waist/feet are transported but not used for tracked leg IK. Normal rigid-body drop does not explicitly write controller release velocity in the reviewed path. Door-grab server use does not spatially revalidate the authored hand-angle/proximity test. Teleport is noclip-privileged and not a robust collision/trigger-aware teleport. Climbing is single-player/map-authored. Gameplay haptics are not wired to the native output endpoint.",
  "seed_vivecraft_case":"FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-06; supplied `VivecraftMod-26.2-1.3.15.zip`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless noted. Vivecraft 1.3.15 is a multiloader Java/Mixin VR retrofit with a dedicated OpenVR provider, explicit multi-pass renderer, staged room/world pose state, physical gameplay trackers, full-body avatar solve, versioned multiplayer VR pose transport and a public extension API. This supplied branch is OpenVR/SteamVR; README explicitly leaves OpenXR support unchecked.\n\nRUNTIME/RENDER: `OpenVRStereoRenderer` uses runtime-recommended eye size, hidden-area meshes and raw asymmetric projection bounds. OpenGL submits texture handles directly; Vulkan submits `VRVulkanTextureData` after validating compositor-required Vulkan extensions. `MCOpenVR.updatePose` is paced by `VRCompositor_WaitGetPoses`, then updates OpenVR action state, HMD/controllers/FBT trackers and eye-to-head transforms. There is no OpenXR predicted-display-time contract in this snapshot. `VRPassHelper`/`VRRenderer` enumerate LEFT/RIGHT/CENTER/THIRD/SCOPEL/SCOPER/CAMERA as explicit render passes and route them through distinct `WorldRenderPass` targets before final compositor submission.\n\nSPACES/TIMING: `VRPlayer` maintains room-pre, world-pre, room-post, world-post and render-interpolated VR snapshots. `VRData` contains HMD, eye, AIM/HAND controller, scope/camera and FBT poses relative to explicit origin/rotation/world scale. `roomToWorldPos`/`worldToRoomPos` centralize conversion. This is the architectural reason physical swing histories can live in room space while contact and networking use world space.\n\nROOMSCALE: `doPlayerMoveInRoom` tests the actual Minecraft player AABB at the physical body target with `level.noCollision`, moves the native player when valid and can search upward in 0.1-block increments for step-up. FBT feet near the floor may establish body X/Z. Free locomotion can orient from HMD/controller/waist/run-in-place/room/treadmill while native Minecraft physics remains authoritative.\n\nFBT/BODY IK: `MCVR` supports waist, both feet, knees and elbows. `calibrateFBT` maps labeled or unlabeled trackers against scaled body reference positions and stores positional/rotational calibration offsets. `VRData.FBTMode` distinguishes ARMS_ONLY, ARMS_LEGS and WITH_JOINTS. `VRPlayerModel` plus arm/leg subclasses place tracked endpoints, align torso from waist, estimate missing elbow/knee joints or use explicit joint trackers, solve connected/split limbs, limit extension and optionally blend vanilla animation. This is genuine player-visible body/limb reconstruction and justifies the Body IK tag.\n\nNETWORK: `VrPlayerState` serializes HMD/hands plus optional waist/feet/knees/elbows with FBT-mode/version negotiation. Client sends pose/world-scale/height; server stores in `ServerVivePlayer` and broadcasts; `ClientVRPlayers` lerps positions/nlerps orientations and reconstructs remote FBT avatars. Active body-part and aim-override packets form a separate semantic gameplay ABI so vanilla server item/projectile code can use tracked spatial origins without receiving runtime-specific data structures.\n\nPROJECTILES/WEAPONS: `ServerVivePlayer.getAimPos/getAimDir` supplies tracked spatial authority. Item/projectile/crossbow/throwable/trident/fishing mixins replace flat eye/view seams but preserve vanilla item/projectile/damage rules. Physical bow geometry wraps the native item-use/release pipeline and sends draw percentage; server projectile speed is adjusted from physical draw.\n\nINTERACTION: `InteractTracker` priority-arbitrates per-hand `InteractModule`s and only activates VR-interact when a module is physically valid. `SwingTracker` measures attack speed in room space and contact in world space with swept hand/tool AABBs, then calls native entity attack/block use/destroy paths. Hotbar, block/entity interaction, backpack zones and physical keyboard all feed ordinary Minecraft actions rather than parallel VR gameplay systems.\n\nLOCOMOTION: Climbey latches hands to climbable geometry, moves the native player opposite hand motion with AABB correction, supports top-out and controller-derived release jump. Teleport integrates a controller ballistic arc and validates destinations with the native player AABB plus server policy limits/energy. HMD height drives native sneak/crawl; physical jump, realistic swim and row trackers convert tracked motion into native player/boat motion.\n\nUI/OPTICS: `GuiHandler` intersects controller rays with a room-space GUI plane and maps hits to ordinary mouse pixels. `PhysicalKeyboard` uses 3D key AABBs with debounce/repeat. Physical telescope activation is eye-to-lens distance/alignment and can request dedicated left/right scope render passes.\n\nHAPTICS/API: OpenVR controller pulses use a scheduler over `VRInput_TriggerHapticVibrationAction`; server events can target semantic `VRBodyPart`s; a separate body-haptic rig spatializes damage/environmental feedback. `VRClientAPI` exposes staged poses/history/FBT/world scale/haptics and registration hooks for custom Tracker/InteractModule extensions; rendering API exposes pass context.\n\nBOUNDARIES: no OpenXR implementation in this supplied branch; `HorseTracker` is present but hard-disabled and is not counted as an active feature. Teleport validates placement but does not replay crossed game triggers. Remote HMD/hands/FBT pose replication is fully implemented in this source.",
  "seed_acecombat7_case":"Source-audit: Ace Combat 7's UEVR plugin narrowly corrects cockpit camera state, adds a manual recovery action and preserves the game's native RC control modes. The reusable pattern is specialized camera-state patching with explicit recovery rather than a global camera overwrite.",

  "pattern_doom3vr_stereo_late_pose":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. Key files/symbols: `neo/vr/Vr.cpp::HMDInitializeDistortion`, `FrameStart`, `HMDGetOrientation`; `neo/d3xp/PlayerView.cpp::EmitStereoEyeView`; `neo/renderer/GLMatrix.cpp::R_SetupProjectionMatrix`; `neo/renderer/OpenGL/gl_backend.cpp::RB_StereoRenderExecuteBackEndCommands`; `neo/renderer/tr_backend_draw.cpp` late HMD delta block; `neo/vr/Vr_Gl.cpp::HMDRender`. Transfer: retain a stable simulation pose but reapply only current-minus-old head delta at the last renderer matrix seam.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_tracking_body_spaces":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. `iVr::HMDGetOrientation` contains the meters-to-engine-scale conversion, axis/quaternion remap, tracking-origin compensation and neck/head/body decomposition. `idPlayer::CalculateRenderView`, `Move` and `UpdateNeckPose` consume different components. Transfer: normalize runtime coordinates once and distribute named pose products to consumers instead of allowing each subsystem to transform XR independently.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_weapon_ik_animation_bridge":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. Key symbols: `idPlayer::CalculateViewWeaponPosVR`, `idPlayer::SetHandIKPos`, `idIK_Reach::Init/Evaluate`, `idIK::SolveTwoArmBones`. The weapon attachment joint animation delta is deliberately preserved inside a controller-driven target. Transfer: treat weapon animation as a local-space correction on top of tracked physical pose, then solve the avatar hand/arm to the corrected target.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_semantic_hand_slots":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. Key symbols: `idPlayerHand`, `CalculateWaist`, `CalculateLeftHand`, `CalculateRightHand`, `WeaponHandImpulseSlot`, `OtherHandImpulseSlot`, `GrabWorld`, `TriggerClickWorld`. Transfer: spatial interaction should resolve into semantic actions before legacy input fallback, with hand state persistent across frames.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_fingertip_native_gui":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. `Player.cpp` touch-GUI path resolves `RindexTip`/`LindexTip`, performs `GuiTrace`, converts the hit to existing mouse events and forwards the returned GUI commands. Transfer: spatial pointer/finger -> native GUI coordinate -> native pointer event is often lower-risk than rebuilding a flat UI in VR.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_roomscale_capsule_lean":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. `idPlayer::Move` is the core: requested HMD body displacement is offered to normal player physics; blocked residual becomes capped lean; collision re-tests later reconcile body origin. Physical duck becomes native crouch. Transfer: preserve authoritative capsule collision and model roomscale as requested body motion plus temporary residual head offset.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_throw_native_projectile":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. `idPlayerHand::TrackWeaponDirection` keeps ten time/position samples, compares current to five samples back and exposes direction/velocity. `Weapon.cpp` consumes them for VR projectile/grenade launch. Transfer: derive only launch direction/speed from controller history and keep native projectile ownership.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_teleport_trigger_replay":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. Key symbols: `GetTeleportBeamOrigin`, `UpdateTeleportAim`, `TeleportPathSegment`, `TeleportPath`, `CheckTeleportPathSegment`, `CheckTeleportPath`. AAS/path segments are replayed through trigger/door contact before relocation. Transfer: teleport must preserve gameplay side effects that ordinary walking would have fired.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_pose_directed_native_melee":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. `idWeapon::Event_Melee` substitutes tracked weapon/muzzle pose in VR but otherwise preserves the original native/server-side melee trace and damage pipeline; fists/chainsaw/flashlight scripts still invoke `melee()`. Transfer: hook origin/direction first, preserve damage authority.",
  "pattern_doom3vr_native_command_network_boundary":"Source-audit 2026-09-06. `UsercmdGen.cpp` maps VR controls into ordinary player commands; `usercmd_t::Serialize` contains no raw XR pose fields. Transfer: for existing multiplayer games, first adapt VR input to the established command vocabulary and keep local head/hands as presentation state unless remote embodiment explicitly requires pose replication.",
  "seed_doom3bfgvr_case":"Source-audit (2026-09-06, supplied DOOM-3-BFG-VR source ZIP). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. ARCHITECTURE — `neo/vr/Vr.h` defines central `iVr` state and the runtime-facing API; `neo/d3xp/Player.h` defines two persistent `idPlayerHand` gameplay objects. The implementation is deliberately cross-layer: runtime/tracking (`Vr.cpp`) feeds renderer (`Vr_Gl.cpp`, `PlayerView.cpp`, `GLMatrix.cpp`, OpenGL backend), input (`UsercmdGen.cpp`), player physics/camera/interaction (`Player.cpp`), animation/IK (`IK.cpp`), weapons (`Weapon.cpp`) and scripts (`ai_player.script`, `weapon_*.script`). RENDERING — `HMDInitializeDistortion` obtains OpenVR `GetProjectionRaw`/recommended target size or Oculus `ovr_GetRenderDesc`/FOV texture size, populates per-eye state in `vr_hmd.h`, creates the primary/resolve framebuffer arrangement and runtime eye textures. `EmitStereoEyeView` offsets individual eye views; `R_SetupProjectionMatrix` uses asymmetric runtime frusta; `RB_StereoRenderExecuteBackEndCommands` renders both eye command streams; `iVr::HMDRender` submits separate textures to the compositor and mirrors independently. FRAME TIMING — Oculus uses predicted display time before `ovr_GetTrackingState`; OpenVR uses `WaitGetPoses`. `common_frame.cpp`/OpenGL swap code establish frame sampling seams. Most importantly, `tr_backend_draw.cpp` computes current-minus-view-build HMD position/orientation immediately before draw, removes/reapplies IPD eye offset, updates the draw-view origin/axis and rebuilds view/MVP matrices: a real late HMD correction after game simulation. COORDINATE SPACES — runtime meters are converted to id units with world scale; runtime XYZ/quaternion axes are explicitly remapped; tracking-origin translation/yaw is removed; then absolute HMD pose, neck/body displacement, head displacement, hand pose and body yaw are retained separately. This prevents camera, locomotion, IK and weapons from fighting over one transform. CAMERA — `CalculateRenderView` layers HMD pose onto gameplay/cinematic cameras, stores last HMD view state and has dedicated projected-cinematic behavior. `GetViewPosVR` suppresses ordinary damage/view kick while retaining required smoothing. `UpdateNeckPose` maps the HMD orientation into the avatar neck, and `AdjustBodyAngles` avoids flat-screen torso pitch blending that would fold the body into the VR camera. ROOMSCALE / COLLISION / CROUCH — `Move` transforms HMD body displacement by body yaw and moves the ordinary physics body with `MotionMove`. Blocked remainder becomes bounded lean state; later collision tests allow the capsule to catch up. A separate eye collision can blank the view at walls. Physical duck amount becomes native crouch input rather than a separate locomotion simulation. INPUT — Touch/OpenVR controller axes and buttons are normalized in the legacy usercmd generator. Movement direction can reference body, HMD look, weapon hand or either controller; snap/comfort turning edits the existing view yaw. Context-sensitive grip/trigger calls VR semantic handlers first and legacy bindings second. HANDS / PHYSICAL INVENTORY — `idPlayerHand` owns per-hand gameplay state, not just a mesh: weapon/ideal/previous weapon, grip/virtual-grab state, trigger/thumb state, slot, throw vector history and haptics. Waist-relative slot spheres are evaluated per hand, mirrored for handedness and converted into weapon/PDA/flashlight actions with haptic entry feedback. `ai_player.script` maintains independent weapon/flashlight hand channels and finger pose combinations from controller states. IK — `idIK_Reach::Init` resolves shoulder/elbow/hand/wrist joints and bind lengths; `Evaluate` solves two arm bones, derives elbow bend direction from the base rig and constrains/distributes controller roll into wrist/forearm orientation. Disabled experimental arm-stretch code is not treated as an active feature. WEAPON TRANSFORMS — `CalculateViewWeaponPosVR` samples controller pose, applies weapon calibration, maps it through body/head space and lets the controller own the weapon transform. `SetHandIKPos` then samples the active weapon's animated hand-attacher joint, converts its current-vs-default delta into the controller target, subtracts hand-attacher-to-wrist offsets/per-weapon correction, and overrides the player hand IK joint. This is the central weapon/animation coexistence technique. AIM / LASER — muzzle helpers expose tracked weapon orientation to firing/laser/teleport code; `UpdateLaserSight` traces from the physical muzzle and positions a world beam/crosshair. THROWING — `TrackWeaponDirection` uses a ten-sample history and a five-sample-back baseline to estimate direction/speed; grenade/projectile launch consumes that native-compatible result, scaled by `vr_throwPower`. MELEE — `Event_Melee` switches from camera origin to tracked muzzle/weapon origin in VR but retains normal native trace, impulse, damage/effects and server-side authority. Fist/chainsaw/flashlight scripts call the same `melee()` event. It is explicitly a pose-directed ray melee implementation, not swept controller collision. RELOAD / WEAPON STATE — player reload invokes the linked native weapon `Reload()` and ordinary scripts retain attack/reload/net-reload/raise/lower/cinematic state. VR presentation is layered around that contract rather than replacing it. GUI / PDA — touch GUI mode obtains the animated index-tip joint, traces through the original 3D GUI surface and injects native mouse motion/down/up events; PDA interaction uses the same physical hand/IK system while preserving native UI command handling. HUD — `DrawHUDVR` captures the existing HUD into `_hudImage`; `UpdateVrHud` places a render entity either body/world locked or face locked using distance/horizontal/vertical/scale/occlusion controls. FLASHLIGHT — tool placement is a state machine over gun/pistol/head/body/hand/inventory modes; `CalculateViewFlashlightPos` derives the proper transform and falls back between mounts when a required weapon/hand state is unavailable. TELEPORT — `UpdateTeleportAim` integrates a gravity arc and traces its path. `TeleportPathSegment` temporarily steps the player physics body through the skipped route in ~8-unit increments, detecting trigger/solid clip models, sending native touch signals/events and enforcing locked/closed-door policy; `TeleportPath` follows AAS walk paths when possible before the actual blink/warp. This is a strong solution to teleport bypassing flat-game trigger logic. COMFORT/CINEMATICS — projected vs immersive/cropped cinematics are distinct camera/presentation states. Comfort code includes snap turn, tunnel/blanking/slow-motion/third-person variants, chaperone forcing around physical swinging/throwing and lean/head-wall blanking. HAPTICS — per-hand gameplay shake is buffered and runtime-specific output uses Oculus vibration or OpenVR haptic pulses. MULTIPLAYER — `usercmd_t::Serialize` keeps the existing synchronized command vocabulary (buttons, movement, angles, position/times, fireCount, speed, impulses) rather than serializing raw XR hand/head transforms. Melee remains server-side and projectiles/weapons stay in the native authority model. REUSABLE ARCHITECTURE — normalize XR once; preserve named spaces; make per-hand state first-class; adapt physical intent to existing gameplay contracts; bridge weapon animation into controller targets rather than disabling it; reconcile roomscale with native collision; replay skipped gameplay side effects during teleport; reuse native UI by converting spatial hits to pointer events; and perform the final HMD correction at the renderer's last safe matrix seam.",
  "seed_quakevr_case":"Source-audit: QuakeVR is a QuakeSpasm-derived source fork with VR integrated into engine/gameplay code. It exposes roomscale movement and jumping, SteamVR action sets, finger tracking, two-handed weapon aiming, weapon weight, dual wielding, holsters, throwing/force grabbing, melee/headbutts, hand collision and positional damage. Release history also includes a fake-VR desktop mode useful for testing VR gameplay logic without a headset.",
  "seed_l4d2vr_case":"Source-audit (2026-09-05, full source verification). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (dllmain.cpp, game.cpp/h, hooks.cpp/h, offsets.h, sigscanner.h, vr.cpp/h).\n\nBOOTSTRAP / INJECTION: Loads as d3d9.dll (DXVK-based proxy). DllMain spawns InitL4D2VR on a new thread; the process is terminated unless the command line contains -insecure. Game constructor obtains module bases (client/engine/server/MaterialSystem/vgui2) with retry, then resolves interfaces via CreateInterface with a static cache. Offsets are constructed with SigScanner::VerifyOffset: each target is validated against a byte signature; mismatch or missing signature fails closed with an error dialog.\n\nRENDERING / STEREO: dRenderView creates two independent CViewSetup structures. Left/right origins come from VR::GetViewOriginLeft/Right (HMD position + eye-to-head offset + IPD scale); angles are shared from the HMD. FOV, aspect, near planes and viewport size are forced from the OpenVR recommended render target size and GetProjectionRaw-derived tan-half-FOV values. Each eye is rendered into a named MaterialSystem render target (leftEye0 / rightEye0) created with MATERIAL_RT_DEPTH_SHARED. Submission uses DXVK Vulkan shared textures (m_VKLeftEye / m_VKRightEye) passed to VRCompositor()->Submit together with per-eye VRTextureBounds_t. A blank texture is submitted while not in-game.\n\nHUD /\n\nUI: A call-sequence detector (IsSplitScreen -> PrePushRenderTarget -> PushRenderTargetAndViewport) identifies the HUD push and redirects the texture to a dedicated vrHUD RT. The HUD is then submitted as an OpenVR overlay (with mouse-scale matching window size). Menu overlays are repositioned in absolute tracking space in front of the HMD. Laser interaction uses controller tip matrix + ComputeOverlayIntersection -> VGUI cursor.\n\nCAMERA / COORDINATE SPACES: HMD and controller poses are converted from OpenVR (Y-up, -Z forward) into Source space. Roomscale is implemented with an explicit m_CameraAnchor + m_HeightOffset; the anchor is advanced only while the player is grounded and not pushing the thumbstick. A wall-clip TraceRay from setup origin through the HMD prevents the camera from entering geometry. Stick turning applies a yaw pivot around the HMD for both head and controllers.\n\nINPUT: Full OpenVR Input action set (ActivateVR, Jump, PrimaryAttack, Reload, Use, Walk, Turn, SecondaryAttack, Next/PrevItem, ResetPosition, Crouch, Flashlight, Menu*, Spray, Scoreboard, ShowHUD, Pause). Digital and analog actions are polled every frame and mapped to Source ClientCmd strings. Controller tip component matrices are queried via RenderModels for accurate laser origin.\n\nWEAPONS: dServerFireTerrorBullets and dClientFireTerrorBullets replace the bullet origin/angles with the right-controller absolute pose for the local player (and for any remote player whose m_PlayersVRInfo entry is marked isUsingVR). Viewmodel is driven by CalcViewModelView using GetRecommendedViewmodelAbsPos/Angle (controller tip + configurable offset).\n\nMELEE (high-value): On the server, dProcessUsercmds detects a VR melee swing from the replicated controller pose history. It reconstructs a continuous arc (10 traces) by rotating the previous forward vector around the swing pivot axis by the measured angle delta, then calls the native TestMeleeSwingCollisionServer for each sample. A velocity threshold on the controller sets the isNewSwing flag and resets the hit counter. During the swing, dEyePosition is overridden so the native code sees the controller position as the eye origin.\n\nMULTIPLAYER POSE REPLICATION (rare & valuable): dWriteUsercmd packs the right-controller position and angles into the existing CUserCmd fields (negative tick_count as signal, command_number encoding for roll, viewangles.x packing for Z+X, upmove for Y, mousedx/y for pitch/yaw). The verified-command checksum is recalculated. dReadUsercmd on the server decodes the same fields into the per-player m_PlayersVRInfo array. Consequently remote VR players receive correct aim and melee without a custom network message.\n\nFRAME TIMING: VR::Update runs every frame after WaitGetPoses + UpdateActionState. Poses are sampled once; there is no additional late-latch beyond OpenVR's prediction. Texture creation is deferred until the first RenderView.\n\nREUSABLE PATTERNS:\n1. Signature-scanned, fail-closed offset table (Offsets + SigScanner).\n2. Forced dual-eye RenderView into MaterialSystem RTs + DXVK Vulkan share for compositor submission.\n3. HUD RT interception by call-sequence detection rather than name matching.\n4. Controller pose packed into stock CUserCmd for multiplayer (no extra netcode).\n5. Server-side multi-trace reconstruction of physical melee from pose history.\n6. Explicit roomscale camera anchor with wall-clip correction and locomotion-state gating.\n7. Action-manifest-driven OpenVR Input mapped to legacy ClientCmd for maximum compatibility.\n\nLIMITATIONS (from source + observed behaviour): Throwables and world interactions still use face-aim; roomscale is disabled while the thumbstick is active; arms model is optionally hidden via DrawModelExecute material override; CPU cost of dual full-scene renders is high. The project is a working prototype, not a polished release.\n\nEVIDENCE LEVEL: All statements above are taken directly from the supplied source files (game.*, hooks.*, vr.*, offsets.h, sigscanner.h, dllmain.cpp). No claims rest solely on README text.",
  "seed_gzdoomvr_case":"Source-audit (2026-09-05): GZDoomVR is a full source fork with `src/`, CMake tooling, OpenVR data and game/mod content. The documented input layer exposes configurable OpenVR bindings and a handedness option: the dominant controller supplies weapon orientation/fire while the off-hand can drive movement or item actions, with grip-based secondary mappings. This is a useful engine-level abstraction because controller role assignment stays in configuration while gameplay actions remain in the source port. The weapon packs remain content/mod data, separate from the VR input layer. LOW-LEVEL STEREO/FRAME-TIMING details: UNKNOWN FROM THE PUBLIC README AUDIT.",

  "pattern_vr_frame_timeline":"Generalized source-audit pattern: OpenXR defines a synchronized frame lifecycle around xrWaitFrame/xrBeginFrame/xrEndFrame and predictedDisplayTime. Sample HMD/controller state once for the target display time, freeze it for the stereo pair and any animation/IK/weapon evaluation in that frame, then submit both eyes. A Present-only hook may be too late; a semantic engine-frame or render-frame hook is preferable when available.",
  "pattern_vr_coordinate_spaces":"Generalized source-audit pattern: keep XR reference space, player tracking anchor, camera/view space and object attachment space distinct. Convert runtime poses once into a stable player/world frame, then derive hand/weapon/body targets locally. Recenter should adjust the tracking anchor/calibration offset. Explicitly isolate handedness, unit scale, eye height, arm length and grip offsets.",
  "pattern_vr_action_abstraction":"Generalized source-audit pattern: define semantic actions such as Move, Turn, Grab, Fire, Reload, Use, Jump, MenuSelect and Recenter, then bind physical controller paths to those actions. Keep pose actions separate from button/axis actions. Handedness becomes a logical role assignment rather than a device-index assumption. OpenXR action sets and interaction profiles formalize this separation.",
  "pattern_vr_weapon_pipeline":"Generalized source-audit pattern: separate weapon grip pose, muzzle pose, optical aim axis, projectile origin/direction and the authoritative fire event. Controller tracking can own the physical weapon while the native game method remains responsible for ammo, recoil, spread, damage and networking. Two-handed weapons can use dominant-hand pose plus a support-hand directional constraint.",
  "pattern_vr_melee_collision":"Generalized source-audit pattern: detect physical melee through swept weapon/hand motion, then call native combat resolution. Track previous/current transforms, derive velocity, sweep the weapon hit volume, filter self/duplicate contacts and gate each accepted impact. Convert motion into attack strength only as intent; let native damage, stamina, reaction, ragdoll and networking remain authoritative. Emit haptics after accepted contact.",
  "pattern_vr_ui_projection":"Generalized source-audit pattern: preserve the native UI state/event system while changing spatial presentation and pointer coordinates. For world-space UI, ray-plane intersect the controller, convert the hit to local/screen coordinates and dispatch native pointer events. Use hand-attached panels for compact interaction, view-relative quads for small HUD/reticle elements, and render-to-texture screens for scenes that cannot safely become immersive.",
  "pattern_vr_hook_validation":"Generalized source-audit pattern: native VR hooks should follow binary identity -> candidate discovery -> structural validation -> activation. Use build identity, pattern signatures, surrounding-byte checks, module bounds and expected object/type invariants. Unknown or failed builds should fail closed and emit diagnostics rather than attempting an unsafe offset. Keep per-build data in a dedicated manifest.",
  "pattern_vr_network_pose":"Generalized source-audit pattern: XR tracking is local; avatar pose replication is a separate network concern. Publish compact player-relative head/hand poses, stance and held-item state with sequence/timestamp data. Remote clients reconstruct/interpolate the avatar without needing an XR runtime. Keep local interaction/authority separate from remote visual pose.",

  "seed_sihvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Plugin.cs` is a BepInEx 5 `BaseUnityPlugin`; `Awake()` initializes settings, installs Harmony patches with `PatchAll`, creates a persistent `SiHVR.Host` GameObject and adds the `VRRuntime` component. `VRRuntime` subscribes to `SceneManager.sceneLoaded`, starts XR once through `XRBoot.Run()`, updates `CameraTracker` during `Update`, and performs controller/locomotion/camera work during `LateUpdate`. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `XRBoot.cs` loads `Unity.XR.Management` and `Unity.XR.OpenXR` assemblies from `Application.dataPath/Managed`, resolves `XRGeneralSettings`, `XRManagerSettings`, `XRLoader` and `OpenXRLoader` by reflection, creates the ScriptableObject instances, attaches the OpenXR loader, assigns the manager/settings singleton, enables available controller interaction-profile feature objects, invokes `InitializeLoaderSync` (or `InitializeLoader`) and then `StartSubsystems`. The loader setup has compatibility fallbacks that manipulate private fields such as `m_Loaders`/`m_RegisteredLoaders` and the XR settings singleton when public members are unavailable; failures are logged and initialization stops. `ConfigureOpenXRSettings()` separately reflects into `OpenXRSettings` to force a multi-pass-style render mode (matched by scanning the render-mode enum's member names for \"Multi\" rather than a hardcoded enum value, another version-tolerance fallback) and to instantiate and register five OpenXR controller-interaction-profile feature objects (Oculus Touch, Valve Index, HTC Vive, Microsoft Motion Controller, KHR Simple) by reflecting a features list/array field on the settings object, so the loader advertises broad controller-profile support without depending on which profiles the shipped OpenXR feature DLLs happen to expose at compile time. After `StartSubsystems` succeeds, `XRSettings.eyeTextureResolutionScale` is set to 1.4 as a fixed supersampling factor. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `CameraTracker.cs` classifies scenes after a 15-frame settling delay. `Custom` and `ADV` are forced to `VirtualScreen`; otherwise a fullscreen perspective screen camera that renders the Default layer establishes an immersive scene. The primary camera is selected by a culling-mask/depth score, stereo is enabled only on that camera, and `Application.onBeforeRender` becomes the late pose seam. At that point the mod resets Unity camera matrices, forces a fullscreen rect, reads `XRNode.CenterEye` local position/rotation, and applies the HMD pose through `Locomotion.GetRigRotation()` and `RigOffset` before rendering. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Locomotion.cs` implements grip-drag by subtracting controller-local position deltas transformed through the current game/rig rotation, stick turning with a 0.2 deadzone and 45-degree-per-second base rate, recentering by solving a new rig rotation/offset from the current HMD pose, and a spawn-placement routine that waits for combined `SkinnedMeshRenderer` bounds to stabilize before placing the player relative to the posed character and floor. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `ControllerInput.cs` polls both hands every frame via `InputDevices.GetDeviceAtXRNode`, reading `CommonUsages.devicePosition/deviceRotation` (grip pose) plus grip/trigger/primary/secondary buttons, trigger analog value, thumbstick axis and click. It separately probes two custom, non-`CommonUsages` features, `InputFeatureUsage<Vector3>(\"PointerPosition\")` and `InputFeatureUsage<Quaternion>(\"PointerRotation\")`, explicitly documented in-source as the OpenXR \"aim\" pose distinct from the grip pose, exposed only if the runtime surfaces it through the legacy `InputDevices` layer; validity and full feature-name lists are logged once per hand for runtime diagnosis, and all button/axis state is force-reset when a device reports invalid. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `LaserPointer.cs` casts a ray for both hands every frame from that aim pose via `VRPose.AimRay(posed, hand, ...)`. Against UI targets it does not use `Physics.Raycast` at all: it transforms the ray into each registered quad's local space with `InverseTransformPoint`/`InverseTransformVector` (the vector form specifically to stay correct under non-uniform scale), solves for the local `z=0` plane crossing, and bounds-tests against a unit square, mapping the local hit to render-texture pixel coordinates for the fake cursor. In `Immersive` scenes with `Settings.CaressClick` enabled, it additionally runs a real `Physics.Raycast(rayOrigin, rayDir, out hit, Caress.Reach, Caress.LayerMask, QueryTriggerInteraction.Collide)` against the character's layer-19 trigger colliders, only overriding the UI-quad hit if the body hit is strictly nearer (UI keeps priority at equal/closer distance); on a body hit the cursor is projected through the posed camera's `WorldToScreenPoint` instead of quad-UV math. Because the game exposes only one mouse cursor for two independently-pointing hands, `SelectDriver()` implements a sticky arbitration: the current driver hand keeps control while still \"engaged\" (trigger held everywhere, plus grip/thumbstick-click in virtual-screen scenes only, since grip/stick are repurposed for locomotion/turning in Immersive scenes), otherwise driver follows whichever hand is hovering a target, with ties resolved by keeping the existing driver. A press-time cursor freeze (`_pressDownCursor`) held until the live cursor moves past a 30px `DragThresholdPixels` disambiguates a click from a drag, and per-hand last-valid-cursor/quad/world-hit state lets a drag continue briefly if the ray momentarily leaves the target. All of `WorldHitActive`, `WorldHitPoint`, `GameMouseActive`, `OnNonGameQuad`, `EffectiveCursorRTPos` and the per-button held/edge-down/edge-up flags are computed here and gate on `effectivelyOnQuad` for held/down states but explicitly not for edge-up, guaranteeing releases are never swallowed off-target. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `InputPatches.cs` is a set of Harmony prefix patches on `UnityEngine.Input` — `mousePosition`, `mousePresent`, `GetMouseButton(int)`, `GetMouseButtonDown(int)`, `GetMouseButtonUp(int)`, `GetAxis(string)`, `GetAxisRaw(string)` and `mouseScrollDelta` — each returning `false` to fully replace the native getter/method with `LaserPointer` state when the laser is active, and returning `true` (falling through to the real Unity input) otherwise. `mousePosition` has two source-driven branches: when `LaserPointer.WorldHitActive`, it projects `LaserPointer.WorldHitPoint` through `CameraTracker.Primary.WorldToScreenPoint`; when `LaserPointer.GameMouseActive`, it reports `LaserPointer.EffectiveCursorRTPos` (the render-texture cursor) directly. Trigger/grip/an equivalent-to-middle-click map to mouse buttons 0/1/2 respectively, using separately tracked held vs. edge-down vs. edge-up state from `LaserPointer`. `GetMouseButtonUp` deliberately skips the `GameMouseActive` gate the other patches use (only suppressing when `OnNonGameQuad`), so a release always reaches the game even after the cursor drifts off a surface mid-click, preventing a stuck drag/press state; NGUI's `GetAxis(Raw)(\"Mouse ScrollWheel\")` and UGUI's `mouseScrollDelta` are both patched from the same `LaserPointer.ScrollY` value so either UI stack scrolls correctly. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `CaressPatch.cs` documents (and `LaserPointer`/`InputPatches` jointly implement) the exact caress bridge: the game's own `Zng_Controller.Zng_Update`/`IC_Flick.Update` raycast `Main_Controller.MainCamera.ScreenPointToRay(Input.mousePosition)` against layer 19 only, hitting named body colliders (`BP_Breast_*`, `milk*`, `HS_pussy00_Coll`, etc.) that the main camera does not itself render. Rather than patching that game code, the mod raycasts its own laser against the same layer, then injects a synthetic `Input.mousePosition` equal to `WorldToScreenPoint` of that hit; the game's unmodified raycast re-casts through the same camera and world point, lands on the same collider, and its native `GetMouseButtonDown`-driven press logic fires normally. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `HandUI.cs` displays the game's existing NGUI `Camera_UI` overlay in world space rather than reimplementing it: `BindBestUICamera()` scans active cameras each frame for the highest-depth active orthographic camera not already targeting its own render texture, saves its `clearFlags`/`backgroundColor`/`cullingMask`, then sets `clearFlags=SolidColor` with a transparent color, ANDs the mod's own `VRLayer.Mask` out of its `cullingMask` (so the display quads are not captured by the camera displaying them, avoiding a feedback loop), and redirects `targetTexture` to a mod-owned `RenderTexture`; original state is restored on camera change or `Hide()`. Three `Quad` primitives (colliders destroyed) display that texture: one per hand, positioned via a fixed local hand-space offset/rotation each frame (`handPos + handRot*(0,0.04,0.10)`, `handRot*Euler(90,0,0)`), and one dockable panel toggled by either hand's secondary button, which captures and freezes the live pose of whichever hand's press triggered the toggle (falling back to `HandRoles.PrimaryHand`, or to a head-relative pose if no hand is tracked). Quad materials/shaders are resolved defensively through an ordered `Shader.Find` fallback list to survive shader stripping in the shipped build. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `HandRoles.cs` resolves only which hand *displays* the panel from `Settings.ImmersivePanelHand`; laser ownership is explicitly decoupled per an in-source comment (\"The laser is no longer owned here\") so both hands always cast independently and either can dock-then-point with the same controller for one-handed use. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `Hands.cs` is a two-method no-op stub (`Update`, `HideAll`) with an in-source comment that hand visuals were dropped because controller-driven hand animation needs animator-driven prefabs or hand-tracking joint data the author didn't have clean access to — no hand mesh, skeleton or finger animation exists in the reviewed source. FRAME TIMING: the source establishes an `Application.onBeforeRender` camera/pose update seam, but the reviewed public source does not expose a complete explicit `xrWaitFrame`/predictedDisplayTime -> simulation -> eye render -> `xrEndFrame` sequence; keep predicted-display scheduling/late-latching beyond Unity's `onBeforeRender` seam UNKNOWN. COORDINATE SPACES: the source explicitly separates XR CenterEye local pose, game camera transform, rig rotation and rig offset; controller-local locomotion deltas are transformed through the current game/rig rotation before changing the player anchor.\n\nINPUT: OpenXR interaction profiles are enabled reflectively (verified in `XRBoot.ConfigureOpenXRSettings`) and controller state is consumed through `ControllerInput`, which verifiably distinguishes grip pose from a separately-queried OpenXR aim pose; the exact per-profile action-path binding code inside Unity's OpenXR package itself was not independently inspected (that layer is upstream of this mod), so do not invent action-path strings. HANDS/UI: fully source-verified. `Hands.cs` confirms no hand mesh/skeleton/finger animation exists (explicit stub). `HandUI.cs`/`HandRoles.cs`/`LaserPointer.cs` confirm a complete, working world-space UI and dual-hand pointer/click/drag/scroll system built entirely on faked native `Input` state (via `InputPatches.cs`) and manual local-space quad raycasting, with no avatar IK anywhere in the reviewed source. CARESS: fully source-verified (previously inferred-only) — `LaserPointer` raycasts layer 19 with real `Physics.Raycast`, `InputPatches` projects the hit through `WorldToScreenPoint` into a faked `Input.mousePosition`, and the game's native `Zng_Controller`/`IC_Flick` raycast (unmodified) resolves the touch normally. WEAPONS/MELEE/MULTIPLAYER: UNKNOWN from the reviewed public source. REUSABLE PATTERN: dynamically bootstrap a missing Unity XR stack at runtime, isolate runtime-version assumptions behind reflection helpers (including scanning an unknown enum's member names for a substring match rather than hardcoding an enum value), keep scene classification/camera policy separate from XR initialization, use `Application.onBeforeRender` for the last camera-pose update available to Unity, and explicitly fall back to a render-to-texture/virtual-screen presentation for scenes whose camera graph is not safe to stereoize. Separately, and just as reusable: (1) a \"raycast-to-native-input-injection\" bridge — Harmony-patch the engine's low-level input surface (`Input.mousePosition`/`GetMouseButton*`/scroll) to feed a VR laser's hit point back through the game's own camera via `WorldToScreenPoint`, so completely unmodified native UI/interaction code (NGUI, or arbitrary raycast-driven gameplay logic like a body-touch system) keeps working without a single gameplay patch; (2) manual local-space plane-intersection raycasting against UI quads instead of physics colliders, avoiding physics-layer bookkeeping for pure display geometry; (3) sticky single-driver arbitration to resolve two independently-tracked hands down to the one cursor concept a legacy game actually understands, with hysteresis so an in-progress gesture never jumps hands; (4) capturing an existing 2D UI camera into an offscreen render texture and self-excluding the mod's own render layer from that camera's culling mask to avoid a capture feedback loop, letting a VR mod reuse an entire native UI system on a 3D quad instead of reimplementing it; (5) explicit separation of an OpenXR \"aim\" pose from the grip pose for pointing accuracy, discovered via a custom string-keyed `InputFeatureUsage` rather than `CommonUsages`. This is especially useful for BepInEx retrofits where the shipped game has no XR package references, where menus/cinematic camera graphs need a different presentation policy from gameplay, and where a legacy Unity UI/interaction stack needs VR input without being rewritten.",
  "seed_unityvrmod_universal_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `VRModCore.cs` owns plugin lifecycle/configuration and starts `VrVisualizationManager` after UniverseLib initialization. `VrVisualizationManager.cs` is the policy layer: it selects `VrCameraSetup_CoreOpenXR` or `VrCameraSetup_CoreOpenVR` through `IVrCameraSetup`, invalidates camera discovery on scene changes, enables automatic Safe Mode around scene transitions, and rebuilds the VR rig when the selected game camera changes. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `VrCameraSetup_CoreOpenXR.cs` creates a dedicated `UnityVRMod_XrRig` with separate left/right Unity Cameras, applies world-scale and eye-height calibration plus scene-specific pose overrides, locates OpenXR views, converts per-eye poses into Unity transforms, derives asymmetric projection matrices from `XrFovf`, renders each eye to intermediate targets, copies those results into native XR swapchain images, and submits `XrCompositionLayerProjectionView` records containing per-eye pose/FOV/swapchain state. VERIFIED FROM DOCUMENTATION: the project targets roughly Unity 5.6 through current releases, supports Mono and IL2CPP, intentionally avoids Unity-version-locked XR packages, and ships OpenXR plus OpenVR builds for Windows DX11. SUPPLEMENTARY RUNTIME EVIDENCE: public runtime logs show interaction-profile binding suggestions, a stage reference space, separate per-eye swapchains, and an OpenXR action set containing trigger/grip/thumbstick plus left/right aim/grip poses; these logs corroborate the existence of an input layer but are not treated as proof of complete gameplay interaction code. CAMERA/COMPATIBILITY PATTERN: asserted hierarchy-path camera overrides provide an explicit escape hatch when automatic camera discovery fails, while Safe Mode gives the mod a recoverable scene-transition failure path. FRAME TIMING: the reviewed public source establishes view-location and projection-layer submission, but not the complete game-simulation ordering around `xrWaitFrame`/`xrBeginFrame`; exact simulation/render timing is UNKNOWN. INPUT/HANDS/IK/WEAPONS/MELEE: controller/action initialization is evidenced, but a complete source-audited physical weapon, body-IK or melee implementation is UNKNOWN. REUSABLE PATTERN: keep engine/camera policy above a narrow runtime-specific XR adapter, and make camera selection, scene transitions, teardown and rig recreation explicit compatibility states rather than scattering runtime checks through gameplay code.",
  "seed_gvrmod_openxr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `build.bat` compiles `vrmod_log.cpp`, `xr_input.cpp`, `d3d_hooks.cpp`, `xr_session.cpp`, `xr_render_d3d.cpp` and `lua_interface.cpp` into `gmcl_vrmod_xr_win64.dll`; the Windows OpenXR path uses `XR_KHR_D3D11_enable`, while project documentation distinguishes Windows D3D9/11 sharing from Linux OpenGL/GLX/togl texture/FBO sharing. VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: `src/input/xr_input.h` makes the native input boundary explicit: action sets and pose-action spaces are cached in native state; the module parses a SteamVR-format action manifest, creates/attaches OpenXR action sets, synchronizes them per frame, exposes boolean/float/vector2/pose queries, triggers haptics, suggests interaction-profile bindings, creates/tears down action spaces, and exposes physical controller-source IDs/values for rebinding. It also exposes `XR_GetSkeletalSummaryData`, an OpenVR-compatible summary that synthesizes finger curl/splay from controller trigger/grip/buttons rather than requiring OpenXR hand-tracking actions. VERIFIED FROM DOCUMENTATION: the Lua addon is the gameplay/UI/bindings layer; `require(\"vrmod_xr\")` selects the OpenXR native module while `require(\"vrmod\")` selects the sibling OpenVR module, with `vrmod_prefer_backend` controlling selection. VERIFIED FROM DOCUMENTATION: the current feature surface includes VR menus/hand panels, pickup, collisions, locomotion, Glide, climbing hooks and controller rebinding.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: Garry's Mod is multiplayer-capable, but the reviewed native source does not establish a complete network pose packet/interpolation design; keep that UNKNOWN. FRAME TIMING: `XR_UpdatePoses` and `XR_RefreshHMDPose` expose explicit pose-update seams, and the latter is documented as locating views at the current predicted time, but the reviewed public material does not prove the full `xrWaitFrame`→simulation→render→submit ordering; keep the complete timeline UNKNOWN. REUSABLE PATTERN: define a small XR ABI consisting of semantic actions, pose spaces, haptics, physical controller sources and backend identity, then keep gameplay in Lua. Pair that with backend-specific graphics-resource adapters so Windows and Linux can use different texture-sharing mechanisms without forcing platform checks into gameplay code. This is stronger and more general than treating OpenXR as a collection of one-off Lua bindings.",

  "seed_portal2vr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The x86 D3D9/OpenVR source uses a native d3d9.dll proxy, Source-engine interfaces and signature scanning. The render path queries OpenVR per-eye projection data, creates separate engine render targets and submits those textures independently to the compositor. Controller poses are converted into Source coordinates, controller-tip geometry drives laser/UI interaction, and legacy Source commands remain the gameplay bridge. The UI path translates VR controller rays into VGUI cursor coordinates. Reusable pattern: runtime pose -> Source transform conversion -> existing engine action, with rendering and UI kept as separate bridges. Frame-prediction/late-latch ordering is not established by the public audit.",
  "seed_crysisvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The Crysis VR repository separates Code/Game/Installer and documents an OpenXR migration from OpenVR. Release history verifies roomscale-offset compensation in weapon positioning, controller smoothing, headset-orientation eye direction for object interaction, binocular camera/render fixes and removal of redundant 2D eye copies. Physical holsters and two-hand weapon support are part of the interaction layer. Reusable pattern: treat binoculars, cutscenes, vehicles and normal first-person play as distinct camera/interaction states. Predicted-display-time ordering is UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_dfuvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): DFUVR is a BepInEx Unity plugin with additional game-folder files. Its configuration contains player height, headset refresh rate, controller profile and sheath offset, while in-game calibration separately adjusts height and scabbard position. This gives a useful physical-calibration pattern: global body scale and a weapon attachment point are independent parameters. The README also exposes a legacy UI boundary—the VR UI is only reliable at 1920x1080 and laser precision degrades near outer edges. Low-level stereo/compositor/frame timing is UNKNOWN from the public source overview.",
  "seed_openmwvr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): The supplied OpenMW-VR camera/UI modules establish additional implementation-level patterns beyond the existing stereo/input audit. `camera.lua` centralizes first/third-person mode, FOV, collision, standing preview, vanity, zoom, crosshair, head-bobbing, 360 movement and POV switching in engine `onUpdate`/`onFrame` handlers, and exposes tagged enable/disable controls for other systems. `third_person.lua` keeps explicit RightShoulder/LeftShoulder/Combat/Swimming camera state, computes speed/pitch-dependent distance, and uses side/forward raycasts to automatically choose an unobstructed shoulder. `first_person_auto_switch.lua` uses backward raycasts from `camera.getTrackedPosition()` to switch to first person when third-person distance becomes too small and restores third person when clearance returns. `move360.lua` converts player movement into camera-relative movement by rotating the movement vector by camera-vs-body yaw, derives pitch change from the same relationship, and applies clamped yaw change while temporarily suppressing standing-preview behavior. `head_bobbing.lua` generates procedural vertical/roll motion from accumulated travel distance, with ground-contact weighting and speed limiting, then applies offsets through the camera API. `settings.lua` registers persistent camera and head-bobbing controls and dynamically disables dependent settings when their parent feature is off. The VR UI modules initialize only in VR mode, create derived spaces, update poses/layers on `onVRFrame`, and reinitialize menu poses after recentering; the tutorial layer resolves active controller bindings and teaches pointer activation, handedness and recentering. Reusable pattern: keep XR tracking and semantic actions in engine VR interfaces, then build camera, locomotion, comfort and UI behaviors as stateful modules that transform existing player/camera controls rather than replacing the native gameplay model. The newly supplied files make the camera/comfort/locomotion portion of the OpenMW-VR architecture concrete enough for an LLM to implement as separate cooperating subsystems.",
  "seed_mothervr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): MotherVR installs as a dxgi.dll proxy and exposes in-game VR configuration. Historical documentation explains the key closed-game technique: Alien: Isolation had an obsolete Oculus SDK statically compiled into the executable, so the mod intercepts SDK calls and redirects them to replacement/fake SDK code that translates and fixes requests for current runtimes. Release notes add an optional openvr_api.dll path for SteamVR and deliberately suppress problematic intro/loading video frames. Reusable pattern: intercept an embedded API contract, translate its state into a modern runtime, and preserve the game's expected call semantics. Full modern OpenXR frame timing is UNKNOWN.",
  "seed_wovr_case": "Source-audit (2026-09-05): WoVR's public repository contains a dedicated dxProxyWoVR project and documents frame-synchronized stereo where one engine frame equals one 3D VR frame. The right-hand angle becomes a world ray for targeting/aiming/UI; the left-hand angle controls locomotion, swimming and flying. Ground-placed AOE attacks, floating world-occluded UI, internal high-resolution headset rendering and VR-specific near/far clipping are documented. Reusable pattern: expose controller pose both as raw transform state and as semantic world-space rays so combat/UI and locomotion can consume different projections of the same tracked input. A complete predicted-display/late-latch sequence is not documented.",

  "case_ervr_eldenring": "Source-audit (2026-09-06; supplied ERVR 0.3.0 archive, binary/configuration inspection). NO PUBLIC GAME-SPECIFIC SOURCE: this audit is intentionally binary/configuration-level, not VERIFIED FROM SOURCE CODE.\n\nBOOTSTRAP / RENDER HOOK: `ERVR.dll` exposes ReShade add-on registration and the binary logs `[reshade] registered as add-on`. It explicitly accepts D3D12 and logs that it stays idle for other graphics APIs. The archive also contains `dinput8.dll` and a bundled `dxgi.dll`, establishing the shipped loader/proxy composition. Exact loader call graph is not reconstructed.\n\nOPENXR: the binary contains the OpenXR loader/session/action/swapchain/frame surface: `xrCreateInstance`, `xrGetInstanceProcAddr`, `xrGetSystem`, `xrGetSystemProperties`, `xrGetD3D12GraphicsRequirementsKHR`, `xrCreateSession`, `xrCreateReferenceSpace`, `xrCreateActionSet`, `xrCreateActionSpace`, `xrCreateSwapchain`, `xrEnumerateSwapchainImages`, `xrAcquireSwapchainImage`, `xrWaitSwapchainImage`, `xrReleaseSwapchainImage`, `xrWaitFrame`, `xrBeginFrame`, `xrLocateViews` and `xrEndFrame`. `XR_KHR_D3D12_enable` is present. Separate eye and HUD/quad swapchains are logged. This establishes an owned OpenXR session integrated with the live D3D12 path, but the exact source-level frame-loop ordering and late-latch strategy are UNKNOWN.\n\nSTEREO REPLAY: ERVR.ini is unusually explicit about engine task replay. `Pass2Keep` includes `106,118,123-125,144-158,164`; `Pass2FullFrame=true` can replay a complete engine frame; `Pass2FullFrameKeep` and `Pass2SafeKeep` define larger and transition-safe task sets. The comments identify exclusions such as input replay, resource deletion and parts of AI/physics/cloth work. The binary contains `FrameBegin`, `GameSceneDraw`, `CameraStepUpdate`, pass counters and a force-pass2 diagnostic. Reusable pattern: treat the proprietary engine frame as a schedulable task graph, replay camera/render producers for the second eye, and explicitly suppress stateful tasks that cannot safely execute twice.\n\nSIMULATION TIMING: binary strings and config expose AOB resolution for a frame-dt global and step-dispatch global. `Pass2Steps` is intended to replace the pass-2 timing value with approximately zero; `Pass2Dt` is a separate experimental control. The configuration explains the goal as a complete second engine frame that advances no simulation. This is strong evidence for same-simulation-time eye replay, but the exact write/restore instructions are not visible from source and should not be reconstructed beyond that claim.\n\nGPU HAZARDS: `GpuSync` creates a D3D12 fence on the present queue and can wait before/after pass 2. The configuration explains the reason: the engine can reuse per-frame upload memory while the GPU is still consuming pass-1 work. This is a reusable D3D12 stereo-replay rule: if the same engine resources are consumed by two CPU-side passes before the GPU has retired the first, a fence or equivalent lifetime barrier may be required.\n\nCAMERA / POSE: `CameraStepUpdate`, `GetDmyPos`, camera follow/finalize/model-position diagnostics and a Havok pose-evaluation hook are present. The config exposes `CameraMode=pre|post|block|passthrough`, `CameraRotation=hmd`, `CameraYawSource=ervr`, `CameraAnchor=head`, smoothing and a foreign-camera-write diagnostic. The implementation therefore has multiple camera-ownership seams and explicitly reasserts skeletal pose after animation/Havok stages. Exact camera structure layouts and solver equations are UNKNOWN.\n\nINPUT: XInput IAT patching is directly evidenced. The binary references xinput1_4.dll, xinput1_3.dll and xinput9_1_0.dll and logs patches to XInputGetState/GetStateEx/SetState/GetCapabilities. This is a clean compatibility architecture: OpenXR action state can be translated into the game's existing XInput semantic layer without modifying every gameplay consumer.\n\nSKELETON / IK: the binary logs player skeleton bone counts, hkbCharacter, arm indices and repeated game rewrites of arm pose after pose/camera/model/Havok stages. The config names Spine, L_UpperArm/L_Forearm/L_Hand and right-side equivalents, plus HandTracking, elbow-pole and arm-stretch parameters. The existence of controller-driven skeletal arms is therefore verified; the exact IK algorithm is UNKNOWN and should not be described as FABRIK/CCD/two-bone unless independently decompiled.\n\nMELEE / NATIVE COMBAT: the binary references `CS::CSChrTaeAnimEvent`, installs TAE behavior-event and generic-event hooks, and logs injected attacks with judge IDs and kinetic-quality values. It resolves `EQUIP_PARAM_WEAPON_ST`, `BehaviorParam_PC` and `AtkParam_Pc`, including weapon variation/weight and attack-row references. This strongly supports the architecture: VR motion decides when/how strongly to inject the game's native attack event; Elden Ring's own attack/parameter systems remain authoritative for the actual attack semantics.\n\nSKILLS / ASHES OF WAR: the generic TAE hook records skill event instances and distinguishes replayable effects such as SpEffect/FFX from attack/bullet behavior. The configuration describes recording skill animation events and replaying selected effects without replaying the original animation, while attack skills can be attached to the next physical swing. Reusable pattern: observe an existing authored animation/event stream once, cache semantic side effects, then trigger those effects from a new VR input path while preserving native parameter resolution.\n\nTWO-HAND / SHIELD: the config exposes two-hand grab radius/axis/min/max and pin-hand behavior; binary diagnostics expose two-hand geometry/grab state. Shield/guard quality and controller posture are also represented. The safest claim is that VR detects physical support-hand geometry and connects it to the game's native two-hand/guard state; exact collision/guard mathematics are not recoverable from the closed artifact alone.\n\nHUD / ALPHA: the binary contains render-target census, UI draw-stream registration, target tracking and UI redirection. The config documents that UI may move between the back buffer and a full-size offscreen target, so the mod re-derives the target. The UI layer uses a separate quad swapchain. For alpha, the config describes dual capture over black/white backgrounds and solving coverage from the pair because the game's UI pipelines do not expose usable alpha. This is a highly reusable technique for flat UIs whose source blend state cannot simply be copied into an XR composition layer.\n\nRESOLUTION: the binary contains a GraphicsConfig.xml repair path and a game-resolution size-check patch; the config exposes GameRes/GameResScale/FOV/IPD controls and describes the game frame as a wide raster from which eye crops are taken. This is verified as a resolution/configuration integration path; the exact projection math is UNKNOWN.\n\nVERSION SAFETY: the binary uses AOB/signature discovery, mid-hook/trampoline diagnostics, structure-size validation and explicit feature-disable messages when a signature or repository validation fails. Examples include failed camera/menu/GameMan/frame-dt AOB paths, `midhook` relocation/allocation checks, parameter row-size checks and repository validation. Reusable pattern: locate -> validate -> hook -> self-test -> degrade/disable. Never copy a stale absolute address between Elden Ring builds.\n\nRECOVERY / STATE MACHINE: OpenXR runtime/session/swapchain failures, D3D12 device loss, missing stereo pairs and transition/warp states have explicit logging and fallback. The config's spawn/menu/warp/safe-pass grace windows show that stereo rendering is treated as stateful around engine transitions. This is an important closed-engine VR lesson: compatibility logic belongs around lifecycle boundaries, not only inside the happy-path render hook.\n\nEVIDENCE BOUNDARIES: VERIFIED FROM BINARY: D3D12/ReShade boundary, OpenXR API surface, eye/HUD swapchains, XInput hooks, AOB/mid-hooks, camera/Havok/skeleton hooks, TAE hooks, parameter identifiers/validation, fence diagnostics and resolution patching. VERIFIED FROM CONFIG: task replay sets, dt/step controls, camera ownership modes, hand/two-hand tuning, combat/skill concepts, HUD target/capture strategy and transition grace policies. VERIFIED FROM NEXUS DOCUMENTATION: shipped feature intent and tested-version claims. UNKNOWN: exact C++ algorithms, complete call graph, exact IK/spring equations, complete predicted-display-time ordering and any implementation detail not recoverable from the distributed binary.\n\nREUSABLE ARCHITECTURE: OpenXR runtime state -> D3D12/ReShade render interception -> game camera ownership -> controlled second engine pass -> per-eye XR swapchains; in parallel, OpenXR actions -> XInput semantic bridge and tracked hand poses -> skeleton/Havok pose arbitration -> native weapon/TAE event injection -> native parameter resolution. Keep HUD extraction/compositing as a separate render subsystem. Keep version-specific AOB discovery and validation isolated from feature logic. This separation is the most transferable lesson from the artifact.",
  "case_driverally_uuvr": `Source-audit (2026-09-06), based on the supplied DRIVERallyVR.zip plus public #DRIVE Rally/UUVR context.

VERIFIED FROM ARCHIVE:
- BepInEx 5.4.23.5 x64 on Windows.
- Unity runtime: 6000.0.73.10577579; Rewired reports 6000.0.73f1.
- UUVR plugin version: 0.4.0, GUID raicuparta.uuvr-modern.
- Uuvr.Patcher.dll is installed and the log shows the preloader patching UUVR, copying XR subsystem files and completing installation.
- Runtime plugins: Uuvr.dll, Uuvr.XR.Management.dll, Uuvr.XR.OpenXR.dll, Uuvr.XR.OpenVR.dll.
- Config defaults: Preferred VR API=OpenXr; Camera Tracking Mode=RelativeTransform; UI Patch Mode=None; Screen-space UI Elements to Patch=None; Preferred UI Plane Render Mode=InWorld.
- UUVR binary string surface exposes camera tracking, stereo target-eye, OpenVR/OpenXR backend and VR-UI infrastructure.
- No game-specific VR C# plugin or custom input/vehicle/interaction implementation is present in the archive.
- Log records Rewired warnings for missing UICancel and Change Quality actions and says Rewired is not initialized.

VERIFIED FROM PUBLIC CONTEXT:
- #DRIVE Rally is a Unity game; its June 2026 v1.4.8 update migrated PC from Unity 2022 to Unity 6.
- Steam community reports describe UUVR as working essentially out of the box / fine in VR, while some menu elements render to the desktop; the developer linked a UUVR preview.
- UUVR is Raicuparta's universal Unity VR project with separate OpenXR/OpenVR/XR management components.

UNKNOWN:
- Active graphics API for the supplied run.
- Any game-specific VR vehicle/cockpit input mapping beyond what UUVR's generic camera/stereo layer provides.
- Whether the supplied package corresponds to the newest public UUVR build beyond the explicit 0.4.0 config/runtime evidence.
`,  "pattern_native_second_view_framegraph": "Source-audit pattern derived from the supplied CyberpunkVR Port source (2026-09-06). VERIFIED: SyncStereo selects an authored entRenderToTextureCameraComponent by virtual-camera name/hash; FrameGraph/NodeDispatch/ViewReuse/CullVisibility/Grading/Hud/Dlss/VisionLayer split the second eye into explicit per-view responsibilities; final color is captured at a known graph completion/acquire boundary; per-frame view-feature bits are rewritten because the engine regenerates them; shared global render state is reused/coordinated where independent execution caused eye disagreement. Reusable lesson: creating a secondary camera is only the first step — audit culling, shared resources, temporal history, upscaler viewport, HUD and final-resource lifetime for that view.",
  "pattern_pose_image_serial_binding": "Source-audit pattern derived from the supplied CyberpunkVR Port source (2026-09-06). VERIFIED: OpenXRFrameLoop selects one target locateTime; camera and hand consumers use that target; StoreRenderEyePose records the render pose by engine sequence; captured MAIN/VRCAM images carry serials and are selected from resource pools by matching serial; stale complete snapshots can be re-submitted with their own render pose when game FPS is below XR cadence. VERIFIED correction: the old AER pair lock is explicitly removed for the current same-frame two-camera path. Reusable lesson: a pose belongs to the pixels it rendered, not to the later Present/xrEndFrame call.",
  "pattern_vr_physical_reload": "Source-audit pattern derived from the supplied CyberpunkVR Port source (2026-09-06). VERIFIED: the reload layer combines a native weapon-rig pose hook with CET/redscript state. Hand grip owns magazine/slide interactions; positions are converted through live weapon geometry; magazine movement follows the well/path; slide travel is projected along a measured weapon axis; finger/wrist ownership and game reload/ammo/audio state are synchronized; 13 weapon-specific modules tune the generic core, while the Overture revolver has a separate cylinder/case/speedloader mechanism. Reusable lesson: separate generic hand/part state from weapon-mechanism data, and keep native gameplay authority instead of simulating ammo in parallel.",


  "l4d2vr_source_audited": "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED SOURCE TREE (snapshot `l4d2vr-master`; filenames and code paths below are directly present). Architecture/bootstrap: `L4D2VR/dllmain.cpp` is the Windows DLL entry and contains the large initialization/configuration path; `hooks.cpp`/`hooks.h` and `hooks/hooks_init.inl` organize native Source-engine hooks. The project builds as a Win32/v142-era native DLL and targets the legacy Source/D3D9 environment. `offsets.h`, `sigscanner.h`, `sdk.h`, and the minimal `sdk/` headers expose the expected version-sensitive offsets, signature scanning, and Source interfaces. Treat all offsets/signatures as build-specific, not stable APIs.\n\nRendering/stereo: `hooks/hooks_render.inl` is the principal render hook implementation (large D3D9/Source material-rendering integration). It tracks render-pass context including main-eye, scope and rear-mirror passes in `HooksNekoPostPass`; this is evidence that render passes need to be distinguished rather than blindly treating every render callback as an eye. The source includes render-pipeline diagnostics and special-case handling for scope/mirror/post-pass candidates. The supplied files establish D3D9/Source renderer interception, but do not justify claiming OpenXR swapchains or a modern OpenXR projection-layer path. Verify exact stereo eye sequencing and compositor handoff in `vr.cpp`/`vr.h` before transplanting.\n\nFrame timing: frame/end-frame and Present-related work is spread across `hooks/hooks_combat_network.inl`, `hooks/hooks_render.inl`, and `vr.cpp`; diagnostics explicitly track end-frame entry and Present spikes. This is a useful example of instrumenting render/frame boundaries, but source inspection alone should not be paraphrased as a guaranteed late-latching implementation. Keep game simulation/user-command updates separate from eye-specific render work to avoid double simulation.\n\nCamera/tracking spaces: `vr/vr_tracking.inl`, `vr/vr_roomscale_prediction.inl`, `vr/vr_lifecycle_update.inl`, and `vr/vr_lifecycle_pose_hud.inl` divide tracking, roomscale prediction, update lifecycle and HUD/pose work. `VR::GetAimRenderCameraDelta()` in `vr_roomscale_prediction.inl` explicitly distinguishes third-person render-camera deltas from the decoupled 1:1 roomscale camera anchor to avoid applying a center delta twice. This is a concrete warning: define tracking/player/world/render-camera spaces and apply each offset exactly once. The tree includes recenter/reset handling on transitions from observer/spectator to live player in `hooks_createmove.inl`.\n\nInput/actions: `SteamVRActionManifest/action_manifest.json` plus controller-specific binding JSON files (Knuckles, Oculus Touch, Vive Cosmos) document SteamVR action bindings. `vr/vr_process_input.inl` handles input processing; `hooks/hooks_createmove.inl` connects VR state to Source's `CUserCmd` path. This is a strong pattern for translating controller actions into existing game semantics rather than replacing authoritative game command processing. Exact per-action mappings should be read from the manifest and binding files; do not assume every controller profile exposes identical controls.\n\nHands/controllers: `vr_hands/` contains an explicit subsystem: `vr_hand_system.*`, `vr_hand_asset_loader.*`, `vr_hand_manifest.*`, `vr_hand_skeleton_runtime.*`, `vr_hand_vm_pose.*`, `vr_hand_renderer_d3d9.*`, `vr_hand_vr_bridge.*`, and `vr_hand_math.h`. `VR_HANDS_SETUP.md` documents pinned cgltf and ozz-animation dependencies and the setup script. This supports a separate asset/manifest/runtime/render bridge instead of embedding hand model parsing in the main VR loop. The hand renderer is D3D9-specific. Detailed skeleton joint semantics and animation-update ordering require inspecting those implementation files before asserting them.\n\nBody/IK: `hooks/hooks_world_pose.inl` explicitly describes multiplayer VR world-model pose reconstruction and layering a visual-only head/upper-body/dual-arm solve over Source's final model setup. The file comments say tracked samples remain player-yaw-local on the wire and are resolved against the current `ModelRenderInfo_t`. This is a source-backed distinction between network-space pose data and render-time model-space reconstruction. Do not infer full-body leg IK or a complete physical avatar from this; the visible description is head/upper body/arms.\n\nWeapons/aiming: `vr/vr_aiming.inl` contains extensive aiming logic and guarded reads because entity/netvar memory may be transient when accessed from Present/render context. `vr/vr_viewmodel_config.inl`, `hooks/hooks_combat_network.inl`, and `hooks/hooks_throwables.inl` are related seams. `hooks_createmove.inl` bridges input to native commands. The README reports full controllers and two-handed interaction, while also warning that interactions and throwables can require face aiming outside VR-only servers. Separate tracked aim/grip transforms from native firing/action calls; the latter must remain the game's authority for ammo, damage and replication. Exact muzzle/recoil/reload algorithms are not claimed here without function-level verification.\n\nPhysical interaction/melee: `vr/vr_object_pull.inl`, `hooks/hooks_throwables.inl`, and combat/network hooks are relevant source surfaces for pulling/interacting and throwable/combat behavior. The README explicitly flags face-aiming limitations for interactions and throwables in non-VR-only-server contexts. Do not claim generic swept melee collision, hand-contact damage, or a complete physics-grab framework unless the specific code path confirms it.\n\nUI/HUD: `vr/vr_lifecycle_pose_hud.inl` and `vr_config_overlay_embedded.inl` cover pose/HUD lifecycle and embedded configuration UI. The supplied tree also has `config.txt` and action manifest/binding files. A full controller-ray/world-space widget toolkit is not established by filenames alone; treat the UI approach as game-specific Source HUD/config integration until its exact draw/input path is audited.\n\nLocomotion/roomscale: `vr/vr_roomscale_prediction.inl` implements roomscale prediction and render-camera delta handling; `vr/vr_tracking.inl` contains tracking/camera logic. `hooks_createmove.inl` is the seam for mapping movement inputs into Source commands. This suggests a reusable split between physical HMD displacement, virtual player origin and command-driven locomotion. Teleportation or climbing are not evidenced by the inspected architecture and are not tagged as implemented.\n\nMultiplayer/pose replication: `vr_pose_protocol.h` defines wire-only shared structures without Source SDK types, allowing client listen-relay and optional dedicated-server plugin to validate the same payload format. `l4d2vr_pose_server.cpp` is the optional server-side implementation; `l4d2vr_pose_server.def`, `.vdf`, and project file provide its plugin/build packaging. `hooks/hooks_world_pose.inl` says tracked samples are player-yaw-local on the wire, then resolved against the current model-render information for visual reconstruction. This is a strong reusable pattern: keep transport schema independent of engine types, transmit compact local-space samples, validate them on both endpoints, and reconstruct remote visuals client-side. Exact packet fields, bounds and version behavior are defined in `vr_pose_protocol.h` and should be followed rather than guessed.\n\nHaptics: `haptics_config.txt` and input/weapon source surfaces establish configuration and integration points, but this audit does not claim specific amplitude/frequency waveforms or event-to-controller mappings without following the exact calls. Check the source for event triggers and runtime API calls before porting.\n\nReusable patterns: (1) Source engine adapter with signature/offset isolation; (2) user-command bridge so VR inputs retain native gameplay semantics; (3) render-pass classification to avoid applying stereo work to scopes/mirrors/post passes; (4) explicit tracking/render-camera delta accounting; (5) separate hand asset/manifest/skeleton/render subsystems; (6) wire-only pose protocol shared by client and server; (7) transmit player-yaw-local pose and reconstruct against the receiver's current model transform; (8) defensive guarded entity/netvar reads when render-thread hooks can race entity lifetime. Use these patterns when the target has similarly constrained legacy renderer or engine interfaces, but do not copy Source-specific offsets or D3D9 assumptions.\n\nEvidence/unknowns: README.md verifies advertised support for singleplayer/multiplayer, 6DoF view, full controllers/two-handed interaction, desktop mirror, Workshop content, ReShade, multi-core rendering and antialiasing. It warns of VAC risk, recommends `-insecure`, and says versus is barely tested. These are documentation claims, not a guarantee for every server/build. This audit is based on the supplied source snapshot and public project layout; it does not establish current binary compatibility, exact runtime versions, all function-level weapon mechanics, complete melee physics, teleport/climbing, full-body legs, or OpenXR support.",

};

// OpenMW-VR full source audit additions (2026-09-06).
CODE_AUDITS["seed_openmwvr_case"] = "VERIFIED FROM THE SUPPLIED FULL OPENMW-VR SOURCE SNAPSHOT (archive commit text 0f520f65c3e085369e66d6a90ce871e817d4533f). ARCHITECTURE: OpenMW-VR is a source-level OpenMW engine fork, not an injected patch against Bethesda's original Morrowind executable. The VR implementation is layered across `components/xr` (OpenXR runtime/session/actions/swapchains/reference spaces), `components/vr` (runtime-agnostic frame/layer/swapchain/viewer abstractions), `components/stereo` (OpenSceneGraph stereo/multiview/frustum handling), `apps/openmw/mwvr` (Morrowind-specific tracking, animation, input, GUI, pointer and combat), native OpenMW gameplay/rendering seams, and Lua VR scripts under `files/data/scripts/omw/vr`. This is a particularly useful reference for a source-accessible flat-to-VR port because XR lifecycle, renderer stereo state, game-world tracking anchors, animation, gameplay and UI can share explicit engine types rather than communicating through memory hooks.\n\nOPENXR FRAME OWNERSHIP / TIMING: `components/xr/session.cpp::syncFrameUpdate` calls `xrWaitFrame`, records `shouldRender`, `predictedDisplayTime` and `predictedDisplayPeriod`, and clears a per-frame pose cache. `components/vr/session.cpp::frameBeginUpdate` publishes that predicted time, invokes frame listeners, then opens a bounded `locateSpace` window; `updateSpaces` closes that window after space consumers have updated. `XR::Session::locateSpace` rejects calls outside frame sync, locates at `VR::getPredictedDisplayTime()`, classifies tracking as Good/Stale/Lost and caches each `(space, referenceSpace)` result for the frame. `VR::Viewer::updateView` locates both LOCAL-space eye views for compositor submission and VIEW-space eye views for rendering using the same predicted display time. The first stereo draw callback calls `xrBeginFrame`; the final eye callback resolves/blits the eye and optional depth images; the swap-buffers callback finally calls `xrEndFrame` with the original predicted display time. This is an explicit timeline: wait/predict -> update/listeners -> pose locate/cache -> stereo views -> begin XR frame at render start -> render/resolve -> end XR frame after the graphics swap. There is no separate source-demonstrated late-latch stage beyond this predicted-time sampling/caching design.\n\nSTEREO / PROJECTION / CULLING: `components/stereo/stereomanager.cpp` supports both brute-force OpenSceneGraph stereo and `OVR_multiview2`. The brute-force path gives OSG SceneViews explicit left/right projection and view-offset callbacks and unique cull-visitor identifiers. The multiview path disables OSG's ordinary stereo and supplies shader defines (`useOVR_multiview=1`, `numViews=2`) plus a multiview framebuffer. Every frame, each runtime eye receives its own pose and asymmetric `Stereo::FieldOfView`; separate normal and reverse-Z perspective matrices are built. The master OSG camera gets a union FOV containing both eyes, while `StereoFrustumManager` transforms both eye clip volumes into the master clip space and unions their bounding boxes; the same custom frustum can be supplied to shared shadow-map culling. Reusable rule: render with the physical per-eye matrices but cull against a conservative union so geometry visible to either eye survives CPU culling.\n\nSWAPCHAINS / GRAPHICS-API INTEROP: `components/xr/platform.cpp` negotiates the graphics binding rather than assuming one API. On Windows it can use native OpenGL via `XR_KHR_opengl_enable`, or—when the runtime/driver path supports it—select `XR_KHR_D3D11_enable` and create a D3D11 device while OpenMW continues rendering through OpenGL. `components/vr/directx.cpp` bridges D3D11 OpenXR images into GL through `WGL_NV_DX_interop2`. The format negotiation explicitly maps only exact DXGI<->GL equivalents; unsupported runtime formats are discarded, and the runtime-specific `disable texture format` list can blacklist troublesome formats. `DirectXSharedImage` locks/unlocks the interop object around GL use and, when an OpenXR runtime image cannot be directly registered, creates an intermediate D3D11 texture and `CopyResource`s it back into the runtime image. This is a concrete source-level solution for a legacy/OpenGL renderer whose OpenXR runtime exposes a D3D11 graphics path.\n\nDEPTH / REPROJECTION / POSTPROCESS: if `XR_KHR_composition_layer_depth` is enabled, OpenMW-VR creates per-eye depth swapchains and attaches `XrCompositionLayerDepthInfoKHR` to projection views. Reverse-Z is handled explicitly by swapping the near/far values submitted to OpenXR. If depth swapchain creation fails, `VR::Viewer::setupSwapchains` logs the failure and disables depth sharing instead of aborting VR. `XR_MSFT_composition_layer_reprojection` is queried for supported modes and depth reprojection is used only when available. The ordinary OpenMW postprocessor is adapted rather than bypassed: `MWVR::PingPongCallback` redirects final ping-pong output into the current VR eye/multiview framebuffer, and at the end copies the appropriate opaque/primary depth into the stereo target so the viewer can submit it. This preserves the engine's existing full-screen postprocess chain while making its output and depth destination VR-aware.\n\nCOORDINATE BOUNDARY / REFERENCE SPACES: `components/xr/typeconversion.cpp` centralizes the OpenXR<->OSG axis remap: XR `(x,y,z)` becomes OSG `(x,-z,y)` and quaternion `(x,y,z,w)` becomes `(x,-z,y,w)`. Positions remain typed as `Stereo::Position`, which distinguishes meters from Morrowind world units. The outbound quaternion conversion also replaces near-zero malformed quaternions with identity because runtimes may fail on invalid orientation data. `components/xr/space.cpp` implements LOCAL/VIEW/STAGE reference spaces and can recenter X/Y/Z independently by recreating the reference space with an offset measured against another reference. On session focus, horizontal and vertical recenter are requested. This keeps runtime coordinate conversion and recentering at one boundary instead of allowing gameplay code to invent ad-hoc axis swaps.\n\nPLAYER/WORLD TRACKING ANCHOR: `apps/openmw/mwvr/vranimation.cpp::VRAnimation::updateSpace` locates VIEW relative to LOCAL and treats the change in physical head yaw as native character yaw. It rotates the real player object through OpenMW's `World::rotateObject`; if some other game system rotated the character, it compensates `mCharLocalSpacePose.orientation` so tracking does not receive that same yaw twice. `updateLocalSpaceWorldPose` derives the player's world origin from the actual animation parent transform, adds measured character-head height, and writes `XR::Session::setReferenceWorldPose(originPose + mCharLocalSpacePose)`. All `locateInWorld()` consumers then use that explicit LOCAL->world anchor. This is the core spatial contract: XR LOCAL is stable physical tracking; the game character remains authoritative for world position/yaw; a rebased reference-world pose maps the two together.\n\nCAMERA: `apps/openmw/mwrender/camera.cpp` owns a dedicated `Mode::VR`. `Camera::onSpaceUpdate` locates the OpenXR VIEW space in world coordinates and stores it as `mTrackedPose`; VR `getPosition` and `getOrientation` return that tracked pose directly and `updateCamera` builds the OSG camera from it. Attempts to switch to ordinary non-VR camera modes are ignored while VR is active. Camera instant transitions are forwarded to VR session listeners so dependent spaces/UI can reset synchronously. This makes the tracked VIEW pose authoritative for the rendered camera while player/body rotation remains a separate gameplay state.\n\nINPUT / CONTROLLER CAPABILITIES: `files/openxrinteractionprofiles.xml` is a data-driven device database. It enumerates controller top-level paths and per-profile Boolean, float, axis, pose and haptic interactions, including interactions requiring optional OpenXR extensions. `components/xr/interactionprofiles.cpp` parses the database, expands `/user/hand/*` into both hands and collects required extensions. `apps/openmw/mwvr/openxrinput.cpp` walks that database to dynamically create every corresponding OpenXR action and action space in separate Lua, Pose and Haptics action sets. Before `xrSuggestInteractionProfileBindings`, bindings are filtered against enabled profile/interaction extensions and actually available paths. All action sets are then attached in the single `xrAttachSessionActionSets` call allowed by OpenXR. This avoids hard-coded Vive/Touch/Index branches inside gameplay code.\n\nSEMANTIC INPUT / HANDEDNESS / SCRIPT EXTENSION: `files/data/scripts/omw/vr/inputs/common.lua` enumerates the runtime-supported interaction paths exposed by the engine, creates raw per-path Lua input actions, splits 2D axes into independently bindable left/right/up/down semantic ranges, applies a 20% dead zone, and loads different per-interaction-profile defaults for right- and left-handed modes. It then maps those device paths to higher-level OpenMW actions/triggers grouped into gameplay/menu interaction sets. It even scans OpenMW settings after startup so mod-added input bindings can be surfaced into the VR binding UI. `apps/openmw/mwlua/vrbindings.cpp` exposes input values, interaction-profile names, derived spaces, frame-gated space location and haptic outputs to Lua. Haptic output is real OpenXR plumbing (`xrApplyHapticFeedback` with clamped amplitude), although this source snapshot does not contain a built-in gameplay system that calls the Lua output API.\n\nHANDS / ANIMATION: this is not full-body IK and not skeletal-finger tracking. `VRAnimation::TrackingController` locates each selected AIM space in world coordinates, converts it to a Morrowind-friendly base wrist orientation, removes the authored wrist child translation, applies user hand calibration and writes the tracked result onto the existing forearm transform. `HandController` overrides the hand-bone rotation with weapon-class-specific comfort angles; `FingerController` can straighten the first two index-finger bones for pointing by temporarily bypassing nested animation callbacks. In `VM_VRFirstPerson`, `updateParts` explicitly removes the head, hair, arms, torso, legs and other body parts, retaining the tracked hand presentation. Controllers are attached/removed cleanly when interaction-profile activity changes. This is a direct animation graft, not an elbow/shoulder/body solver.\n\nWEAPON / MAGIC AIM: a `WeaponDirectionController` creates one explicit aim transform parented under the right-hand model. For bows, crossbows, thrown weapons, spells, arrows/bolts and hand-to-hand it cancels the hand's comfort rotation and produces a stable forward aim; melee weapon presentation instead points up from the hand. Native gameplay code consumes that transform. `mwrender/weaponanimation.cpp::releaseArrow` changes the player projectile orientation to the VR aim node while keeping Morrowind's native charge-derived speed, projectile creation, inventory removal and bow/ammo logic. Thrown weapons use the actual animated weapon node position as launch position and still use native min/max throw speed from `attackStrength`; controller velocity is not used as throw speed. `mwworld/projectilemanager.cpp::launchMagicBolt` substitutes the preferred controller pose for player spell origin/orientation, while `World::castSpell` can raycast from the VR aim node. This is a high-value adapter pattern: override only spatial origin/direction at the native projectile/cast seam and leave damage, projectile state, enchantment, inventory and spell systems authoritative.\n\nREALISTIC\n\nMELEE: `apps/openmw/mwvr/realisticcombat.cpp` is an explicit Ready -> Launch -> Swing -> Impact -> Cooldown state machine. Crucially, it locates the tracked weapon hand in OpenXR LOCAL space, specifically so character/world movement does not contaminate measured swing speed. On tracking reacquisition it resets the previous position before calculating velocity to prevent a bogus high-speed strike. Weapon orientation defines thrust, sharp-edge and side axes; movement velocity is projected onto those axes to classify thrust/chop/slash, with weapon-class rules rejecting invalid blunt-side attacks. A minimum velocity arms the attack, a minimum traveled distance transitions Launch->Swing, peak velocity maps to attack strength, and falling below the threshold registers a miss. During Swing, `World::getVRMeleeHitContact` uses Morrowind's native weapon/hand reach and the VR aim node to query the existing physics `getHitContact`; a contact goes through native `evaluateHit` and `Class::hit(..., vr=true)`. It is physical velocity-driven melee, but the contact test is the engine's directional reach query rather than a swept weapon-volume solver.\n\nWORLD POINTER / PHYSICAL INTERACTION: `UserPointer` uses a `VR::SpaceTransform` sourced from a tracked space, raycasts through `Util::getPoseTarget`, respects native activation distance/telekinesis rules and distinguishes 3D GUI from world geometry. Activation adapts to native game state instead of inventing a second interaction system: drag/drop uses OpenMW's item model to place/drop into the world, console mode selects the object, inventory/container mode picks it up, normal activation calls the Lua manager's native `objectActivated`, and equipped lockpicks/probes reuse the existing `MWMechanics::Security` pick/probe logic. Placement additionally rejects wall-like surfaces by requiring the hit normal to be within 30 degrees of world up. This is a controller-ray-to-native-semantics bridge.\n\nVR GUI: MyGUI is decomposed into named VR layers. Each `VRGUILayer` creates a filtered MyGUI camera and `GUIRTT`, computes the bounding rectangle of the actual widgets/Lua elements, optionally auto-sizes physical extent from pixels-per-meter, and textures stereo-aware OSG quad geometry placed in a configured tracked/derived space. Pickable versus non-intersectable node masks keep controller rays from hitting decorative layers. `VRGUIManager::updateFocus` identifies a hit layer from OSG user data; `computeGuiCursor` maps the local quad hit back through the cropped rectangle into native MyGUI pixel coordinates. The ordinary mouse manager then receives cursor/mouse-wheel state, and clicks first attempt the existing MyGUI widget click path before falling back to native SDL mouse events. `VrVirtualKeyboard` is wired to MyGUI `EditBox` focus and injects ordinary MyGUI key/text events. The code contains `VR::QuadLayer` support scaffolding, but in this supplied source `mVrLayer` is default-null and no assignment was found; the live path therefore uses textured stereo world geometry rather than active OpenXR compositor quad layers.\n\nUI COMFORT / STATE: Lua VR UI code intentionally does not continuously drag menus after the controller/head. `ui/player.lua` captures/updates reference poses when the user enters a paused GUI state, when visible layer arrangement changes, or on recenter; otherwise the existing pose is frozen because continuously tracking the UI is described as uncomfortable/impractical. This is a useful state rule for diegetic/world menus: sample the desired anchor at state transition, then leave the panel stable until another deliberate transition.\n\nLOCOMOTION / TURNING / CROUCH: the baseline OpenMW movement model remains authoritative. Snap turn writes `self.controls.yawChange` in configured angular increments; smooth turn writes a frame-scaled yaw delta from semantic LookLeft/LookRight ranges, so both feed OpenMW's normal player control path instead of applying a renderer-only yaw. Physical sneak compares tracked VIEW height against a recenter/reference pose and returns that result through the ordinary `Sneak` action, optionally OR-ing it with conventional input. Hand-directed movement is implemented lower in the engine: `VRAnimation::updateSpace` locates the chosen hand AIM relative to VIEW, derives hand pitch/yaw without roll, stores it in `VR::Session::movementAngleOffset`, and `mwphysics/mtphysics.cpp` adds that offset to the player physics rotation before native movement simulation. The supplied source does not expose a Doom-style collision-reconciled roomscale translation system; physical tracking is anchored to the native player world pose rather than directly moving the collision body from HMD X/Y displacement.\n\nPOSTPROCESS / MIRROR: the VR viewer owns a multiview framebuffer even when brute-force stereo is used, and blits the resolved per-eye color/depth into acquired XR swapchain images. A configurable desktop mirror can show left, right or both eye targets. Postprocessing gets a VR-specific ping-pong callback so the last full-screen pass resolves into the correct current VR destination rather than the ordinary window FBO. In multiview the callback addresses the center/multiview target; in sequential stereo it chooses left/right based on the frame-pass ordering. This is a practical example of preserving an existing postprocess graph while changing only the final destination and depth-export seam.\n\nMULTIPLAYER / NETWORKING: this OpenMW-VR source is the ordinary single-player OpenMW engine fork. No XR hand/head pose replication path is present in the supplied source, so remote VR avatar reconstruction/network packet design should not be inferred from it.\n\nREUSABLE ARCHITECTURE: OpenXR session/predicted frame -> frame-gated cached spaces/actions -> VIEW/LOCAL tracking -> explicit LOCAL-to-game-world anchor -> tracked camera and hand/forearm graft -> semantic OpenMW actions plus native physics -> native projectile/spell/melee/activation systems; in parallel, asymmetric eye views -> OSG brute-force or multiview stereo -> conservative stereo culling/postprocess -> optional depth -> OpenXR projection submission. The source's strongest general lesson is to keep XR timing, coordinate conversion, game-world anchoring, animation presentation and native gameplay authority as separate seams with explicit data contracts.\n\nEVIDENCE BOUNDARIES: VERIFIED FROM SOURCE for the OpenXR frame loop, stereo/multiview projections, swapchains/interops, coordinate conversion/reference spaces, camera/world anchor, action database, Lua input bridge, tracked forearm/hand graft, projectile/spell seams, realistic melee, pointer/UI, native turning/sneak/hand-directed movement and postprocess/depth route. VERIFIED FROM BUNDLED DOCUMENTATION for user-facing VR setup/realistic-combat descriptions. NOT ESTABLISHED / NOT IMPLEMENTED IN THIS SNAPSHOT: full-body IK, OpenXR skeletal-finger retargeting, controller-velocity throw speed, swept weapon-volume melee, remote XR pose networking, collision-driven roomscale body translation, or an active GUI `VR::QuadLayer` path. The source contains compositor-layer scaffolding, but `mVrLayer` is never assigned in the supplied tree.\n\nIMPORTANT SOURCE FILES: `components/xr/session.cpp`, `platform.cpp`, `space.cpp`, `interactionprofiles.cpp`, `typeconversion.cpp`, `components/vr/session.cpp`, `viewer.cpp`, `directx.cpp`, `components/stereo/stereomanager.cpp`, `frustum.cpp`, `apps/openmw/mwvr/openxrinput.cpp`, `vranimation.cpp`, `realisticcombat.cpp`, `vrgui.cpp`, `vrpointer.cpp`, `vrutil.cpp`, `vrinputmanager.cpp`, `vrvirtualkeyboard.cpp`, `vrpingpongcallback.cpp`, `apps/openmw/mwrender/camera.cpp`, `weaponanimation.cpp`, `postprocessor.cpp`, `pingpongcanvas.cpp`, `apps/openmw/mwworld/worldimp.cpp`, `projectilemanager.cpp`, `apps/openmw/mwphysics/mtphysics.cpp`, `files/openxrinteractionprofiles.xml`, and Lua VR input/UI modules.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_predicted_frame_contract"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `XR::Session::syncFrameUpdate` calls `xrWaitFrame`, stores `predictedDisplayTime/Period` and clears `mPoseCache`. `VR::Session::frameBeginUpdate` publishes those values and opens `setLocatingSpacesAllowed(true)` only after frame listeners run; `updateSpaces` closes the window. `XR::Session::locateSpace` throws outside that window, locates at the frame predicted time, classifies Good/Stale/Lost tracking and caches each `(space, reference)` result. `VR::Viewer::updateView` locates LOCAL and VIEW eye views at that same timestamp; the first stereo draw calls `xrBeginFrame`, final draw resolves/blits, and swap-buffers calls `xrEndFrame` with the original predicted time. Reusable rule: make predicted display time a frame token shared by HMD, controllers, eyes and compositor submission, and make unsynchronized pose reads impossible by API design.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/xr/session.cpp`; `components/vr/session.cpp`; `components/vr/viewer.cpp`; `components/vr/session.hpp`. Key symbols: `syncFrameUpdate`, `frameBeginUpdate`, `updateSpaces`, `locateSpace`, `locateViews`, `initialDrawCallback`, `finalDrawCallback`, `swapBuffersCallback`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Own one XR frame timeline and make every pose consumer use the same predicted display time; reject arbitrary pose reads outside the synchronized update phase.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_osg_stereo_modes"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `Stereo::Manager` accepts runtime `Stereo::View` objects and builds eye view offsets plus separate asymmetric projection matrices. `setupBruteForceTechnique` uses OSG horizontal-split SceneViews with explicit left/right compute callbacks and distinct cull-visitor IDs. `setupOVRMultiView2Technique` instead supplies `useOVR_multiview=1`/`numViews=2`, a multiview framebuffer and per-view matrix arrays. Reverse-Z eye projections are generated alongside normal projections. Reusable rule: make per-eye pose/FOV the renderer-facing interface, then choose sequential stereo versus GPU multiview as an implementation detail rather than letting gameplay know which stereo technique is active.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/stereo/stereomanager.cpp`; `components/stereo/multiview.cpp`. Key symbols: `setupBruteForceTechnique`, `setupOVRMultiView2Technique`, `Manager::update`, `computeEyeProjection`, `computeEyeViewOffset`, `shaderStereoDefines`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Keep the game-facing stereo contract stable while allowing either two OSG eye passes or one multiview render path behind it.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_stereo_union_frustum"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `Stereo::Manager::update` constructs a master FOV from min(left/down) and max(right/up) across both runtime eyes. `StereoFrustumManager::update` then maps all eight clip-space corners of each slave eye projection through inverse eye projection and the master projection, expanding one bounding box. Multiview culling receives that box through `InitialFrustumCallback`; shared shadow cull visitors can receive the same custom frustum. This separates precise per-eye rendering from conservative shared CPU/shadow visibility.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/stereo/frustum.cpp`; `components/stereo/stereomanager.cpp`. Key symbols: `joinBoundingBoxes`, `StereoFrustumManager::update`, `customFrustumCallback`, master union-FOV construction.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Render with each physical eye frustum but cull against a transformed union so CPU culling and shared shadow maps never discard geometry visible to only one eye.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_gl_d3d11_xr_interop"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `XR::Platform` can select `XR_KHR_D3D11_enable` on Windows when `WGL_NV_DX_interop2` exists, while OpenMW itself continues rendering in GL. Runtime swapchain DXGI formats are translated only when an exact GL equivalent exists; incompatible formats are discarded and per-runtime format blacklisting is supported. `DirectXWGLInterop` creates the D3D11 device and imports D3D resources as GL texture names through the NV interop extension. `DirectXSharedImage` locks/unlocks ownership around rendering and, for runtime images that cannot be registered directly, uses an intermediate D3D11 texture then `CopyResource`s into the OpenXR image. Reusable rule: negotiate the runtime graphics API separately from the game renderer and build an explicit GPU-only interop ownership layer.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/xr/platform.cpp`; `components/vr/directx.cpp`; `components/xr/swapchain.cpp`. Key symbols: graphics-extension selection, DXGI/GL format translation, `DirectXWGLInterop`, `DirectXSharedImage::beginFrame/endFrame`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Bridge an OpenGL source renderer into a D3D11-only/preferred OpenXR graphics path without staging eye images through CPU memory.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_depth_reversez_reprojection"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `XR_KHR_composition_layer_depth` enables separate per-eye depth swapchains and attaches `XrCompositionLayerDepthInfoKHR` to the projection views. When `SceneUtil::AutoDepth` uses reversed Z, the near/far values supplied to OpenXR are swapped to match. `XR_MSFT_composition_layer_reprojection` is enumerated and depth reprojection is enabled only if the runtime reports it. If creating a depth swapchain fails despite the extension, `Viewer::setupSwapchains` logs the failure, clears both depth swapchains and disables sharing while keeping color VR alive. Reusable rule: capability -> create -> validate -> degrade to color-only, never fail the entire VR session for optional depth.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/xr/extensions.cpp`; `components/xr/session.cpp`; `components/vr/viewer.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/vrpingpongcallback.cpp`. Key symbols: `initCompositionLayerDepth`, `initMSFTReprojection`, `setupSwapchains`, `submitDepthForView`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Treat depth-assisted reprojection as a negotiated capability rather than a hard dependency, and make the depth metadata match the engine’s actual depth convention.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_coordinate_boundary"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `components/xr/typeconversion.cpp` maps XR vectors `(x,y,z)` to OSG `(x,-z,y)` and quaternions `(x,y,z,w)` to `(x,-z,y,w)` in one place. `Stereo::Position` carries meters versus Morrowind units explicitly. Conversion back to OpenXR validates quaternion magnitude and substitutes identity for a near-zero quaternion because malformed orientations can make runtimes fail. Reusable rule: normalize runtime coordinates once, preserve physical units in the type system, validate outbound transforms, and keep all later gameplay math in the engine convention.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/xr/typeconversion.cpp`; `components/stereo/types.*`. Key symbols: `fromXR(XrVector3f)`, `fromXR(XrQuaternionf)`, `toXR(osg::Quat)`, `Stereo::Position`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Make coordinate conversion a typed boundary so cameras, hands, weapons and UI cannot each invent different axis swaps or world-scale math.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_reference_world_anchor"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRAnimation::updateSpace` compares current and prior VIEW-in-LOCAL yaw and applies only that physical head-yaw delta to the real player with `World::rotateObject`. It separately detects yaw applied by other native game systems and pre-multiplies the local-space anchor by the inverse/equivalent compensation. `updateLocalSpaceWorldPose` takes the actual player animation parent world translation, adds measured head height and calls `XR::Session::setReferenceWorldPose`. Every `Space::locateInWorld` then composes LOCAL tracking through that anchor. Reusable rule: one explicit tracking-to-world anchor mediates native body movement and physical tracking instead of adding world/player transforms independently inside camera, hand and weapon code.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `components/xr/space.cpp`; `components/xr/session.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/vranimation.cpp`. Key symbols: `ReferenceSpace::recenter`, `locateSpaceInWorld`, `setReferenceWorldPose`, `VRAnimation::updateSpace`, `updateLocalSpaceWorldPose`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Keep XR LOCAL stable while making the native character remain authoritative for world placement; compensate external character rotation so it is not applied twice to head/hands.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_tracked_camera_mode"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `MWRender::Camera::onSpaceUpdate` locates the OpenXR VIEW space in world coordinates and stores `mTrackedPose`; VR `getPosition`/`getOrientation` return it directly and `updateCamera` rebuilds the OSG look-at view. While VR is active, `setMode` refuses transitions to flat camera modes. `instantTransition` notifies the VR session so dependent systems can reset together. Reusable rule: do not mix head pose into the same yaw/pitch variables used for avatar steering—make the camera a tracked consumer of the world anchor and let the avatar be a separate authority.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwrender/camera.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/vranimation.cpp`. Key symbols: `Camera::onSpaceUpdate`, `setPose`, VR branches of `getPosition/getOrientation`, `setMode`, `instantTransition`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Give VR its own camera mode whose transform comes only from the tracked VIEW pose, while locomotion/body rotation remains native game state.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_interaction_profile_database"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `openxrinteractionprofiles.xml` lists interaction profiles, top-level controller paths, value types and optional required extensions. `interactionprofiles.cpp` parses it, expands `/user/hand/*` into left/right and collects required extensions. `OpenXRInput::createLuaActions` scans the complete database and creates Boolean, float, axis, pose and haptic actions plus pose spaces. `attachActionSets` filters each profile’s suggested bindings against enabled extensions and known paths before calling `xrSuggestInteractionProfileBindings`, then attaches all action sets in one `xrAttachSessionActionSets` call. Reusable rule: controller support becomes an editable capability database while code consumes a uniform action/space API.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `files/openxrinteractionprofiles.xml`; `components/xr/interactionprofiles.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/openxrinput.cpp`; `components/xr/actionset.cpp`. Key symbols: `loadInteractionProfiles`, `createLuaActions`, `createPoseActions`, `attachActionSets`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Describe controller capabilities as data, then generate the OpenXR action surface and suggested bindings instead of hardcoding runtime/device branches in gameplay.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_lua_semantic_input"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `inputs/common.lua` enumerates only supported runtime interaction paths, creates raw path actions, converts axis components into independent directional ranges, applies a 20% dead zone, and loads per-profile defaults from separate right-/left-handed binding tables. Higher-level templates distinguish Always, Gameplay, Menu and interaction contexts. Existing OpenMW actions are aliased so movement/use/sneak continue through normal control semantics, and after startup the script can inspect settings to expose mod-added input bindings. Reusable rule: runtime capability -> raw action -> semantic action/trigger -> native game control, with handedness and device defaults confined to the mapping layer.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `files/data/scripts/omw/vr/inputs/common.lua`; right/left-handed default-binding modules; `apps/openmw/mwlua/vrbindings.cpp`. Key mechanisms: per-path actions, deadzone, axis splitting, action-set templates, profile defaults and mod binding discovery.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Put a semantic action layer between runtime paths and gameplay so mods, handedness, menus and controller diversity do not leak into engine logic.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_forearm_animation_graft"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRAnimation::TrackingController` locates an AIM space in world coordinates, applies a Morrowind-specific wrist basis, cancels the authored wrist child translation, adds user calibration and writes the result onto the existing forearm matrix in local hierarchy. `HandController` replaces hand rotation with weapon-class comfort offsets while temporarily bypassing nested animation callbacks; `FingerController` can straighten two index-finger joints for pointing. Tracking callbacks are attached/removed when controllers become active/inactive. `VM_VRFirstPerson::updateParts` removes most body/arm parts and keeps the tracked hand presentation. This is an animation-bone graft, not elbow/shoulder/full-body IK or OpenXR skeletal finger retargeting.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwvr/vranimation.cpp`. Key symbols: `TrackingController`, `HandController`, `FingerController`, `enableTracking`, `disableTracking`, `updateParts`. This source explicitly demonstrates transform override, not a full-body IK solver.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Reuse the game’s first-person hand/weapon assets by overriding only selected forearm, hand and finger transforms instead of replacing the whole animation system.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_native_projectile_spatial_adapter"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `WeaponDirectionController` creates a stable aim node under the weapon hand, cancelling comfort rotations for ranged/spell/thrown modes. `WeaponAnimation::releaseArrow` uses that node’s world orientation for player shots, while the actual ammo/weapon node supplies launch position and native `attackStrength` still determines projectile speed. Thrown weapons likewise use the animated weapon world position but retain native min/max throw-speed interpolation—controller velocity is not substituted. `ProjectileManager::launchMagicBolt` uses the preferred controller pose for player bolt origin/orientation, and `World::castSpell` can raycast from the VR aim node. Reusable rule: preserve native projectile/cast ownership and replace only origin/orientation at the narrowest existing spawn/target seam.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwvr/vranimation.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwrender/weaponanimation.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwworld/projectilemanager.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwworld/worldimp.cpp`. Key symbols: `WeaponDirectionController`, `releaseArrow`, `launchMagicBolt`, `castSpell`, `launchProjectile`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Change only the spatial launch contract, then hand control back to the game’s original projectile, ammo, charge and spell systems.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_realistic_melee_local_space"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `RealisticCombat::StateMachine` locates the controller in `ReferenceSpace::Local` specifically to exclude world/player movement. Tracking reacquisition resets the prior position to prevent a superspeed strike. Per-frame velocity is projected against weapon thrust, sharp-edge and side axes; weapon class gates invalid side attacks and orientation/motion classify thrust, chop or slash. Ready->Launch requires minimum velocity, Launch->Swing requires traveled distance, peak velocity maps to strength, Impact/Cooldown prevent immediate repeats. `World::getVRMeleeHitContact` uses native weapon/hand reach and the VR aim node with the existing physics `getHitContact`, after which native `evaluateHit` and `Class::hit(..., vr=true)` remain authoritative. The collision is a directional native reach query, not a swept physical blade volume.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwvr/realisticcombat.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwworld/worldimp.cpp`. Key symbols: `StateMachine::update`, Ready/Launch/Swing/Impact/Cooldown transitions, `getVRMeleeHitContact`, `evaluateHit`, `Class::hit`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Measure physical swing intent in tracking-local space so locomotion cannot create fake velocity, classify the strike, then reuse native reach/physics/damage.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_pointer_native_semantics"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `UserPointer` sources a `VR::SpaceTransform`, raycasts through `Util::getPoseTarget`, applies ordinary activation/telekinesis distance rules and ignores non-intersectable 3D GUI masks. On activation, drag/drop uses an `ItemModel` adapter to place or ground-drop objects, console mode selects the object, inventory/container mode invokes the existing pickup path, and ordinary world use calls Lua `objectActivated`. When a lockpick/probe is equipped, the same target goes through `MWMechanics::Security::pickLock/probeTrap`. Surface placement accepts only normals within 30 degrees of world up. Reusable rule: tracked pose chooses *where*; native game state decides *what the interaction means*.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwvr/vrpointer.cpp`; `vrutil.cpp`; `vrinputmanager.cpp`. Key symbols: `UserPointer::update`, `activate`, `tryProbePick`, `Util::getPoseTarget`, `DropItemAtPointModel`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Use the tracked ray only to choose target/position; dispatch the result through the game’s existing context-specific interaction semantics.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_layered_gui_rtt_pointer"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRGUILayer` builds a filtered MyGUI camera and `GUIRTT` for each named layer, computes the bounding rectangle of visible widgets/Lua elements, and auto-sizes the physical extent from configured pixels-per-meter. The texture is applied to stereo-aware OSG quad geometry and pickability is controlled with separate 3D-GUI node masks. Ray hits carry `VRGUILayerUserData`; `computeGuiCursor` converts local quad coordinates through the cropped rectangle back to MyGUI pixels. `VRInputManager` forwards that position, scrolling and clicks through existing MyGUI/SDL paths. The source has `VR::QuadLayer` scaffolding but `mVrLayer` remains unassigned in this snapshot, so the demonstrated active implementation is world-space OSG geometry rather than an XR compositor layer.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwvr/vrgui.cpp`; `vrinputmanager.cpp`. Key symbols: `GUIRTT`, `VRGUILayer::update/updatePose/updateRect`, `createLayerGeometry`, `VRGUIManager::updateFocus`, `computeGuiCursor`, `injectMouseClick`. Full-tree search found no assignment to `mVrLayer`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Reuse the flat UI renderer per logical layer, crop it to live widgets, place the texture in tracked 3D space, and map controller hits back to native pixel events.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_ui_pose_freeze_keyboard"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `files/data/scripts/omw/vr/ui/player.lua` updates default poses/layers on initialization, VR recenter, UI-mode changes and transition into a paused GUI state, then intentionally leaves those reference poses frozen between transitions because actively tracking windows are described as uncomfortable/impractical. `VrVirtualKeyboard` registers focus delegates on MyGUI `EditBox` widgets, displays a VR keyboard layer and injects ordinary MyGUI text/key events (including Tab/Backspace/Return) into the existing focused control. The pointer manager special-cases the keyboard layer so its individual widgets remain directly interactive through modal UI.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `files/data/scripts/omw/vr/ui/player.lua`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/vrvirtualkeyboard.cpp`; `vrgui.cpp`. Key mechanisms: update-on-transition/recenter UI poses, MyGUI EditBox focus delegates and key/text injection.\n\nGENERALIZATION: For comfort, sample UI anchor poses at menu/recenter transitions rather than making panels constantly chase the user; keep text entry inside the game’s existing widget/event system.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_native_turn_and_physical_sneak"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `inputs/player.lua` stores a recenter/reference VIEW pose. Physical sneak compares current tracked Z against that reference minus a configurable offset and returns the result through OpenMW’s existing `Sneak` action, optionally OR-ing normal input. Snap turn writes a configured discrete `self.controls.yawChange`; smooth turn writes a frame-scaled yaw delta from semantic LookLeft/LookRight ranges, and it only writes when those actions changed so ordinary mouse yaw is not accidentally cancelled. Reusable rule: VR locomotion intent should converge into the native player-control contract whenever that contract already handles collision, animation and game state.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `files/data/scripts/omw/vr/inputs/player.lua`; `inputs/common.lua`. Key mechanisms: `SnapTurnLeft/Right`, smooth-turn `yawChange`, recenter-based physical-sneak reference and native `Sneak` action binding.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Translate physical HMD state and VR turn intent into the same control fields the flat game already understands instead of rotating only the rendered headset camera.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_hand_directed_native_movement"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRAnimation::updateSpace` locates the selected hand AIM relative to VIEW, rotates a forward vector by its orientation and derives pitch/yaw while intentionally ignoring roll. It stores that as `VR::Session::movementAngleOffset`. In `mwphysics/mtphysics.cpp`, only for the local VR player with hand-directed movement enabled, this offset is added to `frameData.mRotation` before the normal actor movement simulation. Baseline remains view-directed locomotion; hand-directed mode is literally the view->hand angular delta. This keeps acceleration, collision, inertia and all ordinary OpenMW movement behavior native.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwvr/vranimation.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwphysics/mtphysics.cpp`. Key symbols: `VRAnimation::updateSpace`, `VR::Session::setMovementAngleOffset`, native physics `frameData.mRotation` addition.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Change what “forward” means without replacing the engine’s movement simulation: derive a hand-relative orientation offset and inject it before native physics consumes movement.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_vr_postprocess_depth_route"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `MWRender::PostProcessor` installs `MWVR::PingPongCallback` on both ping-pong canvases when VR is active. `pingPongBegin` identifies left/right sequential eye or multiview center target and replaces the canvas destination FBO/viewport with the VR viewer’s stereo framebuffer. `pingPongEnd` selects the appropriate opaque-depth or primary FBO and asks `VR::Viewer::submitDepthForView` to copy its depth into the stereo target. `Viewer::blit` later resolves each stereo layer to acquired OpenXR color/depth swapchains. Reusable rule: retain the game’s effect chain, redirect the final destination per eye/multiview, then export depth from the postprocess stage that actually owns the final geometry depth.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwrender/postprocessor.cpp`; `pingpongcanvas.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/vrpingpongcallback.cpp`; `components/vr/viewer.cpp`. Key symbols: `setPingPongCallback`, `pingPongBegin`, `pingPongEnd`, `submitDepthForView`, `Viewer::blit`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Adapt the existing full-screen postprocess graph by changing its terminal framebuffer and depth-export seam rather than rewriting every effect for VR.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_openmwvr_scriptable_vr_surface"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `mwlua/vrbindings.cpp` exposes supported interactions, active controller/profile state, raw input values, derived-space creation, space location, world-space location and haptic output. Space location from Lua is subject to the same frame-gating rule as native C++ and throws outside `onVRFrame`, preserving timing correctness across language boundaries. Lua UI code uses derived spaces to place layers relative to head/hands/world and input scripts use the same path database to build semantic actions. Haptic output clamps intensity and routes to `xrApplyHapticFeedback`; this snapshot exposes the mechanism but does not demonstrate built-in gameplay calls to it. Reusable rule: a scripting API should expose *validated spaces/actions*, not raw runtime handles, and should inherit the engine’s timing/lifecycle invariants.\n\nSOURCE AUDIT FILES: `apps/openmw/mwlua/vrbindings.cpp`; `apps/openmw/mwvr/openxrinput.cpp`; Lua modules under `files/data/scripts/omw/vr`. Key\n\nAPI: interaction/profile discovery, derived spaces, frame-gated locate, world locate and `_setOutputValue` -> `ActionSet::applyHaptics` -> `xrApplyHapticFeedback`.\n\nGENERALIZATION: Make XR concepts first-class scripting primitives so per-game/per-mod VR behavior can be authored above the engine without duplicating runtime plumbing.";


// Far Cry VR full-source audit additions (2026-09-06)
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_dxvk_vulkan_openvr_bridge"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. Files/symbols: `VRRenderer.cpp` imports `dxvkGetCreatedDevice`; `VRManager.cpp` imports `dxvkFillVulkanTextureInfo`, `dxvkTransitionImageLayout`, `dxvkLockSubmissionQueue`, `dxvkReleaseSubmissionQueue`; `VRManager::FinishFrame`. D3D9 eye/HUD textures are exposed as Vulkan images, transitioned and submitted to OpenVR as `TextureType_Vulkan` under explicit queue ownership, then restored. GENERALIZATION: if a legacy renderer is already translated to Vulkan, make the translation layer a deliberate interop boundary and share GPU images directly instead of staging through system memory.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_sequential_stereo_timer_freeze"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRRenderer::Render` calls `AwaitFrame`, then `UpdateVRTransformsPreRender`, then `RenderSingleEye(0/1)`. `RenderSingleEye` disables `ITimer` around the second eye's `RenderBegin/Render`, captures the result and re-enables the timer at frame end. GENERALIZATION: replaying a flat renderer for stereo requires identifying which frame services advance simulation and freezing them on replay passes; otherwise stereo itself can become a gameplay tick multiplier.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_symmetric_overscan_crop"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRManager::Init` reads `IVRSystem::GetProjectionRaw` for both eyes and stores the maximum absolute tangent extents; `GetRenderSize`/`ModifyViewCamera` use that common symmetric FOV; `GetEffectiveRenderLimits` converts each eye's real tangents into `VRTextureBounds_t` before `VRCompositor::Submit`. The asymmetric CryEngine frustum update is commented out. GENERALIZATION: oversized symmetric render + per-eye compositor crop is a practical fallback for cameras that cannot express off-axis projection, but CPU/LOD culling still needs separate treatment.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_hybrid_source_renderer_hooks"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRRenderer::Init` installs hooks for `IDirect3DDevice9Ex::Present` (vtable slot 17), `IRenderer::SetCamera` (slot 36) and `SetWindowPos`; `ChangeRenderResolution` temporarily edits renderer desktop-size fields at offsets `0x016808/0x01680C`. The rest of VR gameplay is typed CryGame source. GENERALIZATION: when only part of an engine is open/moddable, keep binary renderer knowledge in a tiny compatibility module and implement camera/player/weapon/input semantics at source level.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_tracking_reference_transaction"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRManager::{UpdatePlayerTurnOffset,UpdatePlayerMoveOffset}` accumulate uncommitted reference yaw/position; movement converts its consumed local offset back into raw HMD reference space. `OnPostPlayerCameraUpdate` is the commit point for `CommitYawAndOffsetChanges`, which replaces active reference state and rebuilds the HMD transform. `ProcessRoomscale` recalibrates after menu/cutscene skips. GENERALIZATION: treat recenter/body-anchor mutation as a transaction with a known commit seam so camera, hands and movement never observe half-updated spaces.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_incremental_roomscale_native_entity"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CPlayer::ProcessRoomscaleMovement` transforms horizontal `ProcessingCmd.GetHmdPos()` through the player frame, clamps each frame's requested displacement to 0.02, aligns motion with `pe_status_living::groundSlope`, calls `IEntity::SetPos`, then reports the consumed offset to `VRManager`. The source comment explicitly says CryEngine appears to prevent clipping but no direct free-position check is available. GENERALIZATION: roomscale can be consumed through native entity movement in small stable increments, but collision guarantees must be stated at the level the source actually provides.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_physical_stance_native_state"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CPlayer::GetVRPhysicalStance` maps tracked HMD height to stand/crouch/prone thresholds; the normal movement code invokes `GoStand`, `GoCrouch`, `GoProne`; `GetVRBasePos` compensates the native stance eye-height/camera-offset behavior using `m_hmdRefHeight` and `m_curPhysicalStance`. GENERALIZATION: physical crouch works best as an intent source for the game's native stance/capsule system, with an explicit camera-height anti-double-application layer.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_recoil_weapon_not_head"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CPlayer::ProcessAngles` diverts recoil into `m_weaponRecoilAngles` under motion controls rather than applying it to player camera angles; `XPlayerCamera.cpp` zeros legacy weapon-lag state and feeds recoil through `SetFirstPersonOffset`. GENERALIZATION: retain native recoil math but change its transform recipient from HMD/camera to the tracked weapon, preserving feedback without forcing the user's head.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_drawnear_fov_repair"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `Hook_Renderer_SetCamera` recognizes CryEngine's near/viewmodel camera via `zMin==0.01f && zMax==40.0f` and overwrites only its FOV with the active VR view FOV before forwarding to the original renderer. GENERALIZATION: old engines frequently have special near-weapon cameras; audit those secondary projections independently from the world camera and remove flat-only FOV tricks while preserving their depth-range purpose.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_weapon_bone_grip_calibration"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CWeaponClass::InitStaticTransforms` reads `RHOffset`, `RHOffsetAngles`, `LHOffset`, `BoneRightHand`, `BoneLeftHand`, `SpitFireBone`; it samples the first-person CryChar bone matrices and applies a left-handed model mirror correction. `CPlayer::ModifyWeaponPosition` maps tracked controller pose through the inverse dominant grip. GENERALIZATION: define controller-to-weapon calibration in weapon/skeleton space and derive it from real animated attachments whenever possible; treat controller pose as the desired grip pose, not the gun model origin.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_two_hand_weapon_modes"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `WeaponClass.h::WeaponTwoHandedMode` defines DISABLED/SINGLE/FULL. `TryEnableTwoHandedWeaponMode` requires the off hand within 0.3 of `GetLHGripWorldTransform`; `ModifyWeaponPosition` only lets FULL derive orientation from the dominant-to-support vector; SINGLE keeps main-hand orientation. Enabling mode reveals the support hand and suppresses idle animation. GENERALIZATION: explicitly model support-hand authority levels so weapons that cannot tolerate full two-hand geometry can still receive physical stabilization/presentation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_prerender_controller_weapon_refresh"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRRenderer::Render` waits for current OpenVR poses and calls local `CPlayer::UpdateVRTransformsPreRender`; that method directly refreshes both controller transforms from `VRManager` and re-runs `CWeaponClass::MoveToFirstPersonPos`. GENERALIZATION: separate simulation-time tracked state from a local presentation-only late update at the last safe pre-render seam. The source supports this for controllers/weapons; do not mislabel it as HMD late-latching.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_tracked_muzzle_native_fire"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CWeaponClass::GetMuzzlePosAngles` composes `m_vPos/m_vAngles * m_muzzleTransform * RotationY(pi/2)`, stabilizes roll and returns world muzzle pose. `CPlayer::GetFirePosAngles` selects it under motion controls. `CWeaponClass::Fire` remains the normal source of fire-rate limiting, synchronized spread seed, ammo, projectiles/rays, collisions, scripts and damage. GENERALIZATION: tracked pose should replace the spatial firing contract while the game's original weapon state machine remains authoritative.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_tracking_speed_native_melee"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CPlayer::CheckMeleeWeaponSwing` measures speed from player-relative `m_controllerTransform[m_mainHand]`, derives world swing direction from consecutive muzzle locations and runs a small `GetEntitiesInBox` + `CollideEntityWithBeam`/ray precheck before setting `m_stats.firing`. The original melee branch of `CWeaponClass::Fire` then performs its own native fat-beam collision/hit selection and normal hit processing. GENERALIZATION: use tracking-local velocity for physical intent and preserve native collision/damage authority; avoid world-motion-induced false swings.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_animation_preserving_hands"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `Hand.cpp::CHand::Update` aligns a stock hands character's hand bone with the tracked controller. `HideUpperArms` collapses parent bones but preserves the joint position; weapon `HideUpperArms`/`UpdateLeftHandVisibility` do the same around first-person weapon rigs and restore support-arm visibility for reload/two-hand states. GENERALIZATION: if the flat game already has rich hand/reload animation, graft tracking at a distal bone and surgically hide incompatible proximal geometry instead of discarding the whole animation stack.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_semantic_openvr_action_contexts"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRInput::Init` resolves semantic actions from external `steamvr/actions.json` across default/move/weapons/vehicles sets. `ProcessInput*` dispatches by context and calls existing `CXClient::Trigger*` methods. `HandleDoubleBindAction` distinguishes short-on-release from long-hold; `IsHandTouchingHead` adds spatial context for flashlight/thermal and scoped zoom. Vehicle mode has independent mappings and no-op/consumption logic to suppress stale actions across context transitions. GENERALIZATION: semantic runtime actions + game-context arbitration scale better than hardware-button checks embedded in gameplay.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_spatial_scope_binocular_overlay"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRManager` captures the native HUD to an OpenVR overlay and reuses it through `SetHudAttachedToHead`, `SetHudInFrontOfPlayer`, `SetHudAsBinoculars`, `SetHudAsWeaponZoom`; a second SideBySide_Parallel overlay carries stereo cinema/binocular rendering. `ModifyBinocularCamera` anchors to the off hand and exponentially smooths narrow-FOV rotation; weapon zoom camera uses the physical fire pose. Overlay mouse scaling is mapped to Far Cry's render dimensions. GENERALIZATION: decouple 2D content production from spatial policy, then select an anchor according to state instead of rewriting every legacy UI screen.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_manual_ladder_native_velocity"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. On a native ladder with `vr_immersive_ladders`, `CPlayer` zeros ordinary vertical ladder speed, detects per-hand grip edges, snapshots the active hand position and sets `speedxyz[2] = -handDelta.z/frameTime * 5`. Release/distance clears the grip; `CheckLadderDismount` raycasts a floor candidate and requires `CanStand` before relocation. GENERALIZATION: convert hand-over-hand displacement into the existing climbing velocity/state machine so collision, ladder membership and weapon restoration remain native.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_event_haptic_mixer"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VRHaptics::Update` sums sampled envelopes from concurrent `ActiveHapticEffect`s for each hand, clamps and triggers the OpenVR haptic action at a fixed step rate. `CPlayer::TriggerWeaponHapticEffect` routes one-hand or two-hand feedback; `TriggerBHapticsEffect` converts world damage direction/height to vest yaw/Y; ProTube methods expose kick/rumble/shot channels. GENERALIZATION: raise semantic feedback events from gameplay once and fan them out through hardware-specific adapters/mixers.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_farcry_vr_processingcmd_network_transport"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `XEntityProcessingCmd.h` adds motion-controls enabled/dominant hand, HMD position/angles/reference height, and two controller position/angle pairs. `CXEntityProcessingCmd::Write/Read` conditionally serialize these with the existing player command bitstream (`eWorldPos`, `eEulerAnglesHQ`), and `CPlayer::ProcessAngles` copies decoded state into per-player VR transforms. GENERALIZATION: extend an existing authoritative input/command packet if XR pose transport is needed, but keep a separate implementation layer for remote hand/body reconstruction. The README/source do not establish finished multiplayer VR embodiment.";
CODE_AUDITS["seed_farcryvr_case"] = "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-06; supplied `farcry_vrmod-main` ZIP). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. ARCHITECTURE: hybrid CryGame source modification plus narrow MinHook access to the opaque CryEngine renderer and a custom DXVK fork. Key files are `VRManager.cpp/.h`, `VRRenderer.cpp/.h`, `VRInput.cpp/.h`, `VRHaptics.cpp/.h`, `Hand.cpp/.h`, `XPlayer.cpp`, `XPlayerCamera.cpp`, `XClient.cpp`, `XEntityProcessingCmd.cpp/.h`, `WeaponClass.cpp/.h`, vehicle code and FCData weapon scripts. The launcher runs the CryVR mod with custom DXVK environment configuration; `.gitmodules` identifies DXVK, MinHook and OpenVR dependencies. RENDERER/XR: OpenVR is initialized in standing space. `VRRenderer` obtains the D3D9Ex device from `dxvkGetCreatedDevice`, hooks Present/SetCamera, samples HMD poses with `WaitGetPoses`, sequentially replays the native render for both eyes, disables the CryEngine timer during the second replay, captures the eye buffers, captures HUD/mirror at pre-Present and submits after Present. `FinishFrame` uses custom DXVK exports to convert D3D9 textures to Vulkan texture descriptors, transition image layouts and lock queue ownership while submitting eye/HUD/stereo textures to OpenVR; no CPU copy is involved. PROJECTION: raw per-eye FOV tangents are reduced to a conservative symmetric engine FOV, while per-eye compositor texture bounds crop the oversized images back to the real asymmetric extents. The source's direct asymmetric frustum update is commented out; documented distant LOD stereo artifacts therefore remain a limitation. The SetCamera hook repairs CryEngine's special DRAW_NEAR FOV. TIMING/LATE UPDATE: HMD pose is sampled once at render start. After that sample, the local player's controllers are refreshed and first-person weapon positioning is recomputed immediately before eye rendering. This is a real presentation-time controller/weapon update but not a second HMD late latch. TRACKING: `OpenVRToFarCry/FarCryToOpenVR` centralize axis conversion. Reference yaw/position and uncommitted variants implement a deferred rebase: roomscale turn/move alters native player state and accumulates the equal tracking correction, then `OnPostPlayerCameraUpdate` commits it. MENU/CUTSCENE skips cause a recalibration before movement resumes. LOCOMOTION: physical yaw goes through normal player delta angles; horizontal roomscale displacement is incrementally clamped, aligned with ground slope and applied through the ordinary entity position, then consumed from tracking reference. HMD height maps to native stand/crouch/prone states and camera base height is compensated. Immersive ladders convert controller pull deltas into native ladder vertical speed and use a ray+`CanStand` dismount. CAMERA/RECOIL: native camera gives world/body base, HMD gives tracked orientation; legacy weapon sway is suppressed and native recoil is diverted into weapon-offset angles rather than HMD rotation.\n\nINPUT: external SteamVR action manifest -> semantic default/move/weapons/vehicles sets -> existing `CXClient::Trigger*` commands. Short/long press multiplexing and spatial hand-to-head modifiers add VR semantics without replacing the native command layer.\n\nHANDS: stock hand/viewmodel skeletons are aligned to controller grip bones; parent arm bones are hidden/collapsed; native reload/support animation visibility is preserved. No full-body/arm IK or skeletal finger tracking is shown.\n\nWEAPONS: per-weapon script metadata and live `BoneRightHand`, `BoneLeftHand`, offsets and `SpitFireBone` derive dominant/support grip and muzzle transforms; left-handed mirroring is corrected. Two-hand behavior is explicit DISABLED/SINGLE/FULL. FULL derives orientation from the hand-to-hand axis while retaining main-hand translation; SINGLE allows physical support without orientation authority. WEAPON FIRE: tracked physical muzzle replaces `GetFirePosAngles`, after which original `CWeaponClass::Fire` retains cadence, synchronized spread, ammo, ray/projectile, hit and script authority. Grenades remain button-triggered with no controller-velocity throw.\n\nMELEE: tracking-relative controller speed gates a physical swing; world muzzle movement supplies direction; a short broad-phase/beam test avoids attacks into empty space; then original native melee fat-beam collision/damage runs. UI/SCOPES: native HUD is captured to an OpenVR overlay with head, fixed-world, off-hand binocular and physical weapon-zoom attachment policies; a separate side-by-side overlay supports stereo cinema/zoom fallback. Binocular rotation is stabilized; native overlay mouse mapping is retained for menus.\n\nHAPTICS: named sampled effects are mixed per hand and the same semantic events fan out to OpenVR controllers, bHaptics and ProTube. NETWORK: `CXEntityProcessingCmd` actually serializes the VR flag, dominant hand, HMD pose/reference height and both controller poses in Far Cry's existing command stream; `CPlayer::ProcessAngles` consumes them. This demonstrates transport groundwork, but the README explicitly says multiplayer motion controls are not properly implemented and no complete remote tracked-avatar presentation is established. GENERALIZATION: this project is a high-value model for combining source-level gameplay adaptation with an opaque legacy renderer: isolate renderer hooking/interop, make tracking/recenter a coherent transaction, keep physical VR gestures as semantic intent, and reuse native movement/weapon/melee/network/UI authorities wherever possible.";


// Crysis VR full-source audit additions — 2026-09-06
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_d3d101_d3d11_keyed_bridge"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`Code/VR/D3DHooks.cpp`, `VRManager.cpp`, `OpenXRRuntime.cpp`). BOOTSTRAP: `CryVRInitD3DHooks` loads the system `d3d10.dll`/`dxgi.dll`, MinHooks `D3D10CreateDevice`, `D3D10CreateDeviceAndSwapChain` and `CreateDXGIFactory`, and redirects them to D3D10.1/DXGI 1.1. `VRManager::InitDevice` gets the game's `ID3D10Device1`, calls `OpenXRRuntime::GetD3D11Requirements` for the runtime adapter LUID/feature level, creates an `ID3D11Device` on that adapter and passes it into `xrCreateSession` through `XrGraphicsBindingD3D11KHR`. RESOURCE HANDOFF: `CreateSharedTexture` creates `DXGI_FORMAT_R8G8B8A8_UNORM` D3D10 textures with `D3D10_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX`, gets the `IDXGIResource` shared handle and `OpenSharedResource`s the same allocation as D3D11. D3D10 captures/resolves the swapchain buffer while key 0 is owned by the producer; `FinishFrame` releases key 1, D3D11 acquires key 1, submits/copies to XR, releases key 0, and the next D3D10 capture reacquires key 0. GENERALIZATION: forcing the smallest legacy API upgrade that exposes modern DXGI sharing can bridge an old renderer to OpenXR without rewriting rendering or copying through the CPU. FAILURE POLICY: after repeated mutex acquisition failures the mod warns specifically about overlay software, showing that shared-GPU synchronization is a compatibility surface that should be diagnosed explicitly.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_openxr_current_next_frame_pose_timing"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXRRuntime::AwaitFrame`, `FinishFrame`, `OpenXRInput::Update`, `UpdateControllerPoses`, `GetControllerVelocity`, `CalcControllerHudIntersection`, `CGame::Update`). VISUAL TIMELINE: render-side `AwaitFrame` calls `xrWaitFrame`, caches `predictedDisplayTime` and `predictedDisplayPeriod`, calls `xrBeginFrame`, and locates `XR_VIEW_CONFIGURATION_TYPE_PRIMARY_STEREO` views at the current predicted time. `FinishFrame` submits projection/quad layers with `displayTime = m_predictedDisplayTime`. INPUT TIMELINE: normal game update calls `gXR->Update()` before weapon-system/post-update work; that does `xrSyncActions` and locates grip poses/velocities at `GetNextFrameDisplayTime()`, defined as current predicted display time plus one predicted period. The UI controller ray uses the same next-frame target. GENERALIZATION: pose time is part of the API contract, not incidental metadata. A port can intentionally use separate visual and control predictions, but should name them and keep them consistent rather than mixing arbitrary 'latest' transforms. LIMIT: there is no second HMD locate after the render-side `xrLocateViews`, so do not call this HMD late-latching.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_sequential_stereo_sideeffect_gating"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRRenderer::Render`, `RenderSingleEye`, `VR_CryRenderD3D10_Shadows`). `Render` snapshots the original Cry camera, starts XR, invokes the ordinary `ISystem::Render` once for eye 0, calls `ISystem::RenderBegin()` to reset state/culling, then invokes it again for eye 1. Eye 1 temporarily sets the `e_particles_debug` AlphaBit('z') path to prevent the particle system from updating again. The shadow hook calls the native shadow routine only when the current eye requires it, avoiding a duplicate shadow-map build. Each eye is cleared, rendered, HUD faders are handled and the backbuffer is captured. GENERALIZATION: legacy engines frequently mix simulation/update work into drawing. A sequential stereo conversion must classify those side effects and make one eye 'authoritative' for one-per-frame work; simply calling Render twice is not sufficient. The fixed shadow RVA is implementation-specific and should be replaced by validated signature/symbol resolution in a generalized port.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_symmetric_projection_asymmetric_crop"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRManager::ModifyViewCamera`, `GetRenderSize`, `GetEffectiveRenderLimits`, `CCamera::UpdateFrustumFromVRRaw`, `OpenXRRuntime::SubmitEyes`). CryEngine's exposed camera is symmetric, so each eye's raw `XrFovf` tangents are reduced to a conservative symmetric vertical FOV and an oversized target. `GetEffectiveRenderLimits` converts the real left/right/up/down tangents into a normalized rectangle; `SubmitEyes` uses those rectangles as D3D11 copy boxes before placing each image into the side-by-side XR swapchain. With `vr_enable_frustum_tweaks`, `CCamera::UpdateFrustumFromVRRaw` replaces culling planes with the true asymmetric tangents even though the raster projection remains symmetric. GENERALIZATION: projection, culling and compositor cropping can be treated as separate compatibility layers. If an old camera cannot express off-axis projection, overscan + per-eye crop is a viable fallback, while culling can still be corrected independently.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_swapchain_hook_reacquisition"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`D3DHooks.cpp`, `VRRenderer::Render`, `VRManager::SetSwapChain`). `IDXGIFactory::CreateSwapChain` records `g_latestCreatedSwapChain`. Before every stereo render, `VRRenderer` compares this with the swapchain currently owned by `VRManager` and measures elapsed time since its Present hook ran. On mismatch or >1000 ms silence it removes the old Present/Resize hooks, installs them on the newest swapchain vtable and calls `SetSwapChain`; a changed swapchain resets graphics-device state so it can be reinitialized. GENERALIZATION: window mode, resolution changes and renderer restarts can replace swapchains under injected VR code. Treat the swapchain as lifecycle state and make hook ownership recoverable. LIMIT: this resilience does not validate arbitrary executable addresses; the separate shadow hook is still a raw version-specific offset.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_tracking_reference_rebase"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXRRuntime::CreateSession`, `OpenXRToCrysis/CrysisToOpenXR`, `VRManager::RecalibrateView`, `GetReferenceTransform`, `GetEyeTransform`, `GetControllerTransform`, `UpdateReferenceOffset`, `UpdateReferenceYaw`). Session tracking uses `XR_REFERENCE_SPACE_TYPE_STAGE`. Conversion centralizes the axis permutation. Recenter stores horizontal HMD position, HMD reference height and yaw. All eye/controller samples are converted into that recentered frame before the player/camera base is applied. When roomscale translation/yaw is transferred into native player state, the same delta is accumulated into the reference origin so it disappears from subsequent tracked offset. Runtime `XR_TYPE_EVENT_DATA_REFERENCE_SPACE_CHANGE_PENDING` is not ignored: after the event time passes `AwaitFrame` triggers `RecalibrateView`. GENERALIZATION: maintain one explicit XR→recentered-player transform and mutate it only when native body state intentionally consumes physical motion; this prevents camera, hands and weapons from each inventing their own recenter math.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_collision_roomscale_consumption"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`CPlayer::ProcessRoomscaleMovement`, `SweepPlayer`, `FindGround`, `VRManager::GetHmdOffset/UpdateReferenceOffset`). The local player's horizontal recentered HMD offset is rotated by actor yaw and only processed in grounded/free gameplay states. Noise below 0.005 is ignored; requests above 0.05 are clamped. `SweepPlayer` traces the player's own physical entity from current to desired position. On collision, a second sweep 0.25 units higher tests a step-up; valid motion is ground-snapped and applied with `IEntity::SetPos`. A one-update pause lets Cry physics refresh before another manual placement. Crucially the corresponding HMD offset is consumed into the tracking reference even when movement is blocked. GENERALIZATION: if physical roomscale is converted into avatar locomotion, collision-check native body movement first and explicitly define what happens to denied physical displacement. Crysis chooses 'consume denied displacement' rather than persistent lean/retry.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_native_physical_stance"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRManager::GetPhysicalStance`, `GetBaseVRTransform`, `CPlayer::UpdateStance`, `PlayerInput::FigureOutStance`). `GetPhysicalStance` compares raw HMD height to the engine's own prone/crouch stance `viewOffset.z` thresholds (+0.15/+0.2 tolerance) and returns an ordinary `EStance`; seated mode returns stand. That physical stance is stored on `CPlayer` and participates in native stance input/state selection. Camera-base construction then compensates differences between native current stance, physical stance and recentered HMD reference height so the flat stance camera is not stacked on top of real crouching. GENERALIZATION: map HMD height to existing gameplay posture first, then remove the original camera's posture displacement. This keeps capsule, animation and gameplay permissions native while avoiding double camera motion.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_animated_grip_weapon_transform"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`CWeapon::PostProcessArms`, `GetInverseGripTransform`, `VRManager::ModifyWeaponPosition`, `ItemView::UpdateFPPosition`, `CRocketLauncher::GetInverseGripTransform`). `PostProcessArms` samples the current first-person animation's terminal hand joint into `m_gripLocation`. `GetInverseGripTransform` composes that live animation transform with weapon-specific grip correction and inverts it. `ModifyWeaponPosition` chooses the correct logical weapon hand (including dual wield), obtains the controller's calibrated world weapon transform, optionally replaces orientation with the two-hand solve, multiplies by the inverse live grip transform, and writes `FPWeaponPos/Angles`. `ItemView` applies those values to the ordinary weapon entity before native skeleton processing continues. GENERALIZATION: solve 'controller drives gun' as an attachment transform around a live authored socket, not by zeroing the animation hierarchy; this preserves reload/recoil/finger motion and reduces per-animation calibration.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_viewmodel_arm_postprocess_finger_pose"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRManager::CalcWeaponArmIK`, `TwoBoneIKSolve`, `EstimateShoulderPosition`, `CWeapon::CalcHandFromControllerInWeapon`, `CWeapon::PostProcessArms`, `HandPoses.cpp`). The code contains a two-bone solver over upperarm/forearm/hand joints, but it is installed as a post-process on CryEngine's stock first-person weapon/viewmodel skeleton. A gun-attached hand may retain the animation's existing hand target; a detached controller hand may substitute a controller-derived target in weapon-local space. Shoulder placement is estimated with reach constraints, the local chain is solved, and the final hand is corrected onto the target. `HandPoses` independently interpolates authored finger rotations from grip/trigger. GENERALIZATION: post-process an existing animated viewmodel skeleton instead of replacing its weapon/reload animation. CLASSIFICATION LIMIT: do not promote this mechanism to a player-visible `Body IK / limb tracking` feature unless the mod actually exposes tracked avatar arms/body; here it is retained only as an implementation detail.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_two_hand_native_zoom_benefits"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`CWeapon::PostProcessArms`, `VRManager::TryGrabWeaponWithOffHand`, `GetTwoHandWeaponTransform`, `DetachOffHandFromWeapon`). The current animated `hand_L_term` becomes a world-space support-grip location every post-process update. The non-dominant controller must enter a 0.3-unit/meter-scale radius before `m_offHandFollowsWeapon` is enabled. The two-hand transform keeps the primary controller's translation and constructs orientation from the vector to the off hand, with weapon-specific yaw adjustment available. On attach the code calls zoom mode 0's existing `ApplyZoomMod` on the active fire mode; detach resets native recoil and spread modifiers. GENERALIZATION: physical support-hand presence can be connected to an existing flat-game 'aimed/stabilized' balance state instead of inventing new recoil/spread math.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_tracked_muzzle_native_fire"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`CWeapon::GetFiringOriginAndDirection`, `PlayerMovementController::UpdateMovementState`, `Single.cpp`, `Lam.cpp`). Because the entire first-person weapon entity has already been solved from the tracked controller, `GetFiringOriginAndDirection` can use its `weapon_term` helper and world forward axis as the physical muzzle, offsetting origin forward 0.5 to avoid self-hit. Local unmounted weapon movement state replaces `weaponPosition` and `fireDirection` with those values while eye/aim state remains separately available. Existing fire modes subsequently use that movement state for rays/projectiles, spread, ammo, firing cadence and effects. GENERALIZATION: make physical aiming alter the smallest spatial contract consumed by the native weapon system. Do not replace the weapon system itself. NEGATIVE: grenade throw speed is not derived from controller velocity and reload is not a physical magazine interaction.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_melee_velocity_native_preflight"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXRInput::UpdateMeleeAttacks`, `CMelee::PerformRayTest`, `PerformCylinderTest`, `CWeapon::MeleeAttack`). Each hand is eligible only with grip >=0.95, OpenXR linear speed above `vr_melee_trigger_velocity` and velocity dot HMD-forward >0.7. The VR layer then fetches the native `SMovementState` eye position/direction and asks the actual melee fire mode whether its ray or cylinder test would hit. Only a positive native preflight causes `MeleeAttack`, controller vibration and side-specific bHaptics. GENERALIZATION: a tracked gesture can be used as semantic intent while the original gameplay collision query determines whether an attack exists. This avoids creating a second damage authority. EVIDENCE LIMIT: the collision ray/cylinder is eye-directed, not a swept controller/weapon path.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_body_zone_weapon_selection"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRManager::IsHandNearShoulder`, `IsHandNearHip`, `IsHandNearChest`, `IsHandBehindBack`, `CPlayer::SelectNextItem`). Zones are computed from raw HMD/controller tracking with head yaw stripped of pitch/roll so body-relative locations remain ergonomic: shoulder uses side/behind-head tests, hip uses relative height plus downward pointing, chest/back use head-yaw-relative front/back and distance/orientation. `SelectNextItem` with no category translates these zones into Cry inventory categories `medium`, `small` and `explosive`, then delegates to the original category selection. GENERALIZATION: a physical holster need not become an inventory object; use spatial zones as an intent classifier and retain the game's existing inventory ownership, history and switching rules.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_offhand_native_pickup_ray"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRManager::UpdateOffHandRayQuery`, `COffHand::CanPerformPickUp`, `GetFiringOriginAndDirection`, `AI_SmartObjectEvent_Hook`). A cached world ray is emitted from the off-hand controller. In single-player `CanPerformPickUp` replaces eye-origin interaction with that ray/proximity origin while retaining native tests for range, item/entity type, busy/reload state, rigid/breakable objects and ordinary inventory behavior. A support hand currently stabilizing a weapon is explicitly unavailable for pickup. The smart-object hook observes `OnUsed`/`OnUsedRelease` involving the local player and sets a player flag without replacing the original event. GENERALIZATION: move the spatial query origin to the tracked hand while leaving native eligibility/finalization untouched. MULTIPLAYER LIMIT: the source explicitly uses the original eye query when `gEnv->bMultiplayer`.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_semantic_openxr_role_bindings"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXRInput::Init`, `CreateInputActions`, `SuggestedProfileBinding`, `SuggestBindings`, `Update*Actions`, `UpdateBooleanAction`). Three action sets separate ingame/menu/vehicle semantics, but all actions ultimately call CryGame's existing `GameActions`/`IPlayerInput::OnAction`. Binding strings can use `<weapon>`, `<!weapon>`, `<movement>`, `<!movement>`; `SuggestedProfileBinding::AddBinding` resolves those tokens from logical-handedness cvars before `xrStringToPath`. Controller-specific differences (Oculus/HP, Index, optional Valve frame controller, left-hand X/Y face buttons) are localized to the suggested-binding layer. `BooleanAction` supports short/long hold, release and press-on-release contracts. GENERALIZATION: represent handedness as roles at binding time and preserve a semantic action vocabulary above hardware paths. Vehicle state remains its own action context and uses native stick actions rather than fake motion steering.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_hud_quad_pointer_bridge"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRManager::CaptureHUD`, `SetHudInFrontOfPlayer`, `SetHudAttachedToHead`, `SetHudAttachedToOffHand`, `SetVehicleHud`, `OpenXRRuntime::SubmitHud/FinishFrame`, `OpenXRInput::CalcControllerHudIntersection/Update`). After world-eye capture, the backbuffer is cleared transparent and legacy HUD/menu rendering is captured into a keyed-mutex shared texture. The D3D11 side copies this into a dedicated XR swapchain and submits an `XrCompositionLayerQuad`. Spatial policy is independent of content: latched fixed-yaw, HMD-following, off-hand binocular or vehicle-aim pose. Pointer math locates the weapon-hand grip at predicted-next-frame time, applies an orientation correction, transforms the ray into the quad's local frame, intersects y=0, normalizes by physical HUD width/height, flips Y, then ten-sample smooths the result before native/ImGui mouse injection. GENERALIZATION: capture flat UI once, spatialize it independently, and adapt tracked rays back into its existing event coordinates.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_scope_binocular_cinema_modes"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRRenderer::GetRenderMode/Render/RenderSingleEye`, `VRManager::ModifyViewCameraFor3DCinema`, `ModifyViewForBinoculars`, `ModifyCameraFor2D`, `OpenXRInput::UpdateWeaponScopes`, `OpenXRRuntime::FinishFrame`). State selects `RM_VR`, `RM_2D` or `RM_3D`. RM_VR submits true projection views. RM_3D uses the native scripted camera plus configurable synthetic left/right displacement and submits each side as an eye-exclusive quad over the shared stereo texture. RM_2D can render the native flat camera once for binocular/zoom/cinema compatibility and rely on HUD/quad presentation. Physical scoped-weapon activation requires two-hand support plus weapon hand within 0.25 m of the head; it exits when support is released or distance exceeds 0.35 m. `ModifyCameraFor2D` derives zoom camera from the live scope helper/weapon transform. Binocular camera is off-hand anchored. GENERALIZATION: incompatible legacy camera modes should be explicit state machines with different presentation contracts, not exceptions buried inside the normal eye transform. This source does not implement a PiP optic texture.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_reflex_crosshair_physical_aim"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRRenderer::DrawCrosshair`, `UpdateShaderParamsForReflexSight`, movement state weapon fields). The 3D crosshair begins at `weaponPosition` with `fireDirection`, raycasts through Cry physics and draws a small sphere at the hit or maximum reach. It is disabled when a laser or rocket launcher owns aim presentation and during states where the physical weapon ray is not appropriate. For the legacy reflex material, the mod copies its shader resource set, finds/creates `ScopePos`, writes `weaponPosition + 10*fireDirection`, and reapplies the shader parameters so the dot has a world target on the real tracked firing line. GENERALIZATION: aim presentation should consume the same spatial contract as gameplay; shader-based legacy sights can often be repaired by injecting a world target rather than replacing the whole material.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_crysis_multichannel_haptic_events"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenXRInput::SendHapticEvent`, `VRHaptics.cpp`, weapon/player event call sites). Controller feedback uses per-hand OpenXR `XR_ACTION_TYPE_VIBRATION_OUTPUT` actions and `xrApplyHapticFeedback`. `VRHaptics::InitEffects` registers semantic bHaptics tact assets for locomotion, damage, explosions, melee, gun classes, suit modes, heartbeat, swimming/sprint and vehicles. Positional feedback computes vest vertical offset from world impact height versus player/HMD height and yaw from impact direction versus player world yaw. ProTube translates normalized kick/rumble parameters into rifle butt/bolt channels, with two-hand weapon support duplicating output to the off-hand channel. GENERALIZATION: keep gameplay code emitting semantic events/strength/location and fan them out through separate device adapters; feedback remains tied to accepted gameplay events instead of raw button presses.";
CODE_AUDITS["seed_crysisvr_case"] = "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-06; supplied `crysis_vrmod-main` ZIP). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE.\n\nARCHITECTURE / BOOTSTRAP — The mod is a hybrid source-level CryGame conversion plus narrow hooks into renderer surfaces not exposed by the CryENGINE 2 Mod SDK. Principal files: `Code/VR/OpenXRRuntime.cpp/.h` (instance/session/frame/swapchains), `OpenXRInput.cpp/.h` (actions, controller poses, haptics, HUD pointer, melee/scope gestures), `VRManager.cpp/.h` (tracking/reference transforms, graphics bridge, camera, weapon transforms, arm IK, HUD policies), `VRRenderer.cpp/.h` (stereo replay, render modes, Present lifecycle, crosshair/reflex repair), `D3DHooks.cpp` (D3D10→10.1/DXGI hooks), `HandPoses.cpp` (analog finger poses), `VRHaptics.cpp` (bHaptics/ProTube), plus direct changes in `Player.cpp`, `PlayerInput.cpp`, `PlayerMovement.cpp`, `PlayerMovementController.cpp`, `Weapon.cpp`, `OffHand.cpp`, `Melee.cpp`, `Single.cpp`, `ItemView.cpp`, `Fists.cpp`, `IronSight.cpp`, `Throw.cpp`, `Binocular.cpp`, `Lam.cpp`, `RocketLauncher.cpp`, `NanoSuit.cpp` and HUD/menu code. `README.md` verifies full 6DOF, motion controllers, body-zone weapon selection, two-hand stabilization, hand-driven nanosuit menu, weapon accessories/drop and OpenXR runtime support; it explicitly says vehicle/stationary-gun aiming remains thumbstick-driven.\n\nLEGACY GRAPHICS → OPENXR — `D3DHooks.cpp::CryVRInitD3DHooks` loads the system `d3d10.dll` and MinHooks both D3D10 create entry points, redirecting them to `D3D10CreateDevice1`/`D3D10CreateDeviceAndSwapChain1` at feature level 10_1. `CreateDXGIFactory` is redirected to DXGI 1.1 and `IDXGIFactory::CreateSwapChain` is hooked so the newest swapchain is discoverable. `VRManager::InitDevice` gets the game's `ID3D10Device1`; OpenXR reports the required D3D11 adapter LUID/feature level through `xrGetD3D11GraphicsRequirementsKHR`; the mod creates a second D3D11 device on that adapter and binds that device to the OpenXR session. `CreateSharedTexture` creates D3D10.1 `R8G8B8A8_UNORM` eye/HUD textures with `D3D10_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX`, retrieves the DXGI shared handle and opens the same allocation as `ID3D11Texture2D`. `CopyBackbufferToTexture` resolves MSAA or copies the backbuffer on D3D10. Explicit keyed-mutex keys transfer producer/consumer ownership to the D3D11 side before OpenXR submission; repeated failures show a diagnostic for overlay software. This is a GPU-only D3D10.1↔D3D11 compatibility bridge.\n\nOPENXR FRAME OWNERSHIP / TEMPORAL CONTRACT — `OpenXRRuntime::AwaitFrame` polls events, runs `xrWaitFrame`, stores both `predictedDisplayTime` and `predictedDisplayPeriod`, derives a next-frame display time, calls `xrBeginFrame`, then locates the stereo views at the CURRENT predicted display time. `OpenXRRuntime::FinishFrame` builds projection or quad layers and calls `xrEndFrame` using that same time. By contrast, `CGame::Update` invokes `gXR->Update` during normal simulation before weapon-system/post-update work; `OpenXRInput::Update` calls `xrSyncActions`, and controller grip poses/velocities plus HUD pointer ray are located at `GetNextFrameDisplayTime()` (current predicted display time + one predicted period). Therefore Crysis VR has a deliberate current-frame-eye / next-frame-controller prediction split. The source does not contain a second HMD locate after render-side `xrLocateViews`, so it should not be described as HMD late-latching.\n\nSESSION / REFERENCE SPACE — `CreateSession` uses `XrGraphicsBindingD3D11KHR` and creates an `XR_REFERENCE_SPACE_TYPE_STAGE` tracking space. Session READY starts the session; STOPPING ends it; focus transitions can pause the game menu. `XR_TYPE_EVENT_DATA_REFERENCE_SPACE_CHANGE_PENDING` is recorded and recalibration is performed after the event's change time has passed. `OpenXRToCrysis` and `CrysisToOpenXR` are the central coordinate boundary: OpenXR x-right/y-up/-z-forward maps to Crysis x-right/y-forward/z-up. All gameplay-space eye/controller transforms pass through this boundary.\n\nSEQUENTIAL STEREO — `VRRenderer::Render` records the original `CCamera`, calls `gVR->AwaitFrame`, renders eye 0 with the ordinary `ISystem::Render`, calls `ISystem::RenderBegin()` to reset renderer/culling state, then renders eye 1. `RenderSingleEye` changes the engine camera to the eye transform, updates DRAW_NEAR FOV, clears, runs the native world renderer, draws a physical world crosshair and captures the backbuffer. On eye 1 it temporarily switches the particle-system debug/update flag so render-driven particle work is not advanced twice. `VR_CryRenderD3D10_Shadows` can suppress a second shadow-map build. After eyes, the backbuffer is cleared transparent so the game's normal HUD/menu can be rendered once and captured independently. `OnPrePresent` captures that HUD; `OnPostPresent` hands shared eye/HUD resources to D3D11 and ends the XR frame. The shadow hook is a raw `CryRenderD3D10.dll + 0x0bee30` address: useful but explicitly version-sensitive.\n\nPROJECTION / CULLING — The exposed Cry camera is fundamentally symmetric. `VRManager::ModifyViewCamera` takes the per-eye OpenXR raw tangent FOV, chooses a symmetric vertical FOV and renders an oversized image. `GetEffectiveRenderLimits` maps the true asymmetric tangents into a normalized crop; `OpenXRRuntime::SubmitEyes` uses those source boxes when copying each D3D11 eye texture into the side-by-side XR swapchain. Optional `CCamera::UpdateFrustumFromVRRaw` repairs culling planes with the real asymmetric tangents even though raster projection remains symmetric. DRAW_NEAR/viewmodel FOV is repaired separately. This is a three-layer compatibility model: symmetric raster projection, asymmetric CPU culling planes, asymmetric compositor crop.\n\nXR SWAPCHAINS / LAYERS — `OpenXRRuntime::CreateStereoSwapchain` creates one D3D11 stereo swapchain sized for two side-by-side eye regions; `SubmitEyes` acquires/waits an image, crops/copies left and right source eye textures into it and releases it. HUD uses its own swapchain and quad layer. `FinishFrame` chooses presentation by `VRRenderMode`: `RM_VR` submits projection views; `RM_3D` submits two eye-exclusive quads built from the stereo image; HUD is a separate quad when visible. `RM_2D` relies on the flat/HUD path. A frame with no world render can still call `AwaitFrame` late and finish an XR frame so menus/HUD continue updating.\n\nTRACKING / RECENTER / BASE CAMERA — `RecalibrateView` stores horizontal HMD reference position, HMD reference height and yaw. `GetReferenceTransform`, `GetEyeTransform` and `GetControllerTransform` subtract/reorient raw XR data into the recentered tracking frame. `GetBaseVRTransform` then composes that with a native CryEngine base. Normal player VR uses actor world position/yaw plus smoothed height/stance compensation. Vehicles, mounted weapons, cutscenes and other linked native views instead use the active native camera position/yaw so those systems retain their authored spatial anchor; cutscene yaw can remain latched until a configurable angular threshold. `ModifyPlayerEye` updates local movement-state eye position/direction to the physical stereo midpoint/view direction outside vehicle/mounted/flat states.\n\nROOMSCALE TRANSLATION — `CPlayer::ProcessRoomscaleMovement` has extensive state guards (no modal HUD, vehicle, ladder, mounted weapon, grabbed/frozen/animation/cutscene/non-player view, movement limits, airborne/jumping). It only acts when grounded/resting so it does not fight native movement. Horizontal HMD offset is rotated by player yaw, noise below 0.005 is ignored and one move is capped at 0.05. `SweepPlayer` tests the player's physical body from current to desired position. On collision it retries 0.25 higher as a step-up; valid motion is snapped to `FindGround`, applied with `SetPos` and followed by a one-update pause so native physics catches up. The corresponding HMD offset is consumed into `m_referencePosition` whether the body moved or was blocked. This prevents repeated wall-pushing accumulation; it intentionally does not preserve blocked displacement as persistent body lean.\n\nROOMSCALE YAW / STANCE / LOCOMOTION — `ProcessRoomscaleRotation` sends current physical HMD yaw delta into a normal `CMovementRequest::AddDeltaRotation` and only calls `UpdateReferenceYaw` when the movement controller accepts it. `GetPhysicalStance` compares raw HMD height against the native prone/crouch `viewOffset.z` values, with seated mode forcing stand. `CPlayer`/`PlayerInput` fold that physical stance into the normal stance state machine. `GetBaseVRTransform` compensates native stance-camera offsets so crouching is not double applied. Analog locomotion and snap/smooth turn continue through existing input actions. Movement direction can be controller-relative; zero-G/flying paths also use the selected reference. Vehicles remain separate native action mappings.\n\nWEAPON TRANSFORM — `CWeapon::PostProcessArms` reads the current animated `hand_R_term`/`hand_L_term` joint and stores it as the live grip transform. `CWeapon::GetInverseGripTransform` multiplies it by calibration and inverts it. `VRManager::ModifyWeaponPosition` gets the tracked controller's calibrated weapon transform, optionally replaces orientation with the support-hand solve, multiplies by the inverse live grip and writes the result into native `FPWeaponPos/FPWeaponAngles`. `ItemView::UpdateFPPosition` then positions the standard first-person weapon entity and lets normal animation preprocessing continue. The rocket launcher can override grip calibration because its generic helper is unsuitable. Zoomed weapon orientation is exponentially smoothed according to zoom FOV; the HMD is not smoothed.\n\nTWO-HAND WEAPONS — `PostProcessArms` converts the live animated `hand_L_term` to `m_offHandGrabLocation`. `TryGrabWeaponWithOffHand` compares the non-dominant controller with that point and engages support when within 0.3. `GetTwoHandWeaponTransform` preserves dominant-hand translation while constructing orientation from the dominant→support vector plus dominant-hand basis; per-weapon yaw correction is supported. Critically, physical support also calls the flat game's zoom-mode `ApplyZoomMod` on the active fire mode, reusing native accuracy/recoil/spread bonuses. Detach calls native `ResetRecoilMod`/`ResetSpreadMod`.\n\nVIEWMODEL ARM POST-PROCESS / FINGERS — `CalcWeaponArmIK` does contain a two-bone IK calculation over `upperarm_*`, `forearm_*` and `hand_*`, but this operates inside the stock first-person weapon/viewmodel skeleton and is not treated as a registry-level tracked-arm/body feature. A weapon-attached hand keeps the existing animation target, while detached hands can use `CWeapon::CalcHandFromControllerInWeapon`; the local shoulder/elbow chain is then post-processed so the hand reaches that target. `HandPoses.cpp` defines fixed open/closed poses for 20 finger joints on each side and NLerps them by `max(grip, trigger)`. This is viewmodel skeletal correction plus authored finger posing, not full-body IK or OpenXR skeletal hand tracking.\n\nFIRE / AIM CONTRACT — The controller-driven weapon entity becomes the source of truth for firing geometry. `CWeapon::GetFiringOriginAndDirection` gets the `weapon_term` helper in world coordinates and uses the weapon's world forward vector; it offsets the origin 0.5 forward to avoid self collision. `PlayerMovementController::UpdateMovementState` leaves eye/aim state available but replaces local unmounted `weaponPosition` and `fireDirection` with the physical muzzle. `Single.cpp`, `Lam.cpp`, rocket/laser code and normal native weapon systems consume those fields, preserving cadence, spread, ammo, projectile/ray creation, damage and effects. `DrawCrosshair` ray-traces the same physical fire ray. `UpdateShaderParamsForReflexSight` writes a world `ScopePos` along the same ray into the legacy reflex material. The architecture is pose adapter → native weapon authority, not a parallel VR weapon simulation.\n\nMELEE — `OpenXRInput::UpdateMeleeAttacks` treats controller motion as semantic intent. It requires grip >= .95, controller linear speed above `vr_melee_trigger_velocity`, motion dot HMD-forward > .7 and no off-hand weapon support. It then asks the existing `CMelee` fire mode to run `PerformRayTest`/`PerformCylinderTest` from native `SMovementState.eyePosition/eyeDirection`; only predicted contact triggers `CWeapon::MeleeAttack` and haptics. Thus physical speed gates attack intent, but collision remains the game's eye-directed ray/cylinder test. Do not describe this as tracked swept weapon/hand collision.\n\nPHYSICAL INVENTORY / WORLD INTERACTION — `IsHandNearShoulder`, `IsHandNearHip`, `IsHandNearChest`, `IsHandBehindBack` classify controller position relative to HMD/yaw. `CPlayer::SelectNextItem` with no explicit category maps those zones to native `medium`, `small`, `explosive` inventory categories and calls the existing selector. `UpdateOffHandRayQuery` emits a controller world ray; in single-player `COffHand::CanPerformPickUp` substitutes controller ray/proximity for the flat eye query while retaining native object eligibility/range/breakable/inventory logic. While the off hand stabilizes a gun it cannot simultaneously grab an object. Multiplayer intentionally uses the original eye-based pickup path.\n\nOPENXR INPUT — `OpenXRInput` creates `ingame`, `menu`, `vehicles` sets and typed Boolean/float/pose/haptic actions. Suggested bindings are controller-profile-specific but use logical path placeholders `<weapon>`, `<!weapon>`, `<movement>`, `<!movement>` resolved from handedness cvars. It includes Oculus Touch, optional HP MR, Valve Index and optional Valve frame-controller profile support. `BooleanAction` supports short/long-press, release and press-on-release semantics. After `xrSyncActions`, state is translated to CryGame's existing `GameActions`/`IPlayerInput::OnAction`, preserving the game's semantic command vocabulary. Vehicle controls are a separate action context and remain stick-driven.\n\nHUD / MENUS / NANOSUIT — `CaptureHUD` captures the transparent native HUD into the shared D3D10→D3D11 texture, and OpenXR submits it as a quad. `VRManager::Update` chooses spatial policy: fixed/latching for menu/loading/console and flat states, HMD-following for normal HUD, off-hand for binoculars, vehicle-aim for vehicles/mounted guns. `CalcControllerHudIntersection` locates the weapon-hand grip at next-frame predicted time, rotates the pointer, transforms it into HUD-local coordinates and intersects the quad plane; ten samples are averaged before native window/ImGui mouse injection. Menu click actions are translated back to native input events when ImGui is not consuming them. When the nanosuit quick menu opens, `EnableHandMovementForQuickMenu` snapshots the suit-hand controller; subsequent displacement projected onto HMD-right/world-up becomes the existing HUD yaw/pitch action values, so the original quick-menu logic is retained.\n\nSCOPES / BINOCULARS / CINEMA — `VRRenderer` explicitly owns `RM_VR`, `RM_2D`, `RM_3D`. Incompatible optics/cutscene states are not hidden hacks inside normal stereo. Physical scope activation requires the two-hand grip plus weapon hand close to the HMD (enter <0.25, leave >0.35/release), and it triggers the ordinary Cry zoom action. `ModifyCameraFor2D` positions the flat zoom camera from the physical scope or weapon transform. `ModifyViewForBinoculars` anchors binocular camera to the off hand. `RM_3D` gives the native scripted camera an artificial eye separation and submits the results as eye-exclusive quads, while `RM_2D` uses a flat quad presentation. No separate PiP scope render is established.\n\nHAPTICS — OpenXR hand vibration uses `xrApplyHapticFeedback`. `VRHaptics` separately registers `.tact` assets for movement, damage, weapons, suit and vehicles; world hit position/direction is converted to bHaptics vest vertical/yaw offsets. ProTube gunstock output exposes kick/rumble/shot on butt/bolt channels and mirrors response when the support hand is engaged.\n\nHOOK LIFECYCLE / COMPATIBILITY — The newest DXGI swapchain is captured when created. `VRRenderer::Render` detects a changed swapchain or a Present hook that has not fired for >1 second, removes and reinstalls Present/Resize hooks and rebinds `VRManager`, making fullscreen/resolution/swapchain churn recoverable. However, `VR_CryRenderD3D10_Shadows` is installed at fixed `CryRenderD3D10.dll + 0x0bee30` with no signature validation; a generalized port should fail-closed on executable-byte validation. The source contains Win32/Win64 calling-convention branches. README notes 32-bit is often more stable, but SteamVR cannot supply a 32-bit OpenXR runtime.\n\nSOURCE-DEMONTSTRATED LIMITS — No full-body avatar IK, no OpenXR skeletal finger tracking, no physical magazine/reload sequence, no controller-velocity grenade throwing, no physical vehicle/stationary-gun steering/aiming, and no remote XR-pose replication are demonstrated. The melee gesture is not controller-swept contact. Reuse is also constrained by the CryENGINE 2 Mod SDK license.\n\nREUSABLE ARCHITECTURE — Legacy graphics bridge -> explicit OpenXR frame owner -> single coordinate/recenter authority -> native stereo replay with one-eye side-effect ownership -> tracked controller/animated-socket weapon transform -> post-animation arm IK -> native fire/melee/inventory/physics/action semantics -> captured HUD spatialization -> device-independent haptic fan-out. The mod is a particularly strong reference for adding modern OpenXR to an old source-accessible gameplay layer when the renderer itself must be crossed through compatibility hooks rather than rewritten.";


// Half-Life: VR source audit additions (2026-09-06)
CODE_AUDITS["seed_hlvr_case"] = "Source-audit (2026-09-06; supplied `src.zip`, `game.zip`, `docs.zip`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly noted otherwise.\n\nARCHITECTURE: Half-Life: VR is a heavily modified Half-Life SDK/GoldSrc client+game DLL port. The developer guide states that building produces `client.dll` and `hl.dll`. Client-side `cl_dll/VRHelper.*`, `VRRenderer.*`, `VROpenGLInterceptor.*`, `VRInput*`, `StudioModelRenderer.*`, `VRHUDRenderer.*` and `VRInputHLMenu.*` own SteamVR tracking, stereo OpenGL presentation, input and visual hand/UI work. Server/game-DLL `dlls/VRController.*`, `VRControllerInteractionManager.*`, `VRMovementHandler.*`, `VRRotatableEnt.*`, `VRControllerTeleporter.*` and modified Half-Life player/entity/weapon code own authoritative movement, collision, physical interaction and damage. This split is a strong reference for a legacy engine where client rendering/tracking and server-authoritative gameplay already exist as separate modules.\n\nOPENVR / FRAME TIMING: `VRHelper::Init` requires the hardware OpenGL renderer, installs OpenGL hooks, calls `VR_Init(... VRApplication_Scene)` and obtains `IVRCompositor`. It asks `GetRecommendedRenderTargetSize` and creates per-eye OpenGL textures/FBOs. `UpdatePositions` chooses standing/seated tracking and calls `WaitGetPoses`; the source does not expose an OpenXR-style `predictedDisplayTime`, so do not claim one. `GetHMDMatrixProjectionEye` uses SteamVR `GetProjectionMatrix`; `GetHMDMatrixPoseEye` inverts `GetEyeToHeadTransform`. `PrepareVRScene` binds the eye FBO, loads that eye projection/modelview and locks GL matrices. `InternalSubmitImage` sends the GL texture through `IVRCompositor::Submit`; after both eyes, `PostPresentHandoff` completes the handoff.\n\nSEQUENTIAL STEREO / DETERMINISM: `VRRenderer::CalcRefdef` exploits GoldSrc `ref_params_t::nextView`. nextView 0 polls/updates tracking and prepares the first eye; nextView 1 finishes that eye and prepares the second; nextView 2 finishes/submits and returns to ordinary client draw. The renderer backs up the custom random seed on the first view and restores it for the second; EasyHook replaces engine `RandomFloat`/`RandomLong` with this deterministic stream. This prevents randomized client effects from visibly disagreeing between sequential eyes.\n\nFIXED-FUNCTION GL OWNERSHIP: `VROpenGLInterceptor.cpp` hooks legacy GL matrix/viewport calls. While `HLVR_LockGLMatrices` is set, operations that would overwrite the VR viewport/projection/modelview (`glViewport`, `glLoadIdentity`, matrix loads, `glFrustum`, conflicting enables/disables) are suppressed, while allowed nested model transforms still work. This is a practical retrofit seam for a closed/legacy fixed-function renderer: assert eye matrices at a narrow boundary and temporarily prevent the old renderer from replacing them.\n\nCOORDINATE SPACES: `VRHelper` centralizes SteamVR→Half-Life axis/unit conversion. VR position is mapped into Half-Life coordinates (`x`, `-z`, `y`) with configurable scale; orientation basis vectors are remapped similarly. Client HMD/controller matrices are converted into offsets/angles before gameplay transport. Movement/attachment code can choose head, hand, weapon or pose references rather than assuming one global controller orientation.\n\nCLIENT→SERVER VR STATE: `VRHelper::SendPositionUpdateToServer` emits timestamped `vrupd_hmd` and `vrupdctrl` commands through GoldSrc `pfnClientCmd`. HMD messages carry horizontal/vertical playspace offset, forward and yaw/rebase information; controller messages carry validity, logical controller id, mirror/handedness, position, angles, linear velocity, drag and fire state. `dlls/client.cpp` parses them into `CBasePlayer::UpdateVRHeadset`/`UpdateVRController`. The server-side `VRController` rejects stale timestamps and stores current/previous pose plus interaction history. This is local client-to-game-server authority transport, not evidence of complete remote-player VR replication.\n\nROOMSCALE / COLLISION: `CBasePlayer::UpdateVRHeadset` converts HMD playspace displacement into a desired native player origin and calls `VRMovementHandler::DoMovement`. That helper snapshots the global `playermove_t`, movement cvars and hull state, constructs a short synthetic move toward the requested displacement with high speed/instant acceleration, calls GoldSrc server `PM_PlayerMove`, replays touched entities through native `Touch`, and restores the movement structure/cvars. When a wall blocks roomscale movement the client is notified, but the intended offset is still consumed into `vr_ClientOriginOffset`, so continued physical lean into a wall does not accumulate an ever-growing correction. Native `view_ofs.z` is updated from physical head height.\n\nCONTROLLERS / INTERACTION PRIMITIVE: each `VRController` is a server-side gameplay object containing tracked pose/velocity, logical WEAPON/HAND role, mirrored state, current weapon id, drag/fire state, touched/hit entities, model hitboxes and model attachments. `VRControllerInteractionManager` performs reachability/line blocking checks, model/BSP hitbox intersection and stateful touch/drag/hit transitions, then routes results to feature adapters. This central event router is why the same tracked controller can power melee, grabbing, buttons, rotating levers, ladders and NPC gestures without each subsystem inventing its own collision loop.\n\nWEAPONS / AIM: server controller/viewmodel attachments are the main spatial seam. `VRController::GetGunPosition` returns muzzle attachment 0 when present; `GetAim` uses attachment 0→1. `CBasePlayer::GetGunPosition` and `GetAutoaimVector` expose those values to the stock Half-Life weapon code, so Glock/MP5/shotgun/RPG/crossbow/Egon/Gauss/etc. inherit 6DOF muzzle and aim without replacing their ammo, spread, projectile/ray, damage or cadence logic. `AutoaimDeflection` returns zero in VR. `CBasePlayer::VRCanAttack` traces from player origin to the physical muzzle and the base weapon attack check requires that trace to be clear, preventing a controller/weapon held through level geometry from firing through the wall.\n\nMELEE: physical contact is handled in `VRControllerInteractionManager`, not merely by mapping a fast hand to the flat crowbar attack button. For a controller/model hit, `DoDamage` starts with controller linear velocity and adds the apparent velocity of the contacted point caused by controller angular change (`rotatedRelativeHitPos - previousRotatedRelativeHitPos`). Relative speed includes player velocity and subtracts target velocity. `UTIL_CalculateMeleeDamage(weaponId,speed)` decides damage; a synthetic `TraceResult` carries impact point/hitgroup into native `TraceAttack`/`ApplyMultiDamage`, preserving monster/player hit reactions and damage types. World geometry uses separate hit testing to keep texture sounds/decals. This is contact/hitbox-based physical melee with a rotational velocity term, not a continuously swept blade-volume solver.\n\nGRABBING / THROWING: `CBaseEntity` gains a small VR drag contract: `IsDraggable`, `HandleDragStart`, `HandleDragUpdate`, `HandleDragStop`, `DragThink`. The default update gets controller gun position, velocity and angles and writes them to entity state. Specialized grenade, satchel/snark, gib, soda, headcrab and other classes alter movetype/solid/behavior while held. On release ordinary physics resumes with the controller velocity already stored in `pev->velocity`, yielding physical throws while native fuse/explosion/entity logic remains authoritative. Dragged objects can themselves interact with buttons/entities.\n\nBUTTONS / LEVERS: `HandleButtonsAndDoors` converts controller touch/use/drag into the existing `func_door`, `func_button`, `func_rot_button`, `momentary_rot_button` `Use`/`Touch` semantics. `VRRotatableEnt` handles physical levers/valves: it snapshots the hand relative to the entity, chooses the proper rotation plane from spawnflags, calculates signed controller arc using atan2, converts it through the native toggle start/end range (`AxisDelta`), clamps the fraction and calls the entity `SetVRDragRotation`. Large discontinuities reseat the reference.\n\nLADDERS / CLIMBING: immersive ladder mode treats a ladder as the controller dragged entity. While gripping, target player origin is current origin + previous hand offset - current hand offset, so pulling the hand downward moves the body upward. `VRMovementHandler::DoMovement(... allowZ=true)` collision-solves that vertical body motion while normal ladder code is temporarily ignored. Releasing the last hand can apply `-controller velocity` to the player for swing/push-off momentum. `DoMultiControllerActions` separately recognizes both hands pulling down on compatible ledges; `CalculateLedgeTargetPosition` searches for a free duck-hull landing with `PM_TestPlayerPosition`, then snaps or interpolates the player onto the ledge.\n\nTELEPORT: `VRControllerTeleporter` builds a gravity-aware parabolic beam and validates floor slope, standing/duck fit, ladders, Xen jump elements and native trigger teleports. On commit it can touch every relevant SOLID_TRIGGER crossed by the path (`TouchTriggersInTeleportPath`), so changelevel and scripted triggers are not skipped by non-continuous movement. `VRJustTeleported` clears ladder/ledge state and translates grabbed objects with the player. The teleport emits an AI sound based on distance so it cannot become silent gameplay movement.\n\nINPUT: `VRInput` loads SteamVR action manifests/bindings and organizes actions into move/interact/weapon/menu/custom sets. Default bindings cover Vive, Index, Oculus Touch and WMR. Action handlers terminate in the existing GoldSrc semantic controls (`+attack`, `+use`, `+jump`, `+duck`, reload/weapon commands, custom console commands). Logical HAND/WEAPON roles support left-handed mode without forcing every gameplay consumer to know the physical device side.\n\nHANDS / FINGERS: this is not body IK. The client renders controller/weapon/hand studio models. `VRInputAction` reads OpenVR skeletal summary data and can fetch full bone data, but `StudioDrawVRHand` explicitly places the full skeleton model branch under `#if 0` with `TODO: Skeleton hand models not properly working yet`. The active path reads per-finger curl and interpolates selected hand model bones between flat/grab matrices; analog trigger/hand curl provides fallback values. Tag this as tracked hands/finger-curl retargeting, not full skeletal hand tracking or arm/body IK.\n\nHUD / MENU: `VRHUDRenderer` intercepts the native `SPR_Set` and `SPR_DrawAdditive` HUD sprite API. It calls the existing `gHUD.Redraw`, then reconstructs ammo, health, battery, flashlight, damage and train-control sprite regions in 3D at controller- or view-relative origins. The custom menu is an OpenVR overlay with a Nuklear texture; it can be attached relative to HMD/left/right controller. `VRInputHLMenu` explicitly computes a controller-ray/overlay intersection via `ComputeOverlayIntersection` and maps resulting UV to menu coordinates because the old automatic interactive-overlay behavior is no longer sufficient.\n\nNPC / BODY GESTURES: `HandleAlliedMonsters` implements spatial semantics such as gunpoint recognition using physical muzzle distance/orientation/LOS, shoulder grabs to toggle followers, and an open-hand stop gesture held in a body-relative region. `UpdateVRController` also recognizes a bare-hand grip near the player eye/head to toggle the native flashlight. These are good examples of body-space gesture classification followed by existing AI/game actions.\n\nHAPTICS BOUNDARY: server controllers accumulate LIGHT/NORMAL/HARD touch intensity and send `VRTouch` messages to the client; the client aggregates touch, recoil, damage, water/train/shake feedback categories. However, in this supplied source the final `TriggerHapticVibrationAction` calls inside `VRInput::FireFeedback`/`FireDamageFeedback` are commented out. Older release notes say haptics were implemented, but current source wins: document the feedback pipeline, not active controller vibration.\n\nMULTIPLAYER / LIMITS: source comments explicitly warn that `vr_controllermodel` interaction would need different handling for multiplayer crowbar combat. `VRNetworkManager` is optional/test Steam networking behind `ENABLESTEAMSTUFF`; no complete remote VR pose/body reconstruction is demonstrated. No full-body/arm IK, no active full skeletal-hand renderer, no manual magazine reload system and no OpenXR runtime are present in this snapshot.\n\nREUSABLE ARCHITECTURE: OpenVR client pose/render owner -> deterministic GoldSrc eye replay + GL matrix lock -> timestamped pose command bridge -> server tracked-controller primitives -> native movement/collision reuse -> shared native weapon muzzle accessors -> physical interaction router -> existing entity/weapon/damage/AI semantics -> spatialized native HUD/menu.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_nextview_stereo_rng"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRRenderer.cpp`, `VRRandom.*`, engine interceptor). `VRRenderer::CalcRefdef` treats GoldSrc `ref_params_t::nextView` as an eye-stage state machine and calls `VRRandomBackupSeed` on the first view / `VRRandomRestoreSeed` on later views. Engine random calls are intercepted to use that seed. Reusable rule: sequential stereo must freeze not only simulation time but any nondeterministic render-side state that can visually differ between eyes.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_fixed_function_gl_lock"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VROpenGLInterceptor.cpp`, `VRHelper.cpp`). Eye setup loads VR projection/modelview and enables `HLVR_LockGLMatrices`; hooked fixed-function GL calls that would replace viewport/projection/modelview are suppressed until eye rendering ends. Use this when a legacy renderer has no high-level camera API but funnels projection through a small set of fixed-function calls.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_openvr_eye_fbo_submit"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRHelper.cpp`). `VR_Init`, `IVRCompositor`, `GetRecommendedRenderTargetSize`, separate GL FBO/texture eye targets, `GetProjectionMatrix`, inverse eye-to-head transforms, `Submit`, `glFlush` and `PostPresentHandoff` form the compositor path. `WaitGetPoses` is the tracking/frame synchronization primitive; no explicit predicted-display timestamp is exposed in this source.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_client_server_pose_commands"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRHelper.cpp`, `dlls/client.cpp`, `player.cpp`). `vrupd_hmd`/`vrupdctrl` strings carry timestamps, HMD/hand transforms, velocity and semantic state through `pfnClientCmd`; receiving code reconstructs authoritative per-player VR state and rejects stale data. This is an economical way to cross an existing client/game-DLL boundary without modifying GoldSrc packet structs.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_transactional_pmove_roomscale"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRMovementHandler.cpp`, `player.cpp`). `DoMovement` snapshots global `playermove_t`, hull/movement state and cvars, injects a synthetic short movement command, runs server `PM_PlayerMove`, replays `Touch` on contacted entities and restores everything. `UpdateVRHeadset` consumes intended playspace displacement even if the move is blocked. This converts native movement into a VR collision service rather than maintaining duplicate collision code.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_server_controller_primitive"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRController.*`, `VRControllerInteractionManager.*`, `VRPhysicsHelper.*`). A controller carries current/previous pose, velocity, weapon/logical-hand identity, attachments, drag/fire state and touch/hit sets. The interaction manager performs model/BSP intersection plus reachability checks and dispatches state transitions. Reusable rule: centralize tracked-hand collision/history once, then layer entity-specific semantics over it.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_native_muzzle_attachment_authority"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRController.cpp`, `player.cpp`, `weapons.cpp`, stock weapon files). Muzzle attachment 0 supplies origin and attachment 0→1 supplies aim through `CBasePlayer::GetGunPosition`/`GetAutoaimVector`, which existing weapon implementations already call. `VRCanAttack` traces body→muzzle and is included in base weapon attack eligibility. This preserves native weapon authority while solving both 6DOF aim and through-wall muzzle abuse at a shared accessor.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_physical_melee_velocity_contact"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRControllerInteractionManager.cpp`, `crowbar.cpp`, `VRPhysicsHelper`). Contact comes from controller/viewmodel hitboxes. `DoDamage` augments linear controller velocity with angular contact-point displacement between previous/current controller orientation, computes relative speed to the target, maps speed/weapon through `UTIL_CalculateMeleeDamage`, then calls native `TraceAttack`/`ApplyMultiDamage` with a synthetic hit result. This is physical contact + native damage, not button-emulated melee.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_draggable_native_throw"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/cbase.h`, `player.cpp`, `VRControllerInteractionManager.cpp`, `ggrenade.cpp`, `effects.cpp`, `monsters.h`). The VR drag virtuals make native entities opt in. `DragThink` forwards controller origin/velocity/angles; default `HandleDragUpdate` writes entity state; specialized classes change solidity/movetype while held. Release therefore resumes native physics with controller velocity already present, covering grenades, gibs, soda and other objects.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_native_button_door_adapter"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRControllerInteractionManager.cpp`). `HandleButtonsAndDoors` recognizes native door/button classes and forwards tracked interaction into their existing `Use`/`Touch` behavior, preserving spawn-flag and continuous-use semantics. Reachability/blocked-line checks prevent controllers from activating geometry through walls.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_rotatable_lever_drag"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRRotatableEnt.*`, rotating button/toggle classes). The solver measures signed controller angle in the entity configured rotation plane, maps it through the existing toggle axis/range and calls `SetVRDragRotation` with normalized progress. The hand reference is reseated on discontinuity, preventing a physical lever from jumping because of angular wrap.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_hand_over_hand_ladder"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRControllerInteractionManager.cpp`, `VRMovementHandler.cpp`). Gripping `func_ladder` stores it as the dragged entity. Body target is displaced by the inverse of hand movement and collision-solved with `DoMovement(... allowZ=true)` while ordinary ladder detection is suppressed. Optional release momentum uses `-controller.GetVelocity`. This preserves the intuitive climbing invariant: the world hand stays planted while the body moves.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_two_hand_ledge_pull"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRControllerInteractionManager.cpp`). Both controllers must be dragging, moving down fast enough, approximately coplanar and located on valid ledges. `CalculateLedgeTargetPosition` probes free positions with the GoldSrc duck hull; accepted pulls duck the player if needed and either snap or animate origin toward the safe target. Reusable rule: physical climbing needs a body-clearance solver, not just hand gestures.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_teleport_trigger_replay"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRControllerTeleporter.*`, `player.cpp`, `VRController.cpp`). Teleport uses a segmented gravity-aware parabola and validates floor/hull/special trigger cases. `TouchTriggersInTeleportPath` explicitly invokes crossed SOLID_TRIGGER entities; `VRJustTeleported` resets ladder/ledge state and translates dragged entities. AI noise is emitted after teleport. This preserves world scripting and carried-object state across discontinuous locomotion.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_openvr_semantic_action_sets"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRInput.*`, `VRInputAction.*`, `game/actions/*`). SteamVR action sets divide move/interact/weapon/menu/custom input and suggested bindings cover Vive/Index/Touch/WMR. Hand-agnostic actions are resolved through logical roles and handlers issue existing GoldSrc console/button commands. Runtime-specific input therefore ends at a semantic compatibility boundary.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_finger_curl_retarget"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRInputAction.cpp`, `VRInput.cpp`, `StudioModelRenderer.cpp`). OpenVR skeletal summary supplies `fingerCurl`; active studio rendering interpolates corresponding finger bones between authored flat/grab matrices. Although full skeletal bone data is fetched, the alternative full-skeleton hand path is explicitly `#if 0` with a TODO. Do not call this full skeletal-hand tracking.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_spatial_hud_sprite_intercept"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRHUDRenderer.cpp`). The mod wraps `SPR_Set`/`SPR_DrawAdditive` and calls normal `gHUD.Redraw`; intercepted health/ammo/battery/flashlight/damage/train sprites are reconstructed with VR-selected 3D origins/orientations. This lets old HUD business logic continue unchanged while only the final drawing primitive is spatialized.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_openvr_overlay_menu_ray"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`cl_dll/VRInputHLMenu.cpp`). A Nuklear-backed menu texture is uploaded to an OpenVR overlay, optionally attached to a tracked device. `ComputeOverlayIntersection` raycasts the tracked controller against it and returns UV coordinates for menu input. This is a compositor-level UI adapter that avoids modifying GoldSrc world rendering for menu presentation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_npc_spatial_gestures"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/VRControllerInteractionManager.cpp`). Allied NPC semantics include physical gunpoint tests using muzzle distance/facing/LOS, bare-hand shoulder grabs to change follow state and a timed open-hand stop gesture in a body-relative zone. The gesture classifier produces existing NPC state/action changes rather than a parallel VR AI system.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_hlvr_head_flashlight_gesture"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`dlls/player.cpp::UpdateVRController`). A new grip on the bare logical HAND, when not dragging and near the configured eye/head region, toggles the ordinary Half-Life flashlight. Reusable pattern: detect body-relative hand intent in tracking/game space, then call one native semantic action and immediately return to ordinary game authority.";

// RTCWQuest 1.3.0 full-source audit (supplied source ZIP, 2026-09-06)
CODE_AUDITS["seed_rtcwquest_case"] = "FULL SOURCE AUDIT (2026-09-06; supplied `RTCWQuest-1.3.0.zip`). VERIFIED FROM SOURCE unless explicitly noted. VERSION/RUNTIME: this source snapshot is RTCWQuest 1.3.0 for Meta Quest using Oculus Mobile SDK/VrApi, not OpenXR. README/build instructions require Oculus Mobile SDK 1.30.0 and credit gl4es for the legacy OpenGL→OpenGL ES translation.\n\nARCHITECTURE: VR-specific runtime/input/compositor code lives in `Projects/Android/jni/RTCWVR/`; the modified RTCW/id Tech 3 engine is under `rtcw/src/client`, `cgame`, `game`, `renderer`, etc. The port changes renderer, client input, cgame presentation, server/game weapons and props rather than hiding VR behind a camera-only wrapper.\n\nFRAME TIMING: `RTCWVR_incrementFrameIndex` is the sole frame-index increment site and immediately calculates `vrapi_GetPredictedDisplayTime`. HMD pose is sampled via `vrapi_GetPredictedTracking2(Ovr,DisplayTime)` and both tracked remotes via `vrapi_GetInputTrackingState(...,displayTime)`. `SCR_UpdateScreen` samples head/controllers once, renders left and right, finishes both eye buffers and submits through `vrapi_SubmitFrame2` using the same frame index/display time.\n\nSTEREO/PROJECTION: gameplay is sequential stereo: `SCR_DrawScreenField(STEREO_LEFT/RIGHT)` replays native cgame rendering; `CG_DrawActive` separates eyes by ±`cg_worldScale*cg_stereoSeparation/2`. VrApi projection submission uses separate eye color swapchains but the same symmetric `ovrMatrix4f_CreateProjectionFov(vr.fov,vr.fov,0,0,...)`; do not claim asymmetric per-eye projection. Selected right-eye once-per-frame updates are skipped, but source does not establish a comprehensive simulation/RNG freeze.\n\nCAMERA: `tr_scene.c` reconciles game/player yaw with physical HMD yaw. Screen, scope/resync and normal gameplay take different paths; ordinary gameplay uses HMD pitch/roll and updates internal yaw from HMD delta only once per stereo pair. Scope and mounted-state transitions call yaw resync.\n\nSCREEN/UI: `RTCWVR_useScreenLayer` detects UI/console/cinematics/loading/demos/camera mode. Those states submit `BuildCylinderLayer` rather than gameplay projection. Controller UI pointer is angular-delta based (`interactWithTouchScreen`→`PortableMouseAbs`), not a ray-plane test. Gameplay HUD remains stereo-rendered and `CG_AdjustFrom640` applies VR scale/centering/eye offsets around legacy 640×480 coordinates.\n\nSHARED VR STATE: `VrClientInfo.h` defines central `vr_client_info_t` holding HMD/controller, weapon history, recoil, scope/backpack/mounted/teleport/calibration state. `CG_SET_VR_CLIENT_INFO` and `GAME_SET_VR_CLIENT_INFO` pass the same native pointer into cgame and game VMs. This is an in-process VM ABI extension, not VR pose networking.\n\nSPACES: `convertFromVR` reorders VrApi axes, rotates horizontal controller space by `view/player yaw - HMD yaw`, applies sign convention, multiplies by world scale and adds current camera/player origin. Controller offsets are stored relative to HMD and reused by weapons, props and teleport.\n\nINPUT/LOCOMOTION: VrApi remote state is centralized in `HandleInput_Default`, with handedness and optional stick/button switching, then emitted as existing RTCW commands. Joystick plus HMD positional movement feed standard `usercmd_t` forward/right fields; native pmove remains collision/grounding authority. `vr_walkdirection` selects offhand- or HMD-relative locomotion. Snap/smooth turn share `snapTurn` and are disabled during scoped/mounted modes. HMD height affects view height but does not switch native stance; crouch is button-driven.\n\nWEAPONS: `CG_CalculateVRWeaponPosition` applies controller offset, HMD height and tracked angles. `CG_CalculateWeaponPositionAndScale` reads per-weapon `vr_weapon_adjustment_<id>` calibration; `assets/weapons_vr.cfg` contains arsenal values and `VrInputWeaponAlign.c` provides live in-headset adjustment. `CalcMuzzlePoints` on the game side substitutes controller-derived local-player direction/origin while native fire logic keeps ammo, spread, cadence, projectiles/rays, damage and effects. Pistol recoil is stored in `gVR->weapon_recoil`, added to weapon pitch and decayed rather than kicking the HMD.\n\nTWO-HAND/SCOPE: support grip + hand proximity enables `weapon_stabilised`; normal mode aims along the inter-hand vector, virtual-stock/scope mode uses an HMD/eye-adjacent anchor to offhand. `FireWeapon` lowers native `aimSpreadScale`. Physical scope proximity + stabilization invokes native `weapalt`; while scope is engaged weapon orientation becomes view authority, movement is reduced and artificial turn disabled. Binoculars come from backpack and use native zoom commands near HMD.\n\nAKIMBO: `WP_AKIMBO` sets `dualwield`; `BG_AkimboFireSequence` determines whether dominant or offhand controller is used for current presentation/muzzle, preserving native alternating pistol state.\n\nTHROWING: input records timestamped controller offsets relative to HMD. `weapon_grenadelauncher_fire_vr` compares older/newer history samples, computes displacement/time, converts direction into game space and scales launch power before calling native `fire_grenade`. This is genuine controller-velocity throwing while native fuse/missile/splash/damage remain authoritative.\n\nMELEE: knife controller speed over `VELOCITY_TRIGGER` toggles native attack intent; `Weapon_Knife` uses tracked knife orientation and a short native trace/damage path. It is gesture-triggered, pose-directed melee, not swept physical blade collision.\n\nBACKPACK/PROPS: backpack activation requires tracked hand distance, height and orientation tests behind the HMD, then temporary native weapon switching exposes explosives, knife, binoculars plus offhand quicksave/load. `CG_Prop` moves native carried-prop presentation to tracked offhand while keeping original carry state.\n\nTELEPORT: offhand straight-ray targeting validates HMD→controller line of sight and a mostly upward surface normal, then native `TeleportPlayer` commits. No parabolic arc, hull/AAS route validation or trigger replay; README says it can cheat.\n\nMOUNTED GUN: MG42 deliberately remains gaze/native-camera aimed; weapon presentation is suppressed and turning is resynced/disabled. This is a state-specific fallback, not controller aiming.\n\nHAPTICS: `RTCWVR_processHaptics` actively calls `vrapi_SetHapticVibrationSimple` from duration/intensity channels. Game/cgame feedback uses `trap_Vibrate`; semantic descriptions are also translated into JNI haptic event/stop/update calls. Stabilized weapons can vibrate both hands. External Java actuator details are outside the native source, so do not invent hardware targets.\n\nLIMITS: no body/arm IK, no skeletal finger tracking, no manual magazine reload, no physical crouch-to-stance, no swept knife collision, no controller-aimed MG42, no remote tracked-pose replication, symmetric projection only, and incomplete global second-eye side-effect suppression.\n\nREUSABLE FLOW: VrApi predicted-time owner → native sequential stereo + camera-yaw reconciliation → shared `vr_client_info_t` VM state → centralized tracking/world conversion → data-driven tracked weapons/two-hand/scope/throw/backpack adapters → native RTCW combat/pmove/UI/prop authority → projection/cylinder presentation → semantic haptic outputs.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_vrapi_predicted_frame"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `RTCWVR_incrementFrameIndex` is the only frame-index owner and calls `vrapi_GetPredictedDisplayTime`. `RTCWVR_getHMDOrientation` uses `vrapi_GetPredictedTracking2`; `acquireTrackedRemotesData` uses `vrapi_GetInputTrackingState` for each controller with the same display time. `RTCWVR_submitFrame` submits with that frame index/display time before advancing the frame. GENERALIZATION: Sample HMD and both tracked remotes against one VrApi predicted display time, render both eyes, then submit with that same frame index/display time.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_idtech3_sequential_stereo"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `SCR_UpdateScreen` calls `SCR_DrawScreenField(STEREO_LEFT)` and `STEREO_RIGHT`; renderer draw-buffer commands route each into a VrApi framebuffer. `CG_DrawActive` offsets `cg.refdef.vieworg` by ±`cg_worldScale * cg_stereoSeparation/2`. Selected once-per-frame work is skipped on the right eye, but source does not prove a comprehensive simulation freeze. GENERALIZATION: Reuse the engine native stereo enum and cgame renderer to render left/right views while preserving the ordinary RTCW scene pipeline.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_game_hmd_yaw_reconciliation"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `tr_scene.c` has separate screen-layer, scope/resync and normal gameplay camera paths. Normal gameplay uses tracked HMD pitch/roll and adjusts an internal yaw estimate from `hmdorientation_delta` only on one eye of the pair; resync paths rebase against known game yaw. This prevents artificial/native facing and physical HMD yaw from being applied twice. GENERALIZATION: Maintain local head freedom while keeping legacy game/server yaw authoritative by updating render-space yaw only once per stereo pair.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_vrapi_cylinder_screen_layer"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `RTCWVR_useScreenLayer` activates for UI catcher, console, cinematic/loading, demos and camera mode. `RTCWVR_submitFrame` then submits `BuildCylinderLayer(...)` from `VrCompositor.c` instead of the gameplay projection layer. GENERALIZATION: Switch flat legacy presentation out of world stereo and place it on a compositor cylinder anchored to player yaw.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_shared_vr_state_vm_abi"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `vr_client_info_t` contains HMD/controller state, weapon history, scope/backpack/teleport/mounted state and calibration values. Native client code passes `&vr` through `CG_SET_VR_CLIENT_INFO`; server frame passes it through `GAME_SET_VR_CLIENT_INFO`; cgame/game store `cgVR`/`gVR`. This is local in-process ABI sharing, not remote-pose networking. GENERALIZATION: Inject one central tracked-state object into both client-game and server-game VM modules instead of serializing every local VR field through legacy commands.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_tracking_space_conversion"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `convertFromVR` reorders VrApi coordinates, rotates the horizontal plane by `game/view yaw - HMD yaw`, applies legacy sign convention, multiplies by `cg_worldScale` and adds the relevant view/player origin. Equivalent conversion is used by game weapon code. GENERALIZATION: Keep controller offsets relative to the HMD and convert them into engine world space through one axis/yaw/world-scale boundary.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_weapon_model_calibration"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CG_CalculateWeaponPositionAndScale` reads `vr_weapon_adjustment_<weapon>` as scale/right/up/forward/pitch/yaw/roll. `assets/weapons_vr.cfg` supplies calibrated values across the arsenal. `VrInputWeaponAlign.c` control scheme 99 exposes live scale/offset/angle tuning in headset. GENERALIZATION: Calibrate legacy first-person weapon assets independently instead of expecting one controller-to-model transform to fit the arsenal.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_two_hand_virtual_stock"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VrInputDefault` enables `weapon_stabilised` for non-pistols when support grip is held and hands are near. Normal mode points dominant→support hand; scope/classic virtual stock aims from an HMD/eye-adjacent anchor toward the support hand. `FireWeapon` reduces native `aimSpreadScale` while stabilized. GENERALIZATION: Use support-hand proximity/grip to change weapon orientation and feed the flat game existing accuracy mechanics rather than inventing VR-only balance.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_physical_scope_camera_authority"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `scopeready` requires stabilization and dominant-controller proximity to HMD. Scoped weapons invoke native `weapalt`; `updateScopeAngles` makes weapon orientation become effective HMD/view orientation. Movement drops to 30% and thumbstick turn is disabled while scoped. GENERALIZATION: Enter existing sniper mode only when a stabilized physical weapon is brought to the eye, then temporarily let weapon orientation drive the viewing direction.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_tracked_muzzle_native_fire"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `game/g_weapon.c::CalcMuzzlePoints` uses `gVR->weaponangles`, player-yaw/HMD-yaw rebasing and controller offset for the local player. Existing `Bullet_Fire`/projectile paths retain spread, ammo, cadence, effects and damage. Pistol recoil writes `gVR->weapon_recoil`, which decays into weapon pitch rather than HMD pitch. GENERALIZATION: Replace the shared muzzle/view-direction seam for the local player and leave weapon-specific fire logic authoritative.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_controller_history_throwing"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VrInputDefault` maintains timestamped controller offsets relative to HMD. `weapon_grenadelauncher_fire_vr` compares older history samples, computes displacement/time, transforms direction to game space, scales by explosive type and calls native `fire_grenade`, preserving fuse, missile physics, splash and damage. GENERALIZATION: Derive throw direction and speed from timestamped controller history, then hand the result to the native explosive projectile system.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_velocity_native_knife"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. Dominant-controller linear speed above `VELOCITY_TRIGGER` toggles native `+attack`. `Weapon_Knife` uses `gVR->weaponangles_knife`, yaw rebasing and a 48-unit native trace with ordinary hit/miss events and damage. This is gesture-driven pose-directed melee, not swept controller collision. GENERALIZATION: Use controller velocity to establish knife attack intent, but use tracked knife orientation and the original RTCW trace/damage path for contact.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_head_relative_backpack"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `VrInputDefault` combines tracking validity, HMD↔hand distance, relative height, controller-forward angle to down and a horizontal HMD/controller direction test. In-zone dominant holds temporarily select grenades/dynamite, knife or binoculars and restore the prior weapon on release; offhand buttons provide quicksave/load. GENERALIZATION: Require a hand to move behind the shoulder/head in a plausible orientation before exposing quick-access inventory actions.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_vrapi_native_input_bridge"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `acquireTrackedRemotesData` enumerates tracked remotes; `HandleInput_Default` interprets handedness, buttons, triggers and sticks and emits existing commands such as `+attack`, `+activate`, `+movedown`, reload/alt-fire, weapon cycling, kick and notebook. Optional stick/button switching remains entirely in the adapter. GENERALIZATION: Terminate runtime-specific controller parsing at RTCW existing command vocabulary so gameplay state machines remain unchanged.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_native_usercmd_roomscale"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. The VR layer tracks positional forward/side movement separately from joystick movement. `RTCWVR_GetMove` exposes both; client input adds `new_move.pos_forward/pos_side * 127` into ordinary `usercmd_t` movement. `vr_walkdirection` chooses offhand-relative or HMD-relative joystick locomotion. HMD height adjusts camera height but does not choose crouch/prone state. GENERALIZATION: Convert physical HMD translation into the same forward/right command channels as thumbstick locomotion so native id Tech movement/collision stays authoritative.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_controller_ray_teleport_guard"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CG_Teleport` traces 256 units from the offhand, separately traces HMD→controller to reject a hand poked through solid geometry, requires a predominantly upward surface normal and stores `teleportdest`; release commits through native `TeleportPlayer`. There is no parabola, hull/AAS route validation or trigger replay, and the README warns it can cheat. GENERALIZATION: A lightweight teleport can at least validate hand reachability and floor orientation before invoking existing teleport state.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_dual_controller_akimbo"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `cgVR->dualwield` is set for `WP_AKIMBO`. `CG_CalculateVRWeaponPosition` chooses dominant or offhand according to `BG_AkimboFireSequence`; server muzzle logic likewise uses the native akimbo sequence. Input has handedness special-casing so dual-controller roles stay stable. GENERALIZATION: Preserve the flat game alternating dual-pistol fire state while selecting the corresponding physical controller for current presentation/fire.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_offhand_native_prop_presentation"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `cgame/cg_ents.c::CG_Prop` converts `cgVR->offhandoffset/offhandangles` into local world/view presentation for first-person carried props, while ordinary RTCW activate/carry behavior remains authoritative. GENERALIZATION: Move visually held world props to the real support hand without replacing their native entity/carry state machine.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_legacy_hud_screen_pointer"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `CG_AdjustFrom640` contains VR gameplay HUD scaling/centering and stereo-eye offsets. In screen-layer mode, `interactWithTouchScreen` integrates dominant-controller yaw/pitch delta into a clamped normalized cursor and calls `PortableMouseAbs`, ultimately mapping to the native 640×480 UI; trigger/buttons become mouse/escape keys. GENERALIZATION: Adapt a 640×480 id Tech UI with minimal surgery: rescale/recenter HUD per eye and convert controller angular deltas into the existing mouse coordinate path for screen layers.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rtcw_semantic_haptic_bus"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE. `trap_Vibrate` reaches `RTCWVR_Haptic`; per-hand channels store intensity/duration and `RTCWVR_processHaptics` actively calls `vrapi_SetHapticVibrationSimple`. Semantic descriptions for weapon fire, continuous weapons, knife hits, doors and camera/rumble effects are translated into `RTCWVR_HapticEvent`/stop/update JNI calls; stabilized fire can drive both hands. GENERALIZATION: Raise named gameplay feedback once, then fan it into controller vibration and an external JNI event channel.";


// Vivecraft 1.3.15 full-source audit additions (2026-09-06)
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_openvr_dual_graphics_submission"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenVRStereoRenderer.java`, `MCOpenVR.java`). Runtime eye size, hidden-area mesh and `GetProjectionRaw` feed separate eye targets. OpenGL `Texture_t` stores texture IDs; Vulkan populates `VRVulkanTextureData` with image/device/physical-device/instance/queue/queue-family/format/sample metadata after compositor extension checks. `VRCompositor_Submit` + `PostPresentHandoff` are the final submission seam. Transfer: renderer API and compositor API can remain decoupled if the backend exports a runtime-consumable eye-resource descriptor.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_explicit_render_pass_graph"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRPassHelper.java`, `VRRenderer.java`, `RenderPassManager`). Requested pass list contains stereo eyes plus optional CENTER, THIRD, SCOPEL/SCOPER and CAMERA. Each pass selects a `WorldRenderPass`, runs the ordinary renderer into its target and ends frame buffers before eye post-processing/final compositor submit. Transfer: model scopes/mixed reality/cameras as render-pass identities and targets, not flags hidden inside one eye renderer.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_pose_snapshot_space_contract"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRData.java`, `VRPlayer.java`, `VRClientAPI.java`). Pose state is explicitly split into latest room pre-poll/tick, world pre-tick, room/world post-tick and render-interpolated world snapshots; conversion is centralized by room↔world helpers. Transfer: give gameplay/network/render code typed temporal/space snapshots instead of unrestricted latest-pose globals.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_native_collision_roomscale"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRPlayer.doPlayerMoveInRoom`). Desired physical X/Z creates a candidate native player AABB; `level.noCollision` accepts direct movement and optional 0.1-step vertical search. Accepted step-up also adjusts room origin. FBT feet near floor may define physical body pivot. Transfer: reuse the target game collision hull for roomscale body reconciliation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_multisource_freemove_orientation"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRPlayer.freemoveDirection`, movement trackers/settings). HMD, controller, room, run-in-place and waist-relative directions are selectable; waist is used with FBT, otherwise body yaw is estimated. Swimming/flying can include pitch and treadmills have adapters. Transfer: track locomotion reference as policy, then feed one movement vector into native physics.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_fbt_role_calibration"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`MCVR.java`). Semantic indices exist for waist/feet/knees/elbows. `calibrateFBT` supports assigned and unassigned trackers; unassigned devices are matched to expected scaled body reference points after HMD centering/yaw removal, then positional/rotation offsets are recorded. Transfer: calibrate arbitrary tracker hardware once into semantic body slots before avatar code consumes it.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_fullbody_connected_limb_solver"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRData.java`, `VRPlayerModel.java`, `VRPlayerModel_WithArms.java`, `VRPlayerModel_WithArmsLegs.java`, `ModelUtils.java`). FBT modes select hands-only, waist+feet or extended joint tracking. Torso can align to waist; endpoints are tracked; elbow/knee is measured or estimated; connected/split limb functions solve segment positions/rotations and optional stretch limits/animation offsets. This is genuine body/limb solving.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_remote_fbt_pose_replication"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VrPlayerState.java`, `ClientNetworking.java`, `ServerNetworking.java`, `ClientVRPlayers.java`). Packet includes HMD/hands plus FBT-mode-gated waist/feet/knee/elbow poses. Protocol compatibility strips unsupported fields. Server rebroadcasts states; client interpolates positions/quaternions and uses the minimum compatible FBT mode across samples. Transfer: version semantic tracker sets and never interpolate a joint that one sample does not contain.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_active_bodypart_spatial_network_abi"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`ClientNetworking`, `ServerNetworking`, `ServerVivePlayer`). Client sends active `VRBodyPart` and optional short-lived aim direction/position overrides. Server resolves aim from override, roomscale bow two-hand geometry or active body part, with seated/availability fallbacks. Transfer: network semantic acting-part/aim intent rather than runtime controller handles.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_tracked_projectile_native_authority"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`ItemMixin`, `ProjectileMixin`, `CrossbowItemMixin`, throwable/arrow/fishing/trident projectile mixins, `ServerVivePlayer`). Mixins replace shared vanilla eye/view/launch origin/direction with tracked aim; projectile class still owns speed/inaccuracy/damage behavior except explicit bow draw scaling. Transfer: patch common spatial accessors once so broad vanilla/mod projectile ecosystems inherit VR aim.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_interaction_module_arbitration"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`InteractTracker.java`, `InteractModule`, `HeldInteractModule`, gameplay interaction modules). Modules are priority sorted; each hand is tested until a valid module claims it; only that hand receives enabled VR-interact state; press/hold/release and haptics are centralized. Transfer: separate context recognition from action invocation and make physical validity gate the semantic button.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_room_speed_world_sweep_melee"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`SwingTracker.java`). Physical speed comes from controller-tip history in room/pre space; contact comes from world current/previous points and swept AABBs. HMD→hand block clipping prevents around-corner attacks. Entity hits call `gameMode.attack`; block logic reuses native destroy/use/tool paths. This is true physical contact-style melee/mining.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_physical_bow_native_release"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`BowTracker.java`, `BowModule.java`, networking/projectile mixins). Arrow must be near the virtual string and aligned; controller separation becomes normalized draw; aim is arrow-hand→bow-hand. Press enters native use, release sends draw percent/body part then calls native `releaseUsingItem`; server projectile velocity consumes draw. Transfer: physical geometry can parameterize a native charge/release weapon instead of replacing it.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_hand_over_hand_climbey"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`ClimbTracker.java`, climbing packets). Gripped hand latches world/room position; body moves opposite hand delta and candidate player AABBs are collision tested with fallbacks. Top-out checks clearance. Release derives jump from controller movement/speed and synchronizes room origin/server fall-exhaustion state. Transfer: climbing is hand anchoring + body collision solve + release momentum.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_ballistic_teleport_validation"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`TeleportTracker.java`). Controller-directed velocity/gravity generate a segmented ballistic arc; segment clip selects collision; destination validator uses actual block collision shapes and player AABB/noCollision, supports fluid/climbable cases and server-configured horizontal/up/down limits/energy. No arbitrary trigger replay is present. Transfer: validate full body placement and server locomotion policy, not only a ray hit.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_spatial_gui_native_mouse"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`GuiHandler.java`). GUI position/rotation are room-space state. Controller ray-plane intersection maps physical hit to screen coordinates, smoothing before `InputSimulator.setMousePos`; GUI anchors can be head/hand/world/entity related. Transfer: spatialize the flat UI transform but keep the native mouse/widget system authoritative.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_physical_keyboard_hitboxes"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`PhysicalKeyboard.java`). Every key owns a 3D AABB; both controllers are transformed into keyboard local space and tracked independently. 150 ms debounce, 500 ms hold and 100 ms repeat implement tactile typing; callbacks remain ordinary key actions and pulses distinguish press/repeat. Transfer: physical UI requires explicit per-pointer contact state, not only a ray click.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_touch_hotbar"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`InteractiveHotbarModule.java`). Physical hand is projected onto the spatial hotbar line; 0.06 proximity gates selection; normalized line position maps to slots/offhand, haptics report changes. Commit writes native selected slot or sends normal SWAP_ITEM_WITH_OFFHAND packet. Transfer: let a 3D HUD be a physical control surface while preserving inventory authority.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_head_backpack_zone"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`BackpackTracker.java`). Rear-HMD body zone combines hand position/height/orientation and rearm state; incompatible attack/interact/bow/climb contexts suppress it. Valid entry triggers configured `VRInputAction` key binding and haptic. Transfer: use multi-condition body-relative zones before issuing a native semantic command.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_physical_posture_jump"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`SneakTracker`, `CrawlTracker`, `JumpTracker`). Standing calibration minus HMD height drives sneak; crawl uses smoothed HMD height/world scale to set native SWIMMING pose plus server packet/hysteresis; jump can use HMD upward motion or Climbey controller history and then native jump/body velocity. Transfer: physical posture is an intent sensor feeding existing locomotion states.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_physical_swimming"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`SwimTracker.java`). Controller midpoint relative to chest/HMD plus both controller BACK vectors form stroke direction; reduction of hand-to-body distance creates thrust, accumulated with friction. `player.push` applies motion and native swimming/sprint states follow speed. Transfer: physical stroke detector should output native player force, not run a parallel water simulation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_water_aware_rowing"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`RowTracker.java`, `AbstractBoatMixin`). Oar model reconstructs boat attachment points and arm→paddle vectors; liquid test gates force. Underwater point displacement minus boat velocity is projected onto boat forward and clamped; mixin converts per-paddle force/controller-speed terms into native delta rotation and acceleration. Transfer: derive control forces from tracked contact geometry, then modify existing vehicle inputs.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_physical_telescope_pass"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`TelescopeTracker.java`, `VRRenderer.getRenderPasses`, `VRPassHelper`). Lens origin/orientation comes from tracked controller; each eye computes distance/alignment percentage. Valid viewing enters ordinary telescope item use and the renderer can schedule SCOPEL/SCOPER dedicated targets. Transfer: physical optic engagement can select a specialized render pass without hijacking the main HMD camera.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_semantic_haptic_layers"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`OpenVRHapticScheduler`, `MCVR`, haptic network payloads, HapticTracker/BodyLink rig). Scheduled controller pulses call `VRInput_TriggerHapticVibrationAction` for logical body parts/controllers; server can request semantic part-targeted pulses. Separate body rig spatializes damage/environmental feedback. Transfer: expose semantic haptic destinations/events and let output backends schedule device-specific actuation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_mod_facing_vr_api"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (`VRClientAPI`, `VRRenderingAPI`, registration event, Tracker/InteractModule API). API exposes distinct room/world pre/post/render poses, pose history, FBT mode/world scale, haptics and keyboard controls and lets mods register trackers/interact modules before game loop. Transfer: publish the same stable timing/space/action abstractions that internal features use.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vivecraft_mixin_adapter_architecture"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (multiloader tree + `mixin` packages). Core provider/render/gameplay/network code is shared; loader-specific integration uses targeted Mixins/adapters for projectile origin, item POV, player picking, inventory, boat physics, render/UI seams. Transfer: keep version-sensitive injection points thin and place reusable VR policy in testable subsystem classes.";

CODE_AUDITS["seed_valheimvr_case"] = "VERIFIED FROM SUPPLIED VHVR 0.9.21 SOURCE. ARCHITECTURE: `ValheimVRMod` is a BepInEx `BaseUnityPlugin`; `VRManager` loads Unity XR/OpenVR settings from AssetBundle, pre-initializes SteamVR actions, starts XR subsystems and SteamVR, then Harmony patches map tracked state into Valheim. RENDERING: `VRPlayer.enableVrCamera` copies the native camera into a tracked XR camera and splits hands/skybox/world-space UI/mirror layers. `VRTaaComponent` maintains per-eye temporal history/jitter and `FogComponentPatches` uses the active eye frustum. Unity XR owns low-level compositor frame timing.\n\nCAMERA/SPACES: `EyeRotationPatch` separates HMD eye rotation from body/player yaw; roomscale movement counter-translates the rig while native body/Rigidbody displacement is applied elsewhere. BODY IK: FinalIK VRIK has head, pelvis, arm and local leg targets; hip and foot trackers can be auto-detected/calibrated and leg weights are context gated. Remote VRIK intentionally removes legs. WEAPONS/MELEE: mesh-derived weapon geometry and equip-specific two-hand providers position tracked weapons. Weapon/fist/foot colliders use camera-rig-relative motion for physical intent/contact, then start native `Attack`; block/parry logic feeds native `Humanoid.BlockAttack`. PROJECTILES: one `Attack.GetProjectileSpawnPoint` Harmony seam supplies physical bow/crossbow/magic/fishing/throwable origins/aim. Bow draw is physical; crossbow cock/bolt placement drives native reload state; throwing combines linear+angular controller velocity. INTERACTION/UI: independent hand rays call native `Player.Interact`; building manager authors spatial placement while preserving native validation; `VRGUI` reuses Unity GUI through RenderTexture + overlay/quad and fake mouse; `VRHud` redistributes native widgets to wrist/camera/build canvases. LOCOMOTION: roomscale body movement, physical sneak, hand/foot gestured movement, swim, jump/dodge and foot-tracker walk/slide feed native motion semantics. VEHICLES: `ShipSteering` and `Reining` convert physical hand gestures to native ship/saddle controls.\n\nMULTIPLAYER: `VRPlayerSync` writes head/hands/pelvis/velocity/fingers/bow/handedness/two-hand state to ZDO `vr_data`; remote transforms interpolate and drive VRIK, but remote legs are absent and transmitted velocity is not used for extrapolation.\n\nHAPTICS: controller `hapticAction.Execute` and separate bHaptics event patches coexist. SOURCE-BOUNDARIES: no explicit OpenXR predicted-time frame owner in this snapshot; no remote tracked-leg FBT; generic manual-magazine reload is not demonstrated beyond the crossbow physical reload workflow.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_bepinex_xrsdk_asset_bootstrap"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: ValheimVRMod.cs; VRCore/VRManager.cs; Utilities/VRAssetManager.cs; Patches/HarmonyPatcher.cs; ValheimVRMod.csproj; README. Reusable pattern: when a shipped Unity game cannot author new XR project settings/prefabs itself, build those artifacts externally and load them at runtime, while keeping version-sensitive Harmony patches thin.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_camera_stack_layer_replacement"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/VRPlayer.cs; Utilities/CameraUtils.cs; Utilities/LayerUtils.cs. Transfer: copy the target game camera first so title-specific fog/post/depth settings survive, then separate VR-only presentation surfaces by layer instead of mutating every renderer.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_stereo_taa_per_eye_history"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/PostProcessing/VRTaaComponent.cs; Patches/PostProcessingPatches.cs. Key symbols: ConfigureStereoJitteredProjectionMatrices, CopyStereoDeviceProjectionMatrixToNonJittered, SetStereoProjectionMatrix, m_HistoryTextures[eye], Render. Transfer: temporal buffers and projection jitter are eye-local state; never share a flat-camera history buffer between eyes.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_stereo_fog_motionvector_repair"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Patches/FogComponentPatches.cs; Patches/PostProcessingPatches.cs. Transfer: audit every post effect for view-direction/frustum/history assumptions; use active-eye projection/frustum data and ensure moving objects emit camera-relative motion vectors expected by temporal effects.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_hmd_body_yaw_reconciliation"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Patches/EyeRotationPatch.cs; Patches/ControlPatches.cs; VRCore/VRPlayer.cs. Transfer: model HMD view orientation and gameplay body yaw as separate authorities and explicitly consume/rebase artificial/body turns.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_finalik_fullbody_targets"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/VrikCreator.cs. Key symbols: CreateTargets, InitializeTargts, resetVrikHandTransform, VRIK.solver.leftArm/rightArm/leftLeg/rightLeg/spine. This is genuine body/limb IK, not viewmodel-only skeletal correction.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_tracker_autocalibration_dynamic_leg_ik"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/VRPlayer.cs (caliberateBodyTracking, caliberateFeet, detectHipDeviceIndex, detectFootDeviceIndex, updateBodyTracking); Scripts/VrikCreator.cs. Transfer: make tracker semantic assignment/calibration a separate stage from the IK solver, then arbitrate tracked-vs-animation authority dynamically.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_fbt_feet_gameplay_bridge"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/FootCollision.cs; VRCore/VRPlayer.cs; Scripts/GesturedLocomotionManager.cs; Scripts/VrikCreator.cs. Transfer: build one semantic body-part pose and let IK and gameplay consumers use it under their own validity/cooldown rules.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_roomscale_rigidbody_reconciliation"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/VRPlayer.cs; Patches/RoomscalePatches.cs. Source boundary: this is native Rigidbody/ground reconciliation plus clamp/fade detection, not a separate custom swept VR capsule solver.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_posture_sneak_sit"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/VRPlayer.cs; ControlPatches.cs. Transfer: physical posture should be a semantic sensor around the native stance system, with hysteresis and IK arbitration for transitions.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_gestured_locomotion_bus"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/GesturedLocomotionManager.cs; Patches/ControlPatches.cs. Transfer: keep gesture recognizers independent and make their shared output semantic/native movement intent rather than direct physics ownership.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_foot_tracked_walk_slide"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/GesturedLocomotionManager.cs (WalkInPlace, SlideInPlace); VRCore/VRPlayer.cs foot tracking. Transfer: separate tracker calibration from gait classification and require posture/ground checks so arbitrary tracker noise cannot become locomotion.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_mesh_derived_weapon_geometry"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/WeaponWield.cs; Utilities/WeaponUtils.cs; Scripts/TwoHandedGeometry.cs. Transfer: derive a reusable geometric description (axis/length/rear-grip distance) from content rather than maintaining a brittle per-weapon table.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_twohand_geometry_solver"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/WeaponWield.cs; Scripts/LocalWeaponWield.cs; Scripts/TwoHandedGeometry.cs. Transfer: keep hand-order/ownership state separate from geometry policy so unusual weapon classes can swap grip logic without replacing the generic two-hand solver.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_tracking_space_weapon_collision_native_attack"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/WeaponCollision.cs; Patches/CollisionPatches.cs; Utilities/PhysicsEstimator.cs; Utilities/WeaponUtils.cs; Utilities/StaticObjects.cs. Transfer: physical detection should choose spatial intent/contact while the native combat transaction remains authoritative.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_fist_foot_attack"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/FistCollision.cs; Scripts/FootCollision.cs; Scripts/AttackTargetMeshCooldown.cs; Patches/CollisionPatches.cs. Transfer: body-part collision becomes an input device, not a parallel damage implementation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_block_parry_native_authority"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/Block/ShieldBlock.cs; WeaponBlock.cs; FistBlock.cs; Patches/ShieldPatches.cs. Transfer: calculate VR defense intent/geometry before the native block function, not by directly reducing incoming damage.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_bow_draw_native_projectile"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/BowLocalManager.cs; Scripts/BowManager.cs; Utilities/BowAnatomy.cs; Patches/BowAndFishingPatches.cs. Transfer: physical draw should parameterize the flat game’s charge percentage, projectile spawn/aim and release transaction.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_crossbow_physical_cocking_bolt_insert"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/CrossbowMorphManager.cs; Scripts/CrossbowManager.cs; Utilities/CrossbowAnatomy.cs; Patches/BowAndFishingPatches.cs. Transfer: physical reload can gate/drive an existing reload queue without replacing the game’s ammo/load authority.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_throw_velocity_native_projectile"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/ThrowableManager.cs; Utilities/PhysicsEstimator.cs; Utilities/WeaponUtils.cs; Patches/BowAndFishingPatches.cs; Patches/ControlPatches.cs. Transfer: derive physical release kinematics once, then adapt the target game’s projectile-spawn seam.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_shared_projectile_spawn_adapter"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Patches/BowAndFishingPatches.cs; Scripts/BowLocalManager.cs; CrossbowManager.cs; ThrowableManager.cs; MagicWeaponManager.cs; FishingManager.cs. Transfer: patch the narrow spatial accessor shared by many attacks instead of duplicating VR launch logic in every weapon class.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_dual_hand_native_interaction_rays"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Patches/HandBasedInteractionPatches.cs; VRCore/VRPlayer.cs; VRCore/UI/VRControls.cs. Transfer: make hand targeting independent but reuse the game’s hover/use interfaces rather than synthesizing interaction effects.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_building_controller"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/BuildingManager.cs; related Control/UI hooks. Transfer: VR may own how a placement transform is authored, but native placement validation/access rules should remain the final authority.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_mouth_drink_and_context_hand_gestures"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/WeaponCollision.cs; Scripts/FistCollision.cs. Transfer: make body zones highly contextual and require multiple physical conditions before issuing the native action to avoid accidental activation.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_gui_rendertexture_overlay_quad"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/UI/VRGUI.cs; SoftwareCursor.cs; VRGUI_InputModule; UIPatches/TextInputPatches. Transfer: preserve the native widget/event tree and replace only render/pointer surfaces.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_spatial_hud_wrist_camera"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/UI/VRHud.cs; UIPatches.cs; Scripts/LeftHandQuickMenu.cs; RightHandQuickMenu.cs. Transfer: clone/reparent native widgets by semantic group instead of rewriting HUD state from scratch.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_physical_ship_controls"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/ShipSteering.cs. Transfer: physical vehicle gestures should output the flat vehicle’s existing discrete/analog controls, keeping vehicle physics authoritative.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_mount_rein_gestures"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/Reining.cs; BHapticsPatches ride feedback. Transfer: physical reins are a semantic input adapter around native mount authority.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_zdo_pose_finger_weapon_sync"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Scripts/VRPlayerSync.cs; Patches/MultiplayerPatches.cs; Scripts/VrikCreator.cs; Scripts/WeaponWieldSync.cs. Transfer: replicate semantic avatar presentation state alongside existing gameplay networking, and clearly separate locally available FBT from remotely transmitted body parts.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_steamvr_bhaptics_event_layers"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: Patches/BHapticsPatches.cs; Scripts/BhapticsTactsuit.cs; ShieldPatches.cs; BowLocalManager.cs; WeaponCollision.cs; other manager calls. Transfer: semantic feedback should be emitted after accepted gameplay outcomes and independently scheduled per output device.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_vhvr_semantic_input_native_control_bridge"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE: VRCore/VRManager.cs; VRCore/UI/VRControls.cs; Patches/ControlPatches.cs; Scripts/GesturedLocomotionManager.cs. Transfer: make the VR action layer device/runtime-facing and keep the game-facing side expressed in the title’s normal command vocabulary.";



// 2026-10-10 source-audit expansion: RDRVR source snapshot + two cross-engine repositories.
CODE_AUDITS["seed_re5vr_case"] = "VERIFIED FROM REPOSITORY DOCUMENTATION / TREE: official ResidentEvil5VR README (v0.5.0a) and public source tree names the D3D9 proxy, render hooks, stereo test/render-size/HUD-shader modules, OpenXR bridge, D3D12 addon bridge, UI/input-block and dgVoodoo addon projects. Documentation describes a double-wide two-eye pass, per-eye camera plus scissor, runtime-size negotiation, HUD shader recognition and quad panel, plus 6DOF arm IK, two-hand weapon posing, holsters and hand reload. EVIDENCE BOUNDARY: gameplay feature details are README claims; exact IK algorithms and uninspected function bodies are not asserted as verified source. Online is explicitly untested and manual reload has documented limits. No build/headset test was run for this registry update.";
CODE_AUDITS["seed_dxhrvr_case"] = "VERIFIED FROM REPOSITORY DOCUMENTATION / BUILD GRAPH: official DeusExHRVR README and CMakeLists.md document the 32-bit native HD3D renderer, x86 proxy DLL target names, x64 `DeusExHRVRHost.exe`, `NativeBridge`/`NativeTransport` modules, D3D11 keyed-mutex GPU pair transfer, OpenXR two-slice submission, bounded whole-pair drops, diagnostics probes and dual-architecture build instructions. Documentation describes HUD alignment, virtual-screen modes, split movement/interaction heading and experimental controller weapon pose. EVIDENCE BOUNDARY: exact source-function algorithms and all release/runtime behavior are not independently verified by execution. README explicitly states experimental prerelease, no roomscale body movement, no XR haptics and supported-executable restriction.";
CODE_AUDITS["seed_rdrvr_case"] = "VERIFIED FROM THE SUPPLIED RDRVR-main.zip SNAPSHOT. WEAPON SOURCE MAP: body.cpp::fixed_grip / fixed_grip_placed implement per-weapon gun-in-wrist calibration and final object placement; aim.cpp::hk_aim_ray / barrel_ray derive target ray, world muzzle and barrel direction from the placed weapon matrix and the tune's MuzzleOffset; aim.cpp::hk_fire preserves the original fire call with a scoped gameplay-camera swap; aim.cpp::hk_spawn handles the player's firearm-specific accuracy and obstruction seam; aim.cpp::hk_launch keeps native launch while separately adapting tracer origin, throw velocity/origin, copied-sidearm transforms and secondary-gun diagnosis. dual.cpp validates a secondary firearm from weapon-manager slots, tracks the native object/clip and uses generation/tick guards. controls.cpp maps tracked trigger/hand semantics onto native gamepad actions, including aim-trigger synthesis, held-aim lead/tail, left-hand mirroring and two-hand aim arbitration. holster.cpp owns spatial draw/holster/load/foregrip zones; reload.cpp classifies native reload request callsites and commits ammo through ACTOR_ADD_WEAPON_AMMO only after validation; actions.cpp models per-family revolver/lever/pump/bolt/breech/semi-auto/break-action readiness and blocks/re-arms the trigger; gestures.cpp owns hand-velocity throws, punch/stab semantic input, lasso gestures and gun-butt strike segment generation; gun_melee.cpp plus gameplay/main.cpp validate hit requests and invoke the guarded native ActorHit path. controllers.cpp and xinput.cpp map native rumble semantics to OpenXR haptics. EVIDENCE BOUNDARY: those source files were inspected in the supplied archive, but this update did not build the project or execute a gameplay session. README feature descriptions and headset/simulator test notes are documentation evidence, not independent runtime validation. RDR-specific offsets, weapon IDs, native hashes and hook callsites are build-bound. Unknown: exhaustive compatibility for every weapon, runtime, weapon-wheel transition, all throwable release modes, online behavior and every dual-wield/reload combination.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_guarded_proxy_bootstrap"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): ARCHITECTURE / BOOTSTRAP: `src/core/bootstrap.cpp` starts the core from the dinput8 proxy, detects/waits for the `MTLX.dll` stage to unload, and explains that Steam-delivered game code is not patchable until its wrapper has completed. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/bootstrap.cpp, tree/main/src/core/anchors.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_same_frame_dual_pass"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): RENDERING / SYNCHRONIZATION: `src/core/dual_pass.cpp` uses thread-local `t_pass` and `t_double` to identify first and second eye. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/dual_pass.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_openxr_rotation_late_latch"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): FRAME TIMING: `src/core/xr.cpp` obtains `XrFrameState.predictedDisplayTime` from `xrWaitFrame`, locates views against that time, and then performs a near-render `late_latch()` lookup against the stored `g_display_time`. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/xr.cpp, tree/main/src/core/camera_lever.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_per_eye_temporal_histories"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): RENDERING / TEMPORAL STATE: `src/core/taa.cpp` tracks post-process executions and history buffers per eye/pass; `src/core/dlss.cpp` maintains separate DLSS structures/history where supported, corrects jitter units, and has a fallback path when separate eye setup fails. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/taa.cpp, tree/main/src/core/dlss.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_shared_shadow_pass"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): RENDERING / SHADOW OPTIMIZATION: `src/core/dual_pass.cpp` classifies a known shadow call site into Original, Skip or Union. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/dual_pass.cpp, blob/main/README.md. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_runtime_sized_eye_targets"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): RENDERING / RESOLUTION: `src/core/render_res.cpp` arms resolution changes early and tracks whether the requested headset target is feasible; `src/core/eye_shape.cpp` derives an eye-shaped viewport/target from the runtime FOV and panel dimensions, including an upscaler path. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/render_res.cpp, tree/main/src/core/eye_shape.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_camera_anchor_mount_spaces"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): CAMERA / VEHICLES: `src/core/pose.cpp` emits `RdrvrCameraJob` values through the C ABI while `src/gameplay/main.cpp` runs the native camera update at the safe script-tick seam. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/pose.cpp, blob/main/src/common/rdrvr_api.h. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_controller_target_two_bone_arm_ik"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): BODY IK: `src/core/body.cpp` builds explicit upper-arm, elbow and wrist bone sets, separates deforming bone subtrees from attachment subtrees, locates wrist targets, and applies a two-bone solve with bend/pole information and bounded stretch. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/body.cpp, tree/main/src/core/hands.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
CODE_AUDITS["pattern_rdrvr_calibrated_weapon_pose_native_fire"] = "VERIFIED FROM SOURCE (provided RDRVR-main.zip): WEAPONS / NATIVE AUTHORITY: `src/core/aim.cpp` uses grip-local offsets to align each weapon model with the tracked hand, calculates `barrel_ray()` for muzzle origin and direction, and hooks native fire/shot/projectile operations (`hk_fire`, `hk_spawn`, `hk_launch`) to adjust direction at the game’s own shot transaction. Relevant files: tree/main/src/core/aim.cpp. Transfer recommendation and its guardrails are recorded in the implementation details; do not copy game-specific offsets or assume unsupported capabilities.";
